window.LANTERN_LIBRARY = [{"id":"a-photo-for-the-new-cafe","type":"story","title":"A Photo for the New Café","author":"Lantern Original","level":"A1","genre":"comedy","summaryTr":"Nora yeni kafesinin güzel bir fotoğrafını çekmek ister. Fakat kapı her açıldığında fotoğrafa yeni bir sorun ve yeni bir misafir eklenir.","paragraphs":["Nora has a new café. It is small, with four tables and a blue door. There are yellow flowers in the window. Today is Friday. The café opens on Saturday, and Nora needs a photo for the window of the town shop.","Her brother Ben has a camera. He arrives at nine with a big bag and a little notebook. Nora looks at the bag. 'Is all that for one photo?' she asks. 'A good photo takes work,' says Ben. He puts his notebook on a table.","Nora wants a very simple photo. She wants one table, two cups, a cake, and the flowers. 'No people,' she says. 'Just a quiet, beautiful café.' Ben looks around. 'Good. Please stand behind me. Your shoes are in the picture.' Nora moves.","Ben takes a white cup from the shelf. He puts it next to a blue cup. Then he moves the blue cup to the left. He moves it to the right. Nora waits. 'Does the cup know where to go now?' she asks.","Ben does not answer. He puts a small cake between the cups. The cake has a red cherry on top. He sits down, looks through the camera, and stands up again. 'The table is too low,' he says. 'Or you are too tall,' says Nora.","He looks for a small chair. There is one near the kitchen door. It is green, and one leg is short. Nora puts a little piece of paper under the leg. Ben sits on it. The chair makes a loud sound. Ben stands up very quickly.","'That chair says no,' says Nora. Ben gives her the camera. He goes to get another chair. Nora looks through the camera. She can see the table, the cups, and the cake. She can also see Ben's big bag. 'Your shoes are not in the picture,' she says. 'But your whole bag is.'","At that moment, the blue door opens. Mr Bell comes in with a box. He has the bookshop next door. 'Here are your menus,' he says. 'They look very nice.' He opens one. There is a picture of a cup on the front.","'Thank you,' says Nora. 'Can you put them in the kitchen?' Mr Bell walks past the table. He stops and looks at the cake. 'Is that for me?' he asks. 'It is for the photo,' says Ben. 'Oh,' says Mr Bell. 'Can the photo share?'","Nora gives Mr Bell a different cake from the kitchen. He sits at a table by the door. 'I can wait here,' he says. Ben looks through the camera. Now there is a man with a cake in the picture. Ben moves the camera a little.","The door opens again. It is Nora's friend Eva. She has a tall plant in her arms. 'A present for your café!' she says. The leaves are in front of her face. 'Hello, Eva,' says Nora. 'I think it is Eva,' says Mr Bell.","They put the plant near the window. It looks lovely there. Ben looks through the camera. One long leaf is over the small cake. He moves the plant. The leaf is now over the blue cup. He moves it again. The leaf is over his face.","Eva takes the plant to the other window. 'It can watch from here,' she says. 'I can watch too.' She sits next to Mr Bell. Nora gives her a cake. 'The café is not open yet,' Ben says. 'It is very good at being closed,' says Eva.","Ben checks the table again. The cups look nice. The cake looks nice. But something is different. He puts down the camera. 'Where is the cherry?' Nora looks at the cake. The top is white. There is a little red mark in the middle.","Everyone looks at Mr Bell. He holds up his own cake. 'My cake has a cherry,' he says. Everyone looks at Eva. She opens her empty hands. Then they hear a small voice at the door. 'Is the cake shop open, Mum?'","A little boy stands beside a woman with a shopping bag. His name is Max. His mouth is red. 'Max,' says the woman, 'do you have something to tell us?' Max looks at the white cake. 'It needs a cherry,' he says.","His mother says sorry. Nora laughs and gets another cherry. She gives Max a small biscuit and shows him a seat. 'This biscuit is for you. That cake is for a picture.' Max looks at Ben. 'Does your camera eat cake?' he asks.","Ben tries to explain. Max listens carefully. Then he asks, 'Why are there two cups if nobody drinks?' Ben opens his mouth. He closes it again. Nora brings tea for Max's mother. 'That is a good question,' she says.","The sun comes through the window. Now one side of the table is very bright. Ben asks Nora to close the curtain a little. She closes it. 'Too dark,' he says. She opens it. 'Too bright.' Nora looks at him. 'Would you like to talk to the sun?'","Eva has an idea. She holds up a large menu near the window. It makes a little shade on the table. The cake looks good. But Eva's hand is in the picture. She tries to hold the menu higher. Now her arm is in the picture.","Mr Bell gets up to help. He holds the menu from the other side. Eva sits down. Ben smiles. 'Yes! That is good. Nobody move.' The door opens, and Mr Bell turns his head. The menu falls on the cake.","For a moment, nobody speaks. Then Max says, 'The menu is eating it.' Nora starts to laugh. Eva laughs too. Even Ben laughs. Mr Bell lifts the menu very slowly. There is white cream on the picture of the cup.","The new visitor is a woman in a red coat. She carries a small bag of bread. 'I am looking for the bookshop,' she says. Mr Bell raises his hand. 'That is my shop.' 'Good,' she says. 'I need a book about taking photos.'","Ben sits down on the good chair. 'You can have my camera with the book,' he says. Nora gives him a glass of water. She puts a fresh cake on the table. The woman in the red coat looks around. 'What a nice place,' she says.","'Thank you,' says Nora. 'We open tomorrow.' The woman looks at the people drinking tea and eating cakes. She looks at Nora. 'Of course,' she says. 'Tomorrow.' Mr Bell takes her to his bookshop, but he leaves his coat on his chair.","Ben is ready again. He looks through the camera. 'Where are the yellow flowers?' he asks. Nora points to the window. The flowers are there, but the tall plant is in front of them. Eva moves the plant. Max moves his chair to see.","Max's mother moves her shopping bag. Nora moves the flowers. Ben moves his notebook. Everyone is moving something. 'Stop!' says Ben. Everyone stops. He looks through the camera. In the middle of the picture is one large, empty shopping bag.","Nora takes the bag away. Then she sees a small problem with the café sign. It is on the wall, but the last letter is behind the flowers. 'People need to see our name,' she says. Ben moves the flowers. Now the name is clear, but the clock is behind a yellow flower.","Max offers to hold the flowers. He stands beside the table with both hands around the pot. The flowers cover his face. 'I am not a person now,' he says. 'I am a garden.' Nora smiles and puts the pot on a different shelf. Max sits down again. 'Can the garden have some water?' he asks. His mother gives him his glass.","Ben takes a photo at last. He looks at the little screen on his camera. He does not smile. Nora looks too. They can see two cups, a cake, and beautiful yellow flowers. Behind them, the café looks dark and empty.","'Is that our café?' asks Nora. 'Yes,' says Ben. She looks from the screen to the room. Eva is showing Max how to fold a paper boat. His mother is looking at a menu. There is sunlight on the blue door. 'It feels different,' Nora says.","Mr Bell comes back with the woman in the red coat. She has two books now. 'Can we buy some tea?' she asks. Nora looks at Ben. Then she looks at the clock. It is nearly eleven. 'Yes,' she says. 'I think we are open.'","Ben puts the white cup back on the shelf. He gives the blue cup to Nora for the tea. 'What about your photo?' she asks. 'I have another idea,' he says. He picks up his bag and puts it behind the kitchen door.","He stands in the corner. Nora brings tea to the table. Eva helps Max put his paper boat on a plate. Mr Bell shows the woman a page in her new book. Nobody looks at the camera. Ben takes a photo. Then he takes another.","He shows Nora the screen. The room is full of light and people. Nora is smiling. The plant is very big, and one leaf is over Mr Bell's head. Max's paper boat is next to the cake. 'That is my café,' says Nora.","They choose the second photo. Ben writes the name of the café under it. Nora adds the opening times. She looks at the cake on the table. It still has its red cherry. 'You can eat it now, Ben,' she says. 'Your work is finished.'","Ben takes a fork. Everyone watches him. He stops. 'What?' he asks. Max points to the camera. 'First, we need a photo.' Ben puts down his fork. Nora takes the camera, and everyone laughs. This time, the photo is very easy."],"glossary":{"screen":"ekran","shade":"gölge","cream":"krema","fold":"katlamak","cherry":"kiraz","opens":"açılıyor","takes":"çekiyor","leg":"ayak, mobilya ayağı"},"phrases":[{"en":"looks around","tr":"etrafına bakar"},{"en":"next to","tr":"yanında"},{"en":"in front of","tr":"önünde"},{"en":"at that moment","tr":"tam o anda"},{"en":"puts down","tr":"elindekini bırakır"},{"en":"at last","tr":"sonunda"},{"en":"picks up","tr":"yerden alır"},{"en":"opening times","tr":"açılış saatleri"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Nora need a photo?","options":["For a book about flowers","For the town shop window","For Max's school","For Ben's notebook"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Nora, kasabadaki dükkânın vitrinine koymak için kafesinin fotoğrafını ister."},{"q":"What does Eva bring?","options":["A camera","Some menus","A tall plant","A green chair"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Eva kafeye hediye olarak uzun bir bitki getirir."},{"q":"Who eats the first cherry?","options":["Ben","Eva","Mr Bell","Max"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Max'ın ağzı kırmızıdır ve annesi ondan açıklama ister; kirazı yiyen Max'tır."},{"q":"What falls on the cake?","options":["A menu","A book","A paper boat","A shopping bag"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Bay Bell kapıya bakınca tuttuğu menü pastanın üzerine düşer."},{"q":"Why does Nora like the second photo at the end?","options":["The café is empty","All the cups are white","It shows people enjoying the café","Nobody is smiling"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Son fotoğraf insanlarla ve ışıkla doludur; Nora kendi kafesinin gerçek havasını onda görür."}],"words":1651,"length":"medium"},{"id":"pasha-knows-everything","type":"story","title":"Pasha Knows Everything","author":"Lantern Original","level":"A1","genre":"comedy","summaryTr":"Tom'un yeni papağanı Pasha duyduğu her şeyi yüksek sesle tekrar ediyor. Üstelik bu akşam Tom'un patronu yemeğe geliyor ve Tom, patronu hakkında pek de güzel şeyler söylememişti.","paragraphs":["Tom has a new parrot. It is big and green. Its name is Pasha.","Pasha is very clever. He hears every word. Then he says it again, very loudly.","Tom's wife, Ayla, is not happy about this.","\"Be careful, Tom,\" she says. \"Pasha hears everything.\"","\"I am always careful,\" says Tom.","\"No, you are not. Every evening you say, 'My boss is boring.' And Pasha hears you.\"","\"Only from Monday to Friday,\" says Tom.","Ayla looks at him. \"Tom, listen. Tonight Mr. Grant is coming for dinner.\"","Tom's face goes white. \"Tonight? Mr. Grant? My boss?\"","\"Yes. He comes at seven. Pasha stays in the bedroom. And you say only nice things. Only nice things!\"","\"OK, OK,\" says Tom.","At six o'clock, Ayla puts Pasha in the bedroom and closes the door. Pasha is very quiet. Tom does not like it.","At seven, the doorbell rings. Mr. Grant is a tall man with a very yellow tie.","\"Good evening, Mr. Grant!\" says Tom. \"What a beautiful tie!\"","\"Thank you,\" says Mr. Grant. \"My wife hates it.\"","\"She is wrong,\" says Tom quickly.","They sit at the table. Ayla brings the soup.","\"Mmm,\" says Mr. Grant. \"This soup is very good.\"","Tom smiles. Ayla smiles. Everything is fine.","Then a loud voice comes from the bedroom.","\"GRANT IS BORING! GRANT IS BORING!\"","Nobody moves. Mr. Grant puts down his spoon.","\"What is that?\" he asks.","\"The TV,\" says Tom.","\"The TV says my name?\"","\"It is a... new program,\" says Tom. \"About a man called Grant. A different Grant. A very exciting Grant.\"","\"UGLY TIE! UGLY TIE!\" shouts the voice.","Mr. Grant looks at his tie. \"That is a strange program.\"","Ayla laughs very loudly. \"Ha ha ha! More soup, Mr. Grant?\"","Then there is a small click. The bedroom door opens. Pasha walks into the room. He walks across the floor and climbs onto the table. He stops next to Mr. Grant's plate.","Mr. Grant looks at the parrot. The parrot looks at Mr. Grant.","\"Pasha?\" says Mr. Grant.","\"Hello, Grant!\" says Pasha.","\"That is MY parrot!\" says Mr. Grant. \"I gave him to the pet shop last month!\"","Tom looks at Ayla. Ayla looks at Tom.","\"We bought him at the pet shop,\" she says in a small voice.","\"Why did you give him away?\" asks Tom.","Mr. Grant's face goes red. \"No reason. More soup, please.\"","Pasha opens his mouth. Now his voice is deep. It is Mr. Grant's voice.","\"Tom is the best worker in the office!\"","Tom's mouth opens. \"What?\"","\"But I never tell him,\" says the deep voice. \"Because Tom wants my job!\"","Nobody says a word.","\"The parrot is lying,\" says Mr. Grant.","\"TOM WANTS MY JOB! TOM WANTS MY JOB!\" shouts Pasha.","Mr. Grant looks at Tom. \"Do you want my job?\"","Tom looks at his soup. He looks at Ayla. She looks out of the window.","\"Yes,\" says Tom. \"A little.\"","For a long time, Mr. Grant says nothing. Then he starts to laugh.","\"An honest man,\" he says. \"OK, Tom. Next month you get a bigger office and a better job.\"","\"Really? Thank you, Mr. Grant!\"","\"Not MY job,\" says Mr. Grant. \"A different job.\"","\"Of course!\"","\"And one more thing. This parrot knows every secret in my house. Please keep him in YOUR house.\"","\"GRANT SINGS IN THE SHOWER!\" shouts Pasha. \"GRANT SINGS IN THE SHOWER!\"","Mr. Grant closes his eyes.","\"Ayla,\" he says. \"Do you have a very big blanket?\""],"glossary":{"tie":"kravat","program":"televizyon programı","office":"ofis, büro"},"phrases":[{"en":"be careful","tr":"dikkatli olmak"},{"en":"come for dinner","tr":"yemeğe gelmek"},{"en":"What a beautiful tie!","tr":"Ne güzel bir kravat! (What a + sıfat + isim = ünlem)"},{"en":"give away","tr":"(birine) vermek, elden çıkarmak"},{"en":"put down","tr":"yere/masaya bırakmak"},{"en":"for a long time","tr":"uzun süre"},{"en":"in a small voice","tr":"çekingen/kısık bir sesle"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Ayla put Pasha in the bedroom?","options":["Pasha is ill.","Tom's boss is coming for dinner.","Pasha wants to sleep.","She wants to clean the room."],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Ayla, Pasha her şeyi tekrar ettiği için onu yatak odasına koyar; Tom'un patronu Mr. Grant yemeğe gelmektedir."},{"q":"What does Tom say the voice from the bedroom is?","options":["The radio","A neighbour","The TV","A telephone"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Tom, sesin televizyonda yeni bir programdan geldiğini söyler."},{"q":"Where did Mr. Grant give Pasha away?","options":["To a pet shop","To his sister","To a school","To Tom"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Mr. Grant papağanı geçen ay evcil hayvan dükkânına vermiştir."},{"q":"What does Mr. Grant give Tom at the end?","options":["A yellow tie","Mr. Grant's own job","A new parrot","A bigger office and a better job"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Mr. Grant, Tom'a daha büyük bir ofis ve daha iyi bir iş verir, ama kendi işini değil."}],"words":581,"length":"short"},{"id":"cave-under-the-fig-tree","type":"story","title":"The Cave Under the Fig Tree","author":"Lantern Original","level":"A1","genre":"adventure","summaryTr":"Dedenin tek bir kuralı var: Mavi Mağara'ya asla girmeyin. Eleni ve kuzeni Niko kurala uyar mı? Deniz geri gelmeden önce yalnızca on dakikaları var.","paragraphs":["Eleni's grandfather lived in a white house by the sea. Every summer, Eleni stayed with him. Her little cousin Niko stayed there, too.","Grandpa had one rule.","\"Never go to the Blue Cave,\" he said. \"The tide comes in fast. Do you understand?\"","\"Yes, Grandpa,\" said Eleni.","Niko said nothing. He was looking at the rocks.","On Tuesday morning, Grandpa went out early. Niko ran to Eleni.","\"Let's go to the Blue Cave,\" he said.","\"No. Grandpa said no.\"","\"The sea is very low today. Look! We go in, we look, we come out. Ten minutes.\"","Eleni looked at the sea. It really was low. She loved adventures more than rules.","\"Ten minutes,\" she said.","They walked over the wet rocks to the cave. Inside, it was dark, but the walls were blue. Light came up through the water. It was beautiful.","\"Wow,\" said Niko.","Then he saw something on the floor. It was shining.","\"Eleni! A coin! A gold coin!\"","It was old and heavy. Niko held it up like a king.","\"There are more,\" he said. He looked behind every rock, and Eleni helped him. They forgot the time.","Then Eleni heard a sound. Whoosh.","She turned. Water was running into the cave. The door of the cave was already half under water.","\"It's the tide!\" she said. \"The sea is coming back!\"","They ran to the door. The water was up to their knees. A big wave hit them and they fell.","\"Back!\" shouted Eleni. \"Go back!\"","They climbed the rocks inside the cave and sat on a high stone. The water came up, and up, and up.","Niko began to cry. \"I want to go home! I want Grandpa!\" He shouted so loudly that the walls shook.","\"Where is your phone?\" Eleni asked.","\"At home. Where is yours?\"","\"At home.\"","It was cold. It was dark. The water was now very near their feet.","Eleni looked up. Then she saw it: a thin line of light, high above her head. A small hole!","\"Niko, stop shouting. Look!\"","\"Where?\"","\"There!\"","She climbed. The rock was wet and her hands were cold. Two times she almost fell. Niko came behind her, still crying a little.","At the top, a flat stone covered the hole. Eleni pushed it with both hands. Nothing. She pushed again. The stone moved.","Sun! Green leaves! And the smell of coffee.","She pulled herself out, and then she pulled Niko out. They lay on the grass, wet and shaking. Above them was a big old tree. It was the fig tree in Grandpa's garden.","And there, at the table under the tree, sat Grandpa. He had a cup of coffee in his hand. Next to him on the table was a long rope.","He did not look up.","\"One hour,\" he said. \"Not bad. Your mother needed two.\"","\"You knew?\" said Eleni.","\"Of course I knew. I heard Niko. The whole island heard Niko.\"","Niko's face went red.","\"But you said never!\" said Eleni.","\"Yes.\" Grandpa drank his coffee. \"If I say 'go', children stay at home. If I say 'never', they go. My father did the same to me.\"","Niko held up the coin. \"And this?\"","\"Ah.\" Grandpa smiled. \"I put it there this morning at five o'clock.\"","Niko's mouth opened, but no words came out.","\"One coin for each child, every summer,\" said Grandpa. He stood up and put the rope over his shoulder. \"Now go and dry your clothes.\"","\"Can we go again tomorrow?\" asked Niko.","\"No,\" said Grandpa. \"Tomorrow I make a new rule.\"","\"What rule?\"","Grandpa looked at the old boat on the beach. \"Never, ever touch that boat.\"","Eleni looked at Niko. Niko looked at Eleni. They smiled."],"glossary":{"tide":"gelgit (deniz seviyesinin inip kalkması)","fig":"incir","rope":"ip, halat","rule":"kural"},"phrases":[{"en":"Do you understand?","tr":"Anladın mı?"},{"en":"look up","tr":"yukarı bakmak"},{"en":"come in","tr":"içeri gelmek (deniz için: kabarmak)"},{"en":"the whole island","tr":"bütün ada"},{"en":"forget the time","tr":"zamanı unutmak"},{"en":"hold up","tr":"havaya kaldırmak"},{"en":"go again","tr":"tekrar gitmek"},{"en":"Not bad.","tr":"Fena değil."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is Grandpa's rule?","options":["Do not swim at night.","Do not touch the boat.","Never go to the Blue Cave.","Never climb the fig tree."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Dede, çocuklara Mavi Mağara'ya asla gitmemelerini söyler."},{"q":"What does Niko find in the cave?","options":["A gold coin","A key","A map","A phone"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Niko mağarada eski, altın bir madeni para bulur."},{"q":"How do Eleni and Niko get out?","options":["They swim to the beach.","Grandpa pulls them out.","They walk out of the cave door.","Through a hole under the fig tree."],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Mağaranın tepesindeki delikten çıkarlar; delik, dedenin bahçesindeki incir ağacının altındadır."},{"q":"Who put the coin in the cave?","options":["Niko","Grandpa","Eleni's mother","A pirate"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Dede parayı sabah saat beşte oraya kendisi koymuştur."}],"words":620,"length":"short"},{"id":"fables-from-aesop","type":"story","title":"Fables from Aesop","author":"Aesop (adapted by Lantern)","level":"A2","genre":"fable","source":{"name":"Aesop's Fables, translated by George Fyler Townsend — Project Gutenberg #21; simplified adaptation by Lantern","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/21","license":"Public domain (source translation); this graded adaptation is by Lantern"},"summaryTr":"Aesop’un en sevilen sekiz masalı sade ve kısa cümlelerle yeniden yazıldı: aslan ile fare, karınca ile çekirge, tavşan ile kaplumbağa ve daha fazlası. Her masal kısa bir dersle biter; A2 seviyesinde okumaya başlamak için ideal.","paragraphs":["## The Lion and the Mouse","A big lion was sleeping under a tree. A little mouse ran across his face. The lion woke up and was very angry. He caught the mouse with his big paw.","“Please let me go,” said the mouse. “I am very small, but one day I can help you. I promise.”","The lion laughed. “You? Help me? That is very funny!” But he opened his paw, and the mouse ran away.","A few days later, some hunters caught the lion in a net. They tied him to a tree with strong ropes. The lion roared and roared, but he could not get free.","The mouse heard the noise and ran to the lion. “Don’t be afraid,” he said. He began to bite the ropes with his sharp little teeth. Soon the ropes broke, and the lion was free.","“Now you know,” said the mouse with a smile. “A small friend can be a big help.”","Be kind to everyone, because even a small friend can help you.","## The Ants and the Grasshopper","It was a hot summer day. The ants were working hard. They carried seeds and grain to their home under the ground. The grain was heavy, but the ants did not stop.","A grasshopper sat on a leaf and watched them. He had a little violin, and he played a happy song.","“Why do you work so hard?” he asked. “Come and sing with me!”","“We must get ready for winter,” said one ant. “Winter is long, and we can run out of food. You should make a plan too.”","The grasshopper laughed. “Winter is far away. Now is the time to have fun!”","So the ants worked all summer, and the grasshopper sang all summer.","Then winter came. Snow covered the fields, and there was no food anywhere. At last the hungry grasshopper knocked on the ants’ door.","“Please give me some food,” he said sadly. “I have nothing to eat.”","The ant looked at him. “In summer we worked, and you sang,” she said. “Now we have only enough food for ourselves.”","The grasshopper went away, hungry and cold.","Work today, so you are ready for the hard days.","## The Hare and the Tortoise","A hare was very proud of his legs. “I am the fastest animal in the forest,” he said every day. “Nobody can run like me!”","One morning he saw a tortoise on the road. She was walking very slowly, and the hare laughed at her. “You are so slow! Do you ever get anywhere?”","“I get there in the end,” said the tortoise quietly. “Let’s have a race, you and I.”","The hare laughed and laughed. “A race? With you? All right!”","The fox was the judge. He drew a line on the ground and shouted, “Ready, steady, go!”","The hare ran like the wind. In a minute he could not see the tortoise at all. The tortoise walked on, one slow step after another, and she never stopped.","Halfway to the end, the hare stopped. “This is too easy,” he said. “I will have a little sleep under this tree.” Soon he was fast asleep.","The tortoise walked past the sleeping hare and kept going. She was tired, but she did not stop.","At last the hare woke up. He ran as fast as he could, but it was too late. The tortoise was already at the end of the road, and all the animals were shouting her name.","Slow and steady wins the race.","## The Shepherd’s Boy and the Wolf","Once there was a boy. His job was to look after the village sheep. Every day he sat on a hill and watched them. It was boring, and he had nobody to talk to.","One day he thought of a way to have some fun. “Wolf! Wolf!” he shouted. “A wolf is eating the sheep! Help me!”","The men dropped their work and ran up the hill. But there was no wolf. The boy laughed and laughed. “Ha ha! It was a joke!”","A few days later, he wanted to play a trick on them again. “Wolf! Wolf!” he shouted. Again the men ran up the hill, and again there was no wolf.","Then one evening a real wolf came out of the forest. The boy’s legs began to shake. “Wolf! Wolf!” he cried. “A real wolf! Please help me!”","In the village, the men heard him. “He is telling the same lie again,” they said. “We will not go this time.” Nobody came.","That night the wolf had a very big dinner. The boy went home with tears in his eyes and no sheep.","Nobody believes a liar, even when he tells the truth.","## The Crow and the Pitcher","It was a very hot summer. There was no rain for weeks, and the rivers were dry. A thirsty crow flew over the fields and looked for water.","At last he saw a pitcher near a farm. “Water!” he thought, and he flew down to it.","But the pitcher was tall and thin, and the water was very low. His beak was not long enough. He tried to push the pitcher over, but it was too heavy.","“What can I do?” he said. “I don’t want to give up.” He sat and thought for a long time.","Then he saw some small stones on the ground. “I have an idea!” he said. He picked up a stone and dropped it into the pitcher. Plop! One by one, the stones went in, and slowly the water came up.","At last the water was high enough. The crow drank and drank, and he was very proud of his clever idea.","When you really need something, you find a way.","## The Fox and the Grapes","One hot afternoon, a hungry fox walked through a farmer’s field. She had no breakfast and no lunch, and her stomach made a loud noise.","Then she looked up. High on a vine hung some big, purple grapes. They looked sweet and juicy.","“Lunch!” said the fox. She jumped, but the grapes were too high. She ran very fast and jumped as high as she could. Still nothing. She tried again and again, until her legs hurt.","At last she sat down, tired and angry. A little bird in a tree was watching her.","The fox looked up at the grapes and lifted her nose. “I don’t want them anyway,” she said. “They are sour. Only a fool wants sour grapes.”","Then she walked away with her head high, and the little bird laughed.","It is easy to say you do not want something you cannot have.","## The Town Mouse and the Country Mouse","A country mouse lived in a small hole in a field. His life was quiet and simple. He ate seeds, roots and a little corn. It was not a lot of food, but he was happy.","One day his cousin, a town mouse, came to visit. She looked at his dinner and made a face. “Poor cousin! Come to the town with me. In my house there is a lot of food, and it is delicious.”","The country mouse was excited. They walked all night and came to a big house in the town.","The dining room was beautiful. On the table there was bread, cheese, cakes and sweet fruit. The country mouse’s eyes were very big. He took a bite of the cheese. It was the best food of his life.","Suddenly the door opened. “Run!” shouted the town mouse. They ran into a small hole in the wall, and their hearts beat fast. The cook walked in, took a plate and walked out again.","“It is nothing,” said the town mouse. “Let’s go on with our dinner.” But then a big cat jumped on the table! The two mice ran away again, even faster than before.","At last the country mouse spoke in a small voice. “Thank you, cousin, but I am going home. Your food is wonderful, but you are afraid all the time. At home I eat simple food, but I eat in peace.”","Simple food in peace is better than a rich dinner in fear.","## The North Wind and the Sun","One day the North Wind and the Sun had an argument. “I am stronger than you,” said the Wind. “No, I am stronger,” said the Sun.","Just then, a man came down the road. He was wearing a thick, warm coat.","“Let’s have a test,” said the Sun. “Which of us can make that man take off his coat? That one is stronger.”","“Easy!” said the North Wind. “Watch me.” He took a deep breath and blew hard. The trees shook, and dust flew everywhere. But the man held his coat tight. The Wind blew harder and harder, and the man held his coat tighter and tighter. At last the Wind ran out of breath and stopped.","“Now it is my turn,” said the Sun gently. She came out from behind a cloud and began to shine. The air became warm. Soon the man felt very hot. He wiped his face, and then he took off his coat and sat down by a river.","“I did it,” said the Sun. “And I did not need to shout.”","Being kind works better than being strong."],"glossary":{"pitcher":"testi, uzun ve dar ağızlı su kabı","tortoise":"kara kaplumbağa","judge":"hakem; yarışı yöneten kişi","sour":"ekşi (hikâyede: elde edemediği şeyi küçümsemek için söylenen söz)","country":"kır, kırsal bölge (Country Mouse = Kır Faresi)","town":"kasaba, şehir (Town Mouse = Şehir Faresi)"},"phrases":[{"en":"make a plan","tr":"plan yapmak"},{"en":"run out of","tr":"(bir şeyi) bitirmek, tükenmek"},{"en":"give up","tr":"vazgeçmek, pes etmek"},{"en":"fast asleep","tr":"derin uykuda"},{"en":"one by one","tr":"birer birer, tek tek"},{"en":"play a trick on","tr":"(birine) oyun oynamak, şaka yapmak"},{"en":"look after","tr":"bakmak, göz kulak olmak"},{"en":"take off","tr":"(giysiyi) çıkarmak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"How does the mouse help the lion?","options":["He brings the lion some food","He bites the ropes","He shows the hunters the wrong way","He calls the other animals"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Fare, aslanı bağlayan ipleri keskin dişleriyle kemirir ve aslan özgür kalır."},{"q":"Why does the grasshopper have nothing to eat in winter?","options":["The ants take his food","He loses his violin","He does not like the food","He sang all summer and did not get food ready"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Çekirge yaz boyunca şarkı söyler, kış için yiyecek hazırlamaz. Karıncalar ise çalışmıştır."},{"q":"Why does nobody come when the real wolf comes?","options":["The men think the boy is lying again","The men are afraid of the wolf","The men cannot hear the boy","The men are asleep"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Çoban çocuk daha önce yalan söylediği için köylüler bu sefer de şaka yaptığını sanır."},{"q":"Why does the country mouse go home?","options":["He does not like cheese","He is too tired to eat","He is afraid in the town and wants peace","He wants a bigger house"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Şehirde sürekli korku içinde yaşamak istemez; evinde sade yemek yer ama huzurludur."},{"q":"How does the Sun win the test?","options":["He blows very hard","He shines and makes the man warm","He hides behind a cloud all day","He asks the man for his coat"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Güneş nazikçe ısıtır; adam sıcaktan paltosunu çıkarır. Rüzgâr ise ne kadar eserse adam o kadar sıkı sarılmıştır."}],"words":1517,"length":"medium"},{"id":"page-213","type":"story","title":"Page 213","author":"Lantern Original","level":"A2","genre":"romance","summaryTr":"Defne her sabah vapurda aynı adamı görüyor: elinde kalın bir roman var, ama kitabın son sayfaları eksik. Defne ona söylemek istiyor, fakat çok utangaç ve adam cuma günü şehirden ayrılıyor.","paragraphs":["Every morning at ten past eight, Defne took the ferry from Kadıköy to Eminönü. And every morning, the same man was sitting on the same bench, reading the same fat book.","Defne never spoke to him. She was too shy. But she watched him over her tea, and she watched his book, too. Every day, the pages on the left side of the book got thicker, and the pages on the right side got thinner. He was coming to the end.","She did not know his name. In her head, she called him the Book Man.","On Monday, the Book Man reached the last page. Defne held her tea and watched.","He turned the page. There was nothing. He turned back. He turned forward again. He shook the book, as if the missing pages could fall out. Then he looked at the sea and read in a loud, sad voice:","\"'She opened the door, and she saw—' SAW WHAT?\"","A woman with a baby moved to another bench. Defne put her hand over her mouth, because she wanted to laugh.","Then she remembered something. She worked at Blue Door Books, a small English bookshop near the ferry port. She knew that book. It was called The Lighthouse Letters, and she had read it twice. She knew exactly what the girl saw. It was the best last page in the world.","On Tuesday, she put a new copy in her bag. On the ferry, she sat opposite the Book Man. Her heart was going boom, boom, boom.","He looked up. His eyes went to her bag. Big white letters on the bag said BLUE DOOR BOOKS. He looked at the letters for a long time. Then he looked at Defne, and he smiled a small, strange smile.","Defne turned red and looked at the sea. She forgot all her words. When she found them again, the ferry was in Eminönü and he was gone.","On Wednesday, she was ready. She had three sentences in her head. She stood up, took a deep breath and walked to his bench.","At that moment, a seagull flew down, took the simit out of her hand and flew away.","\"My simit!\" shouted Defne.","The Book Man laughed. It was a warm, easy laugh. \"Don't worry,\" he said. \"Istanbul seagulls are professional thieves. One took my sandwich on my first day.\"","Defne laughed, too. Her three sentences were gone, so she said the only thing she could remember. \"Your book stops on page 212, doesn't it?\"","He stared at her. \"How do you know?\"","\"I watched you on Monday. You shouted at the sea.\"","\"I did,\" he said. \"I'm Kofi.\"","\"Defne. I work at a bookshop. We have that book. A complete copy.\"","Kofi's face lit up. \"Really? Can you—\" Then he stopped. \"Wait. Tomorrow I have an early meeting. I take the seven o'clock ferry. And Friday is my last morning in Istanbul. My plane leaves on Friday night.\"","\"Then Friday,\" said Defne. \"I'll bring it.\"","\"Same ferry,\" said Kofi. \"Same bench.\"","On Thursday, the bench was empty. Defne told herself that this was normal. But all day at the shop, she watched the door.","On Friday, she arrived early. She had the book under her arm, in blue paper. At ten past eight, the bench was empty. At eleven past, it was still empty. The ferry's horn sounded and the boat began to move away from the port. Defne's stomach felt like a stone.","Then someone shouted, \"Wait! WAIT!\"","A man was running along the dock with his jacket open. He jumped onto the boat and stood there, breathing hard. It was Kofi.","\"Sorry,\" he said. \"My last morning. Everybody wanted to say goodbye.\"","Defne held out the package. He opened it carefully and turned to the end. His lips moved as he read the last page. When he looked up, Defne expected him to look happy. He looked worried.","\"Defne,\" he said. \"I have to tell you something terrible.\"","He put his hand inside his jacket and took out some folded pages. Eleven pages, with torn edges.","\"These are the missing pages,\" he said. \"They were in my pocket all the time. I tore them out on Sunday night.\"","\"You did what?\"","\"My hands were shaking. It was an awful thing to do to a book.\"","\"But why?\"","\"Because for three weeks, a woman in a green coat looked at me every morning, and she never said hello. And I never said hello, either. I was too afraid. So I thought: if the book is broken, she will have a reason to talk to me. Everybody wants to know how a story ends.\"","Defne stared at him. \"I already knew how it ends! I've read it twice!\"","\"I know that now,\" said Kofi sadly. \"It was not a perfect plan.\"","\"It was a terrible plan. It took a week and a seagull.\"","\"But it worked?\" He smiled his small, strange smile.","Defne wanted to be angry. She really tried. But she was laughing too much.","\"And on Monday?\" she asked. \"You shouted at the sea.\"","\"Oh, that was real,\" said Kofi. \"I felt very sorry for the book.\"","The ferry arrived at Eminönü. The passengers walked off. Nobody moved on the bench.","\"I have to go to work,\" said Defne.","\"My plane is tonight,\" said Kofi.","They looked at each other. The ferry was going to leave again in ten minutes.","Defne sat down next to him. He gave her the first page.","\"'She opened the door, and she saw—'\" she read. Her voice stopped working for a moment.","\"Go on,\" said Kofi.","\"'—him,'\" she read. \"'He was standing in the rain, smiling like a man who had waited a long time.'\"","She looked up. Kofi was smiling his small, strange smile.","The ferry turned around, and neither of them got off. It was the first time in six years that Defne was late for work. She did not mind at all."],"glossary":{"ferry":"vapur, feribot","simit":"simit (susamlı halka ekmek)","fat":"kalın (kitap için)","bench":"bank (oturmak için)","port":"liman","copy":"nüsha (kitabın bir kopyası)"},"phrases":[{"en":"too shy","tr":"çok utangaç (-meyecek kadar)"},{"en":"come to the end","tr":"sona gelmek, bitmek üzere olmak"},{"en":"take a deep breath","tr":"derin bir nefes almak"},{"en":"turn red","tr":"kızarmak"},{"en":"look up","tr":"başını kaldırıp bakmak"},{"en":"hold out","tr":"(elini) uzatmak"},{"en":"tear out","tr":"yırtıp çıkarmak"},{"en":"be late for work","tr":"işe geç kalmak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is wrong with the Book Man's book?","options":["It is too heavy.","It is in German.","It has no cover.","The last pages are missing."],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Kitap 212. sayfada, cümlenin ortasında bitiyor; son sayfalar eksik."},{"q":"What does the seagull take?","options":["Defne's bag","Defne's simit","Kofi's book","Kofi's ticket"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Martı Defne'nin elindeki simidi alıp uçar. Kofi buna gülerken ilk konuşmaları başlar."},{"q":"Why did Kofi tear out the pages?","options":["He was angry with the book.","He wanted to sell them.","He wanted a reason to talk to Defne.","He wanted to give them to the shop."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Kofi, Defne ile konuşmak için bir bahane istiyordu; çok çekingendi."},{"q":"Where does Defne work?","options":["At a bookshop","At a school","At a cafe","On the ferry"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Defne, vapur limanının yakınındaki Blue Door Books adlı küçük bir kitapçıda çalışıyor."}],"words":998,"length":"short"},{"id":"seven-minutes-past-six","type":"story","title":"Seven Minutes Past Six","author":"Lantern Original","level":"A2","genre":"scifi","summaryTr":"Uzay istasyonundaki küçük bir tamir robotu, Nia'nın her sabah 06.07'de öldüğünü ve o sabahı yüz doksan dokuz kez yaşadığını biliyor. Elinde yalnızca son bir sabah kaldı.","paragraphs":["Nia died at seven minutes past six in the morning.","I know this because I watched it happen one hundred and ninety-nine times.","My name is Pebble. I am a repair robot on Station Kestrel, a small space station that goes around the planet Saturn. I am grey and small, about as tall as a kitchen table. I have four wheels, two arms and one round blue light for an eye.","There are four of us on Kestrel: Captain Volkov, Doctor Sato, Nia and me. Nia is the engineer. She is the only one who talks to me like a friend. She gave me my name. Before that, I was called Unit Four.","There is also a machine on the station called the Anchor. The scientists built it to test time. When I push its button, the whole station goes back seven minutes, and everything starts again. Everyone forgets what happened. Everyone except me, because my memory is connected to the machine.","But the Anchor has a problem. It has power for only two hundred mornings. After that, it stops forever.","The first morning began like all the others. At 6:05, Nia went to the Window Room. This is a round room with one huge window, and it is her favourite place on the station. Every day at 6:05, the sun comes around Saturn and the rings of the planet turn gold. Nia says it is the best five minutes of her day.","At 6:06, the alarm system saw a meteor, a small rock from space, coming very fast. The station closed all its doors to protect itself. Nia was inside the Window Room, and the door closed in front of her.","At 6:07, the meteor hit the window.","I was in the corridor. I could not open the door. I could not reach her. So I pushed the button on the Anchor, and the station went back to six o'clock. Nia's alarm clock began to ring, and she woke up smiling. She did not know that she had died.","That was when I began to try.","For the first twenty mornings, I told her the truth.","\"Nia, a meteor will hit the Window Room at 6:07. Please don't go there.\"","\"A meteor?\" She smiled and patted my head. \"Pebble, the screens show nothing.\"","She was right. The screens could not see the meteor before 6:06. So she went to watch the sun, and I pushed the button again.","For the next forty mornings, I stood in front of her door. But I am small, and Nia is strong. Every time, she picked me up with one hand, put me on her bed and said, \"Not today, Pebble.\" Then she walked out.","Then I asked the others for help. I woke the Captain at 6:01.","\"Pebble, it is six in the morning,\" he said, and closed his eyes again.","I woke Doctor Sato. She listened to me for one minute and said, \"I think you need a new program.\" Once she tried to switch me off. After that I was more careful, but nobody believed a robot.","So I tried bigger things. I locked the door of the Window Room. Nia opened it with the emergency handle and said, \"Pebble, this is not funny.\" I used the engines to move the whole station ten metres to the left. The meteor was bigger and faster than the computers thought, and it hit the window anyway.","Every morning at 6:07, I saw the same thing. And every morning, I pushed the button. Nia always woke up happy. She never remembered anything. I remembered everything: every word, every door, every time.","The Anchor had a small screen that showed how many mornings were left. One hundred. Fifty. Twenty. I looked at it more often than I looked at the clock.","On the hundred and seventieth morning, I did something silly. I told Nia everything: the Anchor, the meteor, all the mornings. I talked for the whole seven minutes. She listened with her head on one side. At 6:05, she said, \"Pebble, I think you are working too hard,\" and she went to watch the sun. I felt very tired, and robots do not get tired.","By the hundred and ninety-ninth morning, I had no more ideas. I pushed the button one last time, and the light on the Anchor turned red.","This was the two hundredth morning. There would be no more.","6:00. The alarm clock rang. Nia made her usual morning noise, a long sound like a sleepy cat. I waited in the corner of her room, completely still, and I thought.","For two hundred mornings, I had tried to change Nia's mind or to move Nia's body. Both were impossible. In every plan, I treated her like a box that I had to carry to a safe place. But a person is not a box. A person stops for the things she loves. And I began to think that the answer was small, not big.","I knew every minute of her morning. At 6:01, she put on her grey sweater. At 6:03, she made her coffee. At 6:04, she put the coffee in her cup on the kitchen table and sat down to tie her shoes. At 6:05, she took the cup to the Window Room.","In two hundred mornings, I had never touched that cup.","It was blue, with a crooked yellow star on the side. Nia's daughter, Tola, made it at school when she was six years old. Tola lives on Earth, more than a billion kilometres away. Nia drank from that cup every day, and she never let anyone touch it.","My rules say: Do not break things. And I knew what that cup meant to her.","6:04. Nia sat at the kitchen table and bent down to tie her shoes. The cup stood next to my arm.","I looked at her. \"I'm sorry, Nia,\" I said.","And I pushed the cup off the table.","It was a small sound, but it was the loudest sound I ever heard. The cup broke into five pieces. Coffee ran across the floor. I felt something heavy inside me, and I had no word for it.","Nia stopped. She looked at the pieces. Her face changed.","\"Pebble,\" she said, very quietly. \"What did you do?\"","I did not answer. I could not. She went down on her knees and picked up the pieces one by one, with both hands, as if they were small birds. Her eyes were wet.","6:05. Far away, the sun came around Saturn. In the Window Room, the rings turned gold for nobody.","6:06. The alarm began to shout. \"METEOR. ALL DOORS CLOSING.\"","Nia looked up. \"What?\"","6:07. A deep sound shook the whole station. The lights went out, and then came back. After that, it was quiet.","The screen on the kitchen wall showed a red picture of the Window Room. The window was gone. There was no air in there.","Nia stood up very slowly. She looked at the screen. She looked at the closed door that went to the Window Room. Then she looked at the pieces of the cup in her hands.","\"I was going to be in there,\" she said.","\"Yes,\" I said.","She sat down on the floor. For a long time, she said nothing. I stayed next to her.","\"Pebble,\" she said at last. \"Did you know?\"","I have never told a lie. That is another one of my rules. But on the two hundredth morning, I broke two rules before breakfast.","\"No,\" I said. \"It was an accident.\"","Nia looked at me for a very long time. I do not think she believed me. But she did not say so. She only said, \"Clumsy old tin can,\" in a shaking voice, and then she put her arms around me. No human had ever done that before. My metal was cold, and her sweater was warm.","That was three weeks ago.","Nia mended the cup with glue. The star is still crooked, and now there is a long line across it. She says it is her lucky cup. She is right, but she does not know how right.","The Anchor is dark now. It does not work any more, and I do not need it.","Nia still watches the sun every morning, from the small window in the kitchen. The rings do not look quite so gold from there. She says it is enough.","And every morning, at seven minutes past six, I check the clock. Then I check Nia.","She is always there."],"glossary":{"anchor":"çapa; hikâyede zamanı yedi dakika geri saran makinenin adı","unit":"birim; burada: robotun eski adı (Unit Four = Dördüncü Birim)","program":"yazılım, bilgisayar programı","screens":"ekranlar (istasyonun kontrol ekranları)","crooked":"eğri, düzgün olmayan"},"phrases":[{"en":"go around (a planet)","tr":"(bir gezegenin) etrafında dönmek"},{"en":"push the button","tr":"düğmeye basmak"},{"en":"pick up","tr":"kaldırmak, yerden almak"},{"en":"switch off","tr":"kapatmak (cihazı)"},{"en":"one by one","tr":"tek tek"},{"en":"at last","tr":"sonunda"},{"en":"as if","tr":"sanki"},{"en":"put your arms around someone","tr":"birine sarılmak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Who remembers all the mornings?","options":["Nia","Pebble","Captain Volkov","Doctor Sato"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Pebble'ın hafızası Anchor makinesine bağlıdır; yalnızca o her sabahı hatırlar."},{"q":"Why is Nia in danger at 6:07?","options":["She is in the Window Room when a meteor hits.","The air machine breaks.","She is ill.","The Captain locks her in her room."],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Nia 06.05'ten beri Pencere Odası'ndadır ve 06.07'de bir meteor pencereye çarpar."},{"q":"What does Pebble break on the last morning?","options":["The Anchor","The coffee machine","Nia's blue cup","The Window Room door"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Pebble, Nia'yı odada tutmak için kızının yaptığı mavi fincanı düşürüp kırar."},{"q":"What does Pebble tell Nia about the cup?","options":["The truth","That the Captain broke it","That Doctor Sato broke it","That it was an accident"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Pebble hayatında ilk kez yalan söyler: fincanın kazayla kırıldığını söyler."}],"words":1418,"length":"medium"},{"id":"the-bridge-with-two-ends","type":"story","title":"The Bridge with Two Ends","author":"Lantern Original","level":"A2","genre":"fable","summaryTr":"Dere üzerine yapılan yeni köprü, kunduzun düşündüğünden farklı misafirler getirir. Köprünün gerçekten herkese yararlı olması için tek bir hayvanın iyi fikrinden fazlası gerekir.","paragraphs":["## A fine piece of work","In a green valley, a stream ran between two busy places. On one side stood the animals' homes. On the other side were the market, the school, and the common garden. Most animals crossed on a row of large stones. In dry weather, this was easy. After rain, the water covered the lowest stones, and everyone had to take the long path around the hill.","Beaver decided to build a bridge. He liked making things, and he was very good with wood. He measured the stream with a long string. He chose strong branches and cut them to the same length. Every morning, he worked beside the water. Every evening, he checked what he had made. The other animals brought him food and watched the bridge grow.","Rabbit carried carrots to him. Fox brought nails in a little box. Tortoise brought a pot of soup on a low cart. Beaver thanked them all, but he did not ask them many questions. He already had a picture of the bridge in his head. It was straight, wide, and strong. In his picture, everyone crossed it with a smile.","At last, the bridge was ready. Beaver made a sign and hung it above the entrance. It said, 'A bridge for everyone.' He invited the whole valley to come on Sunday morning. He cleaned the wooden boards, put flowers beside the posts, and stood in the middle. He was tired, but he was also proud. The long walk around the hill was over.","Rabbit arrived first. She jumped onto the bridge and ran to the other side. Fox followed her, carrying his box. Deer crossed slowly and looked down at the water. The boards did not move. Everyone clapped. Beaver bowed so low that his nose almost touched the wood. Then Tortoise came up the path with her empty soup cart and stopped at the first step.","The step was high. Tortoise put one front foot on it, then the other. She could lift herself, but she could not pull the cart after her. Beaver hurried over. 'I will carry it,' he said. He lifted the cart onto the bridge. Tortoise climbed up slowly. At the other end, there was another high step. Beaver carried the cart down again.","'Thank you,' said Tortoise. 'Will you be here tomorrow morning?' Beaver looked surprised. 'I must collect wood tomorrow.' 'And on Tuesday?' she asked. 'I have work then too.' Tortoise nodded. 'I go to the garden every day. My cart goes with me.' Beaver looked at his fine sign. The words were clear, but suddenly he was not sure about them.","## One good answer","That afternoon, Beaver made a long wooden board for each entrance. The boards went from the ground to the bridge like gentle hills. He asked Tortoise to try them. She pulled her cart up one board, crossed the bridge, and went down the other. This time, she did not need help. Beaver smiled. 'Now it is a bridge for everyone,' he said.","On Monday, Mouse came from the market with a bag of seeds. She was very small, and the spaces between the bridge boards were wide for her feet. She crossed slowly, looking down at every step. In the middle, one seed fell from her bag and dropped through a space. Mouse sat beside the rail until Rabbit arrived and walked across with her.","Beaver was sorry when he heard about this. He took some thin pieces of wood and covered the spaces along one side. Mouse tried the new narrow path. Her feet stayed on the wood, and her seed bag stayed beside her. 'That is much better,' she said. Beaver wrote a small note in his notebook. He wanted to remember the size of her feet.","Then Duck came with six little ducks. They liked the new bridge because the water was cold that morning. But at the far end, the wide board was wet and smooth. One little duck sat down suddenly and slid to the bottom. She was not hurt, but she was angry. All five of her brothers and sisters wanted to slide too. Duck wanted to reach school.","Beaver added small wooden strips across the wide boards. Now wet feet could hold them better. Duck tested the entrance before bringing the little ducks again. They walked down without sliding. One of them looked disappointed. Beaver nearly laughed, but he kept quiet. A bridge could not be both a good school path and a good slide at the same time.","By Wednesday, Beaver had worked on the bridge for three extra days. He sat under a tree with his notebook. There were drawings of feet, wheels, and wooden strips on every page. Fox found him there. 'It was such a simple bridge,' Beaver said. 'Now every animal has a new problem.' Fox sat beside him. 'Perhaps the problems were here before the bridge,' he said.","Beaver did not like that answer at first. He had worked hard. The bridge was strong, and most animals could cross it easily. But then he thought of Tortoise's cart. She had not made it difficult on purpose. It was simply her cart, and she needed it. Mouse had not chosen small feet to trouble him. He opened his notebook to a fresh page.","## The meeting in the middle","On Thursday, Beaver invited the animals to a meeting. He put a table in the middle of the bridge. 'Tell me what this bridge needs,' he said. At once, everyone began to speak. Rabbit wanted a straight running path. Mouse wanted a quiet corner. Deer wanted higher rails. Duck wanted a low rail so the little ducks could see the water. Fox wanted space for a market stall.","Beaver tried to write everything. Soon his pencil broke. Tortoise, who had arrived last, waited until the others were quiet. 'Why is the table on the bridge?' she asked. 'For our meeting,' said Beaver. 'Yes,' she said. 'But Hedgehog is behind me with a basket. He wants to cross, and we are in his way.' Everyone turned around. Hedgehog waved politely.","They moved the meeting under the tree. Hedgehog crossed with his basket. Beaver found another pencil. 'Let us try again,' he said. 'Tell us about a time when you need to cross.' This question brought different answers. Rabbit carried fresh bread before breakfast. Mouse returned from the market in the afternoon. Deer crossed at night after visiting her mother. Tortoise took heavy vegetables home slowly.","As they listened, the animals stopped asking for everything at once. Rabbit did not need to run all day. Deer did not need a higher rail if she could see the edges clearly at night. Duck wanted to see the water, but she also wanted her children to stay safe. Fox admitted that his stall could stand near the bridge instead of on it.","Mouse drew a line on the ground with a stick. 'What if the quiet path stays on one side?' she asked. 'The wider part can be for carts and larger feet.' Rabbit looked at the line. She could still carry her bread across. She only needed to slow down when someone else was there. It was not her first choice, but it was possible.","They agreed to try a plan for one week. Beaver would mark the edges with pale paint. Mouse would help make a clear line beside the narrow path. Fox would put his stall on the market side, well away from the entrance. Rabbit would walk across when the bridge was busy. Tortoise would test every change with her cart. Duck would bring a bucket of water to test the wet boards.","For the first time, Beaver did not work alone. Deer carried the paint. Rabbit cleaned the boards. Mouse checked the smallest spaces. Fox held the tools, although he sometimes offered advice when nobody asked for it. By evening, the bridge looked less like Beaver's first drawing. It also looked more like a place the whole valley used. Beaver could not decide how he felt about that.","## The red ribbon","The next morning, Fox opened his market stall. He sold string, baskets, and three kinds of buttons. To help customers find him, he tied a long red ribbon to a post near the bridge. The ribbon moved beautifully in the wind. By midday, several animals had bought things. Fox was pleased. Then a strong wind blew the ribbon across the entrance, directly in front of Tortoise.","Tortoise stopped her cart. Behind her, Duck stopped with the little ducks. Behind Duck, Deer waited with a bag of flour. Rabbit came from the other side with an empty bread basket. She saw the line and called to Fox. He ran over and lifted the ribbon. Everyone moved again. 'It was only a decoration,' he said. Tortoise looked at the full path behind her.","That evening, the animals met under the tree again. Beaver was ready to make another rule, but Mouse raised her paw. 'Can we ask the new question?' she said. Fox sighed. 'I need customers to see my stall.' Rabbit pointed to his plain brown sign. 'We can paint that red. A sign will not blow across the path.' Fox thought about it and agreed.","They painted the sign together. Fox took the ribbon down and cut it into short pieces to tie around small bags of buttons. He sold two bags the next morning. 'The ribbon still has a job,' he told Tortoise. 'It simply has a different place.' Tortoise bought some strong string for her cart. She did not mention the long wait at the bridge.","On the fourth day of the trial, rain fell from morning until evening. Beaver worried about the new paint, but it stayed on the wood. Mouse crossed safely along her narrow path. Duck's family did not slide. Tortoise could pull her cart up both entrances. Everything seemed right. Then Deer arrived at the market side with her mother, who walked slowly and needed frequent rests.","There was nowhere to sit near that end of the bridge. The nearest bench was across the water. Deer's mother could make the crossing, but first she needed to rest. Fox brought a strong wooden box from his stall. She sat on it and thanked him. When Beaver arrived, he looked at the box. 'We have worked on the bridge,' he said, 'but we forgot the places beside it.'","## The other end","Beaver walked from one end to the other with his notebook. On the home side, the path was smooth and there was a bench under the tree. On the market side, the ground was rough and baskets stood near the entrance. He had always started from his own home. For him, the bridge began with a comfortable place. For other animals, it began somewhere different.","He asked Deer and her mother to walk with him. He did not carry tools this time. He listened while they showed him where a bench could stand without blocking anyone. Then he asked Tortoise to bring her cart from the market. One wheel caught between two stones before she reached the bridge. It was a small problem, but it happened every day.","The animals spent Saturday morning improving both paths. They moved the baskets, filled the space between the stones, and built a second bench. Beaver showed Rabbit how to smooth the wood. Fox brought warm drinks. Mouse made a little seat beside the large bench, because the large one was too high for her. Nobody called it a special seat. It was simply another seat.","At the end of the week, they met again. Beaver read out the changes from his notebook. There were more than he expected. Some were made of wood or paint. Others were small agreements about how to use the bridge. Rabbit looked embarrassed when he mentioned walking. 'I forgot once,' she said. Mouse nodded. 'You stopped when I called.' Rabbit promised to keep trying.","'Is it finished now?' asked Fox. Beaver looked at the bridge. It was strong, useful, and busy. Two little ducks were crossing with their school bags. Tortoise waited for them before taking her cart onto the wide path. On the far side, Deer's mother was sitting in the sun. Beaver thought about Fox's question. A week earlier, he had known the answer immediately.","'The building work is finished for now,' he said. 'But we can still listen.' He put his notebook in a small wooden box under the tree. Anyone could leave a drawing or a message there. Mouse could not reach the top of the box, so Beaver lowered it before anyone asked. Tortoise smiled when she saw him do that. Beaver smiled back.","That night, Beaver took down his first sign. The words 'A bridge for everyone' were still bright. He did not throw it away. He painted a smaller line beneath them: 'Tell us how you cross.' In the morning, he hung it where animals coming from either direction could read it. The sign was no longer a promise he had made alone. It was also an invitation.","A few days later, a young Otter arrived in the valley. She looked at the bridge, the two paths, and the benches. 'Who built all this?' she asked. Rabbit pointed to Beaver, who was carrying his tools home. Beaver stopped when he heard her. 'I built the first bridge,' he said. 'Then we learned where its two ends really were.' He went home by the market path, just to see it again."],"glossary":{"beaver":"kunduz","tortoise":"kara kaplumbağası","rail":"korkuluk","stall":"satış tezgâhı","strips":"ince çıtalar","trial":"deneme süresi","cross":"karşıya geçmek","board":"tahta","otter":"su samuru","pale":"açık renkli"},"phrases":[{"en":"on purpose","tr":"bilerek, kasten"},{"en":"in his way","tr":"yolunu kapatacak şekilde"},{"en":"at once","tr":"hemen"},{"en":"at the same time","tr":"aynı zamanda"},{"en":"instead of","tr":"yerine"},{"en":"well away from","tr":"epey uzağında"},{"en":"read out","tr":"sesli okumak"},{"en":"for now","tr":"şimdilik"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is Tortoise's first problem with the bridge?","options":["She cannot find the market","The steps are too high for her cart","She is afraid of the paint","Her soup is too hot"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"İlk köprünün giriş ve çıkışındaki yüksek basamaklar, kaplumbağanın arabasını tek başına geçirmesine engel olur."},{"q":"Why do the animals move their meeting under the tree?","options":["It begins to snow","Fox wants to sleep","They are blocking Hedgehog's way","The bridge breaks"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Köprü üzerindeki toplantı, sepetiyle karşıya geçmek isteyen kirpinin yolunu kapatır."},{"q":"What replaces Fox's long red ribbon?","options":["A higher rail","A second stall","A new bell","A red sign"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Tavşanın önerisiyle tezgâhın kahverengi tabelası kırmızıya boyanır; uçuşan kurdele kaldırılır."},{"q":"What does Beaver notice when Deer's mother needs a rest?","options":["The market end has no nearby seat","The bridge is too short","The water is too cold","The paint has disappeared"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Geyiğin annesi dinlenmek isteyince kunduz, pazar tarafında oturacak yakın bir yer olmadığını fark eder."},{"q":"Why does Beaver add 'Tell us how you cross' to the sign?","options":["He wants every animal to pay","He wants to keep learning about different needs","He wants to close the bridge","He wants only fast animals to cross"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Yeni yazı, hayvanların farklı ihtiyaçlarını anlatmaları için bir davettir; kunduz dinlemeye devam etmek ister."}],"words":2251,"length":"medium"},{"id":"the-other-way-to-the-island","type":"story","title":"The Other Way to the Island","author":"Lantern Original","level":"A2","genre":"adventure","summaryTr":"Lina ve amcası, göldeki küçük adaya bir gösteri perdesi götürürken kapalı bir köprüyle karşılaşır. Başka bir yol arayışları, haritalara ve yardım istemeye farklı gözle bakmalarını sağlar.","paragraphs":["## The rolled-up sky","Lina carried a roll of blue cloth out of her grandfather's workshop. It was longer than she was tall. Her uncle Sam held the other end. Together, they put it on a small handcart and tied it down with two ropes. The cloth was a screen for a shadow show. That evening, people on Willow Island would see birds, boats, and a large paper moon move across it.","Grandfather came to the door with his walking stick. His ankle still hurt, so he could not go with them. 'Keep it dry,' he said. 'The new paint is ready, but the old wood inside is not very strong.' Lina touched the roll carefully. She had helped him paint the cloth. In the middle was a deep blue sky with tiny white windows for stars.","'We will be there before four,' said Sam. 'The little bridge is only twenty minutes from here.' Grandfather gave Lina a paper map. She laughed. She knew the town well. But he put it in her bag anyway. 'There is more than one way around a lake,' he said. Lina took a bottle of water, some bread, and her phone. Then they set off.","The cart made a soft noise on the street. One wheel was smaller than the other, so it pulled to the right. Lina walked on that side and helped Sam keep it straight. At the bakery, a woman asked about the blue roll. 'We are taking the night sky to an island,' Lina told her. The woman smiled and gave them two small apples for the journey.","Soon they could see the water between the trees. Willow Island stood near the middle of the lake. There was a wooden building on it and a long garden beside it. At the eastern end, a short bridge joined the island to the town park. Lina could already hear someone testing music. They turned the last corner and stopped in front of a red gate.","A sign on the gate said, 'Bridge closed. Please use the western entrance.' Beyond the gate, two workers were lifting a broken wooden board. A branch had fallen onto the bridge during the night. The space under the board looked small, but Sam did not touch the gate. 'We need the other entrance,' he said. Lina looked across the water. The western end of the island was far away.","One worker came over. 'You can go around the north side of the lake,' she said. 'The old farm road goes to the west bridge. It takes about an hour on foot.' She looked at the handcart. 'Perhaps a little longer with that.' Lina thanked her. There was still plenty of time. One hour was not a problem. She opened Grandfather's map on top of the roll.","## A line on the map","The map showed a path beside the water. It went through a garden, past an orchard, and down to the western bridge. Lina followed it with her finger. 'Here,' she said. 'We stay close to the lake.' Sam checked the date in the corner. The map was twelve years old. 'We can start here,' he said, 'but we must read the signs too.'","At first, the path was easy. Ducks moved away from the sound of the cart. A man sat on a bench with three empty fishing buckets. Lina wanted to ask him about the path, but he was asleep. They passed him quietly. A little later, the smooth path became stones. The small wheel hit each stone, and the blue roll moved under its ropes.","They stopped to check the screen. Nothing was broken, but the rope on the left was loose. Lina tied it again. Sam put his jacket between the cloth and the cart. 'The map does not show all these stones,' Lina said. 'Maps are good at lines,' Sam answered. 'They are not always good at wheels.' They pushed on more slowly, choosing the smoothest places.","After ten minutes, they reached the garden on the map. There were neat rows of vegetables behind a low fence. A new greenhouse stood across the old path. A woman was putting small plants into pots. Lina called to her from the gate. 'Excuse me. Is there a way through to the orchard?' The woman put down her pot and came to speak to them.","'The public path moved last year,' she said. 'It goes behind the garden now. You must go back to the white sign.' Lina remembered passing a white sign. She had not read it because she was looking at the map. She felt her face grow warm. Sam only nodded. 'Good. Now we know.' They thanked the woman and turned the heavy cart around.","At the white sign, a narrow path went up a small hill. It looked longer than the old route, but the surface was smooth. Lina folded the map and put it in her pocket. This time, she read every sign. Halfway up, they stopped for water. Through a break in the trees, the island looked smaller than before. They were moving away from it to reach it.","Lina took a photo of the blue roll with the distant island behind it. She sent it to Grandfather with a short message about the closed bridge. His answer came quickly. 'A long entrance for a short show. Thank you both.' Then there was another message. 'Please tell Mira at the island hall. She needs time to hang the screen.' Sam called Mira while Lina held the cart.","Mira answered from a noisy room. She knew about the bridge, but she did not know the screen was still on the road. 'The western bridge is open,' she said. 'Come through the side door when you arrive. And do not hurry on the hill.' Lina liked her calm voice. The show was important, but nobody wanted them to rush with the old wooden frame.","## The orchard gate","The new path ended beside an orchard. Red apples hung above the fence. On the map, the road went straight through the trees. In front of them, however, was another closed gate. There was a bell on a post. Sam rang it. A boy about Lina's age appeared between the trees with a basket in his hands. He looked at the roll. 'A carpet?' he asked.","Lina explained about the screen and the show. The boy's name was Joel. His family looked after the orchard. 'The road through here is private now,' he said. 'There is a public road around the field.' He pointed left. Lina could see it climbing another hill. She tried not to look disappointed. They had already used nearly an hour, and they were not close to the western bridge.","'I can ask my mother about this gate,' Joel added. 'Please wait here.' He went to a low house at the back of the orchard. Lina sat on a flat stone and ate an apple from the bakery. The basket beside the gate was full of much larger apples. Joel returned with his mother. She said they could cross, but a delivery van was using the main track.","'You can take the lower track,' she said. 'Joel will show you. It is flat, but there are some soft places after the rain.' Sam thanked her. Lina offered to pay with their second apple, then saw the trees around her. Joel laughed. 'We have one or two already,' he said. Lina laughed too and put the apple back in her bag.","They followed Joel between the trees. The air smelled of wet leaves and fruit. The track was wide enough for the cart, but Sam kept away from its muddy middle. Joel walked ahead and pointed out the firm ground. For a while, the wheels moved easily. Then the right wheel dropped into a hole under some leaves. The cart stopped so suddenly that Lina almost walked into it.","Sam asked Lina to hold the long roll steady. He and Joel tried to lift the front of the cart. The wheel rose, then sank again. Pulling harder did not help. Lina looked around. Near the fence were some short, flat boards. 'Can we put one under the wheel?' she asked. Joel checked with his mother, who was still close enough to hear. She told him which boards they could use.","They untied the screen and carried it to dry grass first. Without its weight, the cart was easier to lift. Joel slid a board under the wheel. Sam pushed while Lina pulled the handle. This time, the cart came out. There was mud on their shoes and on one rope, but the blue cloth was clean. Lina felt tired and pleased at the same time.","They returned the boards and put the screen back. Joel helped tie both ropes. He showed Lina a knot that was easy to open later. 'A rope must hold,' he said, 'but you do not want to fight it at the end.' Lina tried the knot twice. On her second try, it stayed firm. Sam checked it, and they continued along the orchard track.","At the far gate, Joel stopped. The public road was only a few steps away. Lina invited him and his mother to the show. 'There are birds and boats,' she said. 'And a moon bigger than your basket.' Joel said he would ask. They waved goodbye. The way ahead was clear now, and the western bridge was less than half an hour away.","## Wind above the water","The road ran beside an open field. Without the trees, the wind felt stronger. It pushed against the end of the rolled screen. Sam turned the cart so that the wind came from behind them. Lina held the loose edge of the cloth. Across the field, they could see a stone building and, beyond it, the western bridge. For the first time that afternoon, the island was getting closer.","Then Lina heard a light, sharp sound. One of the ropes had rubbed against a metal corner on the cart. A few thin threads were hanging from it. The rope had not broken, but it was weak. She called to Sam, and they stopped beside the stone building. It was a small café. The sign outside showed a cup and a bicycle.","Inside, the owner was cleaning a table. Sam asked if he had any strong string. The man looked at the cart through the window. 'I have a luggage strap for my bicycle,' he said. 'You can borrow it.' It was green, with a metal buckle. They put it around the screen, away from the sharp corner. Lina wrote the café's name in her phone so they could return it.","The owner brought them two glasses of water. Lina checked the time. It was almost four. They were later than planned, but the show did not start until six. She sent Mira another message. 'At the bicycle café. Coming soon.' Mira replied with a picture of an empty stage. Under it she wrote, 'Your sky has a place waiting.' Lina smiled and showed Sam.","They reached the western bridge just after four. It was a wide bridge with a low wall on each side. A woman in a yellow jacket stood at the entrance. She checked their cart and asked them to walk. Lina had no wish to run. Her arms were tired. As they crossed, she could see the eastern bridge on the other side of the island. It looked very small.","Near the middle, the wind lifted the corner of the blue cloth. Lina caught it with both hands. Nothing came loose. The green strap held the roll firmly. She looked down at the water for a moment, then back at the path. Sam kept both hands on the cart. They crossed the final few metres together and stopped under a large tree on the island.","Mira came out of the hall when she heard the wheels. 'The sky is here!' she called. Two helpers carried the screen inside. Lina followed them through the side door. It opened directly beside the stage. In a few minutes, the long roll was on the floor, the ropes were off, and the blue cloth was opening like a very slow, very large flower.","## A road across the screen","They found one small problem. A wooden ring at the top of the screen was missing. Without it, the middle hung too low. Lina looked at the ropes on the floor. She remembered Joel's knot. With Mira beside her, she made a loop in one clean rope. Mira used it in place of the ring. The screen hung straight. Their long journey had brought a useful lesson with it.","Lina sat near the back while the helpers tested the light. A black paper bird crossed the blue sky. Then a boat sailed past the little white stars. The cloth was not wet or torn. She took a picture and sent it to Grandfather. He called at once. 'It looks beautiful,' he said. Lina told him about the greenhouse, the muddy wheel, and the green strap.","At five thirty, Joel arrived with his mother. The café owner came too, carrying a bicycle helmet. Sam gave back his strap and bought him a cup of tea. Lina saved seats for everyone. She put Grandfather's old map on her knees and drew the new path in pencil. Beside the orchard, she added a little gate. Beside the café, she drew a green line.","Before the show, Mira asked Lina to come to the side of the stage. One helper was ill, and someone needed to move the paper boat. Lina agreed. Behind the screen, she could not see the audience. She could see only the light, the wooden handles, and the waiting shapes. Sam stood nearby. 'A small journey this time,' he whispered. Lina held the boat carefully.","The music started. Lina moved the boat slowly across the blue cloth. A paper hill appeared in its path. She raised the boat around it, then brought it down beside the moon. From the dark room came a child's quiet laugh. Lina thought of the closed bridge and the path behind the garden. Sometimes the way forward went around something first.","After the show, the screen stayed at the hall for another evening. Lina and Sam walked home with an empty cart. The eastern bridge was still closed, so they took the western road again. This time, they knew the turns. Lina's new pencil line was in her pocket. It was not the shortest line on the map, but it was a road she could explain."],"glossary":{"screen":"gösteri perdesi","cart":"el arabası","orchard":"meyve bahçesi","strap":"kayış","loop":"halka","frame":"çerçeve","track":"toprak yol","firm":"sağlam, sert","ring":"halka","stage":"sahne"},"phrases":[{"en":"set off","tr":"yola çıkmak"},{"en":"on foot","tr":"yürüyerek"},{"en":"plenty of time","tr":"bolca zaman"},{"en":"kept away from","tr":"uzak durdu"},{"en":"at the same time","tr":"aynı zamanda"},{"en":"in place of","tr":"yerine"},{"en":"at once","tr":"hemen"},{"en":"in its path","tr":"yolunun üzerinde"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the eastern bridge closed?","options":["A boat is under it","A branch damaged it","The show is over","There is a race on it"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Gece düşen bir dal köprüye zarar vermiştir; işçiler kırık tahtayı kaldırmaktadır."},{"q":"Why must Lina and Sam go back at the garden?","options":["They forgot the cloth","They need more apples","The public path has moved","Sam has lost his phone"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Eski yolun üzerinde artık sera vardır; yeni halka açık yol bahçenin arkasından gider."},{"q":"What helps them move the cart out of the hole?","options":["A bicycle","A fishing bucket","A larger wheel","A flat board"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Perdeyi indirip arabayı hafifletirler, ardından tekerleğin altına düz bir tahta koyarlar."},{"q":"What does the café owner lend them?","options":["A green strap","A paper map","A wooden ring","A yellow jacket"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Kafe sahibi yıpranan ipin yerine kullanmaları için yeşil bir bisiklet kayışı verir."},{"q":"How does Lina use Joel's lesson at the hall?","options":["She paints a gate","She makes a rope loop for the screen","She repairs the cart wheel","She folds a paper bird"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Joel'den öğrendiği düğümle temiz ipte bir halka oluşturur; bu halka eksik ahşap halkanın yerini alır."}],"words":2432,"length":"medium"},{"id":"the-piano-at-two-oclock","type":"story","title":"The Piano at Two O'Clock","author":"Lantern Original","level":"A2","genre":"mystery","summaryTr":"Eski bir dağ otelinde, boş balo salonunun kapısı kilitliyken her gece saat tam ikide piyano çalıyor. Anahtar gece resepsiyonisti Ines'in cebinde; otel sahibi ise sırrı çözmesi için ona bir hafta süre veriyor.","paragraphs":["At two o'clock every night, the piano in the ballroom began to play.","Nobody could be in the ballroom. The door was locked, and the only key was in Ines's pocket.","Ines was the night receptionist at the Hotel Alpenrose, a big old hotel high in the mountains. She did not believe in ghosts. But in one week, six guests left early.","\"I can't sleep with that music,\" one woman told her. \"It sounds like someone is crying.\"","On Monday morning, Mr. Brandt, the owner, called Ines to his office. He was a thin man who never smiled.","\"Ines, I'm going to be honest,\" he said. \"If more guests leave, I will close the hotel and sell it.\"","\"It's only an old piano, Mr. Brandt.\"","\"Then find out why it plays. You have one week.\"","That night, Ines sat in the lobby with a cup of tea and watched the clock. At exactly two o'clock, she heard it. Slow, soft, sad music.","She ran to the ballroom, took out her key and opened the door. The room was dark and cold. The music stopped.","She looked behind the curtains and under the tables. Nothing. The windows were closed and locked. But the lid of the piano was open, and the piano stool was still turning, very slowly, as if someone had just stood up.","\"Somebody is playing a trick on me,\" she said quietly.","Ines thought of Jamal, the young porter. He worked at night, too, and he often disappeared for an hour. Maybe he knew another way in.","The next night, she asked him to sit with her in the lobby.","\"Why?\" asked Jamal.","\"Because I want to know where you are at two o'clock.\"","Jamal laughed and opened a sandwich. At two o'clock exactly, the music began. Jamal dropped his sandwich.","\"It wasn't me,\" he whispered. \"I only go to the storeroom to sleep! I swear it wasn't me!\"","\"I know,\" said Ines. Now she was frightened, too. \"I believe you.\"","On Wednesday, she went into the ballroom in the daytime. She looked at everything carefully. The keys of the piano were clean. There was dust on every table and chair, but there was no dust on the keys. There was also a smell in the room: pipe smoke. In a locked room!","Then she saw something on the floor near the big bookcase. Two curved marks, like half a circle.","\"Like a door,\" she said slowly. \"A door that opens.\"","She pushed the bookcase. Nothing. She pulled it. Nothing. Then she moved one heavy red book on the middle shelf, and she heard a click. The bookcase swung open. Behind it, a narrow stair went down into the dark.","Ines did not go down. She did not want to follow the ghost. She wanted to catch it.","On Wednesday night, she locked the ballroom door from the inside and hid behind a long curtain. She waited. The clock said 1:50. Then 1:55.","At two minutes to two, she heard a soft click.","The bookcase opened. A small light came out, and behind it came a very old man in a long coat. His hair was white. He carried a little lamp and a piece of music, and he had a pipe in his mouth. Ines smelled the smoke. So that was the smell in the ballroom! He did not look around. He walked straight to the piano like a man walking into his own kitchen. He put his pipe away, sat down, took a deep breath, and began to play.","It was the most beautiful music Ines had ever heard. It was slow and sad, but it was not frightening at all.","When he finished, Ines came out from behind the curtain.","\"Good evening,\" she said.","The old man jumped up. The stool spun. \"Oh! I'm sorry! Please, don't call the police.\"","\"Who are you?\"","\"My name is Anton Weiss. I was the pianist here for forty years. I played in this room every evening, and my wife sat at the table by the window and listened.\" He looked at the empty table. \"She died three weeks ago.\"","Ines said nothing.","\"Last year, Mr. Brandt stopped the music. He said it cost too much. But I know the old back stairs, because my father was the cook here. So now I come at two o'clock, when everyone is asleep. I play for her. I didn't know I was frightening people.\"","Ines looked at the table by the window. Then she had an idea. She talked to Anton for a long time.","The next morning, she went to Mr. Brandt's office.","\"Did you find the ghost?\" he asked.","\"Yes. He plays beautifully. Tonight he is going to play at nine o'clock, in the ballroom, for all the guests.\"","\"Are you crazy?\"","\"Come and listen first.\"","At nine o'clock, Mr. Brandt stood at the back of the ballroom with his arms crossed. Anton played. After one minute, Mr. Brandt's arms were not crossed any more. When the music ended, the guests clapped for a long time. The woman who wanted to leave said, \"I'm staying another week.\"","By the end of the month, every room in the hotel was full. People came from other towns to hear the ghost of the Alpenrose. Mr. Brandt, who never smiled, smiled twice that week. Ines counted.","Every evening, there was a small candle on the table by the window. Nobody sat there.","\"Is this table reserved?\" a guest asked one night.","\"Yes,\" said Ines. \"For the best listener in the hotel.\""],"glossary":{"keys":"anahtarlar; piyanoda: tuşlar","lid":"kapak (piyanonun)","stool":"tabure","porter":"otel hamalı (bavulları taşıyan görevli)","lobby":"otel girişi, lobi","pipe":"pipo (tütün içmek için kullanılan)"},"phrases":[{"en":"believe in ghosts","tr":"hayaletlere inanmak"},{"en":"find out","tr":"öğrenmek, keşfetmek"},{"en":"playing a trick on","tr":"birine oyun/şaka yapmak"},{"en":"as if","tr":"sanki"},{"en":"call the police","tr":"polisi aramak"},{"en":"hid behind","tr":"-in arkasına saklanmak"},{"en":"left early","tr":"erken ayrılmak"},{"en":"had an idea","tr":"aklına bir fikir gelmek"}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does the piano play?","options":["At two o'clock at night","At nine o'clock in the evening","At noon","At six in the morning"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Piyano her gece tam saat ikide çalar."},{"q":"Why does Ines know that Jamal is not the ghost?","options":["He was away from the hotel.","He told Mr. Brandt the truth.","He was sitting with her when the music started.","He gave her his key."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Müzik başladığında Jamal lobide Ines'le birlikte oturuyordu."},{"q":"How does Anton get into the ballroom?","options":["With a second key","Through a secret door behind the bookcase","Through a window","Through the kitchen door"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Anton, kitaplığın arkasındaki gizli kapıdan ve eski arka merdivenlerden girer."},{"q":"Who does Anton play for at two o'clock?","options":["Mr. Brandt","The hotel guests","Ines","His wife, who died"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Anton, üç hafta önce ölen karısı için çalar."},{"q":"What does Ines smell in the locked ballroom?","options":["Fresh flowers","Pipe smoke","Hot soup","Old paint"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Ines kilitli balo salonunda pipo dumanı kokusu alır. Bu koku Anton'un piposundan gelir."}],"words":927,"length":"short"},{"id":"a-table-for-mr-costa","type":"story","title":"A Table for Mr. Costa","author":"Lantern Original","level":"B1","genre":"comedy","summaryTr":"Deniz kırmızı atkısını takıp ilk görüşmesine gider ve lüks bir restoranda yanlış kişi sanılır. Garsonlar titrer, şef gözyaşlarını güçlükle tutar, asıl sürpriz ise masanın yanında bekliyordur.","paragraphs":["Deniz had two rules for his blind date: wear the red scarf, and don't be boring.","His friend Cem had arranged everything. \"Her name is Mariana,\" Cem had said. \"She wears a green coat, she loves food, and she's very funny. She'll know you by the scarf. The table is booked in her name. Costa. Just say Costa.\"","So at ten to eight, Deniz walked into Casa Lumi, the fanciest restaurant in town, with a red scarf around his neck and his heart beating fast. A man in a black suit stood by the door. His name tag said LUCA, MANAGER.","What Deniz did not know was that Luca had heard a rumour that morning. The famous food critic would visit Casa Lumi tonight. He was the man who could close a restaurant with one bad review. Nobody had ever seen his face. The newspaper printed only his name: M. Costa.","\"Good evening,\" said Deniz. \"I'm here for the Costa table.\"","Luca stopped moving. \"The Costa table,\" he said slowly. \"Of course. Mr. Costa. Welcome to Casa Lumi.\"","\"No, I'm not—\"","\"Say no more.\" Luca put a finger on his lips and winked. \"You wish to stay unknown. Your secret is safe with us.\"","Two minutes later, Deniz had the best table in the room, a glass of champagne that he had not ordered, and three waiters who were afraid to blink. \"I think there's been a small mistake,\" he told the youngest one, a boy called Tomasz.","\"There are no mistakes at Casa Lumi, sir,\" said Tomasz. His smile was shaking. \"Only small surprises.\"","Deniz took out his phone to send Mariana a message. At once, the waiters froze.","\"He's making notes,\" Tomasz whispered. \"He's writing the review!\"","\"Nobody breathe,\" whispered Luca. \"If he writes one bad word, we are finished.\"","Then the food began to arrive. First came a soup with a tiny piece of gold on top. Then a fish, \"caught this morning by the chef's uncle\". Then a second soup, because the first one had only been \"a small hello\". Deniz was too polite to stop them, so he ate everything. At last the chef himself came out of the kitchen. Gaspard was a huge man with shaking hands. Behind him, twelve cooks watched through the little window in the kitchen door.","\"Mr. Costa,\" the chef whispered. \"How is the soup?\"","Deniz looked at the gold, the cream and the faces at the window. He did not understand why the chef was nearly crying, but he wanted to say something kind. \"It's... interesting,\" he said.","Gaspard turned white. From the kitchen came a small sound: twelve cooks saying \"Interesting?\" at the same moment.","\"Interesting,\" the chef repeated. \"Tomasz, is interesting good?\"","\"I don't know, Chef! It isn't excellent!\"","Deniz was trying to think of a way to escape when the front door opened and a woman in a green coat came in. Dark hair, big smile, no scarf. Mariana! He jumped up and waved both arms, and Luca crossed the room like a goalkeeper.","\"Good evening, madam. Do you have a booking?\"","\"Yes,\" said the woman. \"Under Costa.\"","Luca narrowed his eyes. Another Costa? On this of all nights? This woman was clearly a fan who wanted to get close to the great critic. \"I am so sorry,\" he said. \"Mr. Costa is already here. But we do have a small table by the kitchen.\"","The woman looked across the room: the red scarf, the champagne, the three waiters, the chef who was almost crying. One of her eyebrows went up. \"I see,\" she said slowly. \"A small table would be perfect.\"","The small table was next to the toilets. Her bread was hard. Her soup was cold. The waiters did not look at her once, because they were too busy looking at Deniz. The woman took out a pen and began to write on a napkin. She looked as happy as a cat.","Deniz tried to go to her, but every time he stood up, a waiter appeared. \"More fish, Mr. Costa?\" \"The chef has made a surprise for you, Mr. Costa!\" At last he said, \"Excuse me. I need the toilet.\" Nobody can say no to that. He walked past the toilet door and sat down at the small table next to it.","\"Hello,\" he said. \"I'm Deniz. I'm very sorry. I'm not Mr. Costa. I don't know why they think I am.\"","\"I know,\" said the woman. \"I'm Mariana Costa.\"","\"Costa? But that's the booking name! So you're... you're...\" He stopped.","\"M. Costa, food writer at the Evening Post,\" she said. \"Five years, and nobody has ever recognized me. Tonight they gave the critic's table to somebody else.\" She looked at the waiters, who were watching them from every corner like people watching a house on fire. \"For five years, every restaurant has given me its best food. If they knew me tonight, I would have hot soup. But they don't, so I'm eating like a normal person. It's wonderful. It's also terrible.\"","Deniz laughed so loudly that Luca dropped his clipboard.","\"So what do we do now?\" he asked.","\"We eat,\" said Mariana. \"You have seven dishes coming. I have a cold soup. Let's share.\"","Behind them, Tomasz pulled Luca's sleeve. \"Look,\" he whispered. \"Mr. Costa is eating the hard bread.\"","\"What?\" Luca went pale. \"Why is he eating the bad bread?\"","\"It's a test! He's testing us!\"","That was the end of the evening as Luca knew it. Within a minute, the best food in Casa Lumi was running across the restaurant to the table next to the toilets. Hot soup. The fish. The champagne. A cake with a candle on it. The twelve cooks left their places and stood in a line, hoping for one more \"interesting\". The woman in the green coat ate everything, wrote on three napkins, and did not stop smiling.","On Sunday, the Evening Post printed a review. It was short.","CASA LUMI. One star. The food is good, but the staff only see the person they expect to see. My soup was cold. My bread was older than the chef. The man in the red scarf received everything else, and he described it all as \"interesting\". He was right. — M. Costa","Gaspard put the review in a golden frame. Under the words \"One star\", he wrote one more word in red pen: INTERESTING.","Cem phoned Deniz on Monday morning. \"Well? Did she recognize you by the scarf?\"","\"Cem,\" said Deniz, \"everybody recognized me by the scarf.\"","Now Deniz and Mariana eat at Casa Lumi every Friday. They always ask for the table next to the toilets. Luca says it is the only table in the restaurant with a waiting list."],"glossary":{"critic":"eleştirmen (burada: restoran eleştirmeni)","booking":"rezervasyon","blind date":"tanımadığı biriyle ayarlanmış randevu","review":"eleştiri yazısı, değerlendirme"},"phrases":[{"en":"blind date","tr":"tanımadığın biriyle ayarlanmış randevu"},{"en":"Say no more","tr":"Gerisini söyleme, anladım"},{"en":"at once","tr":"hemen, o anda"},{"en":"at last","tr":"sonunda"},{"en":"on this of all nights","tr":"tam da bu gece, hem de bu gece"},{"en":"within a minute","tr":"bir dakika içinde"},{"en":"turned white","tr":"yüzü bembeyaz oldu, rengi attı"},{"en":"get close to","tr":"yakınlaşmak, yaklaşmak"},{"en":"went pale","tr":"rengi soldu, yüzü sarardı"},{"en":"too polite to","tr":"...mek için fazla kibar (kibarlıktan yapamamak)"},{"en":"took out","tr":"(cebinden/çantasından) çıkardı"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Luca think Deniz is Mr. Costa?","options":["Deniz says he is here for the Costa table","Deniz shows him a newspaper","Deniz asks to speak to the chef","Deniz is carrying a camera"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Deniz 'Costa masası için geldim' der; Luca onu gizli kalmaya çalışan eleştirmen sanar."},{"q":"What does Deniz say about the soup?","options":["It is excellent","It is too cold","It is interesting","It is too expensive"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Deniz kibarca 'interesting' der; şef ve aşçılar bu kelimenin iyi mi kötü mü olduğunu anlayamaz."},{"q":"Why does Mariana sit at the small table by the toilets?","options":["She wants to be alone","She does not like the other tables","Luca thinks she is a fan who wants to meet the critic","She arrives too late for a better table"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Luca, Mariana'yı ünlü eleştirmenle tanışmaya çalışan bir hayran sanır ve onu tuvaletin yanındaki masaya oturtur."},{"q":"Who is the real food critic?","options":["Cem","Mariana","Deniz","Tomasz"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"M. Costa aslında Mariana Costa'dır; kimse onun yüzünü tanımıyordur."},{"q":"Why do the waiters think Deniz is eating the hard bread?","options":["He is very hungry","He wants to be funny","He does not like the soft bread","He is testing the restaurant"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Garsonlar, 'Mr. Costa' sandıkları Deniz'in sert ekmeği yemesini restoranı test etmesi olarak yorumlar."}],"words":1125,"length":"short"},{"id":"dracula-abridged","type":"story","title":"Dracula","author":"Bram Stoker, abridged by Lantern","year":1897,"level":"B1","genre":"horror","abridged":true,"work":{"title":"Dracula","titleTr":"Drakula","author":"Bram Stoker","year":1897},"source":{"name":"Dracula — Project Gutenberg #345; abridged and retold by Lantern","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/345","license":"Public domain (source text); this retelling is by Lantern"},"summaryTr":"Genç avukat Jonathan Harker, Londra'da ev almak isteyen gizemli Kont Drakula'yı ziyaret etmek için Transilvanya dağlarındaki ıssız bir şatoya gider; çok geçmeden korku, İngiltere'deki sevdiklerine de ulaşır. Bram Stoker'ın ünlü romanının kısaltılmış, sade bir anlatımı.","paragraphs":["## The Road to the Castle","\"Bistritz, 3 May.\" Jonathan Harker wrote these words in his journal by the light of a small lamp. He was a young English lawyer on the longest journey of his life. His employer had sent him across Europe to meet a rich nobleman in the mountains of Transylvania. The nobleman, Count Dracula, wanted to buy a house in London, and Jonathan was bringing the papers.","When the old landlady of his hotel heard where he was going, her face turned pale. \"Must you go?\" she asked. \"Oh, young sir, tonight is the eve of Saint George's Day. At midnight all the evil things in the world will have power.\" Jonathan only smiled, so at last she took a small crucifix from her own neck and put it round his. \"For your mother's sake,\" she said quietly. Jonathan did not want to be rude, so he kept it.","Next morning he set off in the public coach. When the other passengers heard where he was going, they made the sign of the cross. Late at night the coach reached the Borgo Pass. There a small carriage with four black horses was waiting. The driver was a tall man with a long brown beard and a great black hat that hid his face. His eyes looked red in the lamplight, and his hand was as strong as steel.","Then the wolves came. They stood all around the carriage in the moonlight, with long white teeth and red tongues, howling. The horses screamed with fear. But the driver stood in the road, raised his long arms and spoke a command, and the wolves moved back into the trees.","At last the carriage stopped in the courtyard of a huge, half-ruined castle. Jonathan waited alone in front of a great old door. He heard chains, and the sound of a key, and the door opened. A tall old man stood there, dressed in black from head to foot, with a long white moustache and a silver lamp in his hand.","\"Welcome to my house!\" he said in excellent English. \"Enter freely and of your own will!\" He took Jonathan's hand. His grip was painfully strong, and his hand was as cold as ice, more like the hand of a dead man than a living one. \"I am Dracula,\" he said.","## A Prisoner in the Castle","A good supper was waiting by a warm fire, but the Count did not eat. His face was proud and hard, with a thin high nose and thick eyebrows. His teeth were sharp and white, and his lips were very red for such an old man. His ears were pointed at the top, and there were hairs in the middle of his palms. When he leaned close, his breath was so terrible that Jonathan felt sick.","Outside, the wolves began to howl. The Count's eyes shone. \"Listen to them,\" he said softly. \"The children of the night. What music they make!\"","For several days Jonathan slept by day and talked with the Count by night. The Count wanted to know everything about England and about Carfax, the old house near London that he was buying.","One morning Jonathan was shaving by the window with his little mirror. Suddenly a hand touched his shoulder, and a voice said, \"Good morning.\" Jonathan jumped and cut himself. The Count was standing right behind him, but in the mirror there was nobody. When the Count saw the blood on Jonathan's chin, his eyes burned with a wild anger, and he reached for Jonathan's throat. His hand touched the beads of the crucifix, and at once the anger was gone. \"Take care how you cut yourself,\" he said. \"It is more dangerous than you think in this country.\" Then he threw the mirror out of the window, and it broke into pieces far below.","Jonathan explored the castle. Every door was locked, and the windows looked down on terrible cliffs. \"The castle is a prison,\" he wrote in his journal, \"and I am a prisoner!\" There were no servants either. The Count made the bed and laid the table himself. And the driver with the strong hand at the Borgo Pass: had that been the Count too?","One night, from a high window, Jonathan saw the Count's head come out of a window below. Then the Count climbed out and began to crawl down the castle wall, face down, with his black cloak spread around him like great wings. He moved like a lizard. \"What kind of man is this?\" Jonathan wrote. \"I am afraid, terribly afraid, and there is no escape for me.\"","The Count also made him write three letters home with dates in the future. The last one said that he had already left the castle. Jonathan understood. When the third letter reached England, he would be dead.","## The Three Women","The Count had warned him never to sleep in any other part of the castle. But one night Jonathan fell asleep in an old room where ladies had lived long ago. When he woke, or seemed to wake, he was not alone. Three young women stood in the moonlight, and they threw no shadows on the floor. Two were dark, with high noses like the Count's. The third was fair, with golden hair and eyes like blue jewels. All three had brilliant white teeth and very red lips.","\"Go on,\" said one. \"You are first, and we shall follow.\" The fair woman knelt over him. Jonathan felt her warm breath on his neck and the touch of sharp teeth on his skin.","Suddenly the Count was in the room, in a fury. He took the fair woman by the neck and threw her back. \"How dare you touch him?\" he cried. \"This man belongs to me!\" Then he threw a bag on the floor. Something inside it moved, and Jonathan heard the cry of a small child. The women jumped towards the bag, and Jonathan fainted with horror.","He woke in his own bed. The next day a poor woman came running into the courtyard, screaming, \"Monster, give me my child!\" The Count called out from a high window, and soon a pack of wolves came through the gate. A little later the courtyard was silent.","Szgany came to the castle with big wagons, and Jonathan heard them carrying heavy boxes. One day he climbed out of his window and along the wall into the Count's room. A dark stair led down to a ruined chapel. There he found fifty great wooden boxes full of earth, and in one of them lay the Count. His eyes were open, but he did not breathe.","On his last day Jonathan went down again to look for the key. This time the Count looked younger. His white hair had turned dark grey, and there was fresh blood on his lips. He lay there like a fat, full leech. Jonathan picked up a shovel and struck at the hateful face, but the head turned, and the shovel only cut the forehead. Then he heard the Szgany coming to nail down the boxes, and he ran.","\"I will not stay here with those women,\" he wrote. \"I will climb down the castle wall. If I fall, I shall be at peace. Goodbye, all! Mina!\"","## Mina and Lucy","In England, Mina Murray, the young woman Jonathan was going to marry, waited for news. She was a clever, practical schoolteacher who was learning to type. Her best friend, Lucy Westenra, was a beautiful and lively girl. In one single day, Lucy wrote, three men had asked her to marry them. The first was Dr John Seward, a young doctor who ran a lunatic asylum. The second was Quincey Morris, a brave, friendly American from Texas. The third was Arthur Holmwood, the son of a lord, and Lucy loved him. \"Why can't they let a girl marry three men?\" she joked. She said yes to Arthur, and the other two stayed his loyal friends.","In the summer Mina went to stay with Lucy at Whitby, a little fishing town by the sea. The girls liked to sit on a bench in the old churchyard on the cliff. But Mina was worried. Jonathan had not written for weeks. And Lucy had begun to walk in her sleep again.","On the night of 8 August a great storm hit the coast. Out of the wind and rain, a Russian ship came flying into the harbour, although nobody seemed to steer it. As it touched the shore, a huge black dog jumped from the deck and ran up the cliff into the darkness. On board there was only one man: the captain, dead, tied to the wheel with a crucifix in his hands. The ship was the Demeter, from Varna, and her only cargo was fifty boxes of earth.","The captain's log told a terrible story. During the journey the sailors had disappeared one by one in the night. The men whispered that a tall, thin stranger was on board. The mate went mad and jumped into the sea. At the end the captain was alone, and he tied himself to the wheel so that he could not leave his ship, whatever came.","## A Shape in the Churchyard","A few nights later, Mina woke and found Lucy's bed empty. She ran out in the moonlight to the churchyard. On their bench sat a white figure, and over it something long and black was bending. \"Lucy! Lucy!\" Mina called. The dark shape raised its head, and she saw a white face and red, shining eyes. When she reached the bench, nobody was there but Lucy, asleep and breathing heavily. Mina put her own shawl round her friend and fastened it with a pin. Later she saw two little red marks on Lucy's throat, and she thought, unhappily, that she had made them with the pin.","After that, Lucy grew pale and weak. At night she often sat by the open window, and once Mina saw a big bat flying near it in the moonlight.","Then a letter came from a hospital in Budapest. Jonathan was there, very ill with a brain fever. Nobody knew how he had escaped from the castle. Mina hurried across Europe to him. He was thin and confused, and he gave her his journal. \"The secret is here,\" he said, \"but I do not want to know it. Keep it, and never let me see it, unless some terrible duty makes it necessary.\" Mina sealed it without reading it. They were married there in the hospital, and soon afterwards they went home to England.","## The Professor from Amsterdam","Lucy went back to her home in London, but she did not get better. Arthur asked his friend Dr Seward to examine her. Seward saw that she had lost a lot of blood, but he could not find a reason. So he wrote to his old teacher, Professor Abraham Van Helsing of Amsterdam, an expert on strange diseases.","Van Helsing, a strong, kind man with serious blue eyes, came at once. He knew immediately what Lucy needed: blood. That same day Arthur gave his blood to her in a transfusion, and she grew stronger. But after one night alone she was white and weak again. Then Seward gave his blood, then Van Helsing, and at last Quincey Morris. Each time she grew better, and each time, in the night, something took the blood away. And each morning there were two small marks on her throat.","Van Helsing ordered a box of white flowers from Holland. They were garlic flowers. He hung them around the window and the door and made a necklace of them for Lucy. \"Do not open the window tonight,\" he told her. For one night she slept well. But in the morning her mother, who wanted her to have fresh air, took away the flowers and opened the window. Lucy was worse than ever. After that, Van Helsing and Seward took turns to keep watch by her bed.","One night they were both away, and Lucy and her mother were alone. Lucy heard something beating at the window, like the wings of a great bat. Then the glass broke, and the head of a huge grey wolf came through the window. Lucy's mother had a weak heart. She screamed, pulled the garlic flowers from Lucy's neck, and fell dead. Then the air was full of tiny pieces of dust that turned and danced in the moonlight, and Lucy knew no more.","## \"Kiss Me, Arthur!\"","When Van Helsing and Seward found her in the morning, she was nearly dead. They gave her blood again, but now even the professor knew they could not win. As she slept, her face changed. Her teeth looked longer and sharper.","Arthur came to say goodbye. Suddenly Lucy opened her eyes, and in a soft, strange voice that none of them had heard before, she said, \"Arthur! Oh, my love, I am so glad you have come! Kiss me!\" Arthur bent towards her. But Van Helsing caught him with both hands and pulled him back across the room. \"Not for your life!\" he said. \"Not for your living soul and hers!\" For a moment an angry look passed over Lucy's face, and her sharp teeth closed together.","Then her eyes opened again, and now they were her own gentle eyes. She took Van Helsing's hand and kissed it. \"My true friend,\" she whispered. \"My true friend, and his! Oh, guard him, and give me peace!\" Van Helsing let Arthur kiss her only on the forehead. A few minutes later she was gone.","\"It is all over,\" said Seward sadly.","\"Not so,\" answered Van Helsing. \"It is only the beginning.\"","## The Lady in White","A few days later, Jonathan and Mina were walking in London when Jonathan suddenly held her arm. A tall, thin man with a black moustache and a pointed beard was staring at a pretty girl in a carriage. \"It is the man himself!\" Jonathan whispered. \"But he has grown young!\" The Count was in London.","That night Mina read Jonathan's journal and learned the whole story of the castle. When Van Helsing read it too, he told her, \"Your husband's story is true. Every word.\" With this news, Jonathan got his old strength back. He was not mad after all.","At the same time, strange reports appeared in the newspapers. Small children were disappearing at night on Hampstead Heath, near the cemetery where Lucy lay. They came back in the morning, weak and pale, with small wounds on their throats. They all said that a beautiful lady had asked them to walk with her.","Van Helsing took Seward to Lucy's tomb at night and opened her coffin. It was empty. The next day they came back, and Lucy was lying there, more beautiful than ever, with red lips and sharp white teeth.","At midnight Van Helsing brought Arthur and Quincey to hide near the tomb. Soon a figure in white came walking between the graves with a child in her arms. It was Lucy, but how changed! Her sweetness had become cruelty, and there was fresh blood on her lips. When she saw them, she dropped the child carelessly and moved towards Arthur with open arms. \"Come to me, Arthur,\" she said in a sweet voice. \"Leave these others and come to me. My arms are hungry for you.\"","Arthur opened his arms, but Van Helsing jumped between them and held up a golden crucifix. Lucy stepped back, her face full of hate. She went to the door of her tomb and passed inside through a crack as thin as a knife.","The next day, in the daylight, they opened the coffin again. Van Helsing gave Arthur a wooden stake and a hammer. \"It is the only way to give her peace,\" he said. Arthur was pale, but his hand did not shake. He struck, again and again, while the others read prayers. The thing in the coffin cried out and fought, and then it was still. When they looked again, the cruel creature was gone. It was Lucy, sweet and pure, as they had known her in life. Arthur bent down and kissed her.","\"One part of our work is done,\" said Van Helsing. \"Now we must find the one who did this, and destroy him.\"","## The Enemy Next Door","The friends met at Dr Seward's asylum. Mina typed all their diaries, letters and newspaper reports into one long record. Then Van Helsing explained what they were fighting. The Count was a vampire, centuries old. He could become a wolf, a bat or a mist, and he was as strong as twenty men. But he had weaknesses too. He could not enter a house unless someone invited him. He feared garlic, the crucifix and the holy bread called the Sacred Wafer. By day his power was weak, and he had to rest in the earth of his homeland: the earth in those fifty boxes.","By a strange chance, Carfax, the Count's new house, stood right beside the asylum. And in the asylum there was a patient named Renfield, who ate flies and spiders because he believed their lives gave him more life. \"The Master is at hand,\" he said.","The men decided to keep Mina out of the danger, and they no longer told her their plans. It was a terrible mistake. Mina became pale and tired, and she had bad dreams of a white mist coming under her door, with two red eyes inside it.","One night Renfield was found on the floor of his room, badly hurt. Before he died, he told them the truth. The Count had come to his window and promised him thousands of living creatures, and Renfield had invited him in. But Mina had been kind to him, and when he saw her pale face, he understood. He tried to stop the Count, and the Count threw him down like a toy.","The men ran upstairs to the Harkers' room and broke the door. Jonathan lay on the bed in a heavy, unnatural sleep. The Count stood beside the bed, holding Mina. He had opened a wound in his own chest with his long nail, and he was forcing her mouth to it. When he turned, his eyes burned red. Van Helsing held up the Sacred Wafer, the others raised their crucifixes, and the Count moved back step by step. Then a cloud covered the moon. When the light returned, there was only a thin mist moving under the door.","\"Unclean, unclean!\" Mina cried, and pushed Jonathan away from her. The Count had told her that now she was blood of his blood, and that when he called her, she would come to him across land and sea.","The next morning Van Helsing gently touched her forehead with the Sacred Wafer. There was a smell of burning, and Mina screamed. The Wafer had left a red mark on her skin like a burn. \"Unclean!\" she whispered. \"Even God turns away from me!\" But the men knelt around her and swore that they would never give up until the Count was destroyed.","## The Last Box","They found the Count's boxes in Carfax and in other houses around London, and in each box they put a piece of the Wafer to make sure that he could never rest there again. Then they waited for him in his house in Piccadilly. Late in the afternoon he came, moving quickly like a panther. Jonathan swung his great knife at him. The Count jumped back, and the knife only cut his coat. Banknotes and gold fell to the floor. The Count grabbed a handful and jumped through the window into the yard below.","From there he looked up at them. \"You think you have left me without a place to rest,\" he called, \"but I have more. My revenge has only begun! Time is on my side.\" Then he was gone.","But there was one box they had not found. Mina had an idea. \"The Count's mind is linked to mine,\" she said. \"Hypnotise me, Professor, before sunrise. Perhaps I can hear what he hears.\" Van Helsing did so, and Mina spoke slowly in a dreamy voice. \"I can hear water... little waves... men running on a deck... a chain.\" The Count was on a ship. He was running home.","The ship was the Czarina Catherine, sailing for the port of Varna on the Black Sea. The friends crossed Europe by train and waited for it there. But the Count could also reach into Mina's mind and learn their plans. The ship went to Galatz instead, and the box was carried away before they arrived. Mina asked them to tell her nothing more. She also made them promise one more thing: \"If I change, if I become like him, you must kill me.\" Sadly, they promised.","Then Mina studied the maps and worked out the Count's road. The box would go up the rivers by boat, and then by cart over the mountains to the castle. So the friends separated. Jonathan and Arthur followed the river in a fast steam boat. Seward and Quincey rode along the bank. Van Helsing took Mina by train and carriage towards the Borgo Pass, the same road that Jonathan had travelled months before.","## Snow on the Borgo Pass","The snow fell heavily as Van Helsing and Mina came near the castle. Mina slept more and more by day, and she seemed to know the road, although she had never seen it. On the last evening, Van Helsing made a fire, and around Mina he drew a circle on the ground with crumbs of the Sacred Wafer. \"Now nothing evil can reach you,\" he said.","In the night the horses screamed. Out of the swirling snow came three women, the same women Jonathan had seen in the castle. They smiled and called to Mina in sweet voices: \"Come, sister. Come to us. Come!\" Van Helsing looked at Mina in fear, but she did not move. Her face showed only horror, and he knew that she was still herself. The women could not cross the holy circle, and when the morning came, they disappeared. The poor horses lay dead in the snow.","In the daylight, Van Helsing left Mina safe inside the circle and went into the castle. In the old chapel he found the three women sleeping in their tombs, beautiful and terrible. It was the hardest work of his life. He did for each of them what Arthur had done for Lucy, and each one crumbled into dust. Then he found a great tomb with one word on it: DRACULA. It was empty. He placed a piece of the Wafer inside, so that the Count could never lie there again.","## Sunset","On the afternoon of 6 November, Mina and Van Helsing watched the road below through field glasses. \"Look!\" cried Mina. A band of Szgany, the Count’s servants, on horses was coming up the road at great speed, around a cart with a large square box. The sun was low, and they were in a hurry. At sunset, the Count would be free.","From two directions, four other riders came racing over the snow: Jonathan and Arthur, Seward and Quincey. The wolves were gathering too. \"Halt!\" shouted Jonathan and Quincey together, and the four men pointed their rifles at the Szgany. The Szgany pulled out their knives.","The leader of the Szgany pointed at the sinking sun. But Jonathan was already pushing through the ring of men, and nothing could stop him. He jumped onto the cart, lifted the heavy box with impossible strength, and threw it to the ground. On the other side, Quincey fought his way through the knives. When he reached the box, Mina saw that he was holding his side, and blood was running through his fingers.","Together the two men attacked the lid with their knives, and it came open. The Count lay inside on his earth, as pale as wax. His red eyes saw the setting sun, and the hate in them turned to triumph.","But at that moment Jonathan's great knife flashed across the Count's throat, and Quincey's knife went into his heart. Before their eyes, almost in one breath, the whole body crumbled into dust and was gone. All her life, Mina was glad of one thing: in that last moment there was a look of peace on the Count's face.","The Szgany rode away in fear, and the wolves followed them. Quincey sank down in the snow, with his head on Jonathan's shoulder. He took Mina's hand and smiled. \"I am only too happy to have been of service,\" he said. Then he cried out, pointing at her face, \"It was worth dying for this! Look! Look!\" The red light of the sunset fell on Mina's forehead. The mark was gone, and her skin was as white as the snow. \"The curse has passed away!\" said Quincey. And with a smile, in silence, he died, a brave gentleman.","Seven years later, Jonathan added a short note to their papers. He and Mina had a little boy, born on the same day of the year that Quincey died, and they called him Quincey. Arthur and Seward were both happily married. That summer they had visited Transylvania again. The castle still stood above the empty land. No one would believe such a wild story, but Van Helsing, with the boy on his knee, said that they needed no proofs. \"One day this boy will know what a brave woman his mother is, and how much some men loved her, and what they dared for her sake.\""],"glossary":{"count":"kont (soyluluk unvanı)","pass":"(dağ) geçit","coach":"posta arabası, yolcu arabası","earth":"toprak (Kont'un memleketinden getirdiği toprak)","log":"seyir defteri (gemi günlüğü)","mate":"ikinci kaptan (gemide)","wheel":"dümen (gemi dümeni)","cross":"haç (haç çıkarmak)","master":"efendi, sahip","wafer":"kutsanmış ekmek (hostiya)","stake":"kazık (sivri tahta)","glasses":"(field glasses) dürbün","band":"grup, topluluk"},"phrases":[{"en":"set off","tr":"yola çıkmak"},{"en":"turned pale","tr":"(yüzü) sarardı, benzi attı"},{"en":"fell asleep","tr":"uyuyakaldı, uykuya daldı"},{"en":"keep watch","tr":"nöbet tutmak, gözcülük etmek"},{"en":"give up","tr":"vazgeçmek, pes etmek"},{"en":"make sure","tr":"emin olmak, garantiye almak"},{"en":"worked out","tr":"(düşünerek) çözdü, bulup çıkardı"},{"en":"in a hurry","tr":"acele içinde, aceleyle"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Jonathan see in his mirror when the Count stood behind him?","options":["The Count's red eyes","A big grey wolf","No reflection of the Count","The three women"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Kont tam arkasında durmasına rağmen aynada görünmüyordu; Jonathan ilk kez onun normal bir insan olmadığını anlar."},{"q":"What was the only cargo of the ship Demeter?","options":["Gold coins","Fifty boxes of earth","Garlic flowers","Russian soldiers"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Demeter gemisi Whitby'ye sadece elli sandık toprak getirmişti; Kont gündüzleri bu toprakta dinlenmek zorundaydı."},{"q":"Why did Van Helsing put garlic flowers in Lucy's room?","options":["Because Lucy loved their smell","To help her mother's weak heart","To make the air fresh","To protect her from the vampire"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Sarımsak çiçekleri vampiri uzak tutmak içindi; ama Lucy'nin annesi pencereyi açıp çiçekleri kaldırınca Lucy yine kötüleşti."},{"q":"How did Mina help the men to follow the Count after he left London?","options":["Under hypnosis she could hear what he heard","She found a letter from the Count","She followed the big black dog","Renfield told her the name of the ship"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Van Helsing Mina'yı hipnotize etti; Mina, Kont'la arasındaki bağ sayesinde dalga ve zincir seslerini duydu ve Kont'un bir gemide olduğunu anladılar."},{"q":"What happened to the red mark on Mina's forehead at the end?","options":["It became bigger","It turned into a cross","It disappeared when the Count died","It stayed there for seven years"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Kont toza dönüşünce lanet kalktı; ölmek üzere olan Quincey, Mina'nın alnındaki izin kaybolduğunu gösterdi."}],"words":4259,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-last-repair-ticket","type":"story","title":"The Last Repair Ticket","author":"Lantern Original","level":"B1","genre":"drama","summaryTr":"Mira, babasının yıllardır işlettiği tamir dükkânını kapatmak için kasabasına döner. Son siparişleri teslim etmeye çalışırken, ailesinin aynı yılları birbirinden çok farklı hatırladığını fark eder.","paragraphs":["The shop bell rang when Mira pushed the door, although there was no electricity inside. Her father had always refused an electric bell. A small brass one was enough, he said, and it would never stop working because somebody forgot to pay a bill. Now the bills lay in a thick envelope on the counter. Mira put her suitcase beside a broken fan and opened the curtains. Dust rose in the morning light. Across the window, her father's painted name faced the street: Daniel Reed, Repairs. Under it, in smaller letters, were the words Since 1989.","She had come home to close the shop. Her father had fallen on the back steps six weeks earlier and broken his hip. He could now walk slowly with a frame, but he could not stand at the workbench all day. The rent was increasing in September, and the shop had made very little money for months. Mira had explained the figures to him on the phone. He had said, 'Do what you think is best.' She knew those words. They could mean agreement, anger, or a wish to end the conversation. This time she had accepted them as agreement.","Her younger brother Leo arrived ten minutes later, carrying two coffees. He had been opening the shop on Saturdays while working nights at a warehouse. He looked tired, and his jacket smelled faintly of rain. Mira pointed at the shelves. 'How much of this belongs to customers?' Leo gave her a flat cardboard box. Inside were repair tickets, some white, some yellow, and a few so old that the writing was difficult to read. 'Most things have a number,' he said. Mira looked at a radio with no label. 'Most is going to be a problem.'","They had five days before the landlord wanted the keys. Mira had taken a week away from her office job. Leo could help in the mornings before he slept. Their father was staying in a room on the ground floor of his house, four streets away. He expected them for lunch. Mira began three lists: return to customer, sell, and recycle. Leo watched her write. 'There should be a fourth.' She asked what it was. 'Ask Dad.' Mira tapped the envelope of bills. They could ask him questions, but they could not ask the shop to stay open.","The first customer arrived while they were arguing about a box of cables. Mrs Bell wanted her kitchen clock. Mira found ticket forty-two and a clock with blue numbers. It was already working. She asked for the amount written on the ticket. Mrs Bell frowned. 'Your father said I could pay next month.' Mira began to explain that there would be no shop next month, then stopped. The woman was looking at the empty space where the kettle usually stood. Leo found a small paper bag for the clock. 'Next month is fine,' he said quietly.","After Mrs Bell left, Mira turned to him. 'We cannot keep saying that.' Leo drank the last of his coffee. 'We cannot start by making her feel ashamed either.' Mira felt heat rise in her face. She had not come to be cruel. She had paid two late electricity bills and arranged her father's hospital visits from another city. Every problem seemed to become her problem because she was the one who could count. She wrote Mrs Bell's name on a separate page. Beside it, she added the amount owed and the date they had agreed.","At lunch, Daniel asked whether anybody had come in. Leo described Mrs Bell's clock. Their father nodded. 'The spring only needed cleaning.' Mira put her lists on the table beside his soup. He did not look at them. She explained the plan for the week and asked about several unlabelled items. Daniel answered the first questions clearly. The red toaster belonged to the school caretaker. The sewing machine was waiting for a part. Then she mentioned selling the heavy workbench. His spoon stopped halfway to his mouth. 'That is not part of the stock,' he said.","Mira said there was nowhere to put it. Daniel's house was small, Leo lived in a rented room, and her flat was on the third floor. 'Then leave it,' her father said. 'Let the landlord have it.' Leo looked up. Mira could not tell whether Daniel was making a practical suggestion or trying to hurt them. She explained that selling it might cover the last month's rent. Daniel pushed his bowl away. He said he was tired. Mira collected her papers, and the three of them spent the rest of lunch discussing the weather.","That afternoon, Leo found ticket seventy-three under a drawer. It belonged to a portable radio in a dark green case. The customer's name was Ruth Avery. Beneath the fault description, their father had written: Needed by Friday. Leo recognised the name. Ruth's husband had used the radio when he worked in his greenhouse. He had died earlier that spring. Mira called the number on the ticket. Ruth answered at once. She sounded relieved to hear from the shop. 'Your father promised it would be ready for the evening at the garden,' she said.","Mira asked what evening she meant. Ruth was inviting friends to her husband's greenhouse on Friday. They would share food and remember him. He had listened to the same music programme every week, and she wanted to play it on his radio. Mira looked at the open back of the case. Several parts lay in a small plastic dish. She did not know their names. 'I'll check where we are with it,' she said. Ruth thanked her. After the call, Leo said they had to finish the repair. Mira pointed out that neither of them knew how.","Leo had worked beside their father as a teenager. He could change a plug, clean a switch, and find a broken wire. But Daniel had always kept the difficult jobs for himself. Mira remembered being told to answer the phone while Leo was allowed behind the counter. 'You were better with people,' Leo said when she mentioned it. She gave him a look. 'I was twelve. I wanted to use the tools.' He had never known that. For a moment, they stood beside the radio as children again, each surprised by the shape of the other's memory.","They took it to Daniel that evening. He opened the case and reached automatically towards a place where his tools should have been. Leo brought a small set from the shop. Daniel examined the loose parts for several minutes. Then his hands began to shake with tiredness. 'Tomorrow,' he said. Mira wanted to ask whether he could really do it, but Leo shook his head slightly. They packed the radio away. Outside, she said that delaying would only make the failure worse. Leo answered that she always called something a failure before it had finished happening.","Mira walked back to the shop alone. There were several messages on her work phone. A colleague wanted a report she had promised to send before leaving. She sat at the counter with her laptop, surrounded by dead lamps and silent radios. From the street, she could hear teenagers laughing outside the takeaway. She remembered doing homework here while her father served customers. He had often looked up and asked if she needed help. She had always said no, partly because he was busy and partly because she wanted him to notice without being asked.","On Tuesday morning, an appliance dealer came to see what he could buy. Mira showed him the lamps, small heaters, and unused parts that belonged to the shop. He offered one price for everything. It was much lower than she expected. When she questioned it, he lifted a dusty fan. 'You are selling a lot of work, not a lot of value.' She disliked him for saying it, but the shelves supported his point. Leo arrived while she was considering the offer. He said the price was unfair. The dealer shrugged and gave them until Thursday to decide.","Leo wanted to sell each item separately. Mira asked who would photograph everything, answer messages, and wait for buyers. 'I could,' he said. 'After your night shift? Before or after helping Dad?' She regretted the sharpness immediately. He put his coffee on the counter without drinking it. 'You do not have to remind me I am tired.' Mira looked at his pale face. They were both right about different parts of the problem. That made the argument harder to end. They agreed to set aside six useful items and accept the dealer's price for the rest.","Among the six was a tall reading lamp with a heavy base. Leo cleaned it while Mira called customers. Some were grateful; others had forgotten leaving anything. One man insisted that a cracked food mixer had been perfect when he brought it in. Mira found the original note: Bowl cracked; motor stops. She read it to him and waited through a long silence. Then he apologised. The note was in her mother's handwriting. Their mother had kept the shop records until she died nine years earlier. Mira touched the page after putting down the phone.","She had remembered her mother as somebody who made the shop friendly. Now the box of tickets showed another job. Each one included a date, a clear description, and a price agreed in advance. Later tickets were less complete. Some only said See Daniel. Mira began to understand how the shop had become difficult to manage. Her father could remember a fault in a radio but forget to charge for three hours of work. Her mother had held those two things together. After she died, nobody had properly taken over that part of the business.","At lunchtime, Mira asked her father why he had not hired someone to help with the records. He said there had never been enough money. She showed him several tickets without prices. 'There might have been more if you had written things down.' His face closed. 'I knew my customers.' She wanted to say that knowing people did not pay rent. Instead, she asked him to explain one ticket. He could not remember the item or the owner. He stared at the paper for a long time. Mira put it away and wished she had chosen another example.","After lunch, Daniel asked Leo to fetch a notebook from the kitchen cupboard. It contained names and amounts, written in narrow columns. Some debts were small. Others had grown over several years. Mira added the first page and stopped. 'Why didn't you tell us about this?' Her father looked towards the window. 'Because then you would have asked me to collect it.' Leo turned the pages. Beside one name was a note about a lost job. Beside another was the word Ill. The figures were not just figures, but they were still money the shop had never received.","Mira felt angry in a way that surprised her. She had believed the shop's problems came from old machines and rising rent. Now she saw choices. 'You let me send money while you gave other people free work.' Daniel said he had not asked her to send it. That made it worse. Leo said they should stop for a moment, but Mira was already standing. She told her father that being kind with somebody else's money was easy. As soon as the sentence left her mouth, she saw him pull back as if she had struck the table.","She left before he could answer. Outside, the street looked painfully ordinary. A delivery van blocked the corner. Somebody was washing windows. Mira walked until she reached the playing field behind her old school. She sat on a bench and opened her work email without reading anything. She had meant to explain that her help had cost something. She had missed weekends, delayed repairs in her own flat, and worried at night. Instead, she had turned her father into a selfish man in a single sentence. It was easier to blame him than to describe what she needed.","Leo found her an hour later. He did not sit immediately. 'Dad says you can sell everything.' Mira closed her laptop. 'That is not what I want.' Leo sat at the far end of the bench. For a while they watched two children trying to fly a small kite. Then he said he had wanted to leave the warehouse for months. A training course in building maintenance started in October, but it included afternoon classes. He could not study, work nights, and keep taking their father everywhere. Mira had not known about the course.","'Why didn't you tell me?' she asked. Leo gave a tired laugh. 'You always have enough to worry about.' She recognised the answer because she had used it herself. They had both protected each other from information and then felt alone with it. Mira asked how much the course cost. He said he had saved most of the fee. Time was the problem. They began discussing their father's appointments, shopping, and meals. For the first time that week, they worked on a problem without turning it into an argument about who had cared more.","They made a weekly plan on the back of an old school notice. Mira could arrange deliveries and handle bills from the city. She could come one weekend a month, sometimes two. Leo would visit on three mornings, but not on class days. They would ask their father which tasks he could do himself and whether he would accept paid help for cleaning. Nothing on the page was perfect. Mira still lived far away, and Leo still needed sleep. Yet the plan had edges. It did not assume that love meant being available at every hour.","When they returned, Daniel was asleep in his chair. The green radio sat on the table beside him. He had written the name of a part on a scrap of paper. Leo recognised it and searched online for a replacement. None could arrive before Friday. Mira suggested calling another repair shop in the next town. Leo looked doubtful. Their father and its owner, Mr Ellis, had stopped speaking years earlier. Mira remembered hearing a story about a customer who had taken work from one shop to the other. She had never understood why this mattered so much.","Mr Ellis answered the phone himself. Mira explained who she was and what they needed. He was quiet for a second, then asked for the part number. She read it twice. He thought he had one, but he would need to check a box in his storeroom. Could she call again in an hour? Before ending the call, he asked how Daniel was recovering. His voice sounded concerned, not pleased by a rival's trouble. Mira looked at the sleeping figure in the chair. Some old arguments seemed to survive mainly because nobody tested whether they still existed.","That evening, Daniel woke to find both his children at the table. Mira apologised for what she had said. She did not add an excuse, although several waited in her mind. Her father rubbed his hands together. Then he said he should have told her more about the money. 'I liked being useful,' he said. 'When people could not pay, I could still be useful.' Mira understood that feeling more than she wanted to. She told him she could help with a fixed amount each month, but she needed to know the whole situation. He nodded slowly.","Leo showed him their weekly plan. Daniel objected to cleaning help at once. He said he could manage a cloth. Leo asked him to demonstrate how he would clean the bathroom floor with a walking frame. Their father almost smiled. They agreed to try help twice a month and review it later. Daniel wanted to do his own shopping when he could walk farther. Mira wrote that down too. The plan was no longer something his children had made around him. It included things he wanted to recover, and tasks he was still able to do.","Then Leo mentioned Mr Ellis. Daniel's expression changed. He said they should not have called without asking. Mira said the radio belonged to Ruth, and Friday was approaching. 'We can keep your argument, or we can try to finish her repair.' Daniel looked at the green case. After a long pause, he asked whether Ellis had the part. Mira called back. He did. He would also test the radio after the repair if Daniel wanted. Their father listened without speaking. Finally, he said, 'Tell him I would appreciate that.' The words seemed to cost him considerable effort.","On Wednesday, Mira drove to the next town. Mr Ellis's shop was smaller than her father's and much neater. The needed part waited in a paper envelope. He refused payment until she insisted on at least covering its cost. While he wrote a receipt, she asked whether the old argument had really been about a customer. He looked embarrassed. It had begun with a borrowed tool that was returned broken. Neither man had believed the other's explanation. After that, every small disagreement had joined the first one. 'Foolish, really,' he said, folding the receipt.","Mira drove home thinking about how families also collected old disagreements. A late phone call could become proof that somebody did not care. A question about money could carry ten years of hurt. At the shop, Leo had sold the reading lamp to a student and found the owner of the sewing machine. He looked more hopeful. Together they made a notice for the window: Please collect your repaired items by Friday. For questions after closing, call this number. They used a temporary number that Mira could check. Closing the door did not mean losing everybody behind it.","They also agreed on a fair way to deal with the debts. Customers would receive a clear note showing the work and amount owed. Anyone who could not pay at once could contact them about smaller payments. They would not publish names or chase people at home. Daniel crossed out two amounts he had already promised to forgive. Mira wanted to argue, then remembered that these were earlier promises. She kept them out of the total. With the remaining money, they might cover more of the final bills than she had expected. It would take time, but at least they knew where they stood.","One customer called that afternoon after receiving his note. He said he had paid for the repair when he collected his lamp. Mira checked the notebook and found no record. Instead of arguing, she asked him to describe the payment. He remembered giving Leo the money on a Saturday. Leo searched his jacket and found the folded receipt in an inside pocket. He had forgotten to move it into the shop records. Mira corrected the notebook and apologised to the customer. When she told her father, he did not smile at Leo's mistake. He simply asked where they would keep completed receipts from now on.","On Thursday morning, a buyer arrived for the workbench. He ran a hand across its dark surface and said he planned to cut it in half. His workshop had only a small space. Leo looked at Mira. The price was good, and the bench belonged to their father. They had his permission to sell it. Still, Mira asked the buyer for ten minutes. Outside, she called the community centre. She remembered that it had started a small craft group. The manager said they could use a proper bench, but their budget was very limited.","Mira asked what they could offer. It was less than half the buyer's price. She thanked the manager and turned to Leo. He wanted to donate the bench, which Mira could not accept. They still owed rent. So they did the uncomfortable calculation together. If the centre paid what it could, and each of them added a small amount, the difference would be manageable. Neither would have to pretend the choice was free. They called Daniel. He was silent long enough for Mira to fear another argument. Then he asked whether the centre would collect it safely.","The buyer was disappointed but accepted their decision. That afternoon, two centre volunteers arrived with a van. Daniel came too, using a taxi because walking from the corner would tire him. It was his first visit since the fall. He stood in the doorway, looking at the half empty shelves. Nobody tried to make the moment cheerful. Mira brought a chair behind the counter. Her father sat and watched the volunteers prepare the bench. He warned them about one loose drawer, then asked where it would stand in its new room. They showed him a photograph on a phone.","After the bench had gone, a pale rectangle remained on the wall. Daniel stared at it. 'Your mother wanted me to paint in here,' he said. 'Every summer I said next year.' Mira remembered her mother choosing a warm yellow from a card of colours. She had forgotten that memory until now. Leo stood beside the empty space with his hands in his pockets. The shop had held their mother's work, their father's pride, and pieces of their childhood. It had also held dust and unpaid bills. All of those things could be true without cancelling each other.","Before leaving, Daniel asked to see the repair tickets. Mira gave him the box. He sorted through it until he found one with a drawing on the back. A small house stood beneath an enormous sun. Mira recognised her own childhood handwriting: Dad's holiday house. She had drawn it during a summer when they had not gone away. Daniel said he used to keep it beside the till. 'I always thought I would build you something like that.' Mira looked at the crooked windows. 'I would rather have had you at the beach for a day.'","The sentence was quiet, but it changed his face. Mira worried she had hurt him again. Then Daniel nodded. 'I thought if I worked enough, we could have better days later.' She sat on the edge of the counter. 'Some of the days were good already.' She remembered eating chips on the shop floor during a power cut. Leo remembered their father letting him choose the music on Saturdays. They talked for a while about ordinary afternoons, without trying to prove that everything had been happy. The missing holidays remained missing. The good afternoons remained good.","Back at the house, Daniel began work on Ruth's radio. Leo sat beside him, and Mira stayed to read the small print on a diagram. Their father explained each step before doing it. When his hands became tired, he showed Leo where to place the tool. Mira held a lamp so he could see. It took longer than it would once have taken him alone. Nobody mentioned that. At last, the loose parts were back in place. Leo connected the power and turned the switch. The radio made a thin noise, then fell silent again.","Daniel closed his eyes. Leo looked at the diagram, searching for something they had missed. Mira wanted to say they had done their best, but she knew it would sound like an ending. Instead, she asked what the silence told them. Her father opened his eyes and looked at her. 'That there is another fault.' It was so simple that she almost laughed. They had repaired one part of the problem, not all of it. Daniel checked the volume control and found a damaged connection. This time he asked Mira to hold the wire while he worked.","When they tried again, a voice filled the room. It was reading a weather report. Leo turned the dial until music appeared through a soft crackle. Daniel leaned back in his chair. His face did not show triumph so much as relief. They let the radio play while they cleared the table. The next morning, Mira took it to Mr Ellis for the promised check. He found no further problem and wrote a short note for Ruth explaining what had been repaired. On the back, he added a message for Daniel: I still have your old screwdriver. Call me.","Friday was their last day in the shop. Customers came to collect a kettle, a desk fan, and two clocks. Mrs Bell returned with half the money she owed and a cake wrapped in paper. Mira thanked her without making the payment into a ceremony. The appliance dealer removed the remaining stock. Leo swept beneath the shelves. At three, Mira found one final object in a cupboard: a small red torch. There was no ticket. Daniel remembered fixing it for a boy who used to pass after school, but he could not remember the boy's name.","They put the torch in the window with a note asking its owner to call. It seemed wrong to throw it away simply because their week had ended. Mira took a photograph of the note and sent it to the local community page. Then they packed the torch with the remaining unclaimed items, each carefully listed. The landlord had agreed that they could keep one small box in the back room for a month. Mira had asked in advance this time. There was a difference between leaving a problem and making a clear arrangement for finishing it.","Ruth arrived at four. She carried the radio against her coat after Mira handed it over, as if checking that it was really the same object. Daniel explained the new part and the repaired connection. Ruth asked the price. He looked at Mira before answering. Together they had written down a fair amount for the work, with the donated part of his time clearly shown. Ruth paid it. Then she invited them all to the greenhouse that evening. 'Only if you want to come,' she added. Mira had planned to drive back early. She looked at Leo and said they would be glad to.","At five, Daniel turned the sign to Closed. Mira counted the cash and put the last repair ticket in the box. Leo checked the back door. They stood together for a moment before going outside. Their father locked the front door himself, slowly but without help. The brass bell rang once as the door moved, then became still. Mira expected him to say something about all the years. Instead, he asked if anybody knew where his coat was. It was on the chair inside. They laughed, and he opened the door again to get it.","At Ruth's garden, lamps hung between the trees. People stood around the greenhouse with plates of food. The repaired radio sat on a shelf among empty plant pots. When the music programme began, Ruth lowered her head for a moment. Then she started telling a story about her husband trying to grow enormous pumpkins. Other people added their own memories. Daniel sat beside Mr Ellis, who had also been invited. Mira saw them talking and decided not to listen. Across the garden, Leo was asking a neighbour about the building maintenance course. His future had begun to sound possible when he described it aloud.","Before Mira left on Sunday, she put the weekly plan on her father's fridge. Beside it, he placed a note with Mr Ellis's number and the time of the community centre's craft group. He might visit when he could walk more easily. Leo's course application lay ready to send. There were still bills, difficult days, and conversations they had not finished. Mira no longer expected one successful week to solve a family. At the door, her father asked when she would call. 'Wednesday evening,' she said. It was written on the plan. This time, all three of them knew what the promise meant."],"glossary":{"frame":"yürümeye yardımcı yürüteç","stock":"satılacak mal stoku","fault":"arıza","till":"yazar kasa","connection":"elektrik bağlantısı","dealer":"ikinci el eşya alıp satan kişi","crackle":"radyo cızırtısı","ticket":"tamir fişi"},"phrases":[{"en":"in advance","tr":"önceden"},{"en":"taken over","tr":"işi devralmış"},{"en":"at once","tr":"hemen"},{"en":"set aside","tr":"ayrı bir yere ayırmak"},{"en":"a fixed amount","tr":"sabit bir miktar"},{"en":"putting down the phone","tr":"telefonu kapatmak"},{"en":"where they stood","tr":"içinde bulundukları durum"},{"en":"in a single sentence","tr":"tek bir cümleyle"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why has Mira returned to her hometown?","options":["To open a new cafe","To close her father's repair shop","To buy a greenhouse","To start working at a warehouse"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Babasının sakatlığı ve dükkânın mali sorunları nedeniyle tamir dükkânını kapatmak için döner."},{"q":"Why does Ruth need her radio by Friday?","options":["She is selling it to a dealer","She is moving abroad","She wants music at an evening remembering her husband","She has promised it to a child"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Eşini anmak için düzenlediği akşamda onun sevdiği müzik programını radyodan dinletmek ister."},{"q":"What does Leo need in order to attend his course?","options":["A different care schedule for his father","Permission to buy the shop","Mira's drawing board","A job in another country"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Gece işi, dersler ve babasının bakımı aynı anda yürüyemediği için bakımın yeniden planlanması gerekir."},{"q":"Where does the old workbench go?","options":["To Mira's flat","To a rubbish site","To the landlord's garden","To the community centre"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Merkez bir miktar ödeme yapar; Mira ve Leo farkın bir bölümünü karşılayarak tezgâhın orada kullanılmasını sağlar."},{"q":"What changes most in the family's plans by the end?","options":["They expect Leo to do everything","They agree on clear tasks and times together","They decide money never matters","They promise never to disagree again"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Birlikte belirledikleri açık görevler ve ziyaret zamanları, söylenmemiş beklentilerin yerini alır."}],"words":4666,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-man-who-pointed","type":"story","title":"The Man Who Pointed","author":"Lantern Original","level":"B1","genre":"scifi","summaryTr":"Zaman turlarının en önemli kuralı basit: Hiç kimse fotoğrafa girmeyecek. Ama genç rehber Ayla'nın ilk turunda, tarihin en ünlü fotoğraflarından biri tam gözlerinin önünde çekilmek üzeredir.","paragraphs":["Ayla Demir had only one rule for her first tour, and she repeated it four times before the group even reached the Door. Nobody gets in the photograph.","Three hours later, she would be begging a tourist to break it.","It was the golden rule of Yesterday Tours. In the year 2147, anybody with enough money could spend one afternoon in the past. They could look, listen and breathe the old air. They could not touch things, talk to local people, or stand in front of a camera. A photograph from 1923 can still hang in a museum a hundred and twenty years later. If a stranger with the wrong clothes and the wrong face is standing in it, historians start asking questions. And questions, the company said, were how history changed.","Today's destination was Port Alder, 14 June 1923: Airship Day. It was the biggest day in the town's history. Port Alder was also the home town of Ayla's mother's family, the Marshes, so Ayla had asked for this tour herself.","There were four tourists. Mr. and Mrs. Nakamura were seventy years old and very polite, and they held hands all the time. Kofi was sixteen, with a quiet smile; his grandfather had paid for him to come. The fourth was Mr. Peterson, who had paid double for \"the best seat in history\" and expected the whole past to arrange itself around him.","Before they left, Ayla gave everyone a grey wool coat and a hat from 1923. Mr. Peterson held his coat between two fingers, as if it was a dead animal. \"It itches,\" he said. Under it he was wearing his own jacket, which was bright orange and had a shiny silver zip down the front.","\"Please keep the coat on, Mr. Peterson.\"","\"Of course,\" said Mr. Peterson, and he did not.","The Door looked like a plain metal frame in a white room. When Ayla pressed the green button, the air inside it became soft and silver, like water. One by one, the tourists stepped through.","They came out in a narrow alley behind the fish market in Port Alder. It was five to three on a warm afternoon. The air smelled of fish, coal smoke and horses. Somewhere near, a band was playing badly and very happily. Mr. Peterson threw his coat onto a box and walked out of the alley in his orange jacket, and Ayla had to run after him.","The whole town had come to the harbour. Men wore their best hats, women carried umbrellas against the sun, and children sat on their fathers' shoulders. In forty-five minutes, the airship Silver Whale would arrive, the first passenger airship ever to visit Port Alder. At twenty to four, the mayor would make a speech, the band would play louder, and the town's famous photographer, Edith Holloway, would take pictures from the right end of the harbour wall.","That was why Ayla's route stayed on the left side, as far from the camera as possible. She had checked it a hundred times on her tablet, where the photographer was only a small red circle. \"Stay together,\" she told the group. \"Stay on the left. Look, listen, enjoy.\"","At twenty-five past three, she counted her tourists. One, two, three. She counted again. Three.","\"Of course,\" she said.","She found him on the right side of the harbour, the wrong side, holding a hot pie. He was standing next to a red-and-white ticket booth. And when Ayla saw the booth, her stomach turned cold.","She knew that booth. She knew the crooked lamp-post next to it. She knew the camera on its wooden legs, twenty metres away, and the small woman with her head under a black cloth. And in front of the booth stood a little girl in a blue dress, with a white ribbon in her hair and a red apple in her hand.","Ayla had seen this place a hundred times. It hung in the Port Alder museum. It was in every school book in her mother's town. It was the most famous photograph in the history of the town, and its name was The Man Who Pointed.","In the photograph, a man in a pale, shiny jacket stands with one arm up and his mouth open, pointing at the sky. Next to his knee, a small girl with a white ribbon looks up too. Nobody in a hundred and twenty years had found out who the man was. Under the picture, the museum card always said the same thing: Moments after this photograph was taken, a heavy iron hook fell from the airship's rope. It hit the ground exactly where the girl had been standing. Nobody was hurt.","In a black-and-white picture, an orange jacket looks pale. And a silver zip looks like a line of light.","\"Oh, no,\" whispered Ayla. \"Oh, no, no, no.\"","The Man Who Pointed was Mr. Peterson. The worst tourist in the world was the most famous stranger in Port Alder's history.","She looked at her watch. It was 3.38.","\"Mr. Peterson,\" she said, trying to sound calm. \"We need to stay here for a few minutes.\"","\"I'm bored,\" said Mr. Peterson. \"I'm hot. This pie is mostly pastry. Everyone is wearing silly hats, and I have seen enough. I'm going back to the alley.\"","He started to walk away. Ayla's mind was working very fast. If he left now, there would be no man in the photograph. There would be no shout. Nobody would be looking at the sky. The hook would fall on a six-year-old girl, and a hundred and twenty years later, the picture on the museum wall would change, or disappear, or something much worse. She could not take the risk.","\"Mr. Peterson, wait.\" She took a breath. \"In two minutes, something is going to happen that nobody from our time has ever seen. You paid for the best seat in history. This is the seat.\"","He looked at her. He looked at his pie. \"Two minutes,\" he said.","\"Two minutes.\"","At twenty to four exactly, the Silver Whale came round the end of the harbour wall, and the whole town screamed with joy. It was enormous, as long as a street, silver and shining against the blue sky. Four ropes fell from its nose, and men ran forward to catch them. At the end of each rope hung an iron hook as big as a bucket.","Ayla stood behind Mr. Peterson and held his sleeve. The Nakamuras and Kofi had followed her, and now they were watching the airship with their mouths open. She looked at her watch. 3.41.","Above them, something went wrong with the machine that held the ropes. She heard a sharp metal sound, and one rope began to slide. The hook at the end of it started to fall, slowly at first, and then very fast.","Nobody saw it. Everyone was looking at the front of the airship, laughing and waving. Mr. Peterson was looking at his pie. And the little girl in the blue dress, with her apple in her hand and her mouth open in wonder, was standing exactly under the hook.","Ayla could run. She could reach the girl in three seconds. Her whole body wanted to do it. But the photograph said: the man points. The girl stands next to him. The girl moves a moment later. If Ayla ran, the photograph would no longer be true, and she did not know what happened to a world where a true thing became false.","She took Mr. Peterson's right arm and pushed it up into the air. \"Mr. Peterson!\" she shouted in his ear. \"Look up! Now!\"","He looked up. For one second, his face had no expression at all. Then he saw the hook, and he opened his mouth.","\"LOOK OUT!\" he screamed. \"THE HOOK! GET BACK! GET BACK!\"","His arm was pointing straight at the sky. People turned. A woman screamed and pulled the little girl backwards into her arms. The crowd opened like a door. And then the hook hit the ground with a crash that shook the harbour, and broke the stones, and sent up a cloud of dust, exactly where the girl had been standing.","Behind them, a camera clicked.","For a long moment, nobody moved. Then a man's voice shouted, \"Who is that? The man in the orange jacket!\" Hundreds of faces turned. A policeman in a tall hat began to push through the crowd.","\"Run,\" said Ayla.","It was 3.43. The Door would open in the alley at 3.50, and it would stay open for exactly one minute. Mr. Peterson, who had not run since school, ran like a champion. They went through the fish market, over a table of crabs, under a horse, and round a very angry woman with a basket. Mrs. Nakamura lost her hat, and Kofi picked it up without stopping. Behind them, a whistle blew again and again.","At 3.49, they fell into the alley. At 3.50, the air between the walls turned silver and soft. The policeman appeared at the end of the alley, red in the face. \"Stop!\" he shouted. \"In the name of the King!\"","Ayla counted her people. One, two, three, four. Then she jumped, and the last thing she saw of 1923 was a tall hat and a very surprised face.","Back in the white room, the tourists lay on the floor. Mrs. Nakamura laughed until she cried. Kofi lifted both fists in the air. \"Best. Tour. Ever,\" he said.","Mr. Peterson did not say anything for a long time. At last he looked up. \"The little girl,\" he said. \"Is she all right?\"","\"She's fine.\"","\"You can't know that.\"","\"Yes,\" said Ayla. \"I can.\"","Two days later he sent her a message: Take me to see the photograph. So they went to the museum in Port Alder, just the two of them, and stood in front of the glass.","It was all there. The red-and-white booth. The crooked lamp-post. A man in a bright jacket, with his arm in the air and his mouth open. He looked frightened and brave at the same time. And behind his shoulder, almost hidden, was a young woman in a dark hat. She had one hand on his arm, and she was pushing it upwards. Her face was only a pale shadow. Ayla had seen that shadow a hundred times and had never once wondered about it.","\"That's you,\" said Mr. Peterson.","\"Yes.\"","\"But you said nobody gets in the photograph.\"","\"That's the rule,\" said Ayla. \"It's just not always true.\"","She read the museum card again. This time, she read the last line slowly. The girl with the white ribbon was called Lily Marsh. She lived to be ninety-one, and she had four children.","In Ayla's family, people had a saying. When something went well, they said: \"Thank the stranger.\" Nobody knew why. Ayla's mother said it every birthday. Ayla's grandmother had said it every New Year. Ayla had always thought it was just a funny way of speaking.","\"Nobody will ever believe me,\" said Mr. Peterson quietly. \"I'm the Man Who Pointed, and nobody will believe me.\"","\"No,\" said Ayla. \"They won't.\"","She looked at the small girl, at the white ribbon, at the red apple in her hand. Then she whispered, very softly, so that only the photograph could hear:","\"You're welcome.\""],"glossary":{"door":"Kapı (hikâyede: zamanda yolculuk için kullanılan geçit)","hook":"kanca, demir çengel","airship":"zeplin, hava gemisi","zip":"fermuar","tour":"tur, gezi"},"phrases":[{"en":"look out","tr":"dikkat et!, sakın!"},{"en":"get back","tr":"geri çekil"},{"en":"stand in front of","tr":"önünde durmak"},{"en":"walk away","tr":"uzaklaşmak, yürüyüp gitmek"},{"en":"take the risk","tr":"riske girmek"},{"en":"stay together","tr":"birlikte kalmak"},{"en":"went wrong","tr":"ters gitti, bozuldu"},{"en":"run after","tr":"peşinden koşmak"},{"en":"at the same time","tr":"aynı anda"},{"en":"never once","tr":"bir kez bile ... değil"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the most important rule of Yesterday Tours?","options":["Never talk to the tourists","Nobody gets in a photograph","Always wear a hat","Never visit the year 1923"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Şirketin altın kuralı: Hiç kimse fotoğrafa girmeyecek. Çünkü fotoğraflar yüzyıllarca saklanır ve tarihi değiştirebilir."},{"q":"Why is Ayla afraid when she sees the red-and-white booth?","options":["She has lost her tablet","The policeman is near","She recognizes the place from a famous photograph","The airship is late"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Ayla kırmızı-beyaz bilet kabini ve eğri lamba direğini 'İşaret Eden Adam' fotoğrafından tanır."},{"q":"Why does Ayla not run to save the little girl herself?","options":["She is too far away","She does not see the hook","She wants Mr. Peterson to be a hero","She wants the photograph to stay true"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Ayla, fotoğrafı değiştirmekten korkar; fotoğrafta adam işaret ediyor ve küçük kız bir an sonra kenara çekiliyor."},{"q":"What does Ayla do when the hook starts to fall?","options":["She pushes Mr. Peterson's arm up and tells him to look up","She shouts at the mayor","She takes a photograph of the airship","She pulls the girl away"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Ayla Bay Peterson'ın kolunu yukarı kaldırır ve yukarı bakmasını söyler; Peterson da bağırarak herkesi uyarır."},{"q":"Who is the little girl with the white ribbon?","options":["The photographer's daughter","The mayor's niece","An ancestor of Ayla","A tourist from the future"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Kızın adı Lily Marsh'tır ve Ayla'nın annesinin ailesinden, yani Ayla'nın atalarındandır."}],"words":1905,"length":"medium"},{"id":"the-table-by-the-window","type":"story","title":"The Table by the Window","author":"Lantern Original","level":"B1","genre":"romance","summaryTr":"Küçük bir istasyon kafesinde geçici olarak çalışan Ada, kasabanın eski yürüyüş yollarını çizen bir marangozla aynı masayı paylaşır. Birlikte hazırladıkları harita, gelecekleri hakkında söylemekten kaçındıkları şeyleri görünür kılar.","paragraphs":["On her first morning at the station cafe, Ada put a small sign on the table by the window. It said Reserved. She had not reserved it for a customer. The afternoon light reached that table, and she needed somewhere to draw after the breakfast rush. Her aunt had promised that she could use a corner of the cafe while she worked there for six weeks. Ada planned to earn enough money for the first month of a design course in the city. She had already bought a yellow folder for her application.","At half past nine, a man in a blue work jacket came in, ordered tea, and moved the sign to the next table. He spread several sheets of paper beside the window. Ada watched him from behind the counter. She waited until he took out a pencil, then walked over. 'That table is reserved,' she said. He looked at the sign he had moved. 'It always says that on Tuesdays.' This seemed a strange answer. 'Today is Monday,' Ada replied. He checked his watch, although the date was not on it.","The man gathered his papers at once. 'Then I have made two mistakes before breakfast.' His smile was small, and Ada tried not to return it. Her aunt Vera came through the kitchen door with a tray of warm rolls. 'You have met Ben,' she said. 'He made the shelves. And repaired the door. And promised to stop taking payment in tea.' Ben lifted his cup. 'This is a separate matter.' Vera explained that he usually drew walking maps at the window. Ada looked at the papers again. They showed streets, trees, and the river.","Ben offered to use another table, but Ada had already noticed a line running through the old school yard. 'You cannot walk there now,' she said. 'The fence is locked.' Ben frowned. He had walked that route himself in the winter. Ada told him that a building company was using the yard. She had passed it on her way from the bus station. He rubbed out the line carefully. 'That is why I need help,' he said. 'People remember a town differently from the way it is.' Ada moved her yellow folder to one side.","The map was for a small walking festival at the end of August. Ben had offered to replace the old map, which sent visitors towards a bridge that no longer existed. He could measure a door, build a cupboard, or make a table stand straight on an uneven floor. Drawing clear directions was harder. His hills looked like piles of bread, and his river was too wide. Ada laughed when he said this, then apologised. 'No, you are right,' he told her. 'Last week somebody asked whether this was a map of an island.'","Ada showed him a simple way to mark a path. She used a broken line for a rough track and a solid one for a paved route. Then she drew a small bench. Ben leaned forward. 'That is exactly the bench outside the library.' It was not meant to be, but she liked that he had recognised something in it. They agreed to share the window table after two each afternoon. Ada would help make the map clear. In return, Ben would build a flat board for her drawings. Neither of them called this an important agreement.","For the next three days, their work stayed polite and ordinary. Ada served soup, washed cups, and counted her tips. Ben arrived with dust on his sleeves and information written on the backs of receipts. A path near the river had six steps, not four. The public garden closed at five on Sundays. The bakery owner would let walkers fill their water bottles. Ada put these details in a neat list. Ben tested the pencils she left on the table and always chose the shortest one. By Thursday she had sharpened it before he arrived.","On Friday they went out to check the longest route. Ada wore new boots because they looked suitable for serious walking. After twenty minutes she wished she had worn her old trainers. Ben did not comment when she slowed down. At the next gate, he stopped to measure its width with a tape from his pocket. 'For people with walking frames or pushchairs,' he explained. Ada sat on a low wall while he wrote the number. When they started again, he took a shorter path without suggesting that she needed it.","At a field above the river, they found that the promised view was hidden behind a thick line of bushes. Ben read the old leaflet aloud. 'Enjoy an open view of the valley.' Ada stood on a stone and saw only leaves. 'Perhaps the valley has gone out for lunch.' He laughed so suddenly that she nearly lost her balance. He offered his hand, and she took it to step down. His palm was warm and rough. Afterward, they both became very interested in deciding whether the field deserved a star on the map.","They ate sandwiches beside a closed boat house. Ben had brought two apples and a small knife. He cut an apple into pieces and placed them on the paper around Ada's sandwich. She told him about the course in the city. It lasted a year and included work on signs, books, and public spaces. 'I want to make things people can actually use,' she said. He nodded towards her notebook. 'You already do.' She explained that helping with one map was different from earning a living. 'Different in size,' he replied. 'Perhaps not in purpose.'","Ben had grown up in the town, left to work with a furniture company, and returned three years earlier. Ada asked whether he had missed home. He considered the question. 'I missed having enough time to finish things properly.' At the company, he had built the same kitchen twenty times. At home, customers brought him difficult cupboards, broken chairs, and ideas that were almost possible. The work was less certain, but it belonged to him. Ada liked this answer. He did not make staying sound brave or leaving sound foolish. They were simply different choices.","At the cafe that evening, Vera looked at the red marks on Ada's heels and gave her a bowl of warm water. 'Was the walk useful?' she asked. Ada described the narrow gate, the hidden view, and the boat house. Her aunt listened with an expression that became more cheerful after each detail. 'And Ben?' Ada looked down at her feet. 'He knows where all the paths go.' Vera passed her a towel. 'That was not quite what I asked.' Ada dried one foot slowly and changed the subject to the price of tomatoes.","The following Monday, Ada found her drawing board against the window. Ben had made it from a smooth piece of pale wood. There was a narrow edge at the bottom to stop pencils falling off, and a handle cut into the side. On the back, he had written her name in small, careful letters. She ran her finger across the wood. It was much better than the simple board she had expected. When he arrived, she said thank you twice. He looked pleased by the first thank you and embarrassed by the second.","That afternoon, the festival organiser visited. Mrs Hale wanted the map finished in twelve days. A local printer had offered a lower price if all the pages arrived together. She liked Ada's drawings, especially the little station building. Then she pointed at an empty corner. 'Could you add the summer house at Mill Garden?' Ben put down his pencil. The garden had been closed for years. Mrs Hale said its new owner might open it during the festival. A picture would encourage him. Ada thought this sounded like drawing a promise that nobody had made.","Ben agreed. 'We should ask him first.' Mrs Hale waved one hand. 'Of course. But the old map includes it.' After she left, Ada found the summer house on a faded page. It was a pretty wooden building beside the river. Ben had repaired its roof when he was sixteen. His father had taken him there during a school holiday and trusted him with tools he had never used before. 'Is your father still working?' Ada asked. Ben shook his head. 'He died four years ago.' She waited, allowing him to decide whether to say more.","He said his father had known every small building along the river. They had often argued about work. His father preferred careful repairs; Ben had wanted to make everything new. 'Now I hear myself telling customers exactly what he used to say.' Ada smiled. Her mother did the same thing with recipes. There was a quiet moment between them, full of things neither needed to explain. Then Ben opened his notebook. They would visit the owner of Mill Garden on Wednesday. Ada wrote the appointment beneath a drawing of two people sitting on a bench.","The owner, Mr Finch, met them at a tall green gate. He was not unfriendly, but he was busy and did not want visitors near his house. Ben explained that the public path could reach the summer house without passing the windows. Ada showed him a possible route. Mr Finch studied it, then pointed towards the river. 'The bottom steps are broken. I cannot invite people to fall into the water.' They walked down together. Three wooden steps had become soft, and a rail moved when Ben touched it. The problem was clear enough.","Ben said he could price the repairs. Mr Finch answered that he had no spare money for a building he rarely used. Ada began putting the map away. Then she noticed an old sign under a bush. It said River Walk, with an arrow towards the summer house. 'Was this once part of the path?' Mr Finch said it had been, long before he bought the property. Ada did not argue that it should become public again. Instead, she asked whether he would consider opening it for one day if the festival paid for safe steps.","On their way back, Ben said she had asked the right question. 'You made it possible for him to say yes without giving up his garden.' Ada felt pleased, though she tried to sound practical. They still had no money and only nine days. Ben could provide some wood left from another job, but it would not be enough. Ada suggested asking the festival committee to pay the rest. He shook his head before she finished. 'Their budget is already tight.' She stopped walking. 'You do not know the answer until you ask.'","Ben looked towards the station. 'People here already help me.' Ada could hear the change in his voice. He did not want to talk about it. She could have left the subject alone, but she was tired and wanted him to see her point. 'Helping is not a debt you can never pay back.' He said he knew that. His face suggested he did not. They reached the cafe without solving anything. He collected his bag and went straight to his workshop. For the first time, Ada left the shortest pencil unsharpened on the table.","That night she wrote the first page of her course application. It asked for an example of a design problem and how she had solved it. She described the map, then stopped at the sentence about working with another person. Had she actually listened to Ben? He knew the town's money problems better than she did. But knowing a difficulty was not the same as accepting it forever. She crossed out several sentences. In the end, she wrote that good directions had to make sense to people who did not already know the way.","Ben came early the next morning. He stood by the counter while Ada filled the sugar bowls. 'I was short with you yesterday,' he said. She put down the packet. 'I pushed too hard.' He explained that after his father died, neighbours had kept the workshop open by bringing him small jobs. Some had paid more than the work was worth. He was grateful, but he worried that every request reminded them of that time. Ada had not known this. 'We can ask the committee together,' she said. 'For the festival, not for you.'","They made a proper plan with the cost of wood, screws, and Ben's time. Ada insisted that his time belonged on the page. He agreed, although he used a smaller number than she expected. Mrs Hale took the plan to the committee. By the afternoon, they had enough money for materials and half the work. Ben could donate the remaining hours if he wanted. That choice mattered to him. He shook Ada's hand in a serious way when she brought the news. She laughed, and he kept her hand just a little longer than necessary.","On Saturday, Ada helped carry wood through Mill Garden. Mr Finch had marked the route with pieces of white cloth so they would not damage his plants. Ben removed the broken steps while Ada pulled old screws from the sound boards. She had expected to be useless, but he gave her real tasks and showed her how to do them. At noon, Mr Finch appeared with lemonade. He watched the work for a while, then fetched a chair and began cleaning the old sign. By late afternoon, all three of them had paint on their clothes.","When Mr Finch went inside, Ada and Ben sat in the summer house. The river moved slowly below them. Through a gap in the trees, they could see trains entering the station. Ada said that from here the town looked like something she had once imagined. Ben asked what she meant. 'A place where I might belong without having to become smaller.' He did not answer quickly. Then he said, 'I hope it can be that.' His knee touched hers beneath the narrow bench. Neither moved away, though there was plenty of room.","He walked her home after they had cleaned the brushes. Outside Vera's gate, he asked whether she would like to have dinner with him. Ada looked down at her trousers. 'Without paint?' Ben smiled. 'We can try.' They chose the following Thursday because he had a delivery on Wednesday and she worked late on Tuesday. It was a very ordinary conversation about times and places. Yet after he left, Ada stood at the gate for almost a minute. She had not expected an ordinary conversation to change the appearance of the whole street.","On Monday, an email arrived from the design college. Ada had been invited to an interview. The message included a practical task: bring three examples of work and explain one in detail. The interview was in the city on Thursday afternoon. She read the date twice. Travel would take three hours each way, and the last train home arrived after ten. She began writing to Ben, deleted the message, and started again. Before she could send it, Mrs Hale entered with three new corrections to the map. Ada put her phone under the counter.","The corrections took most of the afternoon. A cafe had changed its opening hours. A path was going to close for repairs. Somebody wanted the car park shown larger than the church. Ben was working outside town and could not help. By the time Ada called him, she had made the interview into a much bigger problem in her mind. 'Thursday is difficult,' she said. He asked if she wanted another day. She heard disappointment where there may only have been a question. 'I have to go to the city.' There was a short pause.","'For the course?' he asked. Ada said yes. 'That is good news.' His voice was careful. She explained that she had not been accepted yet and might not be able to afford everything. 'But you want to go,' he said. She did. He suggested dinner on Friday instead. It was the right suggestion, but Ada felt strangely sad after the call. Until that moment, her future and her time with Ben had existed in separate rooms. Now somebody had opened a door between them, and she did not know how the rooms fitted together.","On Thursday, Ada carried her drawing board onto the early train. She had chosen the walking map, a set of food labels, and a book cover she had made for practice. At the college, the other visitors seemed younger and more confident. One had a huge black case with silver corners. Ada held her wooden board against her knees. When the interviewer asked about it, she explained that a friend had made it. Then she began describing the map. She spoke about clear paths, honest information, and the difference between a beautiful view and a useful route.","The interviewer asked what had been hardest. Ada could have said the short deadline or the changing information. Instead, she described Mill Garden and the conversation with Ben about asking for money. 'The drawing was the easy part,' she said. 'I had to understand what different people were worried about.' The interviewer nodded and asked another question. Ada stopped comparing herself with the other visitors. She knew this work. She had stood beside the broken steps, held the old sign, and checked every narrow gate. She could explain why each line belonged on the page.","When she returned, Ben was waiting outside the station with a paper bag. 'Vera said you might have missed dinner.' Inside were two rolls and an apple. Ada wanted to hug him, but she was suddenly shy. They sat on the wall by the bicycle stands. She told him about the interview, including the large black case. He listened without interrupting. When she finished, he said he thought they would offer her a place. 'Do you want them to?' she asked. Ben looked at the tracks. 'I want you to have the choice.'","Their dinner on Friday was at a small restaurant above a shop. The windows were open, and someone in the square below was practising the trumpet badly. Ada ordered too quickly and received a dish with much more pepper than she expected. Ben exchanged plates with her without making a joke. They talked about brothers, favourite films, and the terrible rooms they had rented in other towns. For an hour, the map and the course disappeared. Ben told a story about carrying a wardrobe up the wrong stairs. Ada laughed until the waiter smiled at them.","After dinner, they walked towards the river. Ben stopped beside the closed market. 'I like being with you,' he said. There was no clever speech around it. Ada was grateful for that. She told him she liked being with him too. He asked whether he could kiss her, and she said yes. The kiss was brief and a little awkward because they both smiled halfway through. Then a bicycle bell rang behind them, and they stepped out of the path. Ada took his hand. They walked on slowly, with the river beside them and no immediate need to speak.","The next morning, the college offered Ada a place. She was washing a large soup pot when her phone rang, so Vera held it near her ear. Ada said thank you several times and promised to confirm by Tuesday. Her aunt hugged her, leaving a wet mark on the back of her shirt. For a few minutes, everything was simple. Then Ada opened the list of costs again. The course fee was covered by her savings, but rent and travel would use almost everything she could earn. There was no comfortable space for mistakes.","Ben found her with a calculator at the window table. He congratulated her, then sat opposite without reaching for a pencil. Ada said she was considering waiting another year. 'Because of the money?' he asked. 'Mostly.' He noticed the last word. She looked at the map between them. 'I have only just found something good here.' Ben was quiet. She wanted him to say that staying would be enough. She also knew she might hate him later if he did. At last he said, 'I would miss you. That is true. It is not a plan for your life.'","Ada knew he meant to be fair, but the words hurt. 'You sound as if you have already decided it cannot work.' He shook his head. 'I do not know how it would work.' She asked whether he wanted to find out. Before he could answer, a group of cyclists entered and ordered lunch. Ada stood up too fast, knocking a pencil onto the floor. Ben picked it up. When she returned, he had gone to the workshop, leaving a note that said they needed to check the final map before four. It was painfully sensible.","They checked the map that afternoon with Mrs Hale sitting between them. Ada found two spelling mistakes. Ben corrected the number of steps at Mill Garden. They approved the final page and watched Mrs Hale send it to the printer. Everyone should have been pleased. Instead, Ada felt as if they had finished something she was not ready to lose. Ben said he had work to do. She said she understood. Both statements were true, but they left out the important part. Vera watched them leave through different doors and wisely said nothing.","On Sunday, Ada went to the summer house alone. The new steps were firm, and Mr Finch had planted flowers beside the entrance. She sat on the bench and opened her notebook. Instead of drawing, she made two lists: what she knew and what she feared. She knew she wanted the course. She knew she liked Ben. She feared being forgotten in the city and becoming unhappy if she stayed. The fears were not facts. Writing them separately did not solve everything, but it made the page less frightening. There was room between the two lists.","Ben arrived carrying a small tin of paint. He looked surprised to see her. 'The sign needs one more coat,' he explained. Ada closed her notebook, then opened it again. 'I think I asked you to promise something neither of us can promise.' He sat down at the other end of the bench. 'I think I hid behind being reasonable.' She showed him the lists. He read them carefully. Then he added one sentence under what she knew: The city has trains. Ada laughed despite herself. 'That is not a complete answer.' 'No,' he said. 'But it is a beginning.'","They talked for nearly an hour. Ben could visit on some weekends, but not all. Ada would have busy periods when she needed to work. Neither wanted the other to spend money they did not have. They agreed to try for three months and then talk honestly about how they felt. It was not the kind of promise Ada had seen in films. It was smaller, with dates and train fares inside it. Yet it felt stronger than pretending distance would not matter. Ben moved closer. This time, when their knees touched, Ada leaned against his shoulder.","On Tuesday, she accepted the college place and paid the deposit. After pressing the button, she felt both excited and sick. Ben sent a message with a drawing of a train. It was a terrible drawing, with wheels of different sizes. She saved it anyway. That afternoon, she found a room in a shared flat near the college. The owner agreed to a video call and showed her the kitchen, the desk, and the view of a brick wall. Ada booked a visit before signing anything. She was learning that hope worked better when she checked the details.","The printed maps arrived on Thursday. Ada opened the first box with a bread knife while Ben held the cardboard steady. The colours were clear. The river curved gently across the page, and the station cafe stood in one corner. On the back, Mrs Hale had included their names. Ada had not expected that. Ben turned the sheet over twice. 'It looks like a real thing,' he said. She understood exactly what he meant. All the afternoons of small choices had become something a stranger could fold, carry, and use to find a way through town.","On the morning of the festival, rain hit the cafe windows before breakfast. Mrs Hale arrived in a large waterproof coat and said the riverside route might need to close. Ben went out to check it while Ada helped Vera make extra soup. At ten, he returned with wet hair and mud on his boots. The lower path was flooded in two places. The summer house could still be reached from the upper lane. Ada took a thick blue pen and marked a temporary route on a large copy of the map. Then she made smaller notices for the gates.","They expected people to complain, but most were glad to know where they could walk safely. A father thanked Ada for showing the steps clearly because his little daughter tired easily. Two older women chose the shorter route and returned with wet shoes and cheerful faces. At noon, the rain stopped. Mr Finch opened the summer house and put a pot of tea on the table. Ben called to say that people were using the new steps. Ada looked around the crowded cafe. Their map had changed, but its purpose had survived. It still helped people find their way.","Late in the afternoon, Vera took over the counter so Ada could visit Mill Garden. She followed the temporary signs and found Ben speaking to a boy who wanted to know how bridges stayed up. Mr Finch had brought out another table. Walkers stood under the trees, sharing biscuits and comparing routes. Ada saw one of their maps sticking out of a woman's pocket, soft at the corners from use. Ben came towards her. 'You missed the largest crowd.' She shook her head. She had not missed what she had come to see. The place was open, and people were there.","Her last week passed quickly. She trained a new cafe assistant, packed her clothes, and visited the city room with Vera. The brick wall outside the window was real, but so were the clean kitchen and the friendly woman who would share the flat. Ben helped carry a small desk from Vera's attic. He repaired one loose leg and refused to paint over the old scratches because Ada liked them. They spent her final evening eating soup at the window table. For once, neither had brought work. The short pencil rested in a jar beside the till.","At the station the next morning, Ben carried her drawing board while she pulled a heavy suitcase. The train was already waiting. Ada felt a sudden wish to make the goodbye either very grand or very quick. Instead, she put her arms around him and stayed there for a moment. They had chosen the date of his first visit. They had checked the price of the ticket. They would still miss each other. Ben handed her the board. 'Call when you arrive,' he said. She kissed him, stepped onto the train, and found a seat by the window.","Three weeks later, Ben visited the city. Ada met him outside the station and gave him a folded sheet of paper. It was a small map showing the way to her flat, a cheap place for dinner, and a park where they could sit if the weather stayed dry. Near the station, she had drawn a slightly uneven train. Ben recognised his own picture and laughed. 'You should have used a better designer.' Ada took his bag with one hand and his hand with the other. 'I liked this one,' she said. Then they followed the first line together."],"glossary":{"reserved":"ayrılmış, rezerve edilmiş","rough":"engebeli, düzgün olmayan","paved":"zemini döşenmiş","rail":"korkuluk","deposit":"ön ödeme, kapora","folded":"katlanmış","till":"yazar kasa","frames":"yürüteçler"},"phrases":[{"en":"in return","tr":"karşılığında"},{"en":"make sense","tr":"anlamlı olmak"},{"en":"put down","tr":"elindekini bırakmak"},{"en":"find out","tr":"öğrenmek"},{"en":"took over","tr":"işi devraldı"},{"en":"at once","tr":"hemen"},{"en":"giving up","tr":"vazgeçmek"},{"en":"out of the path","tr":"yolun dışına, geçişi engellemeyecek yere"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Ada working at her aunt's cafe?","options":["To buy the cafe","To earn money before a design course","To learn to cook professionally","To replace the festival organiser"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Ada tasarım kursunun ilk ayındaki giderleri karşılamak için para biriktirir."},{"q":"Why does Mr Finch first refuse to open the summer house?","options":["The wooden steps are unsafe","He dislikes the map's colours","The garden has no entrance","He is selling the property"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Alt basamaklar çürüktür ve korkuluk gevşektir; ziyaretçilerin düşmesinden endişelenir."},{"q":"What makes asking the committee for money difficult for Ben?","options":["He has already spent its money","He wants to cancel the festival","He feels neighbours have already helped him a lot","He does not know any committee members"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Babasının ölümünden sonra komşuları ona iş vererek destek olmuştur; yeniden yardım istemek onu rahatsız eder."},{"q":"What do Ada and Ben decide about her move?","options":["Ben will close his workshop immediately","Ada will refuse the course","They will stop seeing each other","They will try the relationship and talk again after three months"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Uzak mesafeli ilişkiyi üç ay denemeye ve ardından dürüstçe değerlendirmeye karar verirler."},{"q":"What does Ada give Ben when he visits the city?","options":["A new set of tools","A map with places they can visit together","A ticket back to the town","The key to the summer house"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Ada dairesini, uygun fiyatlı bir yemek yerini ve parkı gösteren bir harita hazırlar."}],"words":4677,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-thirteenth-figure","type":"story","title":"The Thirteenth Figure","author":"Lantern Original","level":"B1","genre":"horror","summaryTr":"Kerem, kasabadaki bir balmumu müzesinde gece bekçisi olarak işe başlar. Tek kuralı vardır: her turda figürleri yüksek sesle saymak. İkinci gece sayı bir fazla çıkar.","paragraphs":["On Kerem's first night at the Marlowe Wax Museum, there were twelve figures. On his second night, there were thirteen.","He had taken the job because he needed money, and because the advertisement had made him smile. NIGHT GUARD WANTED. QUIET WORK. MUST BE GOOD AT COUNTING. The owner, Mrs. Quill, was a small woman with grey hair and very bright eyes. She gave him a torch, a ring of keys and a sheet of rules.","\"Three rounds every night,\" she said. \"At ten, at one and at four. You walk through every room and you count the figures. Out loud, Mr. Aydın. Always out loud.\"","\"Why out loud?\" Kerem asked.","\"Because the building likes to hear a living voice.\"","Kerem laughed. Mrs. Quill did not.","\"And how many figures are there?\" he asked.","\"Twelve. There have always been twelve.\" She looked past him, at the dark door of the first room. \"The last guard left very suddenly. I hope you will be more sensible.\"","The museum stood at the end of Harbour Road, in a seaside town that was empty in winter. Inside, twelve wax figures waited in six cold rooms: a pirate captain, a queen in a green dress, a boxer, a singer in a silver dress, two little girls with a ball, and others. In the light of the torch their glass eyes shone, and their faces looked almost alive.","On the first night, Kerem felt silly. He walked from room to room and counted in a loud, nervous voice. \"One. Two. Three.\" His voice sounded small in the big dark halls. At ten o'clock, he counted twelve. At one, twelve. At four, twelve. Nothing happened. He went home at seven and slept like a stone.","On the second night, everything was the same until four o'clock. The last room on his round was called Victorian Street. It was a small room with a lamp-post and a wooden bench. Kerem shone his torch on the bench, and a man was sitting there.","He wore a grey jacket. His hat lay on his knees. His head was bent, as if he was very tired or very sad.","\"Thirteen,\" Kerem said, before he could stop himself.","The man did not move. Kerem's heart was beating hard. He made himself walk closer. The man's hands were pale and perfect, and his shoes were clean. It's only a wax figure, he told himself. Someone has put it here as a joke. But Mrs. Quill had said twelve. And there was something strange about the grey jacket. It had a small silver badge on the chest. Kerem looked down at his own jacket. It had the same badge.","One of the rules said: Never touch the figures. So he took out his phone and took a photograph instead. Then he looked at the screen. It showed a wooden bench, a lamp-post, and nothing else. The bench was empty.","He looked up. The man was sitting there, head bent, hat on his knees. He looked down at the phone. Empty bench. Up. Man. Down. Nothing.","Kerem finished the round very fast. \"Ten, eleven, twelve!\" he shouted at the empty halls, and he did not say thirteen again.","At seven, Mrs. Quill arrived with a bag of warm bread. Kerem tried to sound calm. \"There's a man on the bench in Victorian Street,\" he said. \"A thirteenth figure.\"","Mrs. Quill stopped with her hand on the kettle. For a moment, her bright eyes were not friendly at all. \"Did you count him?\" she asked.","\"I said the number. Just once.\"","\"That was not clever.\" Then she smiled again, as if nothing had happened. \"There are twelve figures, Mr. Aydın. Tired people see strange things. Go home and sleep.\"","But Kerem did not go home. He went to the staff room and opened the locker with the name DUNN on it. Inside were a brown coat, a pair of gloves, a packet of biscuits that had gone green, and a phone. The battery was dead, so he plugged the phone into the wall. The screen lit up. It showed a photograph of a thin man with a brown moustache, smiling beside an old woman. Under the picture it said: 41 missed calls from Mum. The last one was three weeks old.","There was also a piece of paper in the locker, folded very small. The writing was shaky, as if the writer had been in a hurry.","COUNT OUT LOUD. EVERY ROUND. IF YOU HEAR IT COUNT AFTER YOU, DO NOT STOP AT TWELVE. — T. Dunn","Kerem read it three times. He should leave this job, he knew that. But the rent was due on Friday, and there were forty-one calls on that phone that nobody had answered.","On the third night, the man was no longer on the bench. Kerem found him in the main hall, standing under the big clock. This time his head was lifted, and his eyes were closed. Kerem recognized the thin face and the brown moustache. He pointed his torch at the floor and walked past very quickly, counting loudly. And behind him, in the empty dark, a voice began to count too.","It was a thin whisper, like paper moving. It was always half a second late.","\"One,\" said Kerem.","\"...one,\" whispered the dark.","\"Two.\"","\"...two.\"","He did not turn around. He counted all twelve figures with the whisper following him from room to room. When he said \"Twelve,\" the whisper said \"...twelve,\" and then it waited. Kerem could hear it waiting. He ran back to the guard room, locked the door and sat with his back against it until the windows turned grey.","On the fourth night, he almost stayed at home. But he thought about the phone and the forty-one calls. If he stayed at home, who would ever help Mr. Dunn? At ten o'clock he was at the door with his torch.","At four o'clock exactly, all the lights in the building went out.","The dark was complete. The torch made a small yellow circle on the floor, and the circle was shaking. \"One,\" Kerem said. His voice was thin as a thread.","\"...one,\" said the whisper, very close behind him.","He kept walking. \"Two.\"","\"...two.\"","The steps behind him were slow and heavy, like the steps of a man who has forgotten how to walk. The air grew colder. Kerem's fingers felt stiff on the torch, and white, like candles. He counted the pirate captain, whose glass eyes seemed to follow him. He counted the queen, the boxer, the singer in her silver dress. At last he reached the final room and said, \"Twelve.\"","\"...twelve.\"","Then there was silence.","Kerem stood very still. The silence felt like a question. Behind him, the whisper did not go on. It waited, as it had waited every night, for someone to say the next number. And he thought about the shaky note in his pocket. DO NOT STOP AT TWELVE. Every guard before him had counted twelve, said \"good,\" and gone home. Nobody had ever counted the one who followed them.","His mouth was dry. Slowly, he turned round and lifted the torch. The man in the grey jacket stood less than a metre away. His eyes were open now. They were brown, and they were wet. They did not look like glass at all.","\"Thirteen,\" Kerem said. His voice shook, but he went on. \"Thirteen. Mr. Tom Dunn, night guard of the Marlowe Wax Museum.\"","For a long moment, nothing happened. Then the man's shoulders dropped, as if someone had taken a heavy bag from them. He breathed out, a long, long breath that smelled of old candles. \"Thank you,\" he whispered, and this time his voice was not half a second late. It was only a voice. Then the torch flickered, and there was nobody in front of Kerem. A grey hat lay on the floor.","All the lights came back on at once. In every room, the clocks began to tick.","At six, Mrs. Quill opened the front door. She walked through the museum without a word, her lips moving as she counted silently. In Victorian Street she stopped. The bench was empty. She stood looking at it for a long time.","\"You counted him,\" she said at last. She did not sound angry. She sounded like a woman who had lost something valuable. \"Do you know how much a night guard costs, Mr. Aydın? Salary, holidays, sick days. And for three weeks I had one who never asked for anything.\"","Kerem put the keys and the torch on the table. Then he took the phone out of his pocket. \"I think Mr. Dunn's mother wants to hear from him,\" he said. \"I'll find her.\"","Mrs. Quill looked at the phone, and for a moment Kerem thought she would say no. Instead, she pushed an envelope across the table. \"Your wages,\" she said. \"Count them, if you like.\"","Kerem did not count them. He did not want to count anything ever again.","There were twelve old street lamps along Harbour Road. He knew, because he had counted them on his first day, the way he now counted everything. He counted them again as he walked home, out loud, and his voice sounded strong in the empty morning. Eleven. Twelve.","At the end of the road, there was a thirteenth lamp.","It had not been there before. Under its yellow light stood a thin man with a brown moustache. He did not look like wax now. He looked like a man who was going home at last. He lifted one hand, slowly, like someone waving across a wide river.","Kerem lifted his hand too.","Then the lamp went out, and so did the man. Kerem looked at the road for a long time. There were only twelve lamps. He put his hand in his pocket, felt the phone, and began to walk faster. Somebody's mother had been waiting for three weeks."],"glossary":{"torch":"el feneri (İngiliz İngilizcesi)","figure":"(balmumu) figür, heykel","round":"devriye turu, kontrol turu","wax":"balmumu"},"phrases":[{"en":"out loud","tr":"yüksek sesle, sesli olarak"},{"en":"slept like a stone","tr":"taş gibi uyudu, deliksiz uyudu"},{"en":"as if","tr":"sanki, güya"},{"en":"made himself walk closer","tr":"kendini yaklaşmaya zorladı (make oneself do: kendini zorlamak)"},{"en":"at last","tr":"sonunda, nihayet"},{"en":"for a long time","tr":"uzun süre"},{"en":"went on","tr":"devam etti"},{"en":"stood very still","tr":"kıpırdamadan durdu"},{"en":"shone his torch","tr":"el fenerini tuttu, ışık tuttu"},{"en":"at once","tr":"bir anda, aynı anda"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is Mrs. Quill's most important rule for the night guard?","options":["Never talk to visitors","Touch every figure once","Count the figures out loud on every round","Stay in the guard room all night"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Mrs. Quill, her turda figürlerin yüksek sesle sayılmasını ister: 'Out loud, Mr. Aydın. Always out loud.'"},{"q":"What does Kerem see when he takes a photograph of the bench?","options":["A man in a grey jacket","An empty bench","Mrs. Quill","A pirate captain"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Gözle bankta bir adam görür ama telefondaki fotoğrafta bank bomboştur."},{"q":"Why does Kerem keep going to work after he finds Mr. Dunn's phone?","options":["He wants to see the ghost again","Mrs. Quill offers him more money","He is not afraid of ghosts","He thinks about the unanswered calls from Dunn's mother"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Kerem, telefonda cevapsız kalan 41 aramayı düşünür ve işe gitmeye devam eder."},{"q":"What does Kerem do that helps the thirteenth figure?","options":["He counts him and says his name","He breaks the clock","He runs out of the building","He turns off the lights"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Kerem 'Thirteen. Mr. Tom Dunn' diyerek onu sayar ve ismini söyler; Dunn rahatlar."},{"q":"What is strange about the last street lamp on Harbour Road?","options":["It is much brighter than the others","It was not there before","It is broken","It is the first lamp Kerem sees"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Yolda her zaman on iki lamba vardır; on üçüncü lamba daha önce orada değildi."}],"words":1669,"length":"medium"},{"id":"where-the-river-sleeps","type":"story","title":"Where the River Sleeps","author":"Lantern Original","level":"B1","genre":"adventure","summaryTr":"İstanbul'da bir kemancı çırağı, tamire getirilen eski ve çirkin bir kemanın içinde 1943 tarihli gizli bir harita ve bir anahtar bulur. Zarif ama acımasız bir adamdan kaçarken bu harita onu Kapadokya'nın altındaki karanlık bir nehre, çanlara ve seksen yıllık bir sırra götürür.","paragraphs":["## Chapter 1: The Violin Nobody Wanted","The man in the grey gloves offered five thousand euros for a broken violin, and Elif Arslan nearly dropped her cup of tea.","It was a wet Thursday afternoon in Kadıköy, Istanbul. Rain ran down the windows of Usta Nuri's workshop, and inside it was warm and smelled of wood and glue. Violins hung from the ceiling like strange fruit. Elif had worked here for three years, since she was twenty. She could repair a cracked back, change a bridge and match an old colour so well that even Usta Nuri could not find the repair.","The violin on the workbench was the ugliest in the shop. Its back was cracked, three strings were missing, and a long scratch ran across the top of the neck. Usta Nuri had bought it for eighty euros at a house sale in Vienna, because, as he said, he felt sorry for it.","\"My name is Gregor Voss,\" said the man, and he gave a small bow. He was tall and thin, with a soft German accent, and he never took off his gloves. \"I buy and sell old instruments. Five thousand euros. It is a generous offer, I think.\"","\"It is a crazy offer,\" said Usta Nuri. He was seventy years old, and he was not afraid of anybody. \"Why do you want it?\"","\"I like broken things.\" Voss smiled with his mouth, but not with his eyes. \"Please, name your price.\"","\"My price is six hundred euros, after my apprentice has repaired it. Come back in a week.\"","\"Of course,\" said Voss. \"I will come back.\" He put a white card on the bench and walked out into the rain.","Usta Nuri watched him go. \"Nobody pays five thousand euros for that violin,\" he said. \"Either that man is mad, or this violin is not what it seems.\" Then he took his umbrella and said that he was going to the tea house to meet his old friends, as he did every Thursday. \"Don't work too late,\" he told Elif. Of course, she worked late.","By eight o'clock the street was quiet. Elif opened the cracked back of the violin with a thin flat knife, slowly, the way you open an old box. Dust fell out. Then she stopped. Something was glued to the inside of the top plate: a flat packet, wrapped in dark cloth and tied with thread.","Her hands were shaking when she cut the thread. Inside the cloth there were two things. The first was a small brass key, and the top of the key was shaped like a bell. The second was a sheet of thin paper, folded eight times.","She opened it on the bench, under the lamp. It was a map, drawn in black ink by a careful hand. A wavy line was a river. Above it there was a round sun with rays, and under the sun, seven little doors in a row. Next to the river was a drawing of a bell. At the bottom were two lines of German words that she could not read, and a date: October 1943. Above the date were three more words: Für Marta.","\"Who are you, Marta?\" Elif whispered.","Then the bell above the shop door rang.","\"Usta? You're early!\" She turned round. Gregor Voss was standing in the doorway with rain on his shoulders. He closed the door behind him, and she heard the key turn in the lock.","\"Good evening, Miss Arslan,\" he said. \"I'm afraid I was not honest this afternoon. I don't like broken things. I have been looking for that violin for eleven years, since I found my grandfather's old papers. I missed it at a house sale in Vienna by one day.\"","He walked slowly towards the workbench. \"Emil Hartmann, Vienna, 1943. Does the name mean anything to you?\"","\"No.\"","\"Good. Then you know nothing, and that is lucky for you. Now give me what was inside the violin.\"","Elif held the map behind her back. The key was in her other hand, hidden in her fist. \"I don't know what you mean,\" she said.","Voss smiled and took one more step. \"I think you do.\"","## Chapter 2: The Last Ferry","Elif did not think. Her free hand found the cord of the workshop lamp and pulled, and the lamp crashed to the floor. The room went black.","\"Dragan!\" Voss shouted. \"The back door!\"","She jumped onto the workbench, pushed open the little window above the glue shelf and squeezed through it like a cat. She landed in a puddle in the alley, and cold rain hit her face. She pushed the map into her jacket and the key into her jeans pocket, and she ran.","Behind her a door opened, and heavy steps followed. She did not look back. She knew who it was: the big man in the black coat who had been standing in the courtyard. He was one of the biggest men she had ever seen.","The market in Kadıköy was almost closed, but the fish sellers were still washing their stalls. Elif ran between boxes of ice and fish. \"Sorry! Excuse me! Sorry!\" A man shouted at her. She ran through a baker's shop, in at the front door and out at the back, past a surprised cat and a very surprised baker. The steps behind her were slower now, but they were not far away.","Then she heard a crash and a loud shout in a language she did not know. She looked back for one second. Dragan was lying on the wet stones with an enormous fish on his chest, and an angry fish seller was hitting him with a wet cloth.","\"Sorry!\" Elif shouted again, and this time she really meant it.","She reached the pier just as the last ferry to Karaköy was leaving. The ropes were already off. Between the pier and the boat there was a gap of black water, one metre wide, then one and a half. Elif jumped. For a moment there was nothing under her feet. Then her hands and knees hit the deck. A sailor shouted at her, and an old man offered her his hand, laughing.","From the deck she could see them on the pier, small under the lights: the big man holding his side, and next to him, tall and still, Gregor Voss. He did not shout. He looked at her across the water, lifted one grey glove as if he was saying goodbye, and took out his phone.","Elif sat down among the tired people going home and shook. In Karaköy she went into a small all-night café near the pier and sat in a corner. The only other customer was a young man in a red cap, reading a newspaper. When her fingers worked again, she called Usta Nuri. She told him everything in one breath. \"Stay where you are,\" he said. \"I'll call the police.\" Ten minutes later he phoned back. The shop door was open and the lamp was broken, but nothing was missing. Voss had wanted only one thing.","\"Send me a photograph of the map,\" said Usta Nuri.","She did. For a long time the line was silent.","\"Elif,\" he said at last, \"I learned German when I studied violin making in Germany, and I can read this. It says: Where the river sleeps, the bells sing. And the second line says: Count seven doors from the sun.\"","\"And Marta? Who is Marta?\"","\"I knew a Marta Hartmann once. She was my first violin teacher, when I was a small boy. She came to Istanbul from Vienna at the end of 1943, when she was fourteen. Her father stayed behind, and he died there the next year. She told us that he had hidden something before he died, but she never found out where. She also lost her violin on the journey. At the station in Vienna, a soldier took it out of her hands.\" He stopped. \"She is ninety-four now. She lives in Moda. Elif, that ugly violin...\"","\"Where are the seven doors, Usta?\"","\"A river, a sun, doors in the rock.\" He thought. \"I know only one place in Turkey with thousands of doors in the rock. Cappadocia.\"","Elif thought of Joaquín, a Chilean cave guide who lived in Göreme. They had met at a music festival two years before. She called him, and he answered happily at ten o'clock at night. She sent him the photograph. This time it was Joaquín who was silent. \"Elif,\" he said, \"I think I know this place. Come tomorrow.\"","\"Go to the police,\" said Usta Nuri, when she told him her plan. \"They will come to the shop, and I'll tell them everything.\"","\"Voss knows I have the map, Usta. If I stay here, he will find me. If I go, I can find the place before he does.\"","\"You are as stubborn as an old donkey,\" said Usta Nuri, and she could hear that he was proud. \"Call me every hour.\"","At eleven o'clock, Elif was in the last row of the night bus to Nevşehir, with her jacket zipped up over the map. Voss is in Istanbul, she told herself. He can't know where I'm going. The bus began to move. Rain ran down the windows. Then, at the very last second, the doors opened again, and a big man in a black coat climbed in.","It was Dragan. He had a wet shirt, a red mark on his face and a smell of fish. He walked slowly down the aisle and sat down two rows in front of her. Then he turned his head and smiled. It was not a friendly smile.","Voss, it seemed, knew exactly where she was going. And in front of them lay seven hours of road.","## Chapter 3: Seven Doors from the Sun","Elif made a plan: stay awake, sit near the driver and don't look at Dragan. It was a bad plan, but she had no other. She moved to the front of the bus, and in the dark window she could see Dragan watching her, like a cat watching a bird.","At three in the morning the bus stopped at a large rest area in the middle of nowhere. There were bright lights, a café, big trucks, and tired passengers going in for tea. \"Ten minutes,\" said the driver. Dragan stood up too. Elif went into the café, and he followed, a few steps behind her, like a shadow in a black coat.","At the biggest table, twelve women in colourful scarves were drinking tea and singing. A banner on their minibus said: Hacer Teyze's Pensioners' Club, Trip to Cappadocia. The oldest woman, small and round, with laughing eyes, saw Elif's frightened face. Then she saw Dragan.","\"Teyze,\" said Elif quietly, \"a man is following me. Please.\"","Hacer Teyze did not ask a single question. She looked at Dragan for a long time. \"He looks like a wardrobe in a coat,\" she said. \"Girls! We have a guest!\"","In four seconds, Elif had a flowery scarf on her head, a cardigan on her shoulders and a glass of tea in her hand. She was sitting in the middle of twelve women who were all talking at once.","Dragan stopped at the table. \"Excuse me,\" he said. \"I am looking for a girl—\"","\"A girl?\" Hacer Teyze stood up. She was shorter than his chest. \"Which girl? Are you looking at our granddaughters? At this hour? Have you no shame?\"","\"No, I only—\"","A handbag hit his arm. Another hit his back. \"Go and sit down!\" \"Go back to your mother!\" The women's voices got louder and louder, and the whole café watched a very large man run across the room, chased by twelve women with handbags.","From the café window, Elif watched Dragan stand on the steps of the bus. He looked left and right. The driver was waving, and he had a schedule to keep. In the end, Dragan got on. The doors closed, and the bus drove away without them.","\"Now you travel with us, my child,\" said Hacer Teyze. \"Eat something.\"","Elif slept with her head on Hacer Teyze's shoulder and a bag of dried apricots in her hand. When she woke up, the sky was pink, and it was full of hot-air balloons, hundreds of them, red and yellow and blue, rising slowly over a country of strange rock towers and caves. \"Welcome to Cappadocia,\" said Hacer Teyze.","Joaquín was waiting outside his guesthouse in Göreme, a tall man with curly hair, a muddy jacket and two big backpacks. \"You look terrible,\" he said.","\"Thank you. It was a long night.\"","He looked at the map for a long time. \"Where did you find this?\"","\"Inside a violin.\"","\"Of course. Where else?\" He laughed, then stopped. \"Elif, it's the Sun House. In Pigeon Valley, forty minutes from here. It's a cliff with a round window, high in the rock. The tourists say it was a church. Under the window there are lots of doors, but most of them are old pigeon houses. A thousand years ago, the farmers made holes in the rock for the birds, because pigeon droppings are good for the fields.\"","\"Pigeon droppings?\"","\"Life is not always romantic, Elif.\"","They walked into the valley in the early morning, between pink and white rocks, with ropes and helmets and lamps on their backs. On the way, Elif told him everything. Joaquín listened and said nothing, but now and then he looked at the sky. In the south, over the mountains, black clouds were piling up.","\"Where the river sleeps,\" he said. \"Many valleys here have dry riverbeds. They sleep for months. Then it rains in the mountains, forty kilometres away, and the river wakes up, without any warning.\" He shook his head. \"It's all right. It's raining there, not here. If it was raining here, I would never take you underground.\"","\"Not yet,\" said Elif.","The Sun House was a cliff, a hundred metres wide, with a perfect round window in the middle, like an eye. Under it there was a row of doors, big and small, black against the pink stone.","\"Count seven doors from the sun,\" Elif read.","\"Left or right?\"","She looked at the map. \"On the map they go to the right.\"","They counted aloud. One. Two. Three. The fourth door was huge. The fifth had a broken wall. The sixth was only a hole. The seventh was very small, at the foot of the rock, half hidden by a thorn bush. Joaquín pushed through the thorns and went in.","Inside there was a small room with hundreds of little holes in the walls. On the floor, in the dust, was a round hole, just wide enough for a man.","\"Pigeon houses don't have holes in the floor,\" said Joaquín slowly. He shone his lamp down. It was very deep.","He tied the rope to a rock and went down first. Elif followed, her heart beating like a drum. The shaft was narrow, and the walls were cold. After six or seven metres, her lamp found old iron steps in the rock. She was thinking that someone had built them a very long time ago, for a reason. Then she heard stones falling above her, and a voice.","\"Thank you, Miss Arslan.\"","She looked up. In the small circle of light there was a face, and a grey glove holding the rope.","\"You talk very loudly on the phone, Miss Arslan,\" said Gregor Voss. \"The young man in the red cap works for me. I called him from the pier, and he was waiting for your ferry in Karaköy. In that café he heard every word: Cappadocia, Göreme, a cave guide called Joaquín. Then he followed you to the bus station and called Dragan. A plane is faster than a bus, and this morning I only had to wait outside your friend's guesthouse. You have been so helpful, you and your friend. I don't like caves, so I will wait up here until you have opened the door. Then I will come down.\"","He untied the rope from the rock and let it go. It fell past her into the dark.","\"Please be careful,\" Voss said, almost kindly. The circle of light became a half circle. There was a scraping sound as a big flat stone slid across the hole. Then there was only a thin line of light, and then nothing.","The darkness was complete. For a moment, Elif could hear only her own breathing.","\"Elif,\" said Joaquín's voice from below, very calm. \"Don't panic.\"","\"I'm not panicking.\"","\"Good. Because I am.\"","## Chapter 4: The Bronze Door","Joaquín climbed back up the iron steps and pushed the stone with his back until his face was red. It did not move. \"All right,\" he said at last. \"So we go forward. Cave rule number one: never panic. Rule number two: if you panic, do it quietly.\"","Elif laughed a little. It helped.","At the bottom of the shaft, a low passage led into the rock. It was so narrow that they had to crawl on their elbows, with their lamps touching the stone. Elif hated small spaces, and she kept her eyes on Joaquín's boots. He talked to her all the time about nothing: his mother's cooking, a cow that fell into a cave once. Then she felt wind on her face. \"Wind,\" said Joaquín happily. \"Wind means there is somewhere for it to come from.\"","After fifty metres, the passage opened suddenly into a tall, wide tunnel. The lamps could not reach the roof. The floor was smooth like the inside of a bowl.","\"This is a riverbed,\" said Joaquín, and he lifted his lamp. High above their heads, a dark line ran along the wall. \"That's the water mark. When the river is awake, it fills the tunnel up to there.\"","\"But it's dry now.\"","\"Now. The river is sleeping.\"","Elif looked at the map in her hand and felt cold.","They walked along the riverbed for ten minutes. Then she heard a tiny sound. Ting. She stopped. Ahead of them, hanging from the roof on thin chains, was a line of small bronze bells, fifty or a hundred of them, right across the tunnel. They shone like gold in the lamplight. One was still swinging; Joaquín's helmet had touched it. Ting, it sang again, and the sound ran away down the tunnel.","\"Where the river sleeps, the bells sing,\" whispered Elif. \"They're not decoration. They're an alarm. When the water comes, it pushes the air in front of it, and the air moves the bells.\"","\"Who builds an alarm for a river?\"","\"Someone who hid something very important here, and wanted people to leave in time.\"","They walked under the bells very slowly, with their arms close to their bodies, and neither of them breathed.","Behind the bells, in the rock wall, there was a round bronze door, green with age. In the middle was a keyhole in the shape of a little bell. Elif's hand was shaking. The brass key slid in, turned, and clicked. They pushed together. With a sound like a long sigh, the door opened.","The room beyond was round, dry and cold, and it smelled of old wood. Joaquín's lamp moved slowly over the walls, and for a while neither of them spoke. Shelves cut into the rock held cases: black, brown and green, small and long, dozens and dozens of them. Each had a paper label in a neat hand. Prague Quartet, 1938. Mr. A. Weiss, cello. Miss Rosa Klein, viola.","Elif opened the nearest case with trembling fingers. A violin lay on dark red velvet. The wood shone like honey, as perfect as on the day it was made. She knew from the colour of the varnish what it was. \"This one violin,\" she whispered, \"is worth more than Usta Nuri's whole street.\"","On a stone table in the middle of the room lay a leather notebook and a folded letter. The letter was in German, but under every line someone had written the English words too, as if Emil Hartmann wanted anybody to be able to read it. Joaquín read it aloud, in his careful voice.","\"My dear Marta. If you are reading this, you are grown up and the war is over. These instruments belong to musicians who had to run away from their homes. I could not save the people, but I could save their music. My old student Mehmet, a brave young man from Göreme, has carried them here for me. Turkey is not in the war, and nobody will look for them in an old cave under his valley. Mehmet drew the way for me, and I made the door, the key and the bells in my workshop. I have hidden the map and the key inside your violin, because no soldier looks inside a child's violin. Please find their families and give them back. Do not be afraid of the river. Listen to the bells. Your loving father, Emil.\"","\"But a soldier took her violin at the station,\" said Elif softly. \"So the map stayed in Vienna for eighty years, and nobody opened it.\"","The notebook was a list of names and addresses. Elif turned the pages slowly. There were forty names. Forty families had been waiting for eighty years. Joaquín folded the letter and slid it back inside the notebook.","Then, behind them, there was a tiny sound. Ting.","They turned. Far away down the tunnel, one bell was moving by itself. Joaquín's face changed. \"That isn't us,\" he said.","A second bell began to move. Then four. And from the other end of the tunnel, where the shaft was, two lights came towards them, and a calm voice said: \"Thank you both. That is far enough.\"","Gregor Voss walked out of the dark with Dragan behind him. He was smiling. And above their heads, one by one, all the bells began to sing.","## Chapter 5: The River Wakes","Voss stepped into the bronze room and looked around like a man in a dream. \"Eleven years,\" he whispered. \"Dragan, the cases. All of them.\"","\"Mr. Voss,\" said Joaquín, \"listen to the bells.\"","\"Pretty, aren't they?\"","\"It rained in the mountains this morning. The river is waking up. We have a few minutes, no more.\"","\"A lovely story,\" said Voss.","But Dragan was not moving. He was looking at the bells. \"Boss,\" he said. \"They are ringing very loudly.\"","\"It is the wind.\"","Then they all heard it: far away, a low sound, like a train in a tunnel. It grew louder every second. Dragan's face went grey. Voss's smile died.","Elif's eyes ran round the room. Emil Hartmann had built an alarm for the river. He would not have built it without a way out. Behind the last shelf, in the shadow, she saw iron steps in the rock, going up into a narrow black hole.","\"There!\" she shouted. She grabbed the leather notebook from the table, pushed it inside her jacket, and snatched up the case with the honey-coloured violin. \"That's the way out! Joaquín, go!\"","\"Take them!\" Voss screamed at Dragan. \"Take as many as you can!\" He picked up two cases himself, and Dragan took three. But the iron steps were narrow, and nobody can climb a ladder with two cases in their arms.","The sound became a wall. The water hit the bronze door like a hand and threw it back against the rock. Brown, foaming water poured into the room, knee deep in a few seconds. The lower shelves fell, and cases floated like boats.","Elif climbed with the case under one arm. The steps were wet and cold, and the water was rising behind her like an animal. Joaquín was just below her, pushing her feet up. Thirty steps, forty. At the top, a round wooden cover blocked the way. Joaquín pushed with his shoulder. Nothing.","\"Again!\"","They pushed together, and with a groan the cover moved. Light! Rain! Grass! Elif crawled out onto the wet ground inside the walls of a ruined stone chapel, and Joaquín fell out after her.","Below them, Dragan was climbing hard. He had dropped the cases. Voss was behind him, still holding his two, with the water around his chest. \"Wait!\" he shouted. \"Wait for me!\" Dragan reached the top, and his foot slipped. Joaquín caught his collar, Elif caught Joaquín's belt, and together they pulled. Dragan landed on the grass like a big wet fish.","\"The cases!\" Voss screamed from the hole. \"Somebody take the cases!\"","Elif looked at Joaquín. Joaquín looked at the water. \"Drop them!\" Elif shouted. \"Give me your hand!\"","For a second, Voss did not move. Then he opened his arms. The two cases fell into the brown water and floated away into the dark, and he reached up with his bare hand. They pulled him out too. He lay on the grass in the rain, his expensive coat brown with mud, and his grey gloves were gone.","\"Good morning, Mr. Voss,\" said a voice.","Six police officers stood in the rain around the ruined chapel. Beside them was Usta Nuri with an umbrella, and next to him, in a wheelchair under a blanket, sat a very small old woman with white hair. Nuri had taken the first plane from Istanbul that morning, and he had not come alone. The old lady had said that she had waited eighty years and could not wait another day. When Elif stopped answering her phone, Nuri had called Joaquín's guesthouse. Joaquín always left a note there about where he was going (cave rule number three), so the police went straight to the Sun House. They found Voss's car there, and then they heard shouts from the old chapel on the hill above it.","She looked at the muddy man on the ground. \"Voss,\" she said quietly. Her voice was weak, but nobody in the chapel moved. \"Your grandfather bought my father's shop in Vienna in 1941, for the price of a loaf of bread. He wanted my father's instruments, too. I see that the Voss family never gives up.\" Two officers lifted Voss to his feet. He did not say a word.","Elif walked across the wet grass. She was still holding the case in one hand. With the other she took the leather notebook out of her jacket and put it into the old woman's thin hands. \"Your father left this for you, Mrs. Hartmann,\" she said. \"There is a letter inside.\"","Marta Hartmann looked at the first page. Her fingers were shaking. \"Vati,\" she whispered, and she began to cry.","Later, Joaquín said the river had gone down by the evening, as fast as it had come. The water had never reached the top shelves, and most of the cases there were dry. An old farmer in Göreme told him that his uncle Mehmet had studied in Vienna before the war and had come home in 1943 with a lot of heavy boxes. Mehmet died the next winter, and he never told anyone what was inside them. In the next two years, thirty-one instruments went home to families in nine countries. The first one, the honey-coloured violin that Elif had carried out of the cave, went back to a family in Prague.","A week after the flood, Elif sat in the workshop in Kadıköy, closing the back of the old violin. She had found the crack, cleaned the glue, and matched the colour of the wood so well that nobody could find the repair. Usta Nuri put on the new strings himself. On Friday, a car stopped outside, and Marta Hartmann came in with a blanket on her knees.","Elif put the violin into her hands. Marta looked for a long time at the scratch on the top of the neck. \"An M,\" she said. \"I made it with a pin when I was nine. My father was so angry.\" Slowly, she lifted the violin to her chin. Her bow was shaking. For a moment nothing happened, and then she played three notes, a slow, soft song from a city that she had left eighty years before.","Elif's phone buzzed in her pocket. It was a message from Joaquín, with a photograph of the tunnel. The water was gone, and the bells were hanging quietly from the roof.","The river is sleeping again, he had written."],"glossary":{"apprentice":"çırak","neck":"(kemanda) sap","bridge":"(kemanda) eşik, tellerin üzerinde durduğu küçük parça","back":"(kemanın) arka kapağı","bow":"(1) eğilerek selam verme; (2) keman yayı","shaft":"dikey kuyu, dar ve derin boşluk","teyze":"(Türkçe) yaşlı kadınlara hitap; teyze","usta":"(Türkçe) usta, saygı değer sanatçı"},"phrases":[{"en":"nearly dropped","tr":"neredeyse düşürüyordu"},{"en":"took off","tr":"çıkardı (eldiven, ceket vb.)"},{"en":"squeezed through","tr":"sıkışarak geçti"},{"en":"in one breath","tr":"tek nefeste"},{"en":"at the very last second","tr":"tam son saniyede"},{"en":"found out","tr":"öğrendi, keşfetti"},{"en":"run away","tr":"kaçmak, koşup uzaklaşmak"},{"en":"give them back","tr":"onları geri vermek"},{"en":"in time","tr":"zamanında"},{"en":"never gives up","tr":"asla pes etmez"},{"en":"stayed behind","tr":"geride kaldı"},{"en":"heard every word","tr":"her kelimeyi duydu"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Gregor Voss visit the workshop at the start of the story?","options":["He wants to learn to repair violins","He wants to buy a broken violin for a lot of money","He wants to sell a map to Usta Nuri","He wants to meet Marta Hartmann"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Voss, bozuk kemana beş bin euro teklif eder; çünkü içinde gizli bir şey olduğunu bilir."},{"q":"How does Elif escape from Dragan at the rest area?","options":["A group of older women protects her","She hides in the toilets","She runs into the desert","She calls the police"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Hacer Teyze ve arkadaşları Elif'i saklar ve el çantalarıyla Dragan'ı kovalar."},{"q":"What do the bronze bells in the tunnel do?","options":["They show where the instruments are","They play music for visitors","They open the bronze door","They warn that the river is coming"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Çanlar bir alarm görevi görür: nehir uyandığında su havayı iter ve çanları çalar."},{"q":"What is hidden in the round room behind the bronze door?","options":["Gold and jewellery","Old maps of Cappadocia","Musical instruments that belonged to musicians who had to leave their homes","The workshop of Emil Hartmann"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Oda, evlerinden kaçmak zorunda kalan müzisyenlerin çalgılarıyla doludur; Emil Hartmann bunları ailelerine geri verilsin diye saklamıştı."},{"q":"According to Emil's letter, how did the instruments get from Vienna to Cappadocia?","options":["Emil took them there himself after the war","His old student Mehmet carried them to his home valley","Marta carried them in her violin case","Voss's grandfather sent them there by train"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Mektuba göre Emil'in Göremeli eski öğrencisi Mehmet çalgıları savaşta olmayan Türkiye'ye taşıyıp vadideki mağaraya saklar. Emil de haritayı ve anahtarı Marta'nın kemanına gizler, çünkü hiçbir asker bir çocuğun kemanının içine bakmaz."}],"words":4740,"length":"long"},{"id":"a-fable-with-room-to-think","type":"article","title":"A Fable with Room to Think","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"B2","genre":"fable","summaryTr":"Fabl, sonunda hazır bir ders vermekten başka neler yapabilir? Hayvan karakterler, sınırlı dünya kuralları ve eylemle gösterilen sonuçlar üzerinden okura düşünme alanı bırakan fablları inceliyoruz.","paragraphs":["## Build a small world with a real problem","A fable can present a large question through a small, clear situation. Several animals share a well. A bird controls access to a tree. A bridge helps some travellers but leaves others struggling. These situations are easy to picture, yet they can raise questions about fairness, responsibility, cooperation, and pride. The small scale gives the writer room to make cause and effect visible. The story does not need to describe an entire society if one shared object can bring different needs into contact.","The examples in this article are invented to explore writing choices. Imagine a beaver who builds a bridge between a village and its market. He is skilled and generous. He wants everyone to use his work, so he hangs a sign calling it a bridge for all. Then a tortoise arrives with a cart and cannot climb the entrance step. The problem is practical before it is moral. The reader can see the step, the cart, and the gap between the beaver's intention and the tortoise's experience.","This gap gives the fable a useful starting point. The beaver does not need to be secretly cruel. If he meant to exclude the tortoise, the story would become a different examination of power or selfishness. Here, his mistake comes from designing around the body and habits he knows best: his own. That makes correction possible without requiring his complete defeat. The central question is how a well-meaning character can learn from a result that does not match the promise he made.","The setting should contain enough detail to support the problem and little that distracts from it. We need to know why the animals cross the stream and why the tortoise uses a cart. We probably do not need a full history of the market or a list of every tree in the valley. Details earn their place when they influence decisions. A heavy basket makes the cart necessary. Rain makes the bridge valuable. A second entrance allows the writer to ask whether a builder has considered both directions of travel.","## Give animals more than a label","Animal characters make physical differences immediately available to a story. A mouse has small feet; a deer is tall; a duck travels with young birds who may behave unpredictably. A writer can use these features to create different experiences of the same place. The bridge boards that feel perfectly comfortable to the beaver may leave frightening gaps beneath the mouse. This is a narrative design choice, not a scientific claim about how actual animals would organise a village. The fictional world establishes its own simple rules.","Those rules should remain consistent. If every animal speaks, buys food, and uses tools, the reader can accept that arrangement quickly. Problems arise when a character suddenly gains an ability only because the plot needs it. A mouse who has struggled to lift a small object should not later move a heavy beam without explanation. Physical differences are most useful when they continue to matter. They provide limits within which characters must cooperate, rather than convenient decorations that disappear whenever they become difficult to manage.","A character can also have a personal habit beyond its animal features. The beaver may enjoy accurate measurements but dislike long meetings. The fox may be good at attracting customers and poor at noticing how his display affects a public path. The tortoise may speak briefly because she prefers to demonstrate a problem with her cart. These habits create specific people within the simplified world. They allow the reader to anticipate reactions without reducing each animal to a single word such as clever, lazy, or proud.","Avoid making one species carry all the story's good judgement. If the tortoise is always correct and the beaver is always wrong, their relationship becomes predictable. The tortoise might suggest a bench in a place that blocks deliveries. The beaver can then offer a better position. Both characters gain credibility when they can contribute and revise. The fable's concern becomes the quality of their shared process rather than the discovery of one perfect character who already knows every answer. This also keeps dialogue from becoming a series of lectures.","## Let the lesson emerge through action","A moral stated before the events can make a fable feel like a demonstration with its conclusion already fixed. That approach may suit some purposes, but a writer can also let the reader discover the question through action. The beaver first carries the tortoise's cart across. It is a kind response, and it solves today's difficulty. Then she asks whether he will be there tomorrow. Her question reveals the limit of the solution. The story moves forward because kindness and a lasting arrangement are related but different things.","The next action should test a new idea. The beaver builds a gentle slope at each entrance. Now the cart can cross, but rain makes the smooth surface slippery. Small wooden strips improve the footing. This sequence shows revision as ordinary work. It also prevents the moral from becoming a vague instruction to 'be better'. Readers see what attention changes: the shape of an entrance, the surface beneath a foot, or the position of an object. The ethical concern remains connected to something visible and practical.","Repeated correction can become mechanical if every scene follows exactly the same pattern. Vary the source of the problem. One difficulty may come from the original design; another may appear because a new market stall attracts a crowd. A third may arise from a well-intended meeting that blocks the bridge. These variations expand the central question. A useful shared place depends on construction, on later additions, and on the behaviour of the people using it. The beaver's tools can address some of those matters, but not all of them.","An especially useful turning point occurs when the characters change the question they ask. Instead of demanding a list of improvements, the beaver can ask each animal to describe a particular crossing. A rabbit carrying fresh bread has a different need from a deer walking home after dark. Concrete accounts reveal details that abstract requests may hide. The story can then show listening as an active process: comparing experiences, testing changes, and finding arrangements that respond to more than the loudest voice at the meeting.","## Keep fairness different from sameness","A fable about shared life can explore fairness without pretending that identical treatment always produces identical access. Giving every animal the same high seat may sound equal, but it leaves the mouse unable to sit comfortably. The story can make this difference visible without stopping for a formal definition. A smaller seat beside the larger bench changes the scene. The important question is whether each animal can use the place, not whether every piece of furniture has exactly the same measurements.","At the same time, the story should acknowledge that needs can conflict. The rabbit wants space to run with bread deliveries. The mouse wants to cross without being frightened by fast feet. A solution that ignores either character simply moves the problem. They may agree on a narrow quiet path and a wider shared area, with walking required when the bridge is busy. This is a compromise, and it may not be anyone's first choice. A fable can treat that imperfect agreement as meaningful progress.","The cost of change should be visible too. Building another bench requires wood and time. Moving a stall affects how customers find it. Walking slows the rabbit's morning journey. If every improvement is free and effortless, the characters never need to choose between competing values. Showing a modest cost makes cooperation more substantial. The animals are giving something up, adjusting a habit, or doing additional work because another creature's experience now counts in their decisions. The resulting lesson is supported by action rather than declared generosity.","A mistake also needs a proportionate response. When the fox's decorative ribbon blocks the path, the others could ban him from the market. They could instead explain the problem and help him paint a sign that serves the same purpose. The second response keeps attention on the effect of the ribbon and the fox's legitimate wish to attract customers. It does not require the story to label him permanently bad. This distinction gives a fable room to consider repair, especially when a character is willing to change.","## Choose an ending that leaves a useful question","A fable can finish with a clear result without claiming that every problem has disappeared. The bridge may now work well for the animals we have met. The beaver can be proud of it and still leave a notebook where visitors can describe difficulties. This ending resolves the main conflict: he no longer treats his original design as proof that everyone has been considered. It also preserves the possibility of future learning. The finished structure and the continuing conversation belong together.","An object from the opening can make that change visible. The original sign says, 'A bridge for everyone.' At the end, the beaver adds, 'Tell us how you cross.' The first line expresses a promise; the second invites evidence about whether the promise is being kept. The added words do not reject the original aim. They give it a practical method. Because the reader remembers the tortoise at the step and the mouse between the boards, the sign carries more meaning than it would have carried at the beginning.","A separate moral sentence is optional. If you include one, check that it follows from the events rather than reaching beyond them. 'A good plan improves when people can describe how they use it' fits this invented bridge story better than a sweeping claim that cooperation solves every problem. The narrower statement respects the limits of the example. Readers can still apply it elsewhere, but the story does not pretend to prove a universal rule. A fable can offer a way of looking at a problem without closing every possible discussion.","For a writing exercise, choose one shared object and three characters who use it differently. Give the maker a sincere aim and one blind spot. Let the first solution help in the moment, then reveal why a more lasting change is needed. Include a disagreement in which both sides have a reasonable concern. Finally, return to the shared object after the characters have listened and acted. The reader should be able to describe what changed, who helped change it, and what the characters still need to notice."],"glossary":{"moral":"öykünün ilettiği ders","access":"bir yeri kullanabilme olanağı","blind":"fark edilmeyen","proportionate":"ölçülü, orantılı","footing":"ayağın yere sağlam basması","credibility":"inandırıcılık","revision":"gözden geçirip düzeltme","slope":"eğimli giriş"},"phrases":[{"en":"cause and effect","tr":"neden ve sonuç"},{"en":"well-meaning character","tr":"iyi niyetli karakter"},{"en":"within the simplified world","tr":"basitleştirilmiş dünya içinde"},{"en":"a lasting arrangement","tr":"kalıcı bir düzenleme"},{"en":"turning point","tr":"dönüm noktası"},{"en":"competing values","tr":"birbiriyle yarışan değerler"},{"en":"blind spot","tr":"gözden kaçırılan, fark edilmeyen nokta"},{"en":"in the moment","tr":"o an için"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the bridge a useful central object for the invented fable?","options":["It removes all practical problems","It brings different needs into one clear situation","It requires a full history of the valley","It proves a scientific theory"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Köprü, farklı hayvanların ihtiyaçlarını somut ve kolay izlenebilir tek bir durumda bir araya getirir."},{"q":"What does the tortoise's question about tomorrow reveal?","options":["She does not like the market","The bridge must be destroyed","Carrying her cart once is not a lasting arrangement","The beaver never wanted to help"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Arabayı bir kez taşımak bugünkü sorunu çözer; kaplumbağanın her gün bağımsız geçmesini sağlamaz."},{"q":"How does the article distinguish fairness from sameness?","options":["It says everyone must use the same high seat","It removes all differences between characters","It gives the strongest animal every choice","It asks whether different animals can actually use the place"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Adalet, her şeyin aynı ölçüde olmasıyla değil, farklı ihtiyaçları olanların yeri gerçekten kullanabilmesiyle değerlendirilir."},{"q":"Why does the article prefer a measured response to Fox's ribbon?","options":["A new sign can solve the problem while respecting his purpose","Fox should never change anything","The blocked path does not matter","Every mistake needs permanent punishment"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Yeni tabela hem yolu açar hem de tilkinin müşterilere görünme isteğini karşılar; çözüm doğrudan soruna yönelir."},{"q":"What does the final added line on the bridge sign do?","options":["It cancels the promise of access","It invites users to report their experience","It announces that learning is finished","It allows only the original villagers to cross"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Nasıl geçildiğinin anlatılmasını isteyen ek satır, kullanıcı deneyimini dinlemeyi ve düzeni geliştirmeyi sürdürür."}],"words":1725,"length":"medium"},{"id":"adventure-as-a-series-of-choices","type":"article","title":"Adventure as a Series of Choices","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"B2","genre":"adventure","summaryTr":"Macera yalnızca tehlikeli bir yolculuk mudur? Hedeflerin, kaynakların ve ekip içindeki kararların bir rotayı nasıl anlamlı bir anlatıya dönüştürdüğünü kurgusal örneklerle ele alıyoruz.","paragraphs":["## Give the journey a human purpose","A map can show movement from one place to another, but movement alone does not create an adventure. A character may cross mountains and rivers while the reader remains uncertain about why any of it matters. The journey gains direction when it serves a human purpose. Someone needs to deliver a message, reach a person, recover something useful, or discover whether a promise can be kept. The destination then becomes more than a point on a map. It represents a result that the character wants and may fail to achieve.","For the discussion that follows, imagine an entirely fictional island community preparing an evening performance. A small team must bring a large painted screen from the mainland workshop to the island hall. The nearest bridge has closed, so they must take a longer route. Nobody is saving the world. Nevertheless, the task has meaning. People have prepared the music, children have made paper figures, and an older artist wants to see a design used again. These details give the journey a purpose without requiring a disaster at every turn.","The purpose should be visible early enough for obstacles to matter. If we learn about the performance only after many pages of walking, the earlier difficulties may feel like unrelated travel notes. A short opening can establish what is being carried, who is waiting, and when arrival is needed. It can also introduce a personal reason for the central character to care. Perhaps she helped paint the screen but has never seen an audience respond to her work. Reaching the island now involves both a shared event and a private hope.","A clear purpose does not require a single fixed meaning throughout the story. At first, the character may want to arrive quickly and receive praise. Later, she may decide that protecting the screen or helping a teammate matters more than keeping the original schedule. Such changes make the journey an opportunity for development. The destination can remain the same while the character's understanding of success changes. The reader then follows two movements: the team's progress across a landscape and the central character's progress towards a more considered choice.","## Turn obstacles into decisions","An obstacle becomes dramatically useful when it requires a choice. A closed bridge is information. It becomes part of an adventure when the team must decide what to do about it. They might take the official longer route, ask whether a delivery service can help, or reduce the size of their load. Each option changes something else: time, cost, effort, or control. The writer does not need to present every possible alternative. A few clear options are enough to show that the next action belongs to the characters rather than simply happening to them.","The choice should depend on what the characters know at that moment. If the story gives them perfect information about every path, there is little uncertainty. If it gives them no information at all, their decisions can look like guesses without meaning. A useful middle ground is partial knowledge. They know that the northern path reaches the west bridge, but their map is old. They know that a cart protects the screen from being carried badly, but they do not know whether the path is smooth enough for its wheels.","Consequences connect one decision to the next. Choosing the cart may help on the road and create difficulty in a narrow gate. Removing the screen from the cart may solve that problem but require cooperation between people who have been arguing. A chain of consequences gives the route a shape. The obstacles do not need to become physically larger. They can ask more difficult questions about the same resources: who carries the weight, who chooses the route, and whose judgement the team is willing to trust.","An obstacle can also end an argument by testing a claim. One character insists that the old map is reliable. Another wants to follow new signs. When the mapped path leads to a greenhouse, the story provides evidence. The first character may refuse to change, admit the mistake, or try to hide it. Any of these responses develops the relationship. The greenhouse itself is only a building. Its narrative value comes from the decision before it and the response after it. This is why a modest barrier can carry more meaning than a dramatic but unrelated danger.","## Make resources easy to understand","Adventure often depends on limited resources. These may include time, daylight, energy, equipment, money, or information. The reader does not need a complete inventory, but important limits should be clear. If a rope will later become essential, introduce it while it has an ordinary use. If a character is growing tired, show the effort before the exhaustion changes the plan. Prepared details allow later solutions and difficulties to feel connected to the world of the story instead of appearing only when the writer needs them.","Time is particularly useful because it can influence every decision without constantly producing an emergency. In the fictional screen delivery, the performance begins at six. Arriving at four allows time to hang the screen. Arriving at five creates pressure but may still be manageable. Arriving after six changes the event itself. These stages provide a flexible limit. A story that treats every lost minute as a catastrophe can become tiring. A story that shows how different delays affect different tasks gives the reader a more precise reason to pay attention.","Objects become interesting when their usefulness changes with the situation. A jacket is clothing, then padding under a wooden frame. A short board is ordinary building material, then support beneath a trapped wheel. Such changes can reward observation and make practical problem solving satisfying. The solution should fit what has been established. A weak object should not suddenly carry an impossible weight. Nor should a character discover a perfect tool in a pocket that the story has never mentioned. Clear limits make resourcefulness more convincing.","Information is a resource too. Asking a gardener about a path may be more valuable than owning a detailed but outdated map. This gives conversations an active role in adventure. The team is not pausing the journey to speak; speaking can change the journey. A local person's advice may reveal a safer route, a permission requirement, or a problem ahead. Treat that person as someone with a reason to know and a life beyond helping the visitors. Even one concrete detail can prevent a useful conversation from feeling like a convenient instruction delivered by the setting.","## Let cooperation change the route","A team offers more than several pairs of hands. Its members can notice different details, value different outcomes, and possess different skills. One person reads maps well. Another recognises when equipment is under strain. A third is comfortable asking strangers for help. These differences create opportunities for both friction and cooperation. The group becomes more interesting when no single skill solves every difficulty. Leadership may shift according to the problem, allowing a usually quiet member to become essential at an unexpected moment.","Differences should influence action, not remain as character descriptions. It is easy to write that someone is patient and someone else is impatient. It is more useful to put them at a gate while the owner is being called. The impatient person wants to search for another route immediately. The patient person suggests waiting five minutes because moving the load again will cost energy. Neither quality is always correct. What matters is how the characters explain their positions, listen to evidence, and deal with the result of the decision.","Help can create obligations that continue beyond the scene. If a café owner lends the team a strap, someone must remember to return it. This small responsibility can give the ending another meaningful action. The team does not simply collect assistance and disappear. They remain connected to the people who made the journey possible. In an adventure about a community event, that connection may be central. The route gradually becomes a network of relationships, and the final audience may include people who first appeared as strangers along the way.","Cooperation also allows success to be shared without making the main character passive. She can notice a problem, ask the right person, or combine two other people's suggestions. Agency does not always mean acting alone. A character who learns to request help may make a more significant change than one who becomes capable of every task. To keep that development visible, give her a decision that costs pride or control. Accepting another route matters more when the story has shown why she wanted to lead everyone along her own.","## Make arrival answer the journey","Arrival should resolve the purpose established at the beginning, but it can also reveal what the journey has changed. The team may reach the hall with the screen intact, only to discover a small installation problem. A knot learned on the road can now help hang it. This connection allows an earlier experience to become useful again. The final task should be proportionate, however. Introducing an entirely new crisis at the destination may weaken the satisfaction of arrival if the story has not prepared the reader for it.","The performance itself can provide a brief contrast with the effort of delivery. Behind the screen, the reader knows about mud, gates, a worn rope, and tired arms. In front of it, the audience sees a paper boat moving through a painted sky. The finished effect looks simple because the hidden work succeeded. This difference can carry emotional weight without a speech explaining the lesson. A character watching the audience may understand that the journey mattered precisely because it allowed other people to experience something different from the journey.","A changed object can bring the story to a close. The old map may now contain pencil marks showing the new path. A borrowed strap may return to its owner. An empty cart may travel home more easily than it came. These details do not have to symbolise everything. Their practical meaning is enough to connect beginning and ending. The reader recognises something familiar in a changed condition, just as the central character returns with knowledge that was not available when the journey began.","To plan an adventure, write the destination and purpose first. Then list several obstacles, but place a decision beside each one. Record what the decision costs and what it makes possible later. Check that important resources appear before they become crucial. Finally, describe the arrival in terms of both the task and the person completing it. A route becomes a story when its turns matter to someone, and when the choices made along it leave a trace at the destination."],"glossary":{"agency":"kendi kararlarıyla hareket etme gücü","stakes":"başarısızlık durumunda kaybedilebilecek şeyler","resourcefulness":"eldeki olanaklarla çözüm bulabilme","load":"taşınan yük","strain":"zorlanma","proportionate":"ölçülü, durumla orantılı","inventory":"envanter, eşya listesi","route":"izlenen yol"},"phrases":[{"en":"a point on a map","tr":"haritadaki bir nokta"},{"en":"throughout the story","tr":"hikâye boyunca"},{"en":"partial knowledge","tr":"eksik, sınırlı bilgi"},{"en":"a chain of consequences","tr":"bir sonuçlar zinciri"},{"en":"under strain","tr":"zorlanma altında"},{"en":"according to the problem","tr":"soruna göre"},{"en":"at the destination","tr":"varış noktasında"},{"en":"leave a trace","tr":"iz bırakmak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What gives the fictional delivery journey meaning?","options":["The island is very distant","The screen matters to a shared event and a personal hope","The cart is expensive","Every delay threatens the world"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Perde topluluk gösterisi için gereklidir; ana karakter de kendi emeğinin seyirciyle buluşmasını ister."},{"q":"When does an obstacle become dramatically useful?","options":["When it removes all choices","When it has no connection to character","When it requires a meaningful choice","When it is always physically dangerous"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Engel, karakteri bedeli ve sonucu olan bir karar vermeye zorladığında anlatısal işlev kazanır."},{"q":"Why should an important tool appear before its crucial use?","options":["To provide a complete shopping list","To make the story longer","To hide its limitations","To make the later solution feel prepared"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Önceden tanıtılan araç, çözümün yalnızca ihtiyaç anında uydurulmuş gibi görünmesini önler."},{"q":"How can asking for help show agency?","options":["The character actively chooses someone whose help can solve the problem","It guarantees that no work remains","It removes the character from every decision","It proves that the route was unnecessary"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Doğru kişiden yardım istemek de etkin bir karardır; karakterin her işi tek başına yapması gerekmez."},{"q":"What ending detail connects the route with arrival?","options":["An unrelated crisis replaces the performance","A knot learned on the road helps hang the screen","The map disappears without explanation","The team forgets all borrowed equipment"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Yolda öğrenilen düğümün gösteri perdesini asarken işe yaraması, yolculuğu varıştaki sonuçla bağlar."}],"words":1771,"length":"medium"},{"id":"comedy-through-small-decisions","type":"article","title":"Comedy Through Small Decisions","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"B2","genre":"comedy","summaryTr":"Bir karakterin küçük ve anlaşılır kararları nasıl komik bir olay örgüsüne dönüşür? Özgün, kurgusal örneklerle beklenti, tekrar, ayrıntı ve sonuç ilişkisini inceliyoruz.","paragraphs":["## Start with a reasonable wish","A comic story does not need to begin with a foolish person doing something obviously foolish. It can begin with an ordinary wish: to look prepared, to avoid disturbing a neighbour, or to take one attractive photograph. The wish gives the reader a point of connection. We may never face the character's exact problem, but we can understand the desire behind it. This understanding matters because a sequence of embarrassing accidents can quickly feel empty when we have no reason to care who experiences them.","Consider an invented example that we will develop throughout this article. Ada runs a small repair shop. Before a photographer arrives, she wants the shop to look calm and well organised. The wish is reasonable. She moves unfinished repairs out of sight and arranges three polished objects on a table. Nothing is funny yet. However, she has created a difference between the shop's appearance and its daily life. Customers still need their lamps and clocks. Her assistant still needs the tools she has hidden. The room is ready for a picture, but less ready for work.","The next decision should grow out of that difference. A customer arrives for a repaired fan. Ada asks him to wait behind a screen so that he will not appear in the photograph. He agrees, but the fan is also behind the screen, along with several objects that do not belong to him. Ada must now describe the correct fan without leaving her carefully arranged position. Her attempt to simplify the room has made a simple exchange complicated. The comic situation comes from the relationship between intention and result.","This method gives a writer more control than adding unrelated disasters. A broken window, a sudden goat, and a power cut could all interrupt the photograph, but they would not necessarily develop Ada's character. Her own decisions are more useful material. Each one shows what she values and what she fails to notice. The reader can think, 'I understand why she did that,' while also seeing trouble ahead. That combination allows the story to remain sympathetic even as its practical difficulties become increasingly absurd.","## Let each solution create a cost","Escalation means that a situation becomes more difficult, but difficulty alone is not enough to create a satisfying comic pattern. A useful question is: what does the latest solution make harder? Ada cannot identify the customer's fan from across the room, so she invents a numbering system. She attaches numbered cards to everything behind the screen. Unfortunately, the photographer also uses numbers to organise his pictures. When someone asks for number four, Ada must work out whether they mean a fan, a photograph, or the stool that now carries the fourth card.","This is an invented situation, not a claim about how repair shops operate. Its purpose is to show a chain of causes. The numbers solve a local problem: objects can be identified without being visible. They also introduce a new problem: the same words now belong to two systems. The writer should make both systems clear before using the confusion. If the reader cannot understand the rules, the scene may feel random. If the rules are visible, the reader can follow the mistake and enjoy the characters' different interpretations.","Escalation can change the kind of pressure instead of simply increasing the noise. At first, Ada risks an untidy photograph. Later, she risks giving a customer the wrong object. Finally, she may risk revealing that her assistant knows the shop's work better than she does. These pressures move from appearance to responsibility to personal pride. The story becomes richer because the same small problem touches several parts of her life. More people can enter the scene, but each arrival should expose a new consequence rather than merely fill the room.","There must also be pauses. After several quick exchanges, a quiet action can give the reader time to catch up. The assistant might remove a card from his own sleeve and place it silently on the correct lamp. This small gesture tells us that the system has spread beyond Ada's control. It also gives the assistant a personality without requiring a long speech. Comic pacing is therefore a matter of contrast: explanation and misunderstanding, movement and stillness, confident plans and short moments in which confidence has nowhere to go.","## Control what the reader knows","A writer can create comedy by allowing the reader to know more than a character. Suppose we see the photographer move a chair before Ada enters the room. Later, she gives instructions based on its old position. We understand her mistake before she does. This gap creates anticipation. We are waiting for the moment when her confident description meets the changed room. The eventual discovery does not need to be dramatic. A brief look at the empty space may be enough, because the reader has already done much of the work.","The opposite arrangement is also possible. The reader and Ada may both misunderstand an assistant's message. When the true meaning appears, it changes our view of the earlier exchange. This kind of reveal needs fair preparation. A useful detail should be present, even if its importance is not obvious at first. For example, the assistant might mention that the customer has brought two identical fans. Later confusion can then surprise us without requiring information that the writer deliberately kept outside the scene.","Dialogue becomes more interesting when people use the same words for different purposes. Ada may say, 'We need a clean start,' meaning a better arrangement for the photograph. Her assistant may begin cleaning the table because, in a repair shop, the phrase sounds like a practical instruction. This misunderstanding works only if it fits the people and their situation. Repeatedly making a capable character misunderstand simple language can feel forced. Give that person a reason to hear the practical meaning: perhaps he is holding a wet cloth and has just asked where to begin.","Not every misunderstanding needs a spoken explanation. The writer can let an object settle the question. Ada asks where all the tools have gone. The assistant opens the beautiful display box she chose for the photograph, and the tools are inside. That action reveals the answer, shows the cost of her earlier decision, and returns attention to the picture. A single visible detail can connect several parts of a comic scene. Explaining all those connections afterwards would reduce the reader's opportunity to notice them independently.","## Repeat with a meaningful change","Repetition can help readers recognise a pattern. It becomes less useful when the same event produces the same result without adding anything. Imagine that Ada keeps saying, 'Only one more minute.' The first time, the phrase sounds normal. The second time, a customer sits down and opens a long book. The third time, the photographer removes his coat and asks whether the shop has lunch plans. The repeated words remain the same, but the reactions change their meaning. What began as a promise becomes a measure of lost trust.","A repeated object can serve a similar purpose. At the start, a red stool is simply in the wrong place. Later, it becomes a place to hide a toolbox. Later still, someone needs it to reach the screen that is hiding the repairs. The object moves through different practical roles. When it finally appears in the photograph, the reader recognises its history. This return can create satisfaction because the stool is no longer a random piece of furniture. It carries the memory of several decisions, each of which helped produce the ending.","The return should remain easy to follow. If every object has a secret role and every sentence refers to an earlier joke, the scene may become exhausting. Choose a few details and give them enough space. A comic story benefits from ordinary information too: where people stand, what they want, and how much time remains. Such details support the reader's attention. They make it possible to notice a change when it arrives. Without a stable background, even a clever variation can disappear among too many competing surprises.","Tone also shapes the effect of repetition. Ada's assistant could respond to every mistake with a cruel remark, but that would change the relationship at the centre of the story. A patient response can be just as funny when it is specific. He might prepare a second sign reading 'Almost ready' because he knows she will soon need it. The action gently exposes her habits while also helping her. Comedy does not require kindness, but a writer should understand whether the laughter brings characters closer or leaves someone increasingly alone.","## End with a changed decision","A comic ending can do more than deliver the largest accident. It can show a character choosing differently. In our invented shop story, Ada might finally ask the photographer to record the room during actual work. The customers return to the main space. Tools come out of the display box. The red stool supports someone repairing a shelf. The resulting photograph is busier than Ada planned, but it represents a place where useful things happen. Her original wish survives in a changed form: she still wants a good picture, but her idea of 'good' has developed.","This ending works best if it does not suddenly make her perfect. She may still straighten a crooked label before the photograph. That small action preserves the personality that created the story. Change does not require a complete replacement of character. It may mean that Ada now knows when to stop arranging things. The reader can recognise her old habit and also see its new limit. This creates room for one final comic detail without cancelling the emotional movement of the scene.","When revising your own comic story, follow the main decisions in order. Identify the wish behind each decision, the problem it solves, and the cost it creates. Remove an interruption if it has no connection to those steps, unless it serves another clear purpose. Then check the reader's information. Can we understand the confusion? Have we seen enough to appreciate a later return? Finally, look at the people around the central character. Their reactions should express individual wishes, not merely provide applause or embarrassment on command.","A useful writing exercise is to give one character a modest task and three reasonable solutions that make it harder. Keep the setting small enough to describe clearly. Let one object return in a new role. End when the character makes a decision they could not have made at the beginning. You do not need to promise that every reader will laugh; humour depends partly on personal taste. You can, however, build a clear sequence in which the comic pressure comes from understandable human choices."],"glossary":{"escalation":"giderek artan karmaşa","pacing":"anlatının ilerleme temposu","reveal":"gizli bilginin açığa çıkışı","screen":"paravan","stool":"tabure","return":"önceki bir ayrıntının yeniden kullanılması","pressure":"baskı, zorlayıcı durum","tone":"anlatım tonu"},"phrases":[{"en":"grow out of","tr":"bir durumdan doğmak"},{"en":"a chain of causes","tr":"bir nedenler zinciri"},{"en":"catch up","tr":"olup biteni kavrayıp yetişmek"},{"en":"at first","tr":"ilk başta"},{"en":"settle the question","tr":"meseleyi açıklığa kavuşturmak"},{"en":"in a changed form","tr":"değişmiş bir biçimde"},{"en":"on command","tr":"komut verilince"},{"en":"personal taste","tr":"kişisel zevk"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the article begin with a reasonable wish?","options":["It removes the need for conflict","It helps readers understand the character","It guarantees that every reader will laugh","It makes all accidents equally useful"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Anlaşılır bir istek, okurun karakterle bağ kurmasına ve sonraki kararlarını anlamasına yardımcı olur."},{"q":"What problem does Ada's numbering system create?","options":["The cards are too expensive","Nobody can read numbers","Different systems use the same numbers","The photographer leaves immediately"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Nesneler ve fotoğraflar için kullanılan sayılar çakışır; aynı numaranın neyi belirttiği karışır."},{"q":"What makes repetition useful in the article's examples?","options":["It prevents all changes","It replaces the ending","It hides the setting","A repeated element gains a new meaning or role"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Tekrar edilen söz ya da nesne farklı tepki ve işlevlerle yeni anlam kazanır."},{"q":"How does Ada change at the end of the invented example?","options":["She accepts a photograph of the shop at work","She stops caring about her shop","She removes every customer permanently","She becomes a photographer"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Ada, kusursuz görünen boş bir düzen yerine gerçek işleyişi gösteren bir fotoğrafı kabul eder."},{"q":"Which revision question does the article recommend?","options":["Can every character tell the same joke?","What problem does each decision solve and create?","Can all ordinary details be removed?","Is the last accident the loudest?"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Kararların hangi sorunu çözüp hangisini doğurduğunu izlemek, komik olay zincirini değerlendirmeyi sağlar."}],"words":1773,"length":"medium"},{"id":"dracula","type":"summary","title":"Dracula","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"B2","genre":"horror","work":{"title":"Dracula","titleTr":"Drakula","author":"Bram Stoker","year":1897},"source":{"name":"Bram Stoker — Dracula (Project Gutenberg)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/345","license":"Kaynak roman; metin Lantern English için yazılmış özgün özet ve incelemedir."},"summaryTr":"Bram Stoker'ın Drakula romanının B2 düzeyinde özgün İngilizce özeti ve okuma incelemesi. Transilvanya'dan İngiltere'ye uzanan olayları, karakterleri ve temaları ele alır; romanın sonu dahil tüm olayları açıklar.","paragraphs":["## 1. A Business Journey into Fear","This original summary and reading review discusses the whole of Bram Stoker's Dracula, first published in 1897. It includes major spoilers, including the ending. The novel follows a small group who discover that an ancient vampire has entered their modern world. Its story begins with an ordinary business visit and grows into a struggle involving illness, friendship, dangerous journeys, and the fear of losing control over one's own body. The account below follows the novel's events, then considers its characters, themes, and unusual way of telling a story.","Jonathan Harker, a young English solicitor, travels to Transylvania to help Count Dracula buy property in England. Jonathan expects to complete a professional task and return to his fiancée, Mina Murray. He records train times, meals, landscapes, and local customs in his journal. These practical details make him seem like a careful observer. However, the people he meets become frightened when they hear his destination. An innkeeper's wife begs him to delay his journey and gives him a crucifix. Jonathan accepts it politely without fully accepting the beliefs behind her warning.","The journey to the castle becomes increasingly strange. A mysterious driver collects Jonathan at a mountain pass and takes him through darkness. Wolves surround the carriage, but the driver appears able to control them. The landscape is beautiful, yet Jonathan has little power over where he is going or when he will arrive. At the castle, Dracula welcomes him with formal courtesy. The Count is tall and pale, with sharp teeth and an unusually strong grip. Despite his comfortable room and generous meals, Jonathan begins to feel uneasy.","Dracula is deeply interested in England. He has studied books, maps, and directories, and he asks Jonathan about English speech and behaviour. He wants to move among strangers without attracting attention. Jonathan explains the purchase of Carfax, an old estate near London with an abandoned chapel. At first, these conversations belong to the familiar world of legal work. Gradually, however, Jonathan understands that his knowledge may help a dangerous person enter his own country. The business relationship gives Dracula access to information as well as land.","The castle contains no visible servants, although someone prepares meals and performs household tasks. Jonathan never sees the Count eat. One morning, while shaving, he discovers that Dracula has no reflection in his mirror. When Jonathan cuts himself, the sight of blood produces a frightening reaction in his host. The crucifix seems to stop Dracula from attacking him. Soon Jonathan finds that doors are locked and escape routes are missing. The building stands above a steep drop. He is a prisoner, even though his host continues to speak as if he were a guest.","## 2. The Prisoner in the Castle","Jonathan tries to keep calm and understand the rules of his situation. Dracula warns him against sleeping anywhere outside his own rooms. The warning suggests that other dangers exist within the castle, beyond the Count himself. Jonathan also sees Dracula climbing down an outside wall with his body facing the stone, moving in a way no ordinary person could. This destroys the last comfortable explanation of his host's behaviour. Jonathan's journal becomes a record of discoveries that he can barely believe, even while he is writing them down.","One evening, Jonathan enters another part of the castle and falls asleep there. Three beautiful women approach him. Their attraction quickly becomes a threat: they seem eager to bite him. Dracula interrupts and insists that Jonathan belongs to him for the moment. His intervention saves Jonathan from an immediate attack, but it offers no real safety. The women receive a bag containing a child instead. Later, Jonathan hears the child's mother outside demanding its return. Dracula calls wolves, and her desperate attempt to reach the castle ends in horror.","The Count forces Jonathan to write letters with future dates, saying that his work is finished and that he will soon be travelling home. These letters will create a false account of his movements. Jonathan also sees Dracula wearing his clothes. If people notice the Count outside, they may believe they have seen the English visitor. Both actions show that Dracula plans carefully. He does not rely only on supernatural strength. He also uses the ordinary evidence by which people judge what has happened: handwriting, clothing, dates, and messages.","Jonathan attempts to send secret letters, but the effort fails. Workmen and carriers arrive at the castle, yet he cannot persuade them to rescue him. By climbing along the walls, he reaches rooms that Dracula has kept from him. In an old chapel, he discovers boxes of earth. The Count lies in one of them, apparently without breath or heartbeat. On a later visit, Dracula looks younger and swollen with blood. Jonathan realises that the boxes are connected with the vampire's survival and that they are being prepared for transport.","Jonathan tries to strike Dracula with a shovel, but he cannot end the threat. The Count leaves the castle while Jonathan remains trapped with the three women. A direct attempt to leave through the main door has already revealed wolves waiting outside. Jonathan decides that a dangerous climb offers a better chance than staying. His written account ends before the reader learns whether he succeeds. This break leaves his fate uncertain while the narrative turns towards England, where people connected with him are living without any knowledge of the danger approaching.","## 3. Whitby and the Arrival of the Demeter","In England, Mina corresponds with her close friend Lucy Westenra. Mina works as an assistant schoolmistress and practises shorthand and typing, skills she hopes will be useful to Jonathan. Lucy describes an extraordinary day on which three men propose marriage to her. They are Dr. John Seward, who runs an asylum; Quincey Morris, an American; and Arthur Holmwood, whom she loves and accepts. Seward and Quincey are disappointed, but their affection for Lucy does not become lasting hostility towards Arthur. Their existing friendship later makes cooperation possible.","Mina visits Lucy in the seaside town of Whitby. The two women spend time above the harbour, near the churchyard, enjoying the view and talking. Lucy has a habit of sleepwalking, which becomes a source of concern. Mina also worries about Jonathan because his letters provide little comfort and then stop arriving. These private anxieties develop beside the town's ordinary summer activities. The reader knows something that the women do not: the strange events in Transylvania may soon reach this apparently peaceful English setting.","A terrible storm brings a ship called the Demeter into Whitby harbour. Its captain is dead at the wheel, tied there so that he could maintain his position. No living crew members remain aboard. A large dog escapes from the ship and disappears. The captain's log describes a voyage during which sailors vanished one after another and a terrifying presence seemed to move about the vessel. The ship has carried Dracula towards England. Its cargo includes fifty boxes of earth, which will be transported to Carfax.","During the night, Lucy leaves her bedroom while sleepwalking. Mina searches for her and eventually finds her in the churchyard, where a dark figure is bending over her. The figure disappears as Mina approaches. Lucy is pale and weak, and Mina notices two small marks on her neck. At first, she wonders whether she accidentally injured Lucy while fastening a shawl around her. This small misunderstanding matters because a familiar explanation hides an unfamiliar danger. Mina brings Lucy home, but neither woman understands the cause of the encounter.","Lucy continues to have disturbing nights. At times, a bat appears near the window, and her health begins to decline. Meanwhile, Mina receives news that Jonathan is alive in a hospital in Budapest, recovering from a severe illness. He escaped the castle, but his experiences have left him physically and emotionally exhausted. Mina goes to him, and they marry there. Jonathan gives her his journal, although he cannot bear to revisit its contents. She seals it, choosing trust in him before demanding an explanation of everything he has endured.","## 4. Lucy's Illness and the Limits of Medicine","After Mina leaves, Lucy's condition becomes more serious. She loses strength and colour without any clear explanation. Arthur asks Seward to help her, and Seward calls on his former teacher, Professor Abraham Van Helsing. Van Helsing is a doctor and scholar from Amsterdam with a wider knowledge of unusual beliefs. He recognises that Lucy's illness does not fit an ordinary medical pattern. However, he does not immediately tell everyone what he suspects. This secrecy helps maintain the mystery, while also making it harder for others to understand his instructions.","The men attempt to save Lucy through blood transfusions. Arthur gives blood first, and Seward, Van Helsing, and Quincey also give blood at different points. Each treatment produces temporary improvement, but the unexplained loss continues. Their efforts express affection through physical sacrifice: they give part of themselves to keep Lucy alive. Yet the medical treatment replaces what has been taken without removing the attacker. The repeated cycle of recovery and collapse shows how effective care can still fail when the cause of suffering remains hidden.","Van Helsing places garlic flowers around Lucy and her room, and he insists that certain protective arrangements must remain in place. Lucy's mother does not know the reason for them. Believing that fresh air will help her daughter, she removes the flowers and opens the window. Her action is loving, but it exposes Lucy to renewed danger. The episode depends on a gap in shared knowledge. One person understands a threat, another controls access to the patient, and neither has enough information from the other to act safely.","Another disaster occurs when a wolf breaks a bedroom window. Lucy's mother, who has a serious heart condition, dies from the shock. The servants are unable to help because their wine has been drugged. Lucy is left almost alone in the broken room, with the protections around her disturbed. She writes down what has happened, preserving evidence even as her strength fails. A delayed message also prevents help from arriving when it is needed. The danger grows through several failures together, including secrecy, interrupted communication, exhaustion, and deliberate attack.","Despite the men's desperate care, Lucy dies. Before her death, her appearance and behaviour become unsettling. She asks Arthur to kiss her, but Van Helsing stops him when he sees the danger. The woman they love seems both familiar and changed. Arthur also loses his father and inherits the title Lord Godalming, leaving him with grief from two directions. Lucy's death appears to end the medical struggle, but Van Helsing understands that the problem may continue beyond burial. His next duty will require him to convince her friends of something they find almost impossible to accept.","## 5. The Woman in the Tomb","After Lucy's funeral, reports appear about children who have gone missing for a time and returned with small wounds on their necks. They describe a beautiful woman who has approached them. Van Helsing suspects that Lucy has become a vampire. Seward resists this conclusion because it conflicts with his professional training and his memories of her. The professor gradually brings him to the tomb, where they find that Lucy's body is not always in its coffin. Evidence begins to challenge Seward's belief that death must have ended her story.","Van Helsing eventually takes Arthur and Quincey with them. They see Lucy return carrying a child. Her beauty remains, but her actions are cruel and her affection has become a means of attracting victims. She tries to draw Arthur towards her. The men now face a painful distinction between the friend they remember and the creature using her body. Van Helsing explains that ending the vampire's existence will also free Lucy from it. Without intervention, she will continue to harm others, especially children who cannot protect themselves.","Following Van Helsing's instructions, Arthur drives a wooden stake through Lucy's heart while she lies in her coffin. The action is emotionally terrible for him because it looks like violence against the person he wanted to marry. Yet the change in her face afterwards persuades the men that they have released her from suffering. The protective process is completed by cutting off her head and placing garlic in her mouth. Lucy can finally remain at peace, and the children she attacked are freed from the vampire's influence.","This scene changes the direction of the novel. The men can no longer treat the strange events as separate illnesses or private losses. They have witnessed a supernatural threat and acted against it together. Their grief becomes a reason to prevent further victims. Lucy is therefore central even after her death: the effort to understand what happened to her brings the future hunters into a common purpose. However, they still need to identify the force behind her transformation and discover how that force entered their lives.","## 6. Records, Allies, and the Boxes of Earth","Back in England, Jonathan sees Dracula in London, looking younger than he did at the castle. The sight causes another collapse and convinces Mina that the sealed journal must be read. She copies its contents, making the difficult handwritten record easier to share. Van Helsing examines Jonathan's account and confirms that it supports his own discoveries. This recognition has a powerful effect on Jonathan. His experiences were real, and he no longer has to carry the fear that his mind simply invented them. Knowledge begins to restore his ability to act.","Mina collects diaries, letters, newspaper reports, and other records. She also types Seward's spoken diary from his phonograph recordings. By placing the material in order, she allows everyone to compare events that once appeared unrelated. Jonathan's business visit explains Carfax; Lucy's illness connects the vampire with Whitby; transport records reveal the movement of the boxes. The group can now piece together a larger picture. Mina's work is practical, patient, and essential. Without it, each person would possess only a fragment of the information needed to understand Dracula.","Seward's patient Renfield provides another disturbing connection. He catches flies and feeds them to spiders, then feeds spiders to birds. He believes that consuming living creatures will increase his own life. His interest echoes Dracula's need to take life from others. Renfield sometimes speaks as though a powerful master is near, and his behaviour changes with Dracula's movements. Since Seward's asylum stands beside Carfax, the patient is unusually close to the vampire's hiding place. Seward observes him carefully, but does not always understand the warnings within his behaviour.","Van Helsing explains the enemy's strengths and limits. Dracula can command certain animals, influence the weather, and take forms such as a bat or mist. He needs suitable earth in which to rest, and religious objects can oppose him. Daylight restricts his powers; it does not automatically burn him into ashes. The group plans to find his resting places and make them unusable. Their first search at Carfax shows that only twenty-nine of the fifty boxes remain. Dracula has moved the others to different properties, giving himself several places of safety.","## 7. Mina Becomes a Target","The men decide to protect Mina by leaving her out of further discussions. They admire her contribution, but believe the violent work ahead is unsuitable for a woman. The decision has an immediate cost: the person who has organised their knowledge is separated from new information. Mina becomes tired and troubled by strange dreams, but she does not understand how urgently she should report them. While the hunters concentrate on Dracula's houses, the danger enters the place where they believe she is safe. Their attempt to protect her creates another gap in communication.","Renfield urgently asks Seward to let him leave the asylum. He speaks with unusual seriousness, but cannot fully explain the danger. Seward refuses because he sees no sufficient reason to release him. Later, Renfield is found with terrible injuries. Before he dies, he reveals that Dracula promised him countless lives to consume and persuaded him to invite the vampire inside. Renfield had hoped to gain power through service. When he realised that Mina was being harmed, however, he resisted his supposed master and tried to stop him.","Dracula's superior strength defeats Renfield. His final account gives the others enough warning to rush to the Harkers' room. There they discover Jonathan lying helplessly asleep while Dracula holds Mina and forces her to drink blood from a wound in his chest. He has already taken blood from her. This exchange creates a dreadful connection between them. Religious objects drive him away, but the attack has done lasting harm. Dracula also destroys some of the group's records, trying to remove the knowledge that threatens him, although other copies survive.","Mina fears that she will become a vampire after her death. When Van Helsing touches her forehead with a piece of sacred wafer, it leaves a burn, showing the effect of Dracula's influence. She feels shame, although she has been attacked and has not chosen what happened. Jonathan and the others remain loyal to her. Her condition makes the search more urgent: killing Dracula may free her before the change becomes permanent. The hunters now understand that withholding information did not protect her, and her participation again becomes central to their decisions.","Mina discovers that the connection also offers a possible advantage. Under hypnosis at sunrise or sunset, she can sometimes describe sounds and sensations from Dracula's surroundings. Yet this method is dangerous because the connection may work in both directions. Dracula might learn the group's plans through her. Mina asks them to consider that risk, and she also asks for help if she eventually becomes a threat to others. Her response combines fear with clear judgement. She remains involved in the struggle even while her own body is becoming uncertain ground.","## 8. Driving Dracula out of London","Jonathan investigates the removal of the boxes by speaking with transport workers and following business records. His legal training becomes useful in a new way. The group searches Dracula's properties and places sacred wafer in the earth, making each resting place unavailable to him. The work depends on accurate counting, addresses, travel, and access to locked buildings. These are ordinary tasks directed against an extraordinary enemy. Dracula's dependence on material things gives the hunters a practical method: they can reduce his choices even when they cannot catch him directly.","At a house in Piccadilly, the men confront Dracula. Jonathan attacks him with a large knife, but the blow cuts clothing and releases money rather than ending the vampire's life. Dracula escapes, threatening them and claiming power over the women they love. His confidence is frightening, yet his position has weakened. The group continues until forty-nine boxes have been made useless. One remains beyond their reach. With his English refuges removed, Dracula chooses to return to Transylvania, where he expects familiar territory and his castle to offer protection.","Mina's hypnotic reports suggest movement by water. The hunters connect this information with shipping records and discover that Dracula's remaining box has left England aboard the Czarina Catherine. They travel across Europe to intercept the ship at Varna. Their journey reverses the direction of Dracula's arrival, turning the English characters into pursuers in foreign territory. Railways and rapid messages give them advantages, but they cannot control the ship's progress or the weather. They must wait, compare reports, and accept that their carefully made plan may need to change.","The ship reaches Galatz instead of Varna, defeating their first attempt to meet it. Dracula's box is removed and passed to people who will carry it inland. The hunters investigate the arrangements, using names, payments, and local contacts to follow its route. Mina studies maps and the available evidence to work out which river could take the box towards the castle. Her reasoning matters as much as the supernatural information obtained through hypnosis. The group responds to the changed situation by dividing its members among different routes.","Arthur and Jonathan travel by a small steam launch, while Seward and Quincey follow on horseback. Van Helsing takes Mina towards the castle by land. Each pair has a different role, but all aim to prevent Dracula from recovering his freedom in the mountains. Time becomes increasingly important as the days shorten and winter conditions make travel harder. Mina's reports grow less reliable as the vampire's influence changes. The hunters must therefore act on incomplete information, trusting both their companions and the conclusions drawn from the evidence they already possess.","## 9. The Return to the Castle","During the final journey, Mina begins to show more signs of Dracula's influence. Her sleep and appetite change, and Van Helsing worries about what will happen after sunset. Near the castle, the three vampire women appear and call to her. Van Helsing protects their camp with sacred wafer, forming a barrier that keeps the women away. The same protection also reveals how deeply Mina has been affected. The professor must protect her from outside attack while watching for the possibility that she herself is moving towards the condition of their enemies.","Leaving Mina within the protected circle, Van Helsing enters the castle during daylight. Jonathan's journal helps him understand the building and avoid being trapped there. He finds the three women in their tombs and destroys them, overcoming the strange attraction that makes the task difficult. He also places sacred wafer in Dracula's own tomb and secures the entrances against the vampire. The castle will no longer offer the refuge Dracula expects. The professor's actions connect the final pursuit with Jonathan's first discoveries, showing the continuing value of that early record.","Outside, the different groups converge on a cart carrying Dracula's box. Armed escorts are trying to bring it to the castle, and sunset is approaching. If the vampire reaches the right moment with his powers restored, he may escape again. Jonathan and Quincey force their way towards the cart while the others help surround it. The confrontation is brief and violent. Quincey receives a serious wound, but continues fighting. The hunters have crossed countries and gathered hundreds of details, yet their success now depends on a few remaining moments.","Jonathan and Quincey force the lid open, and Jonathan cuts Dracula's throat with his kukri knife. At the same time, Quincey drives his Bowie knife into the vampire's heart. Dracula's body crumbles into dust. Mina notices a momentary look of peace on his face before he disappears. The mark on her forehead vanishes, confirming that his hold over her has ended. Dracula is destroyed by the two men's knives; the final scene is different from Lucy's earlier death by a wooden stake. Quincey sees that Mina is safe, then dies from his wound.","A short closing note, written seven years later, looks back on these events. Jonathan and Mina have a son, whose many names link him to the friends who shared their struggle; the family calls him Quincey, and his birthday is the day Quincey died. The survivors have returned to ordinary life, but their happiness includes awareness of the person who did not come home. Jonathan also observes that their collected papers offer little conventional proof of the extraordinary events. For those involved, the lasting result is not public recognition. It is Mina's survival, their continued friendship, and the child who represents a future Dracula nearly destroyed.","## 10. How the Novel Builds Its Story","Dracula is told through journals, letters, telegrams, newspaper reports, a ship's log, and other documents. This form is often called an epistolary novel, although it includes much more than letters. There is no single narrator who knows everything. Each writer records events from a limited position. Jonathan can describe the castle, but knows nothing about Lucy's illness while he is trapped there. Seward can observe a patient, but misunderstand what the behaviour means. Readers must compare their accounts and notice connections before the characters fully recognise them.","The documents give impossible events an appearance of everyday accuracy. A train time, a delivery address, or a dated medical observation creates a familiar frame around the supernatural. These details also have a practical role in the plot. The group wins ground by checking when things happened and where objects travelled. Mina's organisation of the records therefore mirrors the reader's task. She turns separate experiences into a connected narrative, and that narrative becomes a tool for action. Telling the story well is part of surviving it.","Limited knowledge also controls suspense. The reader recognises danger in Lucy's neck wounds before her friends understand them. Later, Mina's tiredness feels threatening because Lucy has already shown similar signs. Stoker uses such patterns to make an ordinary bedroom or a delayed message frightening. Some episodes move slowly because the characters must wait for evidence. Others become urgent when a door opens, a body disappears, or sunset approaches. The movement between investigation and immediate danger gives the long novel its changing rhythm.","Dracula himself rarely explains his private thoughts. Readers encounter him mainly through the fear, injuries, and observations of others. This distance helps preserve his mystery, but it also means that the novel gives much more emotional space to the people resisting him. The title names the enemy, while the documents develop the friendships that oppose him. A useful way to read the book is to follow how information moves: who notices a fact, who believes it, who records it, and who is left without it.","## 11. Characters, Themes, and a Reading Response","Mina is one of the novel's most capable thinkers. Her typing, memory, emotional attention, and ability to compare evidence repeatedly help the group. Jonathan changes from a polite visitor into a determined pursuer, while Van Helsing brings different kinds of knowledge together. Arthur, Seward, and Quincey show that love can continue as service after romantic hopes fail. Their strengths become useful through cooperation. No single hero has all the necessary skills, and the group's mistakes are often caused by failing to share what someone else needs to know.","Blood connects several of the novel's major concerns. Given freely in the transfusions, it expresses care and sacrifice. Taken by Dracula, it becomes a means of control. The same physical substance therefore links generous friendship with violent possession. The vampire also threatens personal identity: the greatest fear is not simply dying, but becoming something that harms those one loves. Lucy's transformation makes that fear visible, while Mina's condition gives it a continuing emotional focus. Her friends must preserve both her life and her ability to remain herself.","The novel brings modern tools into contact with older religious beliefs. Typewriters, recorded speech, railways, and medical procedures all assist the hunters, but these alone cannot stop Dracula. They also need knowledge that their education initially encourages some of them to reject. This does not make careful observation useless. Instead, the characters must reconsider the limits of their explanations. At the same time, the book reflects restrictions and prejudices of its period. Its assumptions about women and its suspicious descriptions of foreign peoples invite critical attention from a present-day reader.","As a reading experience, Dracula offers more than a sequence of vampire attacks. Much of its power comes from watching ordinary routines become uncertain and then seeing frightened people learn to work together. The repeated notes and journeys can slow the pace, but they make the final pursuit feel earned. The ending offers relief with a lasting cost: Mina is saved, yet Quincey cannot share the future he helps secure. The novel leaves its survivors with a form of victory based on loyalty, shared knowledge, and the duty to remember."],"glossary":{"solicitor":"hukuki işlemler ve danışmanlık yapan avukat","count":"kont, soyluluk unvanı","estate":"geniş arazi ve üzerindeki malikâne","asylum":"akıl hastanesi (dönemin kullanımı)","transfusions":"kan nakilleri","stake":"sivri uçlu tahta kazık","wafer":"kutsanmış ince ekmek parçası","phonograph":"ses kaydedip çalan eski cihaz, fonograf","epistolary":"mektup ve belge biçiminde anlatılan","launch":"küçük motorlu tekne","kukri":"geniş, içe kıvrık ağızlı bıçak","bowie":"uzun ve geniş ağızlı av bıçağı","carfax":"Dracula'nın İngiltere'de satın aldığı mülk","demeter":"Dracula'yı Whitby'ye taşıyan gemi","czarina":"Dracula'nın dönüş gemisinin adının ilk sözcüğü","galatz":"Dracula'nın dönüşte ulaştığı liman","varna":"Karadeniz kıyısındaki liman kenti","whitby":"İngiltere'nin kuzeydoğusundaki kıyı kasabası","transylvania":"Transilvanya, romanda Dracula'nın yurdu","renfield":"Dr. Seward'ın hastası, roman kişisi","quincey":"Lucy'nin taliplerinden Amerikalı dost"},"phrases":[{"en":"attracting attention","tr":"dikkat çekmek"},{"en":"keep calm","tr":"sakin kalmak"},{"en":"a source of concern","tr":"bir endişe kaynağı"},{"en":"beyond their reach","tr":"ulaşabilecekleri yerin dışında"},{"en":"piece together","tr":"parçaları birleştirerek anlam çıkarmak"},{"en":"lasting harm","tr":"kalıcı zarar"},{"en":"make them unusable","tr":"onları kullanılamaz hâle getirmek"},{"en":"act on incomplete information","tr":"eksik bilgiye dayanarak hareket etmek"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Dracula want Jonathan's help at the beginning of the novel?","options":["To recover a stolen family journal","To buy English property and learn about life in England","To find a doctor for the women in his castle","To locate a ship that has disappeared"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Jonathan hem Carfax'ın satın alınmasına yardım eder hem de Dracula'ya İngilizce konuşma ve İngiltere'deki yaşam hakkında bilgi verir."},{"q":"Why do the blood transfusions fail to save Lucy permanently?","options":["Lucy refuses to accept help from her friends","The men cannot agree on which hospital to use","Dracula continues taking her blood while the cause remains hidden","Van Helsing leaves England before examining her"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Kan nakilleri Lucy'yi geçici olarak güçlendirir; ancak Dracula'nın saldırıları sürdüğü için kan kaybının asıl nedeni ortadan kalkmaz."},{"q":"What makes Mina's work with the documents so important?","options":["She connects separate experiences so the group can understand and pursue Dracula","She proves that Jonathan never left England","She hides the names of Dracula's properties from the men","She persuades Lucy's mother to protect every bedroom window"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Mina günlükleri, mektupları ve diğer kayıtları sıraya koyarak kişilerin tek başlarına göremediği bağlantıları ortaya çıkarır."},{"q":"What is the main danger in using Mina's connection with Dracula?","options":["She can describe only places she has visited before","Her reports make the sacred wafer lose its power","The connection prevents the group from travelling by train","Dracula may learn the group's plans through her"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Bağın iki yönlü olabilmesi tehlikelidir: Mina Dracula'nın çevresini algılarken Dracula da onun zihninden grubun planlarını öğrenebilir."},{"q":"What happens in the final confrontation near Dracula's castle?","options":["Arthur destroys Dracula with the stake used against Lucy","Jonathan and Quincey destroy Dracula with knives, and Quincey dies from his wound","Sunlight burns Dracula before the hunters reach his box","Mina returns Dracula to his tomb and becomes a vampire"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Jonathan Dracula'nın boğazını kukri bıçağıyla keserken Quincey Bowie bıçağını kalbine saplar. Mina kurtulur, fakat Quincey aldığı yaradan ölür."}],"words":4540,"length":"long"},{"id":"frankenstein-abridged","type":"story","title":"Frankenstein","author":"Mary Shelley, abridged by Lantern","year":1818,"level":"B2","genre":"scifi","abridged":true,"work":{"title":"Frankenstein","titleTr":"Frankenstein","author":"Mary Shelley","year":1818,"editionYear":1831},"source":{"name":"Frankenstein — Project Gutenberg #84 (1831 text); abridged and retold by Lantern","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/84","license":"Public domain (source text); this retelling is by Lantern"},"summaryTr":"Genç bilim insanı Victor Frankenstein, cansız bir bedene hayat vermeyi başarır; ama yarattığı varlığa sırtını döndüğü an, ikisini de buzlarla kaplı kuzeye kadar sürükleyecek bir kâbus başlar. Mary Shelley'nin ünlü romanının B2 düzeyinde kısaltılmış, sadık bir anlatımı.","paragraphs":["## Letters from the Ice","My dear Margaret, I am writing to you from the far north of Russia, where my ship is almost ready to sail. For six years I have dreamed of this voyage. I want to reach the North Pole and find a passage through the ice where no one has ever been. I am not afraid of the cold or the danger. My only sadness is that I have no friend on board, no one who shares my dreams or can tell me when I am wrong.","August. Margaret, something so strange has happened that I must write it down. Last week our ship was trapped by ice. When the fog lifted, we saw a sledge pulled by dogs crossing the ice towards the north, about half a mile away. In it sat a figure shaped like a man, but enormous, far larger than any man I have ever seen. We watched him through our telescopes until he disappeared.","That night the ice broke up. In the morning my sailors were leaning over the side, talking to someone in the sea. On a floating piece of ice lay another sledge with one living dog and a man, half frozen. He was no wild giant but a European, and he spoke to us in English with a foreign accent.","\"Before I come on board your ship,\" he said, \"will you be kind enough to tell me where you are going?\"","Imagine my surprise! The man was close to death, and he wanted to know our direction. Only when I told him we were sailing north did he agree to come on board. He was terribly thin, and his arms and legs were nearly frozen. For two days he could not speak. Later, when my lieutenant asked why he had come so far across the ice, his face grew dark. \"To find someone who ran away from me,\" he said.","Over the following days I came to love this stranger. He is gentle and wise, but deeply unhappy. One evening I told him I would give anything, even my life, for the knowledge I was seeking. A look of pain crossed his face. \"Unhappy man!\" he cried. \"Do you share my madness? Then listen to my story, and learn from it.\" His name was Victor Frankenstein. Every night I have written down his words as closely as I can. This is his story.","## A Childhood by the Lake","My family is one of the most respected in Geneva. My father, Alphonse Frankenstein, married late. My mother, Caroline, was the daughter of a poor friend of his; my father had found her weeping beside her father's coffin and had taken care of her. I, their first child, was born while they were travelling in Italy.","When I was about five, we stayed beside Lake Como. My mother often visited the poor, and in one small cottage she found a farmer's wife feeding five hungry children. Four were dark-eyed and strong, but the fifth was a thin little girl with golden hair and clear blue eyes. She was not the farmer's child. Her mother had died, and her father, an Italian nobleman who had fought for his country's freedom, had disappeared. The girl's name was Elizabeth Lavenza, and my parents took her home with them.","The evening before, my mother had said playfully, \"I have a pretty present for my Victor. He will have it tomorrow.\" When she gave me Elizabeth, I took her words seriously, as children do. Elizabeth was mine to protect and to love. We called each other \"cousin\", although we were not related by blood. She was more than a sister to me.","We grew up between Geneva and a house on the shore of the lake, with the mountains all around us. Elizabeth loved the beauty of the world; I wanted to know its causes. My best friend was Henry Clerval, a merchant's son who loved stories of knights and adventure. Later two brothers were born, Ernest and little William. No child ever had a happier home.","But I had a dangerous hunger for secrets. At thirteen I discovered the books of the old alchemists, and I dreamed of banishing disease from the human body. At fifteen I watched a violent storm come over the mountains. A stream of fire burst from an old oak tree near our house, and when the light vanished, only a blasted stump remained. A visitor explained to us the power of electricity, and I was filled with wonder.","When I was seventeen, my parents decided to send me to the university of Ingolstadt in Germany. Before I could leave, Elizabeth caught scarlet fever. My mother nursed her, and although Elizabeth recovered, my mother caught the fever herself. On her deathbed she joined our hands. \"My children,\" she said, \"my firmest hopes of future happiness were placed on your marriage. Elizabeth, my love, you must take my place for the younger children.\" A few weeks later I left for Ingolstadt.","## The Secret of Life","The first professor I visited, M. Krempe, was a short, rough man who laughed at my old books. \"Every minute you have wasted on those books is utterly lost,\" he told me. But a few days later I heard a lecture by M. Waldman, a gentle man with the sweetest voice I had ever heard. \"The old masters promised impossible things and achieved nothing,\" he said. \"Modern scientists promise very little, yet they have performed real miracles. They can even command the thunder of heaven.\"","As he spoke, one thought filled my mind. So much has been done, but I will achieve far more. I will explore unknown powers and show the world the deepest secrets of creation.","From that day, chemistry and natural philosophy became my whole life, and Waldman became my friend and guide. In two years I had made discoveries that were admired by the whole university. Then one question took hold of me: where does life come from? To understand life, I knew, I must first study death. I spent days and nights in vaults and graveyards, watching how the human body decays.","Then, after many exhausting nights, a sudden light broke over me. I discovered the cause of life itself. More than that, I became able to give life to lifeless matter. I will not tell you the secret, my friend. You can see what it has done to me. Learn from my example how dangerous knowledge can be.","I decided to build a human being. Because the smallest parts were so difficult to work with, I made him enormous, about eight feet tall. I collected bones from burial houses and took materials from the dissecting room and the slaughterhouse. I worked alone in a room at the top of the house. My cheeks grew pale and my body thin. I dreamed that a new race of beings would bless me as their creator.","Seasons came and went, and I did not notice them. I stopped writing to my family. Every night I had a slight fever, and the fall of a leaf made me jump like a criminal. But I could not stop.","## A Dreary Night in November","It was on a dreary night in November that I saw the result of my work. It was one o'clock in the morning; the rain was beating against the windows, and my candle had nearly burned out. I gathered the instruments of life around me, so that I could put a spark of life into the lifeless thing that lay at my feet. By the dying light I saw the dull yellow eye of the creature open. It breathed hard, and its arms and legs began to move.","I had chosen his features to be beautiful. Beautiful! His yellow skin hardly covered the muscles beneath it. His hair was black and flowing, his teeth as white as pearls, but these only made his watery eyes and straight black lips more horrible. I had worked for nearly two years for this moment. Now the dream vanished, and my heart filled with horror and disgust. I rushed out of the room.","At last I fell asleep in my clothes, and I dreamed of Elizabeth. I kissed her, but her lips turned the colour of death, and suddenly I was holding the body of my dead mother. I woke in a cold sweat. In the yellow moonlight I saw the creature standing by my bed. He had lifted the curtain and was staring at me. A grin wrinkled his cheeks, and one hand reached out, perhaps to stop me, but I escaped down the stairs. I spent the rest of the night in the courtyard, shaking at every sound.","In the morning I walked the wet streets for hours, afraid to go home. Outside an inn a coach stopped, and out stepped Henry Clerval! My father had finally allowed him to come and study at Ingolstadt. When we reached my rooms, I went in first, terrified that the monster would still be there. The rooms were empty. He had gone.","My relief turned into wild laughter, and then I collapsed. A fever held me for several months, and Henry nursed me through the whole winter. In my fever I talked endlessly about the monster, but Henry believed it was the illness speaking. When spring came, I slowly recovered, though the word chemistry made me tremble. Little by little, with Henry's help, I began to feel like the happy young man I had once been.","## Death in Geneva","Then a letter came from my father. \"Victor,\" he wrote, \"William is dead! That sweet child has been murdered.\" One warm evening in May, the family had gone for a walk at Plainpalais. William had run ahead to hide and could not be found. Early the next morning my father found him lying on the grass, dead, with the marks of the murderer's fingers on his neck. That evening William had been wearing a small portrait of my mother, and the portrait was gone.","It was dark when I reached Geneva, and the city gates were already shut, so I went to see the place where William had died. A great storm broke over the mountains, and lightning lit up the lake like a sheet of fire. Then, in one bright flash, I saw a figure among the trees, gigantic and more hideous than any human being. It was the creature I had made. At once I knew: he had murdered my brother. Before I could move, he climbed the steep rocks of Mont Salève with impossible speed and disappeared.","Who would believe my story? People would think I was mad. So I said nothing.","At home I learned that someone had already been arrested: Justine Moritz, a gentle girl who had lived with our family for years and loved William like a mother. The portrait of my mother had been discovered in her pocket. \"But she is innocent!\" I cried. Elizabeth believed in her too.","At the trial Justine explained that she had spent the night searching for William and had slept in a barn when the gates were closed. She could not explain how the portrait had reached her pocket. Elizabeth spoke bravely for her, but the evidence was against her, and worse, Justine had confessed. When we visited her in prison, she told us the truth. \"I confessed a lie,\" she wept. \"My priest threatened me with hell until I almost believed I was the monster he said I was.\"","The next day Justine was executed. Two innocent people had died because of me, and I had said nothing. My guilt sat in my chest like a stone that I could not lift.","## The Sea of Ice","I could not sleep or eat. At last I set out alone for the valley of Chamonix, hoping that the great mountains would calm me. Early one morning I climbed Montanvert and walked down onto the glacier that people call the Sea of Ice. Its surface rose and fell like the waves of a stormy sea, and above it all stood Mont Blanc.","Suddenly I saw a figure crossing the ice towards me with superhuman speed, jumping over the deep cracks that I had stepped around so carefully. It was the creature. I trembled with rage. \"Devil!\" I shouted. \"How dare you come near me? I wish I could destroy you and bring back the people you have murdered!\"","\"I expected this,\" he said calmly. \"All people hate the miserable. Yet you, my creator, hate me, your own creature. Do your duty towards me, and I will do mine towards you and the rest of mankind. If you refuse, I will kill until death is satisfied with the blood of your remaining friends.\"","I threw myself at him, but he moved away easily. \"Be calm,\" he said. \"You made me stronger than yourself, but I will not fight you. Remember that I am your creature. I ought to be your Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel. Everywhere I see happiness, and I alone am shut out from it. I was kind and good; misery made me a devil. Make me happy, and I will be good again.\"","\"Go away!\" I said. \"We are enemies.\"","\"Listen to my story,\" he begged. \"Even criminals are allowed to speak in their own defence. Then, if you can, destroy the work of your hands.\" He led me across the ice to a hut on the mountain and lit a fire. For the first time I wondered what a creator owes to his creature. I sat down, and he began his tale.","## What the Creature Saw","I remember very little of my first days. Light, sound and smell came to me all at once. I left your rooms and found shelter in a forest near Ingolstadt. I ate berries and drank from streams. I was cold and hungry, and I sat down and cried. Then the gentle moon rose over the trees, and I felt a kind of wonder.","One day I found a fire that some travellers had left behind. I put my hand into it and cried out with pain. How strange, I thought, that the same thing could give such warmth and such pain! When food became scarce, I walked to a village, but people screamed when they saw me. Some ran away; others threw stones until I escaped, hurt and frightened. That night I found a low hovel built against the wall of a cottage. Through a crack in the wall I could see into the house. I lived there for many months.","Three people lived in the cottage: an old blind man who played sweetly on the guitar, a young man named Felix and his sister Agatha. They were poor, but they treated each other with such love that I wept as I watched them. When I realised that I was making them poorer by stealing their food, I stopped. At night I cut wood for their fire and left it at their door. They began to speak of a \"good spirit\" who helped them, and my heart filled with joy.","Slowly I learned their words: fire, milk, bread, wood. Then one day I saw my reflection in a pool. At first I jumped back, unable to believe that the monster in the water was me. When I understood, I was filled with the bitterest sadness.","In the spring a beautiful stranger arrived. She was Safie, the daughter of a Turkish merchant, and Felix loved her. Felix had once helped her father escape from a French prison, and that was why the family had lost everything. Safie did not speak their language, so Felix began to teach her. Hidden behind my wall, I learned with her, and I learned faster. Soon I could speak and read. And I asked myself: what am I? I had no money, no friends and no family. No mother had ever smiled at me.","Later I found a leather bag in the woods with three books inside. One of them, Paradise Lost, moved me deeply. Like Adam, I had been made by a creator. But Adam had come from God's hands happy and protected, while I was alone. More often I felt like Satan, the fallen angel, burning with envy at the happiness of others.","Then, in the pocket of the coat I had taken from your laboratory, I found your journal of the four months before my creation. I read every disgusting detail of how I was made. \"Cursed creator!\" I cried. \"Why did you make a monster so ugly that even you turned away from me in disgust?\" Yet I still had hope. The cottagers were good. If I could win their friendship before they saw my face, perhaps they would accept me.","## The Door of the Cottage","I chose a day when the young people had gone for a walk and the old man sat alone. I knocked at the door. \"Come in,\" said De Lacey, for that was his name. I told him I was a traveller who needed rest by the fire. His kindness gave me courage. I told him that I had good friends nearby who might think I was a monster when they saw me. \"I am blind and cannot judge your face,\" he said, \"but your words sound honest. Perhaps I can help.\"","At that moment I heard footsteps outside. I seized the old man's hand. \"Now is the time!\" I cried. \"Save me and protect me! You and your family are the friends I am looking for!\"","\"Great God!\" he cried. \"Who are you?\"","The door opened, and Felix, Safie and Agatha came in. Agatha fainted, and Safie ran out of the house. Felix tore me away from his father, threw me to the ground and struck me violently with a stick. I could have torn him to pieces as a lion tears an antelope, but my heart sank inside me and I did not fight back. I escaped to my hovel.","Two days later the family left the cottage for ever. That night something broke inside me. I danced around the empty cottage with a burning branch and watched the flames destroy it.","Where could I go? I knew from your papers that you came from Geneva, so I travelled there, walking only at night through the long, bitter winter. One spring morning I saw a young girl slip from a river bank into the fast water. I jumped in and pulled her out. But a man ran up, tore her from my arms and, when I followed, shot me. That was my reward for saving a human life. Lying in pain, I swore eternal hatred against all mankind.","Weeks later, in a field outside Geneva, a beautiful little boy came running towards me. I thought: this child is too young to hate ugliness. I can teach him to be my friend. I caught him, but he covered his eyes and screamed. \"Monster! Let me go, or I'll tell my papa. My papa is M. Frankenstein. He will punish you!\"","Frankenstein! The son of my enemy! I took him by the throat to silence him, and in a moment he lay dead at my feet. I felt a wild, cruel joy. I too can create misery, I thought. Around his neck I found a portrait of a lovely woman. Then, in a barn, I found a young woman asleep in the straw. She would never smile at me, I thought, so let her suffer. I put the portrait into a fold of her dress and ran away.","\"I am alone and miserable,\" the creature said at last. \"No human being will live with me. But a woman as ugly as I am would not turn away from me. You must create a companion for me, a female of my own kind. That is my demand.\"","## Work on a Lonely Island","At first I refused angrily. He answered quietly. \"Make me this companion, and we will go to the wild places of South America. We will live on acorns and berries, and no one will ever see us again. But if you refuse, you will curse the hour you were born.\" His words moved me. Did I not owe him a little happiness? At last I gave him my promise. \"I will watch your progress,\" he said, and he ran down the mountain faster than an eagle flies.","At home I could not bring myself to begin. My father asked whether I still wished to marry Elizabeth. I said yes, with all my heart, but first I wanted to travel to England to learn what I needed for the work. Henry came with me. We visited London, Oxford and finally Scotland. There I left Henry with friends and went alone to one of the most distant of the Orkney Islands, a rocky place with only a few poor huts.","I began the work again. The first time I had been blinded by enthusiasm; now I saw clearly what I was doing, and it made me sick. One evening terrible thoughts came to me. What if the female was even more evil than he was? What if they hated each other? And if they had children, a whole race of devils might spread over the earth. Did I have the right to buy my own peace at such a price?","I looked up. In the moonlight, the creature was at the window, watching me with a ghastly grin. He had followed me all the way. Trembling with passion, I tore to pieces the thing I was making. He gave a howl of despair and disappeared.","Hours later he came through my door. \"Do you dare to break your promise?\" he said. \"I have followed you through cold and hunger. You are my creator, but I am your master. Obey!\"","\"Never,\" I answered. \"I will never make another like you.\"","\"Shall every man find a wife, and every beast have its mate, and I be alone?\" he cried. \"You can destroy my other hopes, but revenge remains. Remember this: I will be with you on your wedding night.\" Then he was gone, and I saw his boat shoot across the dark water.","## The Body on the Shore","So he would kill me on my wedding night, I thought. Not once did I imagine that he meant someone else.","Before dawn I put the remains of my work into a basket with heavy stones, rowed out to sea and threw it into the water. Then, exhausted, I fell asleep in the boat. When I woke, a strong wind was driving me far from land, and for hours I was at the mercy of the waves. In the evening I reached a small town on the coast of Ireland.","A crowd stood watching me land. \"You must come to Mr. Kirwin,\" a man told me, \"and give an account of yourself.\" Mr. Kirwin was the magistrate. A young man had been found dead on the beach the night before, with black marks of fingers on his neck, and fishermen had seen a boat like mine nearby.","They took me to see the body. It was Henry Clerval. My dearest friend, so full of life and joy, lay cold before me. \"Have my evil plans taken your life too, my dearest Henry?\" I cried. Then I fell into a violent fever.","For two months I lay in prison, close to death. Mr. Kirwin was kind to me, and he wrote to my father, who came at once. It was proved that I had been on the Orkney Islands when Henry was killed, and I was set free. On the journey home I said to my father wildly, \"I am the murderer of William, of Justine and of Henry.\" He thought my illness had disturbed my mind.","In Geneva, Elizabeth welcomed me with tears. I thought of the creature's threat. If he came to fight me, I would fight him to the death. If I won, I would be free to be happy with her. So we fixed the wedding for ten days later, and from then on I carried pistols and a dagger at all times.","## The Wedding Night","The day of our wedding was bright, and for a few hours I was almost happy. After the ceremony we crossed the lake by boat to spend our first night at Evian. When we reached the inn, night fell and a heavy storm came down. A thousand fears rose in my heart. I kept one hand on the pistol hidden in my coat.","Elizabeth watched me in frightened silence. \"What is it, my dear Victor?\" she asked at last. \"What are you afraid of?\"","\"Oh, peace, peace, my love,\" I replied. \"After this night all will be safe. But this night is dreadful, very dreadful.\"","I did not want her to see the fight I was expecting, so I begged her to go to bed. I walked up and down the passages of the inn, searching every corner. I found nothing, and I was beginning to hope, when I heard a terrible scream from the room where Elizabeth had gone.","At that moment the whole truth rushed into my mind. I ran into the room. Elizabeth lay across the bed, lifeless, her face pale and half covered by her hair. The marks of the creature's hands were on her neck.","I looked up. The shutters had been thrown open, and in the pale yellow light of the moon I saw a hideous figure at the window. The creature was grinning, and with one finger he pointed at the body of my wife. I fired my pistol, but he jumped from the window and plunged into the lake.","Men searched the water with boats and nets, but they found nothing. I returned to Geneva with the terrible news. My father, who had loved Elizabeth like a daughter, could not bear this last grief. A few days later he died in my arms.","For many months, I was told, I was kept in a cell as a madman. When I came back to my senses, I had only one thought left: revenge. I told a magistrate the whole story, but he did not truly believe me, and he said it would be impossible to catch such a creature. So I decided to hunt him myself.","Before I left Geneva, I went at night to the cemetery where William, Elizabeth and my father lay. I knelt on the grass and swore to follow the devil who had caused this misery until he or I died. A loud, terrible laugh answered me out of the darkness. Then a voice I knew whispered close to my ear: \"I am satisfied, miserable wretch! You have decided to live, and I am satisfied.\"","## The Edge of the World","For months I followed him: along the river Rhone to the Mediterranean, onto a ship bound for the Black Sea, across the wild lands of Tartary and Russia. Sometimes he left messages cut into trees or stones, because he was afraid that I would lose track of him, give up and die. \"My reign is not over yet,\" one said. \"Follow me to the everlasting ice of the north, where you will feel the misery of cold and frost, which cannot hurt me. Come on, my enemy.\"","At the edge of the frozen ocean I bought a sledge and dogs, and I began to gain on him. One day I saw him less than a mile ahead. But just then the ice broke with a sound like thunder, and the sea rose between us. I was left drifting on a piece of ice while my dogs died around me. Then your ship appeared. That is why I asked where you were going. If you had been sailing south, I would have stayed on the ice.","\"Walton,\" he said when his story was finished, \"if I die, swear to me that he will not escape. Swear that you will find him and kill him.\"","Margaret, I have now heard the whole of this terrible story. When I asked him how he had made the creature, he refused to tell me. \"Are you mad?\" he said. \"Do you want to create an enemy for yourself and the whole world?\"","Our own situation is now desperate. Mountains of ice surround the ship and threaten to crush it, and several of my men have died of the cold. My sailors came and demanded that, if the ice opened, I should turn back south at once. Victor, although he was very weak, raised himself and spoke to them with sudden fire. \"Did you not call this a glorious expedition? It was glorious because it was full of danger. Be men, or be more than men!\" His words moved them, but in the end I agreed to return. I am coming back ignorant and disappointed.","When the ice broke and the way south opened, the sailors shouted with joy. Victor tried to rise from his bed. \"You may give up your purpose,\" he said, \"but I will not.\" But he was too weak, and he fell back. Later he called me to him. \"I created a living, thinking creature, and I owed him happiness,\" he said. \"But I owed more to my own kind, so I was right to refuse him a companion. Farewell, Walton. Seek happiness in a quiet life and avoid ambition, even the ambition of making discoveries in science. Yet why do I say this? I have failed, but another may succeed.\" Half an hour later he pressed my hand weakly, and his eyes closed for ever.","At midnight I heard a voice from the cabin where his body lay. I went in. Over the coffin bent a gigantic figure, his face hidden by long, ragged hair. When he heard me, he sprang towards the window. I had never seen anything so horrible as his face. I called to him to stay.","\"That is also my victim!\" he cried, looking down at Victor. \"Oh, Frankenstein! What use is it now to ask you to forgive me, when I destroyed you by destroying everything you loved? He is cold. He cannot answer me.\"","\"It is too late to be sorry,\" I said. \"If you had listened to your conscience before, he would still be alive.\"","\"Do you think I felt no pain?\" he answered. \"My heart was made for love, and when misery turned it to hatred, I suffered a torture that you cannot imagine. You hate me, but you cannot hate me as much as I hate myself. Do not fear that I will do any more harm. I will go to the most northern point of the earth. There I will build my funeral fire and burn this miserable body to ashes, so that no one can ever make another like me. I shall die, and I will no longer feel these agonies. Farewell!\"","He jumped from the cabin window onto the ice raft that lay close to the ship. He was soon carried away by the waves and lost in darkness and distance."],"glossary":{"creature":"yaratık; burada Victor'un yapıp hayat verdiği varlık","cousin":"kuzen; burada kan bağı olmayan, evlatlık Elizabeth'e verilen sevgi sözcüğü","spark":"kıvılcım; burada cansız bedene verilen 'hayat kıvılcımı'","companion":"yoldaş; burada yaratığın istediği eş","mate":"eş (hayvanlar için de kullanılır)","wretch":"zavallı, sefil yaratık (aşağılayıcı hitap)","philosophy":"felsefe; 'natural philosophy' eski dilde doğa bilimleri (fizik, kimya)","raft":"sal; burada denizde yüzen büyük buz parçası (ice raft)","master":"efendi, hükmeden kişi"},"phrases":[{"en":"little by little","tr":"azar azar, yavaş yavaş"},{"en":"lose track of","tr":"izini kaybetmek"},{"en":"at the mercy of","tr":"-in insafına kalmış"},{"en":"give an account of yourself","tr":"kendini açıklamak, hesap vermek"},{"en":"bring myself to","tr":"(bir şeyi yapmaya) kendini razı edebilmek, elinden gelmek"},{"en":"give up","tr":"vazgeçmek, bırakmak"},{"en":"break your promise","tr":"sözünü tutmamak, sözünden dönmek"},{"en":"gain on","tr":"(kovalarken) arayı kapatmak, yetişmeye başlamak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the half-frozen stranger ask Walton before he agrees to come on board?","options":["Whether there is a doctor on the ship","Who the captain of the ship is","Where the ship is going","Whether the sailors have seen a giant"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Victor gemiye binmeden önce geminin nereye gittiğini sorar; çünkü güneye gidiyorsa buzda kalıp yaratığı kovalamaya devam edecektir."},{"q":"How did Elizabeth become part of the Frankenstein family?","options":["Victor's parents adopted her after finding her in a poor farmer's cottage","She was the daughter of Victor's uncle","She came to work for the family as a servant","Her father, an old friend, left her in their care before he died in Geneva"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Victor'un annesi, Como Gölü yakınında yoksul bir çiftçinin evinde Elizabeth'i bulur ve aile onu evlat edinir; Victor'la kan bağı yoktur."},{"q":"How does the creature learn to speak and read?","options":["Victor teaches him in the laboratory","An old man in a village school teaches him","He studies Victor's journal from the first day","He secretly listens while Felix teaches Safie"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Yaratık, kulübenin duvarındaki çatlaktan izlerken Felix'in Safie'ye verdiği dersleri dinleyerek konuşmayı ve okumayı öğrenir."},{"q":"Why does Victor destroy the female creature on the Orkney Islands?","options":["Henry Clerval discovers his secret work","He fears that the two creatures might bring more evil and suffering into the world","The materials he needs are stolen","Elizabeth writes and asks him to stop"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Victor, dişi yaratığın daha kötü olabileceğinden ve ikisinin çocuklarının insanlığı tehdit edebileceğinden korkar; pencerede yaratığı görünce yaptığı şeyi parçalar."},{"q":"What does the creature say he will do at the end of the story?","options":["Return to Geneva and live near Victor's grave","Find the De Lacey family again","Go to the far north and burn his own body","Stay on Walton's ship and sail to England"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Yaratık, dünyanın en kuzey noktasına gidip kendi cenaze ateşini yakacağını ve bedenini kül edeceğini söyler, sonra buz salıyla karanlıkta kaybolur."}],"words":5183,"length":"long"},{"id":"love-in-the-practical-details","type":"article","title":"Love in the Practical Details","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"B2","genre":"romance","summaryTr":"Romantik anlatılarda yakınlık yalnızca büyük itiraflarla kurulmaz. Bu yazı; dikkat, birlikte çalışma, sınırlar ve günlük kararların bir aşk öyküsünü nasıl inandırıcı kıldığını özgün örneklerle inceliyor.","paragraphs":["## Attention before attraction","A romantic story may begin with an impressive entrance, but it often becomes convincing through something much smaller. One character notices that another person always chooses the chair facing the door. Later, that chair is left empty for them. The action suggests attention without requiring a speech about attraction. Yet a detail like this only matters if the story gives it a particular meaning. Perhaps the person is waiting for a late sister. Perhaps they dislike having people move behind them. The reader needs enough context to understand what has been noticed and why remembering it changes the relationship.","Imagine two fictional characters, Iona and Max, who organise a small neighbourhood film night. Iona manages the programme; Max repairs the borrowed projector. At their first meeting, he gives her coffee with sugar because everybody else takes it that way. She quietly leaves it untouched. At the next meeting, he asks how she likes her coffee. This is a modest change, but it does useful work. He moves from making an assumption to asking a question. Attraction begins to appear as curiosity about a particular person, rather than a general desire to be admired by someone attractive.","The difference is important when reading acts of care. A character can perform generosity without paying much attention to its receiver. Suppose Max arrives with an expensive new projector after Iona explains that the event must remain cheap and easy to repeat. His gift may solve tonight's technical problem while creating a larger problem for her project. If the narrative treats the gift as automatically romantic, it asks us to ignore her stated purpose. A more interesting scene lets her appreciate his effort and explain why the solution does not fit. His response then tells us more than the price of the gift.","Attention also needs limits. Knowing somebody's preferences does not mean knowing every part of their life. Max might remember Iona's coffee and still misunderstand why she refuses a weekend trip. A convincing romance leaves space for that uncertainty. When characters ask instead of guessing, their closeness can grow through an answer that surprises them. When they guess wrongly, the mistake can reveal a habit they need to change. The useful reading question is not simply whether one character notices another. It is whether attention makes room for a person who can disagree, change, and remain partly unknown.","## A shared task creates meaningful choices","A shared task gives romantic characters reasons to meet and opportunities to reveal how they behave. It also places their attraction inside a wider world. Iona and Max cannot spend every scene discussing their feelings because the film rights need checking, the seats need arranging, and the projector still overheats. These practical concerns do not have to distract from the romance. They can carry it. A disagreement about where to place the screen may show that Iona thinks first about people with limited movement, while Max thinks first about sound. Both concerns are reasonable, and combining them requires conversation.","The task becomes especially useful when it creates a choice with a cost. If Max helps only when he has nothing else to do, his helpfulness tells us relatively little. Suppose he has promised to finish a paid repair on the same evening that Iona needs equipment moved. He cannot responsibly do both. The scene should not require him to abandon his customer as proof of love. He might explain the conflict early and arrange another way to transport the equipment. Reliability can be romantic because it makes his promise believable. Uncontrolled sacrifice may be dramatic, but it can also create future resentment.","A shared project also allows competence to become attractive. Iona might be drawn to Max because he explains a technical problem without making her feel foolish. Max might admire the way she changes the seating plan after listening to a worried parent. These are visible behaviours, not labels supplied by a narrator. They give the reader evidence for the characters' interest. Compare 'He admired her kindness' with a scene in which she makes a difficult adjustment for someone who cannot repay her. The second version lets admiration develop from something both Max and the reader have witnessed.","However, the project should not become a machine that produces endless convenient meetings. If every broken cable places the couple alone in a beautiful room, the pattern starts to feel arranged for them. Other people should have needs, skills, and schedules of their own. Perhaps a volunteer fixes the problem before Max arrives, leaving him with no reason to stay. He must then choose to invite Iona for a walk directly. That small change matters. Their practical partnership has brought them together, but a romantic relationship begins when they acknowledge that they also want time together without a task to justify it.","## Conflict should reveal a real difference","Romantic tension becomes thin when a problem exists only because two reasonable people refuse to say one simple sentence. Silence can be believable, but it needs a reason connected to character and circumstance. Iona might hesitate to describe a job offer because she has previously been made to feel selfish for leaving home. Max might avoid asking about it because he believes questions will sound like pressure. Each thinks they are protecting the other. Their silence then grows from understandable habits. The eventual conversation must address those habits, rather than merely reveal the job's start date.","A real difference gives the conversation something to resolve. Iona may want to move to another city for a year. Max may want to keep his workshop in the neighbourhood. Neither desire needs to be wrong. If the story immediately makes the distant job unpleasant, it removes Iona's meaningful choice. If the workshop suddenly fails, it removes Max's. A stronger version allows both opportunities to remain valuable. The characters must decide what kind of relationship they can attempt under those conditions. The difficulty comes from having two worthwhile lives, not from failing to recognise a villain.","Notice the difference between a compromise and a surrender. If Iona gives up the job because Max looks sad, the plot may reach a happy scene while leaving her ambition unresolved. If Max closes his workshop overnight, the same problem appears in reverse. A compromise takes both sets of needs into account and makes its costs visible. They could try visits for a fixed period, divide travel according to income, and agree to discuss the arrangement again. Such details may sound unromantic in isolation. Within the story, they can show that each person takes the other's future seriously.","Conflict also gives apologies a purpose. 'I am sorry' is a beginning, but the reader needs to understand what changes afterward. If Max has repeatedly answered difficult questions with silence, his growth might appear when he starts a conversation before Iona asks. If Iona tends to decide what other people feel, she might learn to leave a question open long enough to hear the answer. These actions need not be perfect. A believable improvement can be partial and awkward. What matters is a visible connection between the hurt, the apology, and a different choice when a similar situation returns.","## Small objects can gather emotional meaning","Objects offer a compact way to show the history of a relationship. At first, a roll of yellow tape in Max's toolbox is simply useful for marking wires. During the film project, Iona borrows it to label the seats. Later, she uses a piece to mark the date of his visit on her calendar. The object has not become magical. Its meaning changes because the reader has seen it pass through different situations. It connects practical cooperation with an emotional expectation. The final appearance gains force from the earlier ordinary uses, which prevent it from feeling like a symbol placed there only for the ending.","Repetition alone does not create this effect. If the yellow tape appears in every scene accompanied by a reminder that it represents love, the reader has little left to discover. Each return should add or alter something. Perhaps Max finds the roll empty and assumes Iona has taken it without asking. Their first argument concerns a cheap object but reveals different attitudes towards shared belongings. Later, she replaces it and writes her name on her own roll. The detail can carry affection and a boundary at the same time. Closeness does not require every possession or preference to become common property.","An object can also expose the gap between intention and experience. Max might build Iona a beautiful wooden box for her programmes, only to discover that it is too heavy for her bicycle. The scene could become a joke at his expense, but it can do more. Iona can explain the problem without rejecting the care behind the gift. Max can change the design without treating criticism as rejection. The revised box then records a process of listening. Its emotional value comes partly from being altered to fit the person who will use it.","When reading a recurring object, ask what would disappear if it were removed. If the answer is only a decorative image, the object may be adding atmosphere rather than developing the relationship. That is not necessarily a fault; atmosphere has its own value. But if removing the box also removes an apology, a misunderstanding, and a shared joke, it has become part of the story's structure. The reader remembers those earlier scenes when it returns. A simple object can therefore hold several kinds of feeling without forcing a character to explain them all aloud.","## An ending can promise a practice","A romantic ending does not have to prove that the characters will never face another difficulty. It needs to resolve the central uncertainty the story has created. If the question has been whether Iona and Max can speak honestly about different futures, a final kiss alone will not answer it. The ending should show them making a choice with that difference in view. A kiss may belong in the scene, but so might a train ticket, a clear goodbye, or a question asked without fear. The emotional result follows from the way they now deal with uncertainty together.","One possible ending brings Max to Iona's new city. She has drawn a route from the station to her flat, but roadworks block part of it. They stop, compare options, and choose another street. This would be a weak ending if the story had never cared about planning or disagreement. After a narrative built around shared tasks and competing needs, however, the small adjustment can show an established change. They do not need a perfect route to continue. They need enough trust to notice a problem, name it, and decide together what to do next.","Another ending might keep them apart for a while. Iona sends a photograph of the first film event she has organised in the new city. Max replies with a specific question about something visible in the picture. He is interested in the life she is building, not merely waiting for her return. This is a smaller promise than a wedding, but it can still be emotionally complete if it answers the story's concern. The form of commitment should grow from these characters and their circumstances, rather than from a fixed rule about which final event counts as happiness.","To read romance closely, follow the movement from attraction towards a repeated way of caring. Look at who asks, who listens, whose work is recognised, and whose future remains possible. Notice whether generosity fits its receiver, whether apologies alter behaviour, and whether a shared object carries the marks of earlier choices. Grand declarations can be moving, but their force depends on the life around them. In the imagined story of Iona and Max, love becomes credible through coffee, cables, a heavy box, and a difficult conversation. The ordinary details matter because two particular people learn how to respond to them together."],"glossary":{"competence":"bir işi iyi yapabilme becerisi","resentment":"içerleme, birikmiş kırgınlık","surrender":"kendi isteklerinden tamamen vazgeçme","recurring":"tekrar ortaya çıkan","receiver":"ilgi veya hediyeyi alan kişi","rights":"bir filmi gösterme hakları"},"phrases":[{"en":"paying much attention","tr":"çok dikkat etmek"},{"en":"makes room for","tr":"yer açar"},{"en":"under those conditions","tr":"bu koşullar altında"},{"en":"takes both sets of needs into account","tr":"iki tarafın ihtiyaçlarını da hesaba katar"},{"en":"at the same time","tr":"aynı zamanda"},{"en":"in isolation","tr":"bağlamından ayrı olarak"},{"en":"gives up","tr":"vazgeçer"},{"en":"in view","tr":"göz önünde tutarak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is asking about Iona's coffee preference meaningful?","options":["It proves Max can afford expensive gifts","It shows Max replacing an assumption with curiosity","It removes every future disagreement","It allows Max to avoid speaking about the project"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Max herkesin aynı şeyi istediğini varsaymak yerine Iona'nın tercihine dikkat eder."},{"q":"What can a shared task reveal in a romance?","options":["Only the characters' professional qualifications","That romantic characters need no lives outside the relationship","How people make choices and respond to others' needs","That practical problems are always easy"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Ortak iş, karakterlerin seçimlerini ve diğer insanların ihtiyaçlarına tepkilerini görünür kılar."},{"q":"What makes the job and workshop conflict meaningful?","options":["Both characters want something valuable","One character is clearly a villain","Neither character understands the travel timetable","The job is certain to disappear"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"İki kişinin de değerli bir yaşam planı vardır; gerilim bir tarafın haksız olmasına dayanmaz."},{"q":"How can a recurring object gain emotional value?","options":["By being described as expensive every time","By appearing without any connection to action","By replacing all dialogue","By connecting different choices and scenes in the relationship"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Nesne önceki kararları, yanlış anlaşılmaları ve yakınlaşmaları bir araya getirdiğinde anlam kazanır."},{"q":"What should the ending resolve, according to the article?","options":["Every possible future problem","The central uncertainty created by the story","The lives of every minor character","The exact cost of every gift"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Son, gelecekteki tüm sorunları değil, öykünün kurduğu temel belirsizliği çözmelidir."}],"words":1972,"length":"medium"},{"id":"man-against-the-wilderness","type":"article","title":"Man Against the Wilderness: The Survival Story","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"B2","genre":"adventure","summaryTr":"Yolculuk, tehlike ve hayatta kalma hikâyeleri neden bizi bu kadar çekiyor? Yazı, Odysseia'dan Jack London'ın 'To Build a Fire' öyküsüne uzanarak insan ile doğa arasındaki çatışmayı inceliyor.","paragraphs":["A man walks alone along a frozen river. A more experienced traveller once told him that nobody should make such a journey without a companion, but he scorned the idea. His thoughts are practical, his confidence is unshakable, and the temperature is far lower than anything he has known. We do not need to be told how this story will end; we sense it from the first paragraph. Yet we keep reading. Why do stories of danger and survival hold us so firmly, and what do they reveal about the way we see ourselves?","## The oldest journey","Adventure is arguably the oldest storytelling genre. Homer's Odyssey, composed in ancient Greece, follows Odysseus on his long and dangerous voyage home from the Trojan War, a journey that takes ten years. The structure is simple and powerful: a hero leaves a safe place, meets a series of dangers, and either returns changed or does not return at all. The American scholar Joseph Campbell argued in The Hero with a Thousand Faces (1949) that myths from many cultures share this pattern of departure, trial and return, which he called the monomyth. Not every scholar accepts such a sweeping claim, but it helps to explain why adventure stories feel familiar even when we meet them for the first time.","In English, the modern adventure story is often traced to Daniel Defoe's Robinson Crusoe (1719), in which a shipwrecked man must build a life from almost nothing. In the nineteenth century, writers such as Jules Verne, with Around the World in Eighty Days (1872), and Robert Louis Stevenson, with Treasure Island (1883), turned the form into popular entertainment full of pirates, treasure and rapid travel. In these books the world is a place to be explored and conquered, and the hero's courage and ingenuity are usually enough to win.","## When the enemy is indifferent","Jack London's \"To Build a Fire\" (1908) takes the same raw materials and reaches a very different conclusion. London knew the north personally: he travelled to the Klondike during the gold rush of 1897 and 1898, and the experience supplied him with settings and themes for much of his best-known fiction. In the story, a man and his dog travel along a frozen trail in the Yukon on an extremely cold day. The man is confident but inexperienced, and he is quick to dismiss the advice of an old-timer who warned him about the dangers of travelling alone. He has no name, which suggests that he could be anyone.","London's approach to this material changed over time. In 1902 he published an earlier, gentler version of the story in a magazine for young readers. When he returned to the idea in 1908, he made it far harsher, and it is the harsher version that has become famous. The revision suggests that he wanted his readers to feel the full weight of the situation, and to understand that courage alone does not guarantee survival.","The real antagonist of the story is not a villain but the cold itself, which has no intentions, no malice and no mercy. Critics often place London in the school of literary naturalism, a movement influenced by Darwin's theory of evolution that presented human beings as creatures shaped by environment and chance rather than by destiny or divine care. In this vision, nature does not punish arrogance; it simply continues to follow its own laws, and the man's mistakes receive no forgiveness. The dog, which lacks the man's intelligence but possesses instinct, reads the danger more accurately than he does. This contrast is the story's quiet argument: knowledge can be less valuable than humility.","## How tension is built","Survival writers rely on techniques that are easy to recognise once they have been named. First, they establish clear stakes: if the fire fails, the man dies. Secondly, they present problems one at a time, with each solution creating new complications. Thirdly, they use concrete physical detail, so that the reader can almost feel the numb fingers, the wet boots and the shrinking supply of matches. London's sentences are often plain and repetitive, imitating the monotony of the trail and the narrowing of the man's attention. Finally, they exploit dramatic irony, since we see the danger more clearly than the character does. Every small decision, such as where to build a fire, becomes a test in which a minor error produces a major consequence.","## Why we read about danger from a warm room","The enjoyment of survival stories is not as strange as it first appears. Psychologists suggest that fiction can work as a kind of simulator: by imagining ourselves in a crisis, we rehearse our responses without any risk. We learn, in a small way, what we might do when the stakes are high, and we discover how quickly confidence can disappear. There is also an emotional element. Stories of endurance remind us how much we depend on others, on knowledge and on preparation, and by contrast they make an ordinary, comfortable evening feel valuable.","Another attraction is the moral clarity of such stories. In everyday life our problems are tangled and our goals often unclear, but in a survival narrative the aim is brutally simple: stay alive. Every action either helps or harms, and the reader can judge each choice at once. This simplicity can be strangely restful, even when the events are terrible.","Finally, adventure stories dramatise one of the most familiar classifications of conflict taught in schools: person against person, person against society, person against self and person against nature. The last type is perhaps the most humbling. Opponents can be negotiated with, defeated or deceived; weather and distance cannot. Whereas Stevenson's young hero returns with treasure, London's reader is left with a different kind of knowledge, the sober understanding that human skill has limits. Both kinds of story are valuable. One feeds our hope, the other our respect, and together they describe the full range of what it means to venture into the unknown."],"glossary":{"monomyth":"tekmit; kahramanın ayrılış, sınanma ve dönüş yolculuğu kalıbı","naturalism":"doğalcılık; insanı çevre ve rastlantının ürünü gören edebi akım","indifferent":"kayıtsız, umursamaz","old-timer":"bölgede uzun yıllar yaşamış, deneyimli kişi","klondike":"Klondike; Kanada'daki altın arama bölgesi","antagonist":"karşıt karakter, kahramanın karşısındaki güç","ingenuity":"yaratıcılık, bulucu zekâ"},"phrases":[{"en":"rely on","tr":"-e güvenmek, -e dayanmak"},{"en":"by contrast","tr":"buna karşılık, tersine"},{"en":"the stakes are high","tr":"riskler yüksek, kaybedilecek şey büyük"},{"en":"shaped by","tr":"-in biçimlendirdiği, -den etkilenmiş"},{"en":"venture into the unknown","tr":"bilinmeyene doğru atılmak"},{"en":"dismiss the advice of","tr":"-in tavsiyesini hiçe saymak"},{"en":"raw materials","tr":"ham maddeler, temel malzemeler"},{"en":"a test in which","tr":"... bir sınav niteliğinde olan durum"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Joseph Campbell's 'monomyth' describe?","options":["A story told by a single narrator","A pattern of departure, trial and return found in myths from many cultures","A myth that has only one possible ending","A story in which nature is the hero"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Campbell'ın monomit kavramı, birçok kültürün mitlerinde görülen ayrılış, sınanma ve dönüş kalıbını anlatır."},{"q":"Why does the article call the cold the 'real antagonist' of \"To Build a Fire\"?","options":["Because the man has made many enemies in the Yukon","Because the cold is controlled by another character","Because it acts without intention, malice or mercy","Because the cold appears only at the end of the story"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Doğa, naturalizm anlayışında kayıtsızdır: kötü niyeti ya da merhameti yoktur, yalnızca kendi yasalarına göre davranır."},{"q":"What does the dog's behaviour suggest, according to the article?","options":["Animals are more dangerous than the weather","Humility and instinct can be more valuable than confident knowledge","The man should have trained the dog better","The dog is the true hero of every adventure story"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Köpek, adamdan daha az zekâya ama daha çok içgüdüye sahiptir ve tehlikeyi daha doğru okur; yani bilgi, alçakgönüllülükten daha değerli olmayabilir."},{"q":"How, according to some psychologists, do survival stories benefit readers?","options":["They allow readers to rehearse responses to a crisis without real risk","They teach readers the exact skills needed to survive in the Arctic","They convince readers that nature is always friendly","They replace the need for real travel"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Metne göre kurgu bir simülatör gibi çalışır: krizde ne yapabileceğimizi risk almadan zihnimizde prova ederiz."}],"words":971,"length":"short"},{"id":"no-obstacle-no-story","type":"article","title":"No Obstacle, No Story: How Love Stories Build Tension","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"B2","genre":"romance","summaryTr":"Aşk hikâyeleri neden engellere ihtiyaç duyar? Yazı; dış ve iç engelleri, dramatik ironiyi ve O. Henry'nin 'The Gift of the Magi' öyküsündeki sürpriz sonu inceliyor.","paragraphs":["Imagine a love story in which two people meet, fall in love and live happily ever after, all on the first page. It would be pleasant for the characters and unbearably dull for the reader. This is the paradox at the heart of romance fiction: readers want love to triumph, but they want it to be difficult first. Obstacles are not an unfortunate interruption of the genre; they are its engine.","## Obstacles from outside","The simplest barriers are external. Families disagree, social classes divide, wars separate, or rules forbid. Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet, written in the mid-1590s, is the classic example: the lovers belong to feuding families, and every attempt to solve the problem makes it worse. Jane Austen's Pride and Prejudice (1813) uses a subtler kind of pressure. Money, inheritance and the rules of marriage matter enormously in a society where a woman's future depended heavily on whom she married. External obstacles are effective because they can be understood at once; the reader immediately sees what the lovers are up against.","## Obstacles from within","Yet the most memorable romances usually add an internal obstacle. Elizabeth Bennet and Mr Darcy are not kept apart only by social differences. They are also separated by their own faults: his pride and her prejudice, the two failings that give the novel its title. Because the barrier is inside the characters, the plot can be resolved only when they change. Love becomes a kind of education, and the happy ending feels earned instead of accidental.","Misunderstanding is a related device. A letter does not arrive, a remark is overheard, or a character assumes the worst. Modern readers sometimes complain that such misunderstandings are artificial, and it is true that a careless writer can use them as a lazy trick. Used well, however, they reveal something true about relationships: we rarely see another person's heart directly; we guess it from signals that can easily be misread.","## The reader knows more","Romance also exploits dramatic irony, the situation in which the audience knows something that a character does not. When we understand that two people care for each other but neither dares to say so, every polite conversation becomes charged with meaning. We want to shout at the page. This feeling of helpless involvement is exactly what keeps us turning the pages, and the writer controls it by deciding how much to reveal, and when.","Pacing matters too. Most love stories follow a recognisable shape: a meeting, a growing attraction, a complication, a crisis and a resolution. The crisis usually arrives at the moment when the relationship seems to be lost for good, a low point that writers often call the dark moment. It is the most painful part of the book to read, yet it is also the part that gives the ending its force, because the happiness that follows has been bought at a price.","## The twist at the end","Some love stories do not end in the way readers expect. The American writer O. Henry, the pen name of William Sydney Porter (1862 to 1910), became famous for stories that finish with a sudden reversal, so famous that critics still speak of an \"O. Henry ending\". His best-known example, \"The Gift of the Magi\" (1905), is a Christmas Eve love story set in a small, shabby flat. Readers who have not yet read it may wish to do so before continuing, because the next paragraph reveals how it works.","A young husband and wife, Jim and Della, each want to buy a Christmas present for the other, but they have almost no money. Della sells her long hair to buy a chain for Jim's watch; meanwhile, Jim sells his watch to buy combs for her hair. Each sacrifices the possession he or she values most, and each gift is made useless by the other's sacrifice. On the surface, the outcome is a joke at the lovers' expense. In reality, it is a proof of love, because the loss itself demonstrates what each would give up for the other.","Aristotle used two words that describe this kind of ending well: peripeteia, a sudden reversal of fortune, and anagnorisis, a moment of recognition. In O. Henry's story the two coincide. The reversal shows both characters, and the reader, what they truly value. A successful twist needs three qualities. It must surprise us, it must seem inevitable in retrospect, and it must change how we understand the earlier events. The details of the hair and the watch are introduced at the very beginning, so the ending feels fair rather than arbitrary.","## Why the happy ending still matters","Critics sometimes dismiss romance as predictable. They are partly right: the genre offers a promise that love will, in some sense, succeed. But predictability is not the same thing as poverty of imagination. Like a sonnet or a sonata, romance works within a known form and gives pleasure through variation. The reader knows where the story is going and enjoys discovering how it will get there, and what price the characters will pay on the way.","There is also an emotional reason for its popularity. In a world where relationships are uncertain, romance fiction lets us practise hope. The best examples teach a quiet lesson: that love is measured less by grand declarations than by what a person is willing to give up. When the final page is turned, we are left with a mixture of amusement, tenderness and a small ache, which is exactly what the genre has been promising from the start."],"glossary":{"peripeteia":"ani talih değişimi, olayların beklenmedik biçimde tersine dönmesi","anagnorisis":"tanıma, gerçeği fark etme anı","dramatic irony":"dramatik ironi; okurun karakterden fazlasını bilmesi","feuding":"birbirine düşman olan, kan davalı","twist":"sürpriz dönüş, beklenmedik son","shabby":"köhne, eski püskü"},"phrases":[{"en":"up against","tr":"karşı karşıya olmak, -le mücadele etmek zorunda olmak"},{"en":"in retrospect","tr":"geriye dönüp bakınca"},{"en":"on the surface","tr":"görünüşte, yüzeyde"},{"en":"in reality","tr":"gerçekte"},{"en":"reversal of fortune","tr":"talih dönüşü, işlerin beklenmedik biçimde tersine dönmesi"},{"en":"at the lovers' expense","tr":"âşıkların aleyhine, âşıkların pahasına"},{"en":"feels earned","tr":"hak edilmiş gibi hissettirir"},{"en":"a mixture of","tr":"bir karışımı, karışık bir duygu"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the article's main point about obstacles in romance fiction?","options":["They make stories too sad for most readers","They are a weakness that skilled authors avoid","They drive the plot and make the final success meaningful","They should always come from society, never from the characters"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Yazara göre engeller türün motorudur; aşkın zorlukla kazanılması hikâyeyi hem ilginç hem anlamlı kılar."},{"q":"Why do internal obstacles often make a romance more memorable?","options":["The plot can be resolved only when the characters change","They are easier for the reader to understand immediately","They remove the need for a happy ending","They allow the author to avoid misunderstandings"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Engel karakterlerin içindeyse hikâye ancak onlar değiştiğinde çözülür; bu yüzden mutlu son hak edilmiş gibi hissettirir."},{"q":"What makes the ending of \"The Gift of the Magi\" feel fair rather than arbitrary?","options":["The narrator explains the ending in the first paragraph","Jim and Della are rich at the end of the story","A new character appears to solve the problem","The important details about the hair and the watch are introduced early"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Saç ve saat ayrıntıları hikâyenin başında verildiği için sürpriz son keyfi değil, adil görünür."},{"q":"Which statement best reflects the author's view of predictable endings in romance?","options":["Predictability proves that the genre lacks imagination","The pleasure comes from variation within a known form","Only twist endings can be considered good literature","Readers prefer sad endings to happy ones"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Yazar, romansı bir soneye benzetir: biçim bilinir, ancak zevk o biçim içindeki çeşitlemelerden gelir."}],"words":902,"length":"short"},{"id":"why-fables-endure","type":"article","title":"Small Animals, Large Truths: Why Fables Never Grow Old","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"B2","genre":"fable","summaryTr":"Konuşan hayvanlar neden binlerce yıldır dinleniyor? Yazı; Ezop'tan Kelile ve Dimne'ye ve Nasreddin Hoca fıkralarına uzanarak masalın kültürler arasında nasıl yolculuk ettiğini anlatıyor.","paragraphs":["A fox looks at a bunch of grapes hanging just out of reach, decides that they are probably sour, and walks away. This tiny scene has given English a whole expression, \"sour grapes\", which we still use for people who pretend that they never wanted what they could not get. Few literary forms are as short as the fable, and none has travelled as far.","## Animals with human faces","A fable is a brief story, usually with animals as its characters, that ends with a moral lesson. The animals behave as people do. They flatter, bargain, boast and betray, but their traits are simple enough to remember: the fox is clever, the lion is powerful, the donkey is foolish and the ant is hardworking. Because the characters are types rather than individuals, the listener does not need any background knowledge to understand them. A fable can therefore be told in a minute and remembered for a lifetime.","The roles are more stable than the animals who play them. In one tradition the cunning trickster is a fox, in another a jackal or a hare, and in a third a small spider or tortoise, but the function of the character remains the same. This explains why a fable can cross a border so easily. The audience needs only to find a local animal that matches the part, and the story is at home in its new country, with its lesson intact.","There is also a political reason for the animal mask. Throughout history, fables have allowed people who could not speak openly to say difficult things safely. Criticising a ruler directly may be dangerous, but criticising a vain lion surrounded by flattering courtiers is merely a story. The mask gives the storyteller protection and gives the audience a pleasant chance to recognise the real target for themselves.","## A genre without a single author","The name most closely associated with the form is Aesop, who, according to ancient tradition, was a slave in Greece around the sixth century BC. Historians cannot confirm the details of his life, and some doubt that he was a single historical author at all. What we call Aesop's fables is a collection that grew over centuries, as storytellers added their own examples to a famous name. Later, the Roman writer Phaedrus put such stories into Latin verse in the first century AD, and the Frenchman Jean de La Fontaine, whose first collection appeared in 1668, gave them new wit for readers at the court of Louis XIV.","Independent streams of fables flowed elsewhere. In India the Panchatantra, a collection of animal tales framed as lessons in statecraft for young princes, began to take shape in Sanskrit more than a thousand years ago. A Persian version, made in the sixth century, was in turn rendered into Arabic in the eighth century by Ibn al-Muqaffa, who gave the book the title Kalila wa Dimna after two jackal advisers at the heart of its stories. Through further translations the book travelled into Greek, Hebrew, Latin and many other languages, and Ottoman writers later produced Turkish versions. When we read it today, we notice that the tales are often less about general moral rules than about political survival: how to win a ruler's ear, whom to trust, and what flattery really costs.","## The Hodja's cousins","Turkish readers will recognise a related spirit in the anecdotes of Nasreddin Hodja, the wise fool traditionally linked with the town of Akşehir in the thirteenth century. His stories are not animal fables, but they share the fable's economy and its moral intelligence: a very short narrative, a surprising turn, and a lesson that the audience must often work out for itself. Consider the Hodja who searches the street for a lost key in the light outside his house, although he dropped it indoors, because it is brighter out there. The story is funny, but it is also a compact diagnosis of how people look where it is convenient rather than where the answer lies.","The same tales travelled across the Middle East, the Balkans and Central Asia under different names, such as Mullah Nasruddin, which suggests that the wise-fool figure can speak to something universal. Whether the hero is a fox, a jackal or a Hodja on a donkey, the method is identical. Take a familiar human weakness, shrink it to a few sentences, and let the listener laugh before the lesson arrives.","## Is the moral a gift or a cage?","Traditional fables state their moral at the end, which makes them ideal for teaching children. Yet an explicit moral can also be limiting. Does the story of the grapes teach us that people look down on what they cannot have, or that it is wise to move on from impossible goals? Readers have found different lessons at different times, and sometimes the moral attached to a fable seems oddly unrelated to the tale that precedes it. This openness is part of the reason the genre has survived. Because the characters are so simple, readers of each generation can read their own concerns into them.","## Fables in the modern world","Modern writers continue to use the form. George Orwell's Animal Farm (1945), which he subtitled A Fairy Story, uses a farm and its pigs to show how a revolution can turn into a new tyranny. Business books retell the story of the tortoise and the hare as a lesson about persistence, and teachers still reach for the boy who cried wolf when they want to explain trust. The reason is practical: abstract arguments are easily forgotten, but a story about a fox and a crow stays in the mind. A good fable is like a seed, small enough to carry anywhere, and it grows differently in every soil."],"glossary":{"moral":"ahlaki ders, hikâyenin verdiği öğüt","fable":"fabl, hayvanların konuştuğu ve ders veren kısa hikâye","jackal":"çakal","statecraft":"devlet yönetimi sanatı","wise fool":"bilge budala; aptal görünen ama aslında akıllı olan tip","anecdote":"fıkra, kısa ve ilginç olay anlatısı","flattery":"dalkavukluk, pohpohlama","courtier":"saray adamı, saraylı"},"phrases":[{"en":"sour grapes","tr":"ulaşamadığı şeyi küçümseme (\"koruk\" tavrı)"},{"en":"work out for itself","tr":"kendi başına çözmek, anlamak"},{"en":"in turn","tr":"sırayla, bunun üzerine de"},{"en":"take shape","tr":"şekillenmek, biçim almak"},{"en":"according to ancient tradition","tr":"eski geleneğe göre"},{"en":"read their own concerns into","tr":"kendi kaygılarını ... içine yüklemek"},{"en":"rather than","tr":"-den ziyade, yerine"},{"en":"speak to something universal","tr":"evrensel bir şeye seslenmek"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a fable be told in a minute and remembered for a lifetime?","options":["Its characters are simple types, so no background knowledge is needed","It always uses real historical figures","It is written in a very difficult poetic style","It avoids having a lesson at the end"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Karakterler birey değil tiptir (tilki kurnaz, aslan güçlü); bu yüzden dinleyicinin önceden bir şey bilmesi gerekmez ve hikâye kolay akılda kalır."},{"q":"According to the article, why have fables been useful to people without power?","options":["They allowed rulers to give orders in a gentle way","They were easier to write than other kinds of literature","The animal mask let storytellers criticise the powerful more safely","They were always written by kings"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Hayvan maskesi, anlatıcıyı korur: Bir hükümdarı doğrudan eleştirmek tehlikeli olabilir, ama kibirli bir aslanı anlatmak yalnızca bir hikâyedir."},{"q":"What does the article say about the journey of Kalila wa Dimna?","options":["It was written first in Arabic and never translated","It came from the Indian Panchatantra through Persian and Arabic into many other languages","It was invented by Aesop and brought to India by Greek traders","It is a Turkish book that was later translated into Sanskrit"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Hint kökenli Panchatantra, Farsçaya, oradan sekizinci yüzyılda Arapçaya (Kelile ve Dimne) ve sonra birçok dile çevrildi."},{"q":"What does the story of the Hodja and the lost key illustrate?","options":["Wise people never lose their belongings","Neighbours should always help each other","Light is more important than truth","People often search where it is convenient instead of where the answer is"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Hoca anahtarı evde düşürdüğü hâlde dışarıda aydınlık olduğu için orada arar; bu, insanların kolay olan yerde aramasının kısa bir tanısıdır."}],"words":937,"length":"short"},{"id":"gift-of-the-magi","type":"story","title":"The Gift of the Magi","author":"O. Henry","year":1905,"level":"B2","genre":"romance","source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #7256","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7256","license":"Public domain"},"summaryTr":"Noel'e bir gün kala elinde sadece bir dolar seksen yedi sent bulunan Della, kocası Jim'e layık bir hediye alabilmek için çok zor bir karar verir. O. Henry'nin sevgi ve fedakârlık üzerine yazdığı ünlü Noel öyküsü.","paragraphs":["One dollar and eighty-seven cents. That was all. And sixty cents of it was in pennies. Pennies saved one and two at a time by bulldozing the grocer and the vegetable man and the butcher until one’s cheeks burned with the silent imputation of parsimony that such close dealing implied. Three times Della counted it. One dollar and eighty-seven cents. And the next day would be Christmas.","There was clearly nothing to do but flop down on the shabby little couch and howl. So Della did it. Which instigates the moral reflection that life is made up of sobs, sniffles, and smiles, with sniffles predominating.","While the mistress of the home is gradually subsiding from the first stage to the second, take a look at the home. A furnished flat at $8 per week. It did not exactly beggar description, but it certainly had that word on the lookout for the mendicancy squad.","In the vestibule below was a letter-box into which no letter would go, and an electric button from which no mortal finger could coax a ring. Also appertaining thereunto was a card bearing the name “Mr. James Dillingham Young.”","The “Dillingham” had been flung to the breeze during a former period of prosperity when its possessor was being paid $30 per week. Now, when the income was shrunk to $20, though, they were thinking seriously of contracting to a modest and unassuming D. But whenever Mr. James Dillingham Young came home and reached his flat above he was called “Jim” and greatly hugged by Mrs. James Dillingham Young, already introduced to you as Della. Which is all very good.","Della finished her cry and attended to her cheeks with the powder rag. She stood by the window and looked out dully at a gray cat walking a gray fence in a gray backyard. Tomorrow would be Christmas Day, and she had only $1.87 with which to buy Jim a present. She had been saving every penny she could for months, with this result. Twenty dollars a week doesn’t go far. Expenses had been greater than she had calculated. They always are. Only $1.87 to buy a present for Jim. Her Jim. Many a happy hour she had spent planning for something nice for him. Something fine and rare and sterling—something just a little bit near to being worthy of the honor of being owned by Jim.","There was a pier glass between the windows of the room. Perhaps you have seen a pier glass in an $8 flat. A very thin and very agile person may, by observing his reflection in a rapid sequence of longitudinal strips, obtain a fairly accurate conception of his looks. Della, being slender, had mastered the art.","Suddenly she whirled from the window and stood before the glass. Her eyes were shining brilliantly, but her face had lost its color within twenty seconds. Rapidly she pulled down her hair and let it fall to its full length.","Now, there were two possessions of the James Dillingham Youngs in which they both took a mighty pride. One was Jim’s gold watch that had been his father’s and his grandfather’s. The other was Della’s hair. Had the queen of Sheba lived in the flat across the airshaft, Della would have let her hair hang out the window some day to dry just to depreciate Her Majesty’s jewels and gifts. Had King Solomon been the janitor, with all his treasures piled up in the basement, Jim would have pulled out his watch every time he passed, just to see him pluck at his beard from envy.","So now Della’s beautiful hair fell about her rippling and shining like a cascade of brown waters. It reached below her knee and made itself almost a garment for her. And then she did it up again nervously and quickly. Once she faltered for a minute and stood still while a tear or two splashed on the worn red carpet.","On went her old brown jacket; on went her old brown hat. With a whirl of skirts and with the brilliant sparkle still in her eyes, she fluttered out the door and down the stairs to the street.","Where she stopped the sign read: “Mme. Sofronie. Hair Goods of All Kinds.” One flight up Della ran, and collected herself, panting. Madame, large, too white, chilly, hardly looked the “Sofronie.”","“Will you buy my hair?” asked Della.","“I buy hair,” said Madame. “Take yer hat off and let’s have a sight at the looks of it.”","Down rippled the brown cascade.","“Twenty dollars,” said Madame, lifting the mass with a practised hand.","“Give it to me quick,” said Della.","Oh, and the next two hours tripped by on rosy wings. Forget the hashed metaphor. She was ransacking the stores for Jim’s present.","She found it at last. It surely had been made for Jim and no one else. There was no other like it in any of the stores, and she had turned all of them inside out. It was a platinum fob chain simple and chaste in design, properly proclaiming its value by substance alone and not by meretricious ornamentation—as all good things should do. It was even worthy of The Watch. As soon as she saw it she knew that it must be Jim’s. It was like him. Quietness and value—the description applied to both. Twenty-one dollars they took from her for it, and she hurried home with the 87 cents. With that chain on his watch Jim might be properly anxious about the time in any company. Grand as the watch was, he sometimes looked at it on the sly on account of the old leather strap that he used in place of a chain.","When Della reached home her intoxication gave way a little to prudence and reason. She got out her curling irons and lighted the gas and went to work repairing the ravages made by generosity added to love. Which is always a tremendous task, dear friends—a mammoth task.","Within forty minutes her head was covered with tiny, close-lying curls that made her look wonderfully like a truant schoolboy. She looked at her reflection in the mirror long, carefully, and critically.","“If Jim doesn’t kill me,” she said to herself, “before he takes a second look at me, he’ll say I look like a Coney Island chorus girl. But what could I do—oh! what could I do with a dollar and eighty-seven cents?”","At 7 o’clock the coffee was made and the frying-pan was on the back of the stove hot and ready to cook the chops.","Jim was never late. Della doubled the fob chain in her hand and sat on the corner of the table near the door that he always entered. Then she heard his step on the stair away down on the first flight, and she turned white for just a moment. She had a habit of saying a little silent prayer about the simplest everyday things, and now she whispered: “Please God, make him think I am still pretty.”","The door opened and Jim stepped in and closed it. He looked thin and very serious. Poor fellow, he was only twenty-two—and to be burdened with a family! He needed a new overcoat and he was without gloves.","Jim stopped inside the door, as immovable as a setter at the scent of quail. His eyes were fixed upon Della, and there was an expression in them that she could not read, and it terrified her. It was not anger, nor surprise, nor disapproval, nor horror, nor any of the sentiments that she had been prepared for. He simply stared at her fixedly with that peculiar expression on his face.","Della wriggled off the table and went for him.","“Jim, darling,” she cried, “don’t look at me that way. I had my hair cut off and sold because I couldn’t have lived through Christmas without giving you a present. It’ll grow out again—you won’t mind, will you? I just had to do it. My hair grows awfully fast. Say ‘Merry Christmas!’ Jim, and let’s be happy. You don’t know what a nice—what a beautiful, nice gift I’ve got for you.”","“You’ve cut off your hair?” asked Jim, laboriously, as if he had not arrived at that patent fact yet even after the hardest mental labor.","“Cut it off and sold it,” said Della. “Don’t you like me just as well, anyhow? I’m me without my hair, ain’t I?”","Jim looked about the room curiously.","“You say your hair is gone?” he said, with an air almost of idiocy.","“You needn’t look for it,” said Della. “It’s sold, I tell you—sold and gone, too. It’s Christmas Eve, boy. Be good to me, for it went for you. Maybe the hairs of my head were numbered,” she went on with sudden serious sweetness, “but nobody could ever count my love for you. Shall I put the chops on, Jim?”","Out of his trance Jim seemed quickly to wake. He enfolded his Della. For ten seconds let us regard with discreet scrutiny some inconsequential object in the other direction. Eight dollars a week or a million a year—what is the difference? A mathematician or a wit would give you the wrong answer. The magi brought valuable gifts, but that was not among them. This dark assertion will be illuminated later on.","Jim drew a package from his overcoat pocket and threw it upon the table.","“Don’t make any mistake, Dell,” he said, “about me. I don’t think there’s anything in the way of a haircut or a shave or a shampoo that could make me like my girl any less. But if you’ll unwrap that package you may see why you had me going a while at first.”","White fingers and nimble tore at the string and paper. And then an ecstatic scream of joy; and then, alas! a quick feminine change to hysterical tears and wails, necessitating the immediate employment of all the comforting powers of the lord of the flat.","For there lay The Combs—the set of combs, side and back, that Della had worshipped long in a Broadway window. Beautiful combs, pure tortoise shell, with jewelled rims—just the shade to wear in the beautiful vanished hair. They were expensive combs, she knew, and her heart had simply craved and yearned over them without the least hope of possession. And now, they were hers, but the tresses that should have adorned the coveted adornments were gone.","But she hugged them to her bosom, and at length she was able to look up with dim eyes and a smile and say: “My hair grows so fast, Jim!”","And then Della leaped up like a little singed cat and cried, “Oh, oh!”","Jim had not yet seen his beautiful present. She held it out to him eagerly upon her open palm. The dull precious metal seemed to flash with a reflection of her bright and ardent spirit.","“Isn’t it a dandy, Jim? I hunted all over town to find it. You’ll have to look at the time a hundred times a day now. Give me your watch. I want to see how it looks on it.”","Instead of obeying, Jim tumbled down on the couch and put his hands under the back of his head and smiled.","“Dell,” said he, “let’s put our Christmas presents away and keep ’em a while. They’re too nice to use just at present. I sold the watch to get the money to buy your combs. And now suppose you put the chops on.”","The magi, as you know, were wise men—wonderfully wise men—who brought gifts to the Babe in the manger. They invented the art of giving Christmas presents. Being wise, their gifts were no doubt wise ones, possibly bearing the privilege of exchange in case of duplication. And here I have lamely related to you the uneventful chronicle of two foolish children in a flat who most unwisely sacrificed for each other the greatest treasures of their house. But in a last word to the wise of these days let it be said that of all who give gifts these two were the wisest. Of all who give and receive gifts, such as they are wisest. Everywhere they are wisest. They are the magi."],"glossary":{"flat":"daire, apartman dairesi","fob":"köstek (cep saatinin zinciri)","mistress":"evin hanımı (ev sahibesi)","mendicancy":"dilencilik","imputation":"suçlama, ima","parsimony":"cimrilik, aşırı tutumluluk","bulldozing":"zorla ikna etmek, ezip geçmek","vestibule":"apartman girişi, giriş holü","janitor":"kapıcı","sterling":"gerçek, öz, saf (değerli)","chaste":"sade, gösterişsiz","meretricious":"gösterişli ama değersiz","tresses":"uzun saçlar","truant":"okuldan kaçan","airshaft":"binanın iç havalandırma boşluğu","chops":"pirzola","dandy":"harika, şahane","magi":"Doğu bilgeleri (İsa'ya hediye getiren üç bilge)"},"phrases":[{"en":"flop down","tr":"kendini yığılırcasına bırakmak (oturmak/uzanmak)"},{"en":"beggar description","tr":"tarife sığmamak, anlatılamayacak kadar (kötü/güzel) olmak"},{"en":"on the sly","tr":"gizlice, sinsice"},{"en":"turn inside out","tr":"ters yüz etmek, altüst edip aramak"},{"en":"go far","tr":"(para) yetmek, işe yaramak"},{"en":"many a","tr":"birçok (tekil isimle: 'birçok mutlu saat')"},{"en":"give way to","tr":"yerini bırakmak, yenik düşmek"},{"en":"at length","tr":"sonunda, uzun bir süre sonra"},{"en":"put away","tr":"kaldırmak, bir kenara koymak"},{"en":"in the way of","tr":"... türünden, ... konusunda"}],"quiz":[{"q":"How much money does Della have at the beginning of the story?","options":["Twenty dollars","One dollar and eighty-seven cents","Eight dollars","Twenty-one dollars"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Öykünün ilk cümlesi: Della'nın elinde yalnızca bir dolar seksen yedi sent vardır."},{"q":"How does Della get money to buy a present for Jim?","options":["She asks Jim's boss for money","She sells her gold watch","She sells her long hair","She borrows from a friend"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Della uzun, güzel saçlarını Madame Sofronie'ye yirmi dolara satar."},{"q":"What does Della buy for Jim?","options":["A platinum chain for his watch","A new leather strap","A warm overcoat","A pair of gloves"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Della, Jim'in babasından kalma altın saati için platin bir zincir alır."},{"q":"Why can Della not use Jim's present at first?","options":["The combs are too small","The combs are broken","She does not like tortoise shell","She has cut off her hair"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Jim ona saç için takma tarak almıştır, ama Della saçlarını kestirip satmıştır."},{"q":"Why can Jim not use Della's present?","options":["He has lost his watch","He has sold his watch to buy the combs","He gave the watch to his father","The chain is too short"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Jim, Della'ya tarak alabilmek için saatini satmıştır; zincirini takacağı saati yoktur."}],"words":2071,"length":"medium"},{"id":"the-monkeys-paw","type":"story","title":"The Monkey's Paw","author":"W. W. Jacobs","year":1902,"level":"B2","genre":"horror","source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #12122","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12122","license":"Public domain"},"summaryTr":"Rüzgârlı bir gecede White ailesini ziyaret eden eski bir asker, Hindistan'dan getirdiği kurumuş bir maymun pençesinin sahibine üç dilek hakkı verdiğini anlatır. Aile bunu bir oyun sanır, ama kaderle oynamanın bedeli beklenenden çok daha ağır olabilir.","paragraphs":["## I","Without, the night was cold and wet, but in the small parlour of Laburnam Villa the blinds were drawn and the fire burned brightly. Father and son were at chess, the former, who possessed ideas about the game involving radical changes, putting his king into such sharp and unnecessary perils that it even provoked comment from the white-haired old lady knitting placidly by the fire.","“Hark at the wind,” said Mr. White, who, having seen a fatal mistake after it was too late, was amiably desirous of preventing his son from seeing it.","“I’m listening,” said the latter, grimly surveying the board as he stretched out his hand. “Check.”","“I should hardly think that he’d come to-night,” said his father, with his hand poised over the board.","“Mate,” replied the son.","“That’s the worst of living so far out,” bawled Mr. White, with sudden and unlooked-for violence; “of all the beastly, slushy, out-of-the-way places to live in, this is the worst. Pathway’s a bog, and the road’s a torrent. I don’t know what people are thinking about. I suppose because only two houses in the road are let, they think it doesn’t matter.”","“Never mind, dear,” said his wife, soothingly; “perhaps you’ll win the next one.”","Mr. White looked up sharply, just in time to intercept a knowing glance between mother and son. The words died away on his lips, and he hid a guilty grin in his thin grey beard.","“There he is,” said Herbert White, as the gate banged to loudly and heavy footsteps came toward the door.","The old man rose with hospitable haste, and opening the door, was heard condoling with the new arrival. The new arrival also condoled with himself, so that Mrs. White said, “Tut, tut!” and coughed gently as her husband entered the room, followed by a tall, burly man, beady of eye and rubicund of visage.","“Sergeant-Major Morris,” he said, introducing him.","The sergeant-major shook hands, and taking the proffered seat by the fire, watched contentedly while his host got out whiskey and tumblers and stood a small copper kettle on the fire.","At the third glass his eyes got brighter, and he began to talk, the little family circle regarding with eager interest this visitor from distant parts, as he squared his broad shoulders in the chair and spoke of wild scenes and doughty deeds; of wars and plagues and strange peoples.","“Twenty-one years of it,” said Mr. White, nodding at his wife and son. “When he went away he was a slip of a youth in the warehouse. Now look at him.”","“He don’t look to have taken much harm,” said Mrs. White, politely.","“I’d like to go to India myself,” said the old man, “just to look round a bit, you know.”","“Better where you are,” said the sergeant-major, shaking his head. He put down the empty glass, and sighing softly, shook it again.","“I should like to see those old temples and fakirs and jugglers,” said the old man. “What was that you started telling me the other day about a monkey’s paw or something, Morris?”","“Nothing,” said the soldier, hastily. “Leastways nothing worth hearing.”","“Monkey’s paw?” said Mrs. White, curiously.","“Well, it’s just a bit of what you might call magic, perhaps,” said the sergeant-major, offhandedly.","His three listeners leaned forward eagerly. The visitor absent-mindedly put his empty glass to his lips and then set it down again. His host filled it for him.","“To look at,” said the sergeant-major, fumbling in his pocket, “it’s just an ordinary little paw, dried to a mummy.”","He took something out of his pocket and proffered it. Mrs. White drew back with a grimace, but her son, taking it, examined it curiously.","“And what is there special about it?” inquired Mr. White as he took it from his son, and having examined it, placed it upon the table.","“It had a spell put on it by an old fakir,” said the sergeant-major, “a very holy man. He wanted to show that fate ruled people’s lives, and that those who interfered with it did so to their sorrow. He put a spell on it so that three separate men could each have three wishes from it.”","His manner was so impressive that his hearers were conscious that their light laughter jarred somewhat.","“Well, why don’t you have three, sir?” said Herbert White, cleverly.","The soldier regarded him in the way that middle age is wont to regard presumptuous youth. “I have,” he said, quietly, and his blotchy face whitened.","“And did you really have the three wishes granted?” asked Mrs. White.","“I did,” said the sergeant-major, and his glass tapped against his strong teeth.","“And has anybody else wished?” persisted the old lady.","“The first man had his three wishes. Yes,” was the reply; “I don’t know what the first two were, but the third was for death. That’s how I got the paw.”","His tones were so grave that a hush fell upon the group.","“If you’ve had your three wishes, it’s no good to you now, then, Morris,” said the old man at last. “What do you keep it for?”","The soldier shook his head. “Fancy, I suppose,” he said, slowly. “I did have some idea of selling it, but I don’t think I will. It has caused enough mischief already. Besides, people won’t buy. They think it’s a fairy tale; some of them, and those who do think anything of it want to try it first and pay me afterward.”","“If you could have another three wishes,” said the old man, eyeing him keenly, “would you have them?”","“I don’t know,” said the other. “I don’t know.”","He took the paw, and dangling it between his forefinger and thumb, suddenly threw it upon the fire. White, with a slight cry, stooped down and snatched it off.","“Better let it burn,” said the soldier, solemnly.","“If you don’t want it, Morris,” said the other, “give it to me.”","“I won’t,” said his friend, doggedly. “I threw it on the fire. If you keep it, don’t blame me for what happens. Pitch it on the fire again like a sensible man.”","The other shook his head and examined his new possession closely. “How do you do it?” he inquired.","“Hold it up in your right hand and wish aloud,” said the sergeant-major, “but I warn you of the consequences.”","“Sounds like the Arabian Nights,” said Mrs. White, as she rose and began to set the supper. “Don’t you think you might wish for four pairs of hands for me?”","Her husband drew the talisman from pocket, and then all three burst into laughter as the sergeant-major, with a look of alarm on his face, caught him by the arm.","“If you must wish,” he said, gruffly, “wish for something sensible.”","Mr. White dropped it back in his pocket, and placing chairs, motioned his friend to the table. In the business of supper the talisman was partly forgotten, and afterward the three sat listening in an enthralled fashion to a second instalment of the soldier’s adventures in India.","“If the tale about the monkey’s paw is not more truthful than those he has been telling us,” said Herbert, as the door closed behind their guest, just in time for him to catch the last train, “we sha’nt make much out of it.”","“Did you give him anything for it, father?” inquired Mrs. White, regarding her husband closely.","“A trifle,” said he, colouring slightly. “He didn’t want it, but I made him take it. And he pressed me again to throw it away.”","“Likely,” said Herbert, with pretended horror. “Why, we’re going to be rich, and famous and happy. Wish to be an emperor, father, to begin with; then you can’t be henpecked.”","He darted round the table, pursued by the maligned Mrs. White armed with an antimacassar.","Mr. White took the paw from his pocket and eyed it dubiously. “I don’t know what to wish for, and that’s a fact,” he said, slowly. “It seems to me I’ve got all I want.”","“If you only cleared the house, you’d be quite happy, wouldn’t you?” said Herbert, with his hand on his shoulder. “Well, wish for two hundred pounds, then; that ’ll just do it.”","His father, smiling shamefacedly at his own credulity, held up the talisman, as his son, with a solemn face, somewhat marred by a wink at his mother, sat down at the piano and struck a few impressive chords.","“I wish for two hundred pounds,” said the old man distinctly.","A fine crash from the piano greeted the words, interrupted by a shuddering cry from the old man. His wife and son ran toward him.","“It moved,” he cried, with a glance of disgust at the object as it lay on the floor.","“As I wished, it twisted in my hand like a snake.”","“Well, I don’t see the money,” said his son as he picked it up and placed it on the table, “and I bet I never shall.”","“It must have been your fancy, father,” said his wife, regarding him anxiously.","He shook his head. “Never mind, though; there’s no harm done, but it gave me a shock all the same.”","They sat down by the fire again while the two men finished their pipes. Outside, the wind was higher than ever, and the old man started nervously at the sound of a door banging upstairs. A silence unusual and depressing settled upon all three, which lasted until the old couple rose to retire for the night.","“I expect you’ll find the cash tied up in a big bag in the middle of your bed,” said Herbert, as he bade them good-night, “and something horrible squatting up on top of the wardrobe watching you as you pocket your ill-gotten gains.”","He sat alone in the darkness, gazing at the dying fire, and seeing faces in it. The last face was so horrible and so simian that he gazed at it in amazement. It got so vivid that, with a little uneasy laugh, he felt on the table for a glass containing a little water to throw over it. His hand grasped the monkey’s paw, and with a little shiver he wiped his hand on his coat and went up to bed.","## II","In the brightness of the wintry sun next morning as it streamed over the breakfast table he laughed at his fears. There was an air of prosaic wholesomeness about the room which it had lacked on the previous night, and the dirty, shrivelled little paw was pitched on the sideboard with a carelessness which betokened no great belief in its virtues.","“I suppose all old soldiers are the same,” said Mrs. White. “The idea of our listening to such nonsense! How could wishes be granted in these days? And if they could, how could two hundred pounds hurt you, father?”","“Might drop on his head from the sky,” said the frivolous Herbert.","“Morris said the things happened so naturally,” said his father, “that you might if you so wished attribute it to coincidence.”","“Well, don’t break into the money before I come back,” said Herbert as he rose from the table. “I’m afraid it’ll turn you into a mean, avaricious man, and we shall have to disown you.”","His mother laughed, and following him to the door, watched him down the road; and returning to the breakfast table, was very happy at the expense of her husband’s credulity. All of which did not prevent her from scurrying to the door at the postman’s knock, nor prevent her from referring somewhat shortly to retired sergeant-majors of bibulous habits when she found that the post brought a tailor’s bill.","“Herbert will have some more of his funny remarks, I expect, when he comes home,” she said, as they sat at dinner.","“I dare say,” said Mr. White, pouring himself out some beer; “but for all that, the thing moved in my hand; that I’ll swear to.”","“You thought it did,” said the old lady soothingly.","“I say it did,” replied the other. “There was no thought about it; I had just—What’s the matter?”","His wife made no reply. She was watching the mysterious movements of a man outside, who, peering in an undecided fashion at the house, appeared to be trying to make up his mind to enter. In mental connection with the two hundred pounds, she noticed that the stranger was well dressed, and wore a silk hat of glossy newness. Three times he paused at the gate, and then walked on again. The fourth time he stood with his hand upon it, and then with sudden resolution flung it open and walked up the path. Mrs. White at the same moment placed her hands behind her, and hurriedly unfastening the strings of her apron, put that useful article of apparel beneath the cushion of her chair.","She brought the stranger, who seemed ill at ease, into the room. He gazed at her furtively, and listened in a preoccupied fashion as the old lady apologized for the appearance of the room, and her husband’s coat, a garment which he usually reserved for the garden. She then waited as patiently as her sex would permit, for him to broach his business, but he was at first strangely silent.","“I—was asked to call,” he said at last, and stooped and picked a piece of cotton from his trousers. “I come from ‘Maw and Meggins.’”","The old lady started. “Is anything the matter?” she asked, breathlessly. “Has anything happened to Herbert? What is it? What is it?”","Her husband interposed. “There, there, mother,” he said, hastily. “Sit down, and don’t jump to conclusions. You’ve not brought bad news, I’m sure, sir;” and he eyed the other wistfully.","“I’m sorry—” began the visitor.","“Is he hurt?” demanded the mother, wildly.","The visitor bowed in assent. “Badly hurt,” he said, quietly, “but he is not in any pain.”","“Oh, thank God!” said the old woman, clasping her hands. “Thank God for that! Thank—”","She broke off suddenly as the sinister meaning of the assurance dawned upon her and she saw the awful confirmation of her fears in the other’s averted face. She caught her breath, and turning to her slower-witted husband, laid her trembling old hand upon his. There was a long silence.","“He was caught in the machinery,” said the visitor at length in a low voice.","“Caught in the machinery,” repeated Mr. White, in a dazed fashion, “yes.”","He sat staring blankly out at the window, and taking his wife’s hand between his own, pressed it as he had been wont to do in their old courting-days nearly forty years before.","“He was the only one left to us,” he said, turning gently to the visitor. “It is hard.”","The other coughed, and rising, walked slowly to the window. “The firm wished me to convey their sincere sympathy with you in your great loss,” he said, without looking round. “I beg that you will understand I am only their servant and merely obeying orders.”","There was no reply; the old woman’s face was white, her eyes staring, and her breath inaudible; on the husband’s face was a look such as his friend the sergeant might have carried into his first action.","“I was to say that ‘Maw and Meggins’ disclaim all responsibility,” continued the other. “They admit no liability at all, but in consideration of your son’s services, they wish to present you with a certain sum as compensation.”","Mr. White dropped his wife’s hand, and rising to his feet, gazed with a look of horror at his visitor. His dry lips shaped the words, “How much?”","“Two hundred pounds,” was the answer.","Unconscious of his wife’s shriek, the old man smiled faintly, put out his hands like a sightless man, and dropped, a senseless heap, to the floor.","## III","In the huge new cemetery, some two miles distant, the old people buried their dead, and came back to a house steeped in shadow and silence. It was all over so quickly that at first they could hardly realize it, and remained in a state of expectation as though of something else to happen —something else which was to lighten this load, too heavy for old hearts to bear.","But the days passed, and expectation gave place to resignation—the hopeless resignation of the old, sometimes miscalled, apathy. Sometimes they hardly exchanged a word, for now they had nothing to talk about, and their days were long to weariness.","It was about a week after that the old man, waking suddenly in the night, stretched out his hand and found himself alone. The room was in darkness, and the sound of subdued weeping came from the window. He raised himself in bed and listened.","“Come back,” he said, tenderly. “You will be cold.”","“It is colder for my son,” said the old woman, and wept afresh.","The sound of her sobs died away on his ears. The bed was warm, and his eyes heavy with sleep. He dozed fitfully, and then slept until a sudden wild cry from his wife awoke him with a start.","“The paw!” she cried wildly. “The monkey’s paw!”","He started up in alarm. “Where? Where is it? What’s the matter?”","She came stumbling across the room toward him. “I want it,” she said, quietly. “You’ve not destroyed it?”","“It’s in the parlour, on the bracket,” he replied, marvelling. “Why?”","She cried and laughed together, and bending over, kissed his cheek.","“I only just thought of it,” she said, hysterically. “Why didn’t I think of it before? Why didn’t you think of it?”","“Think of what?” he questioned.","“The other two wishes,” she replied, rapidly. “We’ve only had one.”","“Was not that enough?” he demanded, fiercely.","“No,” she cried, triumphantly; “we’ll have one more. Go down and get it quickly, and wish our boy alive again.”","The man sat up in bed and flung the bedclothes from his quaking limbs. “Good God, you are mad!” he cried, aghast.","“Get it,” she panted; “get it quickly, and wish—Oh, my boy, my boy!”","Her husband struck a match and lit the candle. “Get back to bed,” he said, unsteadily. “You don’t know what you are saying.”","“We had the first wish granted,” said the old woman, feverishly; “why not the second?”","“A coincidence,” stammered the old man.","“Go and get it and wish,” cried his wife, quivering with excitement.","The old man turned and regarded her, and his voice shook. “He has been dead ten days, and besides he—I would not tell you else, but—I could only recognize him by his clothing. If he was too terrible for you to see then, how now?”","“Bring him back,” cried the old woman, and dragged him toward the door. “Do you think I fear the child I have nursed?”","He went down in the darkness, and felt his way to the parlour, and then to the mantelpiece. The talisman was in its place, and a horrible fear that the unspoken wish might bring his mutilated son before him ere he could escape from the room seized upon him, and he caught his breath as he found that he had lost the direction of the door. His brow cold with sweat, he felt his way round the table, and groped along the wall until he found himself in the small passage with the unwholesome thing in his hand.","Even his wife’s face seemed changed as he entered the room. It was white and expectant, and to his fears seemed to have an unnatural look upon it. He was afraid of her.","“Wish!” she cried, in a strong voice.","“It is foolish and wicked,” he faltered.","“Wish!” repeated his wife.","He raised his hand. “I wish my son alive again.”","The talisman fell to the floor, and he regarded it fearfully. Then he sank trembling into a chair as the old woman, with burning eyes, walked to the window and raised the blind.","He sat until he was chilled with the cold, glancing occasionally at the figure of the old woman peering through the window. The candle-end, which had burned below the rim of the china candlestick, was throwing pulsating shadows on the ceiling and walls, until, with a flicker larger than the rest, it expired. The old man, with an unspeakable sense of relief at the failure of the talisman, crept back to his bed, and a minute or two afterward the old woman came silently and apathetically beside him.","Neither spoke, but lay silently listening to the ticking of the clock. A stair creaked, and a squeaky mouse scurried noisily through the wall. The darkness was oppressive, and after lying for some time screwing up his courage, he took the box of matches, and striking one, went downstairs for a candle.","At the foot of the stairs the match went out, and he paused to strike another; and at the same moment a knock, so quiet and stealthy as to be scarcely audible, sounded on the front door.","The matches fell from his hand and spilled in the passage. He stood motionless, his breath suspended until the knock was repeated. Then he turned and fled swiftly back to his room, and closed the door behind him. A third knock sounded through the house.","“What’s that?” cried the old woman, starting up.","“A rat,” said the old man in shaking tones—“a rat. It passed me on the stairs.”","His wife sat up in bed listening. A loud knock resounded through the house.","“It’s Herbert!” she screamed. “It’s Herbert!”","She ran to the door, but her husband was before her, and catching her by the arm, held her tightly.","“What are you going to do?” he whispered hoarsely.","“It’s my boy; it’s Herbert!” she cried, struggling mechanically. “I forgot it was two miles away. What are you holding me for? Let go. I must open the door.”","“For God’s sake don’t let it in,” cried the old man, trembling.","“You’re afraid of your own son,” she cried, struggling. “Let me go. I’m coming, Herbert; I’m coming.”","There was another knock, and another. The old woman with a sudden wrench broke free and ran from the room. Her husband followed to the landing, and called after her appealingly as she hurried downstairs. He heard the chain rattle back and the bottom bolt drawn slowly and stiffly from the socket. Then the old woman’s voice, strained and panting.","“The bolt,” she cried, loudly. “Come down. I can’t reach it.”","But her husband was on his hands and knees groping wildly on the floor in search of the paw. If he could only find it before the thing outside got in. A perfect fusillade of knocks reverberated through the house, and he heard the scraping of a chair as his wife put it down in the passage against the door. He heard the creaking of the bolt as it came slowly back, and at the same moment he found the monkey’s paw, and frantically breathed his third and last wish.","The knocking ceased suddenly, although the echoes of it were still in the house. He heard the chair drawn back, and the door opened. A cold wind rushed up the staircase, and a long loud wail of disappointment and misery from his wife gave him courage to run down to her side, and then to the gate beyond. The street lamp flickering opposite shone on a quiet and deserted road."],"glossary":{"without":"dışarıda (eski kullanım)","parlour":"oturma odası, salon","check":"şah (satranç)","mate":"mat (satranç)","bawled":"bağırdı, haykırdı","condoling":"dert ortağı olmak, teselli etmek","burly":"iri yarı, heybetli","rubicund":"al yanaklı, kıpkırmızı yüzlü","visage":"yüz, çehre","proffered":"uzattı, sundu","doughty":"yiğit, cesur","fakir":"fakir (Hint dervişi)","wont":"alışkın","talisman":"tılsım, büyülü nesne","enthralled":"büyülenmiş, kendinden geçmiş","henpecked":"karısının sözünden çıkmayan","antimacassar":"koltuk örtüsü (dantelli)","credulity":"saflık, çabuk inanma","simian":"maymuna benzer","prosaic":"sıradan, ruhsuz","avaricious":"açgözlü, para düşkünü","bibulous":"içkiye düşkün","broach":"(konuyu) açmak","sinister":"uğursuz, tekinsiz","liability":"sorumluluk","mutilated":"parçalanmış, sakat bırakılmış","ere":"-den önce","fusillade":"art arda gelen vuruşlar","aghast":"dehşete düşmüş","bracket":"konsol, duvar rafı"},"phrases":[{"en":"make up his mind","tr":"karar vermek"},{"en":"jump to conclusions","tr":"aceleyle sonuca varmak"},{"en":"I dare say","tr":"sanırım, muhtemelen"},{"en":"at length","tr":"sonunda, uzun uzun"},{"en":"ill at ease","tr":"huzursuz, rahatsız"},{"en":"all the same","tr":"yine de, her şeye rağmen"},{"en":"that’s a fact","tr":"doğrusu, bu bir gerçek"},{"en":"burst into laughter","tr":"kahkahaya boğulmak"},{"en":"screwing up his courage","tr":"cesaretini toplamak"},{"en":"slip of a youth","tr":"ufak tefek, körpe delikanlı"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is special about the monkey's paw?","options":["It protects soldiers in battle","It grants three wishes to each of three men","It brings good luck to anyone who touches it","It can heal sick people"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Yaşlı fakir, pençeye üç ayrı adamın her birine üç dilek hakkı verecek bir büyü yapmıştır."},{"q":"What does Mr. White wish for the first time?","options":["To become an emperor","A larger house","Two hundred pounds","To meet the fakir"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Mr. White, evin borcunu kapatmaya yetecek iki yüz sterlin diler."},{"q":"How does the money finally arrive?","options":["A man from Herbert's employer comes to offer compensation for Herbert's death","Sergeant-Major Morris sends it from India","The Whites find a bag of money on their bed","A letter from a bank arrives"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Para, oğulları Herbert'in iş kazasında ölmesinin tazminatı olarak gelir. Dileğin kötü bir bedeli vardır."},{"q":"About a week after the funeral, what does Mrs. White want her husband to do?","options":["Throw the paw into the fire","Sell the paw to a stranger","Give the paw back to Morris","Use a second wish to bring their son back"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Mrs. White, pençede iki dilek daha kaldığını fark eder ve kocasından oğullarını geri getirmesini ister."},{"q":"What does Mr. White do just before his wife opens the door?","options":["He runs out of the house","He makes his third wish","He calls the police","He burns the paw"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Mr. White, karısı kapıyı açmadan hemen önce pençeyi bulur ve üçüncü ve son dileğini diler; kapıdaki vuruşlar kesilir."}],"words":3964,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-necklace","type":"story","title":"The Necklace","author":"Guy de Maupassant","year":1884,"level":"B2","genre":"drama","source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #73893 (Short Story Classics (Foreign), Vol. 5; translated by Mathilde Weissenhorn)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/73893","license":"Public domain"},"summaryTr":"Güzel ama sahip olduklarıyla yetinemeyen Mathilde, tek bir balo gecesi için arkadaşından mücevher ödünç alır. O geceden sonra hayatı bir daha eskisi gibi olmaz. Maupassant'tan, kaderin cilvesini anlatan ünlü bir öykü.","paragraphs":["She was one of those charming girls, born by a freak of destiny in a family of toilers. She had no fortune, no expectations, no means of satisfying her ambitions, except by a marriage with a rich and distinguished man, and, as she knew none, in order to escape from her surroundings, she married a clerk in the office of the Minister of Public Instruction.","She dressed simply, because she had no means of adornment; but she was as unhappy as though she had fallen from a high social position, for the women who have neither caste nor race use their beauty, grace, and charm as stepping-stones to those heights from which they are otherwise barred, their natural tact and instinctive elegance and quick perceptions being their only inheritance, and, skilfully used, make them the equal of their more fortunate sisters. She suffered incessantly when she glanced around her humble home, and felt the absence of all those delicacies and luxuries which are enjoyed only by the rich. In short, all the little nothings, that another woman of her caste would not have seen, tortured and wounded her. The sight of the old Breton peasant woman who performed her simple household duties awakened in her vain longings and troubled dreams.","She dreamed of beautiful halls, discreetly lighted by candles in great bronze candlesticks, whose rich carpets gave back no sounds and whose walls were covered with silks from the Orient, and of obsequious footmen half asleep in their large armchairs, ready to attend to your every want at a moment's notice; of large salons draped in ancient silks; of \"étagers\" covered with priceless bric-à-brac. She thought also of coquettish small salons, made expressly for the \"five o'clock,\" when one receives only one's intimates or distinguished men of letters, from whom it is every woman's ambition to receive attentions.","When she was seated at the table (whose cloth had already done duty for three days) or opposite her husband—who evinced his entire satisfaction with the evening's repast by such exclamations as: \"Oh, the good 'pot-au-feu'! I know nothing better!\"—her imagination carried her away to stately banquet halls, whose walls were covered with rich tapestries, portraying scenes in which ancient personages and strange birds were pictured in the middle of a fairy-like forest. She pictured the glittering silver, strange dishes, exquisitely served on marvelous plate, and gallantries whispered and listened to with the sphinx-like smile with which a woman of the world knows so well how to conceal her emotions, all the while eating a rosy trout or dallying with a wing of a lark. She had no toilets, no jewels, and it was for these things that she longed, as the fleet Arabian longs for his native desert. What pleasure to have pleased, been envied, to be seductive and sought after!","She had a rich friend, a comrade from the convent, whom she no longer visited, because she suffered from seeing the things she could not have, and on returning wept whole days for grief, regret, despair, and distress.","One evening her husband came home radiant, holding in his hand a large envelope.","\"See,\" said he, \"here is something for you.\"","She nervously tore open the envelope, drew out a card, on which these words were printed:","\"The Minister of Public Instruction and Madame Georges Ramponeau beg the honor of the company of Monsieur and Madame Loisel for the evening of Monday, January 18th.\"","Instead of being wild with delight, as he had expected, she threw the invitation on the table, with an exclamation of disgust, saying sullenly:","\"What do you wish me to do with that?\"","\"But, my dear, I thought you would be so pleased. You never go out, and this is an event. I only obtained it after infinite trouble. Everybody wants one; they are much sought after, and they are not generally given to employees. You will see there all of the official world.\"","She looked at him with supreme disdain, and said impatiently:","\"What would you like me to wear?\" The secret was out. Manlike, he had not thought of that.","\"But—the dress—that you wear to the theatre,\" stammered he. \"You always look beautiful to me in that.\"","He stopped speaking, stupefied and dismayed on seeing his wife in tears. Two large tears trickled slowly down her cheeks.","\"What is the matter? What is the matter?\" asked he tenderly. By violent effort she conquered her grief and calmly said, while wiping her humid cheeks:","\"Nothing; only I have no toilet, and, of course, can not go. Give the card to one of your comrades whose wife is fortunate enough to have something suitable for the occasion.\"","Despairingly he said:","\"See, Mathilde, how much will a dress cost to wear to this ball; one which can also be used for other occasions—something very simple.\"","She reflected a few moments, figuring in her own mind the sum she could ask without danger of immediate refusal and frightening her economical husband. Finally she hesitatingly said:","\"I do not know exactly; but it seems to me I might manage with about 400 francs.\"","He paled a little, because he had been saving just that sum to buy a gun for the following summer, when he would go with some of his friends to the plains of Nanterre on Sundays to shoot larks. Stifling his regrets, however, he replied:","\"Very well, I will give you 400 francs, but try to have a beautiful dress.\"","The day of the fête drew near; but Madame Loisel seemed sad, anxious, and uneasy. Her toilet was ready, what could it be? Her husband said to her one evening:","\"What is the matter? You have been so queer for the last few days!\"","She replied: \"It worries me that I have not one jewel, not a precious stone to wear. What a miserable figure I shall be! I think I would rather not go at all!\"","\"You can wear natural flowers; it is all the rage at this season, and for ten francs you can have two or three magnificent roses.\"","But she was not convinced.","\"No; there is nothing more humiliating than to be poorly dressed among so many rich women.\"","\"But how silly you are! Go to your friend, Madame Forestier, and ask her to lend you her jewels. You are friendly enough with her to do that.\"","She gave a cry of joy.","\"Yes; that is true—I had not thought of it.\"","The following day she went to her friend and explained her predicament. Madame Forestier went to a closet and took out a large casket, and, opening it, said:","\"Choose, my dear; they are at your service.\"","She saw first bracelets, then a necklace of pearls, a Venetian cross, gold and precious stones of exquisite workmanship. She tried them on before the glass, unable to decide whether to wear them or not.","\"Have you nothing else?\" said she.","\"Oh, yes; look them over, I don't know what might please you.\"","Suddenly she opened a black satin case, disclosing to view a superb rivière of diamonds, her heart beat furiously with the desire of possession. She took them in her trembling hands and put them on over her simple high-neck gown, and stood lost in an ecstasy of admiration of herself. Then, fearfully, hesitatingly, dreading the agony of a refusal:","\"Can you lend me only that?\"","\"Why, certainly; if it pleases you.\"","She fell on her friend's neck, embraced her tempestuously, and then left hastily with her treasure.","The day of the ball arrived. Madame Loisel was a success. Among all the beautiful women she was the most beautiful, elegant, gracious, and smiling with joy. She attracted the attention of some of the most distinguished men present, and on all sides was heard:","\"Who is she?\"","All the attachés of the cabinet sought her dancing card eagerly, and even the Minister himself expressed his approval. She danced with pleasure, thinking of nothing but the triumph of her beauty and the glory of her success. Intoxicated by all the admiration, she seemed to float through a cloud of happiness, intensified by her complete victory and the tribute paid to her charms, so sweet to the hearts of women. She left about four o'clock in the morning; her husband had slept since midnight in a small room, deserted except by two or three gentlemen who also awaited their wives.","He threw over her shoulders the modest cloak which she had brought, whose shabbiness seemed to mock the elegance of the ball toilet. She felt the incongruity, and walked swiftly away in order not to be seen by those whose rich furs were more in accordance with the occasion.","\"Wait,\" said her husband, \"you will take cold; I will call a carriage.\"","But she heeded him not, and rapidly descended the staircase. When they reached the street, there was no carriage in sight, and they were obliged to look for one, calling to the drivers who passed by, but in vain. Shiveringly they walked toward the Seine and finally found on the quay one of those nocturnal coupés one finds only in Paris after dark, hovering about the great city like grim birds of prey, who conceal their misery during the day. It carried them to their door (Rue de Martyrs), and they slowly and sadly entered their small apartments. It was ended for her, and he only remembered that he would have to be at his desk at ten o'clock.","She took off her cloak in front of the glass in order to admire herself once more in all her bravery, but, suddenly, she cried out: \"The diamonds are gone!\" Her husband, almost half asleep, started at the cry and asked:","\"What is the matter?\"","She turned toward him with a frightened air.","\"I—I have lost Madame Forestier's necklace!\"","He rose dismayed.","\"What—how! But it is not possible!\" And they immediately began to search in the folds of the dress, the cloak, in the pockets—everywhere, and found nothing.","\"Are you sure that you had it when you left the ball?\"","\"Yes; I felt it while still in the vestibule at the Minister's.\"","\"But if you had lost it in the street we should have heard it drop. It ought to be in the carriage.\"","\"Yes; it is possible. Did you take the number?\"","\"No; and you have not looked at it, either?\"","\"No.\"","They looked at each other fearfully; finally Loisel dressed himself.","\"I shall go over the whole ground that we traveled on foot, to see whether I can not find it.\"","He went out. She sat still in her brilliant ball toilet; no desire to sleep, no power to think, all swallowed up in the fear of the calamity which had fallen upon them.","Her husband came in at seven o'clock. He had found nothing. He had been to the Prefecture of the Police, to the papers offering a reward, to all small cab companies, anywhere, in short, where he could have the shadow of hope of recovery.","She waited all day in the same state of fear in the face of this frightful disaster.","Loisel returned in the evening pallid and haggard. No news as yet.","\"You must write to your friend that you have broken the clasp of the necklace and are having it repaired. That will give us time to look around.\"","At the end of the week they had lost all hope, and Loisel, to whom it seemed this care and trouble had added five years to his age, said:","\"We must try and replace the jewels.\"","The following day they went to the jeweler whose name was stamped inside the case. He consulted his books: \"I did not sell that necklace, madame, I only furnished the case.\"","Then they went from jeweler to jeweler, racking their memories to find the same, both of them sick with grief and agony. At last, in a small shop in the Palais Royal, they found one which seemed to them like the one they had lost. With beating hearts they asked the price.","Forty thousand francs; but they could have it for 36,000 francs.","They asked the jeweler not to dispose of it for three days, and he also promised to take it back at 34,000 francs if the first one was found before the end of February.","Loisel had inherited 18,000 francs from his father. He borrowed the rest.","He borrowed a thousand francs from one, five hundred from another, five louis here, five louis there—he gave notes, made ruinous engagements, had recourse to the usurers, ran the whole gamut of money-lenders. He compromised his whole existence risking his signature, without knowing that it would be honored, terrified by the agony of the future, by the black misery which enveloped him, by the prospect of all the physical privations and moral tortures. He went for the new necklace and deposited on the counter his 36,000 francs.","When Madame Loisel returned the necklace to Madame Forestier, she coldly said:","\"You should have returned it sooner, as I might have needed it.\"","She did not open the case, the one thing Madame Loisel had dreaded. What if she had discovered the change—what would she have thought? Would she not be taken for a thief?","From that time on Madame Loisel knew what life meant to the very poor in all its phases. She took her part heroically. This frightful debt must be paid. Her share of privations was bravely borne. They discharged their one domestic, changed their location, and rented smaller apartments near the roof.","She knew now what meant the duties of the household, the heavy work of the kitchen. Her pretty hands soon lost all semblance of the care of bygone days. She washed the soiled linen and dried it in her room. She went every morning to the street with the refuse of the kitchen, carrying the water, stopping at each flight of stairs to take breath—wearing the dress of the women of the people; she went each day to the grocer, the fruiterer, the butcher, carrying her basket on her arm, bargaining, defending cent by cent her miserable money.","They were obliged each month to pay some notes and renew others in order to gain time. Her husband worked in the evening balancing the books of merchants, and often was busy all night, copying at five cents a page.","And this life they endured for ten years.","At the end of this time they had paid all the tax of the usurers and compound interest.","Madame Loisel seemed an old woman now. She had become strong and hardy as the women of the provinces, and with tousled head, short skirts, red hands, she was foremost among the loud-voiced women of the neighborhood, who passed their time gossiping at their doorsteps.","But sometimes when her husband was at his office she seated herself at the window and thought of that evening in the past and that ball, where she had been so beautiful and so admired.","What would have happened if she had not lost the necklace? Who knows? Life is a singular and changeable thing, full of vicissitudes. How little it takes to save or wreck us!","One Sunday as she was walking in the Champs Elysées to divert herself from the cares and duties of the week she suddenly perceived a lady, with a little child, coming toward her. It was Madame Forestier, still young, beautiful and charming. Madame Loisel stopped short, too agitated to move. Should she speak to her? Yes, certainly. And now that the necklace was paid for she would tell her everything. Why not?","She walked up to her and said: \"Good day, Jeanne.\"","Madame Forestier did not recognize her and seemed astonished at being spoken to so familiarly by this woman of the people.","\"But—madame—I do not—I think you are mistaken.\"","\"No; I am Mathilde Loisel.\"","\"Oh!—my poor Mathilde, how you are changed!\"","\"Yes; I have had lots of trouble and misery since last I saw you—and all for you.\"","\"For me! And how was that?\"","\"Do you remember the necklace of diamonds you lent me, to wear to the Minister's ball?\"","\"Yes; well?\"","\"Well, I lost it.\"","\"Lost it! How could you, since you returned it to me?\"","\"I returned you one just like it, and for ten years we have been paying for it. You know, it was not easy for us, who had nothing—but it is finished, and I am very happy.\"","\"You say that you bought a necklace of diamonds to replace mine,\" said Madame Forestier.","\"Yes; and you never found it out! They were so much alike,\" and she smiled proudly.","Touched to the heart, Madame Forestier took the poor, rough hands in hers, drawing her tenderly toward her, her voice filled with tears:","\"Oh, my poor Mathilde! But mine were false. They were not worth more than 500 francs at most.\""],"glossary":{"toilet":"kıyafet, giysi (eski kullanım)","toilets":"kıyafetler, giysiler (eski kullanım)","evinced":"gösterdi, belli etti","obsequious":"yaltaklanan, aşırı itaatkâr","fête":"şenlik, balo","attachés":"(bakanlıktaki) görevliler, ataşeler","rivière":"sıra sıra elmaslardan yapılmış gerdanlık","casket":"mücevher kutusu","incongruity":"uyumsuzluk, yersizlik","quay":"rıhtım, nehir kıyısı yolu","coupés":"iki kişilik kapalı at arabaları","haggard":"bitkin, yıpranmış","usurers":"tefeciler","louis":"louis (eski Fransız altın parası, 20 frank)","domestic":"hizmetçi","refuse":"çöp, atık","fruiterer":"manav","notes":"senet, borç senedi","vicissitudes":"inişler çıkışlar, değişken durumlar","divert":"oyalamak, eğlendirmek"},"phrases":[{"en":"freak of destiny","tr":"kaderin garip bir cilvesi"},{"en":"stepping-stone","tr":"basamak, bir şeye ulaşmak için araç"},{"en":"carry someone away","tr":"birini kendinden geçirmek, sürüklemek"},{"en":"all the rage","tr":"çok moda, herkesin peşinde olduğu"},{"en":"in vain","tr":"boşuna, nafile"},{"en":"run the whole gamut of","tr":"(bir şeyin) tamamını denemek, hepsinden geçmek"},{"en":"stop short","tr":"birden durmak"},{"en":"take someone for","tr":"birini ... sanmak (ör. hırsız sanmak)"},{"en":"cent by cent","tr":"kuruş kuruş"},{"en":"at most","tr":"en fazla, olsa olsa"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Mathilde unhappy at the beginning of the story?","options":["Her husband is cruel to her","She is ill","She is poor and dreams of a rich life","She has no friends"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Mathilde güzeldir ama fakirdir; lüks bir hayatın hayalini kurduğu için mutsuzdur."},{"q":"What does Mathilde borrow from her friend for the ball?","options":["A dress","A pair of gloves","A bracelet","A diamond necklace"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Mathilde, arkadaşı Madame Forestier'den bir elmas gerdanlık ödünç alır."},{"q":"What happens after the ball?","options":["Mathilde loses the necklace","Her husband loses his job","Madame Forestier asks for the necklace back","Mathilde falls ill"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Eve döndüklerinde Mathilde gerdanlığın kaybolduğunu fark eder."},{"q":"How do the Loisels pay for the new necklace?","options":["They win money","They sell their house","They borrow a lot of money and work hard for ten years","Madame Forestier helps them"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Kaybolan gerdanlığın yerine yenisini alırlar; borç para bulurlar ve on yıl boyunca borcu ödemek için çalışırlar."},{"q":"What does Madame Forestier say at the end of the story?","options":["She is very angry","Her necklace was false and worth very little","She wants more money","She does not know who Mathilde is"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Madame Forestier'nin gerdanlığı sahte elmaslardan yapılmıştır; en fazla 500 frank eder."}],"words":2855,"length":"medium"},{"id":"the-open-window","type":"story","title":"The Open Window","author":"Saki","year":1914,"level":"B2","genre":"comedy","source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #269 (Beasts and Super-Beasts)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/269","license":"Public domain"},"summaryTr":"Sinirleri bozuk Framton Nuttel, dinlenmek için gittiği kırsalda bir hanımı ziyaret eder ve onu beklerken genç bir kızla baş başa kalır. Kızın anlattığı hikâye, öğleden sonrayı hiç beklenmedik bir yöne çevirir.","paragraphs":["“My aunt will be down presently, Mr. Nuttel,” said a very self-possessed young lady of fifteen; “in the meantime you must try and put up with me.”","Framton Nuttel endeavoured to say the correct something which should duly flatter the niece of the moment without unduly discounting the aunt that was to come. Privately he doubted more than ever whether these formal visits on a succession of total strangers would do much towards helping the nerve cure which he was supposed to be undergoing.","“I know how it will be,” his sister had said when he was preparing to migrate to this rural retreat; “you will bury yourself down there and not speak to a living soul, and your nerves will be worse than ever from moping. I shall just give you letters of introduction to all the people I know there. Some of them, as far as I can remember, were quite nice.”","Framton wondered whether Mrs. Sappleton, the lady to whom he was presenting one of the letters of introduction, came into the nice division.","“Do you know many of the people round here?” asked the niece, when she judged that they had had sufficient silent communion.","“Hardly a soul,” said Framton. “My sister was staying here, at the rectory, you know, some four years ago, and she gave me letters of introduction to some of the people here.”","He made the last statement in a tone of distinct regret.","“Then you know practically nothing about my aunt?” pursued the self-possessed young lady.","“Only her name and address,” admitted the caller. He was wondering whether Mrs. Sappleton was in the married or widowed state. An undefinable something about the room seemed to suggest masculine habitation.","“Her great tragedy happened just three years ago,” said the child; “that would be since your sister’s time.”","“Her tragedy?” asked Framton; somehow in this restful country spot tragedies seemed out of place.","“You may wonder why we keep that window wide open on an October afternoon,” said the niece, indicating a large French window that opened on to a lawn.","“It is quite warm for the time of the year,” said Framton; “but has that window got anything to do with the tragedy?”","“Out through that window, three years ago to a day, her husband and her two young brothers went off for their day’s shooting. They never came back. In crossing the moor to their favourite snipe-shooting ground they were all three engulfed in a treacherous piece of bog. It had been that dreadful wet summer, you know, and places that were safe in other years gave way suddenly without warning. Their bodies were never recovered. That was the dreadful part of it.” Here the child’s voice lost its self-possessed note and became falteringly human. “Poor aunt always thinks that they will come back some day, they and the little brown spaniel that was lost with them, and walk in at that window just as they used to do. That is why the window is kept open every evening till it is quite dusk. Poor dear aunt, she has often told me how they went out, her husband with his white waterproof coat over his arm, and Ronnie, her youngest brother, singing ‘Bertie, why do you bound?’ as he always did to tease her, because she said it got on her nerves. Do you know, sometimes on still, quiet evenings like this, I almost get a creepy feeling that they will all walk in through that window—”","She broke off with a little shudder. It was a relief to Framton when the aunt bustled into the room with a whirl of apologies for being late in making her appearance.","“I hope Vera has been amusing you?” she said.","“She has been very interesting,” said Framton.","“I hope you don’t mind the open window,” said Mrs. Sappleton briskly; “my husband and brothers will be home directly from shooting, and they always come in this way. They’ve been out for snipe in the marshes to-day, so they’ll make a fine mess over my poor carpets. So like you men-folk, isn’t it?”","She rattled on cheerfully about the shooting and the scarcity of birds, and the prospects for duck in the winter. To Framton it was all purely horrible. He made a desperate but only partially successful effort to turn the talk on to a less ghastly topic; he was conscious that his hostess was giving him only a fragment of her attention, and her eyes were constantly straying past him to the open window and the lawn beyond. It was certainly an unfortunate coincidence that he should have paid his visit on this tragic anniversary.","“The doctors agree in ordering me complete rest, an absence of mental excitement, and avoidance of anything in the nature of violent physical exercise,” announced Framton, who laboured under the tolerably widespread delusion that total strangers and chance acquaintances are hungry for the least detail of one’s ailments and infirmities, their cause and cure. “On the matter of diet they are not so much in agreement,” he continued.","“No?” said Mrs. Sappleton, in a voice which only replaced a yawn at the last moment. Then she suddenly brightened into alert attention—but not to what Framton was saying.","“Here they are at last!” she cried. “Just in time for tea, and don’t they look as if they were muddy up to the eyes!”","Framton shivered slightly and turned towards the niece with a look intended to convey sympathetic comprehension. The child was staring out through the open window with dazed horror in her eyes. In a chill shock of nameless fear Framton swung round in his seat and looked in the same direction.","In the deepening twilight three figures were walking across the lawn towards the window; they all carried guns under their arms, and one of them was additionally burdened with a white coat hung over his shoulders. A tired brown spaniel kept close at their heels. Noiselessly they neared the house, and then a hoarse young voice chanted out of the dusk: “I said, Bertie, why do you bound?”","Framton grabbed wildly at his stick and hat; the hall-door, the gravel-drive, and the front gate were dimly-noted stages in his headlong retreat. A cyclist coming along the road had to run into the hedge to avoid an imminent collision.","“Here we are, my dear,” said the bearer of the white mackintosh, coming in through the window; “fairly muddy, but most of it’s dry. Who was that who bolted out as we came up?”","“A most extraordinary man, a Mr. Nuttel,” said Mrs. Sappleton; “could only talk about his illnesses, and dashed off without a word of good-bye or apology when you arrived. One would think he had seen a ghost.”","“I expect it was the spaniel,” said the niece calmly; “he told me he had a horror of dogs. He was once hunted into a cemetery somewhere on the banks of the Ganges by a pack of pariah dogs, and had to spend the night in a newly dug grave with the creatures snarling and grinning and foaming just above him. Enough to make anyone lose their nerve.”","Romance at short notice was her speciality."],"glossary":{"presently":"birazdan, kısa süre sonra","directly":"hemen, birazdan (İngiliz kullanımı)","endeavoured":"çabaladı, uğraştı","caller":"ziyaretçi, misafir","migrate":"taşınmak, yerleşmek","moping":"kara kara düşünüp somurtmak","rectory":"papaz evi","moor":"çalılık ve bataklık yaylalar","bog":"bataklık","engulfed":"yutuldu, içine gömüldü","snipe":"çulluk (bir av kuşu)","spaniel":"spaniel (bir av köpeği cinsi)","pariah":"sokak köpeği (Hindistan'da başıboş köpek)","mackintosh":"yağmurluk","bolted":"birden kaçtı, fırladı","mess":"pislik, dağınıklık","ghastly":"korkunç, ürkütücü","speciality":"uzmanlık alanı, en iyi yaptığı iş"},"phrases":[{"en":"put up with","tr":"katlanmak, idare etmek"},{"en":"letter of introduction","tr":"tavsiye mektubu"},{"en":"get on someone's nerves","tr":"sinirlerine dokunmak"},{"en":"break off","tr":"(sözü) yarıda kesmek"},{"en":"rattle on","tr":"durmadan konuşmak, gevezelik etmek"},{"en":"swing round","tr":"birden dönmek, arkasına dönmek"},{"en":"dash off","tr":"apar topar gitmek"},{"en":"lose one's nerve","tr":"cesaretini kaybetmek, ödü kopmak"},{"en":"at short notice","tr":"kısa sürede, önceden hazırlık yapmadan"},{"en":"go off","tr":"çıkıp gitmek"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Framton Nuttel visit Mrs. Sappleton?","options":["He wants to buy her house","His sister gave him a letter of introduction to her","He is her doctor","He is a friend of her husband"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Framton'ın kız kardeşi ona bölgedeki insanlara tavsiye mektupları vermiştir; Framton da bu mektuplarla ziyaretlere çıkar."},{"q":"According to Vera, why is the window kept open?","options":["The room is too hot","Her aunt likes fresh air","Her aunt thinks the men will come back through it","The window is broken"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Vera'ya göre teyzesi, üç yıl önce kaybolan kocasının ve erkek kardeşlerinin bir gün o pencereden gireceğini umut etmektedir."},{"q":"What does Vera say happened to her aunt's husband and brothers?","options":["They disappeared in a bog while shooting","They went to war","They moved abroad","They died in a fire"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Vera, üç erkeğin avlanmaya giderken bir bataklığa gömüldüğünü ve cesetlerinin bulunamadığını söyler."},{"q":"What does Framton do when he sees three men walking across the lawn?","options":["He says hello to them","He asks for a cup of tea","He hides under the table","He runs out of the house"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Framton, hayalet gördüğünü sanıp şapkasını ve bastonunu kapar ve evden kaçar."},{"q":"What does Vera tell the family about Mr. Nuttel's sudden departure?","options":["He felt ill","He was late for a train","He was afraid of dogs","He was angry with them"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Vera, Framton'ın köpeklerden korktuğunu ve geçmişte kötü bir deneyim yaşadığını uydurur."}],"words":1219,"length":"medium"},{"id":"ransom-of-red-chief","type":"story","title":"The Ransom of Red Chief","author":"O. Henry","year":1907,"level":"B2","genre":"comedy","source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #1595 (Whirligigs)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1595","license":"Public domain"},"summaryTr":"İki dolandırıcı, kasabanın zengin adamının oğlunu kaçırıp fidye istemeye karar verir; ama işler hiç de planladıkları gibi gitmez. O. Henry'den kahkaha attıran bir kaçırma hikâyesi.","paragraphs":["It looked like a good thing: but wait till I tell you. We were down South, in Alabama—Bill Driscoll and myself—when this kidnapping idea struck us. It was, as Bill afterward expressed it, “during a moment of temporary mental apparition”; but we didn’t find that out till later.","There was a town down there, as flat as a flannel-cake, and called Summit, of course. It contained inhabitants of as undeleterious and self-satisfied a class of peasantry as ever clustered around a Maypole.","Bill and me had a joint capital of about six hundred dollars, and we needed just two thousand dollars more to pull off a fraudulent town-lot scheme in Western Illinois with. We talked it over on the front steps of the hotel. Philoprogenitiveness, says we, is strong in semi-rural communities; therefore and for other reasons, a kidnapping project ought to do better there than in the radius of newspapers that send reporters out in plain clothes to stir up talk about such things. We knew that Summit couldn’t get after us with anything stronger than constables and maybe some lackadaisical bloodhounds and a diatribe or two in the Weekly Farmers’ Budget. So, it looked good.","We selected for our victim the only child of a prominent citizen named Ebenezer Dorset. The father was respectable and tight, a mortgage fancier and a stern, upright collection-plate passer and forecloser. The kid was a boy of ten, with bas-relief freckles, and hair the colour of the cover of the magazine you buy at the news-stand when you want to catch a train. Bill and me figured that Ebenezer would melt down for a ransom of two thousand dollars to a cent. But wait till I tell you.","About two miles from Summit was a little mountain, covered with a dense cedar brake. On the rear elevation of this mountain was a cave. There we stored provisions. One evening after sundown, we drove in a buggy past old Dorset’s house. The kid was in the street, throwing rocks at a kitten on the opposite fence.","“Hey, little boy!” says Bill, “would you like to have a bag of candy and a nice ride?”","The boy catches Bill neatly in the eye with a piece of brick.","“That will cost the old man an extra five hundred dollars,” says Bill, climbing over the wheel.","That boy put up a fight like a welter-weight cinnamon bear; but, at last, we got him down in the bottom of the buggy and drove away. We took him up to the cave and I hitched the horse in the cedar brake. After dark I drove the buggy to the little village, three miles away, where we had hired it, and walked back to the mountain.","Bill was pasting court-plaster over the scratches and bruises on his features. There was a fire burning behind the big rock at the entrance of the cave, and the boy was watching a pot of boiling coffee, with two buzzard tail-feathers stuck in his red hair. He points a stick at me when I come up, and says:","“Ha! cursed paleface, do you dare to enter the camp of Red Chief, the terror of the plains?","“He’s all right now,” says Bill, rolling up his trousers and examining some bruises on his shins. “We’re playing Indian. We’re making Buffalo Bill’s show look like magic-lantern views of Palestine in the town hall. I’m Old Hank, the Trapper, Red Chief’s captive, and I’m to be scalped at daybreak. By Geronimo! that kid can kick hard.”","Yes, sir, that boy seemed to be having the time of his life. The fun of camping out in a cave had made him forget that he was a captive himself. He immediately christened me Snake-eye, the Spy, and announced that, when his braves returned from the warpath, I was to be broiled at the stake at the rising of the sun.","Then we had supper; and he filled his mouth full of bacon and bread and gravy, and began to talk. He made a during-dinner speech something like this:","“I like this fine. I never camped out before; but I had a pet ’possum once, and I was nine last birthday. I hate to go to school. Rats ate up sixteen of Jimmy Talbot’s aunt’s speckled hen’s eggs. Are there any real Indians in these woods? I want some more gravy. Does the trees moving make the wind blow? We had five puppies. What makes your nose so red, Hank? My father has lots of money. Are the stars hot? I whipped Ed Walker twice, Saturday. I don’t like girls. You dassent catch toads unless with a string. Do oxen make any noise? Why are oranges round? Have you got beds to sleep on in this cave? Amos Murray has got six toes. A parrot can talk, but a monkey or a fish can’t. How many does it take to make twelve?”","Every few minutes he would remember that he was a pesky chief, and pick up his stick rifle and tiptoe to the mouth of the cave to rubber for the scouts of the hated paleface. Now and then he would let out a war-whoop that made Old Hank the Trapper shiver. That boy had Bill terrorized from the start.","“Red Chief,” says I to the kid, “would you like to go home?”","“Aw, what for?” says he. “I don’t have any fun at home. I hate to go to school. I like to camp out. You won’t take me back home again, Snake-eye, will you?”","“Not right away,” says I. “We’ll stay here in the cave a while.”","“All right!” says he. “That’ll be fine. I never had such fun in all my life.”","We went to bed about eleven o’clock. We spread down some wide blankets and quilts and put Red Chief between us. We weren’t afraid he’d run away. He kept us awake for three hours, jumping up and reaching for his rifle and screeching: “Hist! pard,” in mine and Bill’s ears, as the fancied crackle of a twig or the rustle of a leaf revealed to his young imagination the stealthy approach of the outlaw band. At last, I fell into a troubled sleep, and dreamed that I had been kidnapped and chained to a tree by a ferocious pirate with red hair.","Just at daybreak, I was awakened by a series of awful screams from Bill. They weren’t yells, or howls, or shouts, or whoops, or yawps, such as you’d expect from a manly set of vocal organs—they were simply indecent, terrifying, humiliating screams, such as women emit when they see ghosts or caterpillars. It’s an awful thing to hear a strong, desperate, fat man scream incontinently in a cave at daybreak.","I jumped up to see what the matter was. Red Chief was sitting on Bill’s chest, with one hand twined in Bill’s hair. In the other he had the sharp case-knife we used for slicing bacon; and he was industriously and realistically trying to take Bill’s scalp, according to the sentence that had been pronounced upon him the evening before.","I got the knife away from the kid and made him lie down again. But, from that moment, Bill’s spirit was broken. He laid down on his side of the bed, but he never closed an eye again in sleep as long as that boy was with us. I dozed off for a while, but along toward sun-up I remembered that Red Chief had said I was to be burned at the stake at the rising of the sun. I wasn’t nervous or afraid; but I sat up and lit my pipe and leaned against a rock.","“What you getting up so soon for, Sam?” asked Bill.","“Me?” says I. “Oh, I got a kind of a pain in my shoulder. I thought sitting up would rest it.”","“You’re a liar!” says Bill. “You’re afraid. You was to be burned at sunrise, and you was afraid he’d do it. And he would, too, if he could find a match. Ain’t it awful, Sam? Do you think anybody will pay out money to get a little imp like that back home?”","“Sure,” said I. “A rowdy kid like that is just the kind that parents dote on. Now, you and the Chief get up and cook breakfast, while I go up on the top of this mountain and reconnoitre.”","I went up on the peak of the little mountain and ran my eye over the contiguous vicinity. Over toward Summit I expected to see the sturdy yeomanry of the village armed with scythes and pitchforks beating the countryside for the dastardly kidnappers. But what I saw was a peaceful landscape dotted with one man ploughing with a dun mule. Nobody was dragging the creek; no couriers dashed hither and yon, bringing tidings of no news to the distracted parents. There was a sylvan attitude of somnolent sleepiness pervading that section of the external outward surface of Alabama that lay exposed to my view. “Perhaps,” says I to myself, “it has not yet been discovered that the wolves have borne away the tender lambkin from the fold. Heaven help the wolves!” says I, and I went down the mountain to breakfast.","When I got to the cave I found Bill backed up against the side of it, breathing hard, and the boy threatening to smash him with a rock half as big as a cocoanut.","“He put a red-hot boiled potato down my back,” explained Bill, “and then mashed it with his foot; and I boxed his ears. Have you got a gun about you, Sam?”","I took the rock away from the boy and kind of patched up the argument. “I’ll fix you,” says the kid to Bill. “No man ever yet struck the Red Chief but what he got paid for it. You better beware!”","After breakfast the kid takes a piece of leather with strings wrapped around it out of his pocket and goes outside the cave unwinding it.","“What’s he up to now?” says Bill, anxiously. “You don’t think he’ll run away, do you, Sam?”","“No fear of it,” says I. “He don’t seem to be much of a home body. But we’ve got to fix up some plan about the ransom. There don’t seem to be much excitement around Summit on account of his disappearance; but maybe they haven’t realized yet that he’s gone. His folks may think he’s spending the night with Aunt Jane or one of the neighbours. Anyhow, he’ll be missed to-day. To-night we must get a message to his father demanding the two thousand dollars for his return.”","Just then we heard a kind of war-whoop, such as David might have emitted when he knocked out the champion Goliath. It was a sling that Red Chief had pulled out of his pocket, and he was whirling it around his head.","I dodged, and heard a heavy thud and a kind of a sigh from Bill, like a horse gives out when you take his saddle off. A rock the size of an egg had caught Bill just behind his left ear. He loosened himself all over and fell in the fire across the frying pan of hot water for washing the dishes. I dragged him out and poured cold water on his head for half an hour.","By and by, Bill sits up and feels behind his ear and says: “Sam, do you know who my favourite Biblical character is?”","“Take it easy,” says I. “You’ll come to your senses presently.”","“King Herod,” says he. “You won’t go away and leave me here alone, will you, Sam?”","I went out and caught that boy and shook him until his freckles rattled.","“If you don’t behave,” says I, “I’ll take you straight home. Now, are you going to be good, or not?”","“I was only funning,” says he sullenly. “I didn’t mean to hurt Old Hank. But what did he hit me for? I’ll behave, Snake-eye, if you won’t send me home, and if you’ll let me play the Black Scout to-day.”","“I don’t know the game,” says I. “That’s for you and Mr. Bill to decide. He’s your playmate for the day. I’m going away for a while, on business. Now, you come in and make friends with him and say you are sorry for hurting him, or home you go, at once.”","I made him and Bill shake hands, and then I took Bill aside and told him I was going to Poplar Cove, a little village three miles from the cave, and find out what I could about how the kidnapping had been regarded in Summit. Also, I thought it best to send a peremptory letter to old man Dorset that day, demanding the ransom and dictating how it should be paid.","“You know, Sam,” says Bill, “I’ve stood by you without batting an eye in earthquakes, fire and flood—in poker games, dynamite outrages, police raids, train robberies and cyclones. I never lost my nerve yet till we kidnapped that two-legged skyrocket of a kid. He’s got me going. You won’t leave me long with him, will you, Sam?”","“I’ll be back some time this afternoon,” says I. “You must keep the boy amused and quiet till I return. And now we’ll write the letter to old Dorset.”","Bill and I got paper and pencil and worked on the letter while Red Chief, with a blanket wrapped around him, strutted up and down, guarding the mouth of the cave. Bill begged me tearfully to make the ransom fifteen hundred dollars instead of two thousand. “I ain’t attempting,” says he, “to decry the celebrated moral aspect of parental affection, but we’re dealing with humans, and it ain’t human for anybody to give up two thousand dollars for that forty-pound chunk of freckled wildcat. I’m willing to take a chance at fifteen hundred dollars. You can charge the difference up to me.”","So, to relieve Bill, I acceded, and we collaborated a letter that ran this way:","Ebenezer Dorset, Esq.:","We have your boy concealed in a place far from Summit. It is useless for you or the most skilful detectives to attempt to find him. Absolutely, the only terms on which you can have him restored to you are these: We demand fifteen hundred dollars in large bills for his return; the money to be left at midnight to-night at the same spot and in the same box as your reply—as hereinafter described. If you agree to these terms, send your answer in writing by a solitary messenger to-night at half-past eight o’clock. After crossing Owl Creek, on the road to Poplar Cove, there are three large trees about a hundred yards apart, close to the fence of the wheat field on the right-hand side. At the bottom of the fence-post, opposite the third tree, will be found a small pasteboard box. The messenger will place the answer in this box and return immediately to Summit. If you attempt any treachery or fail to comply with our demand as stated, you will never see your boy again. If you pay the money as demanded, he will be returned to you safe and well within three hours. These terms are final, and if you do not accede to them no further communication will be attempted.","TWO DESPERATE MEN.","I addressed this letter to Dorset, and put it in my pocket. As I was about to start, the kid comes up to me and says:","“Aw, Snake-eye, you said I could play the Black Scout while you was gone.”","“Play it, of course,” says I. “Mr. Bill will play with you. What kind of a game is it?”","“I’m the Black Scout,” says Red Chief, “and I have to ride to the stockade to warn the settlers that the Indians are coming. I’m tired of playing Indian myself. I want to be the Black Scout.”","“All right,” says I. “It sounds harmless to me. I guess Mr. Bill will help you foil the pesky savages.”","“What am I to do?” asks Bill, looking at the kid suspiciously.","“You are the hoss,” says Black Scout. “Get down on your hands and knees. How can I ride to the stockade without a hoss?”","“You’d better keep him interested,” said I, “till we get the scheme going. Loosen up.”","Bill gets down on his all fours, and a look comes in his eye like a rabbit’s when you catch it in a trap.","“How far is it to the stockade, kid?” he asks, in a husky manner of voice.","“Ninety miles,” says the Black Scout. “And you have to hump yourself to get there on time. Whoa, now!”","The Black Scout jumps on Bill’s back and digs his heels in his side.","“For Heaven’s sake,” says Bill, “hurry back, Sam, as soon as you can. I wish we hadn’t made the ransom more than a thousand. Say, you quit kicking me or I’ll get up and warm you good.”","I walked over to Poplar Cove and sat around the postoffice and store, talking with the chawbacons that came in to trade. One whiskerando says that he hears Summit is all upset on account of Elder Ebenezer Dorset’s boy having been lost or stolen. That was all I wanted to know. I bought some smoking tobacco, referred casually to the price of black-eyed peas, posted my letter surreptitiously and came away. The postmaster said the mail-carrier would come by in an hour to take the mail on to Summit.","When I got back to the cave Bill and the boy were not to be found. I explored the vicinity of the cave, and risked a yodel or two, but there was no response.","So I lighted my pipe and sat down on a mossy bank to await developments.","In about half an hour I heard the bushes rustle, and Bill wabbled out into the little glade in front of the cave. Behind him was the kid, stepping softly like a scout, with a broad grin on his face. Bill stopped, took off his hat and wiped his face with a red handkerchief. The kid stopped about eight feet behind him.","“Sam,” says Bill, “I suppose you’ll think I’m a renegade, but I couldn’t help it. I’m a grown person with masculine proclivities and habits of self-defense, but there is a time when all systems of egotism and predominance fail. The boy is gone. I have sent him home. All is off. There was martyrs in old times,” goes on Bill, “that suffered death rather than give up the particular graft they enjoyed. None of ’em ever was subjugated to such supernatural tortures as I have been. I tried to be faithful to our articles of depredation; but there came a limit.”","“What’s the trouble, Bill?” I asks him.","“I was rode,” says Bill, “the ninety miles to the stockade, not barring an inch. Then, when the settlers was rescued, I was given oats. Sand ain’t a palatable substitute. And then, for an hour I had to try to explain to him why there was nothin’ in holes, how a road can run both ways and what makes the grass green. I tell you, Sam, a human can only stand so much. I takes him by the neck of his clothes and drags him down the mountain. On the way he kicks my legs black-and-blue from the knees down; and I’ve got to have two or three bites on my thumb and hand cauterized.","“But he’s gone”—continues Bill—“gone home. I showed him the road to Summit and kicked him about eight feet nearer there at one kick. I’m sorry we lose the ransom; but it was either that or Bill Driscoll to the madhouse.”","Bill is puffing and blowing, but there is a look of ineffable peace and growing content on his rose-pink features.","“Bill,” says I, “there isn’t any heart disease in your family, is there?”","“No,” says Bill, “nothing chronic except malaria and accidents. Why?”","“Then you might turn around,” says I, “and have a look behind you.”","Bill turns and sees the boy, and loses his complexion and sits down plump on the ground and begins to pluck aimlessly at grass and little sticks. For an hour I was afraid for his mind. And then I told him that my scheme was to put the whole job through immediately and that we would get the ransom and be off with it by midnight if old Dorset fell in with our proposition. So Bill braced up enough to give the kid a weak sort of a smile and a promise to play the Russian in a Japanese war with him as soon as he felt a little better.","I had a scheme for collecting that ransom without danger of being caught by counterplots that ought to commend itself to professional kidnappers. The tree under which the answer was to be left—and the money later on—was close to the road fence with big, bare fields on all sides. If a gang of constables should be watching for any one to come for the note they could see him a long way off crossing the fields or in the road. But no, sirree! At half-past eight I was up in that tree as well hidden as a tree toad, waiting for the messenger to arrive.","Exactly on time, a half-grown boy rides up the road on a bicycle, locates the pasteboard box at the foot of the fence-post, slips a folded piece of paper into it and pedals away again back toward Summit.","I waited an hour and then concluded the thing was square. I slid down the tree, got the note, slipped along the fence till I struck the woods, and was back at the cave in another half an hour. I opened the note, got near the lantern and read it to Bill. It was written with a pen in a crabbed hand, and the sum and substance of it was this:","Two Desperate Men.","Gentlemen: I received your letter to-day by post, in regard to the ransom you ask for the return of my son. I think you are a little high in your demands, and I hereby make you a counter-proposition, which I am inclined to believe you will accept. You bring Johnny home and pay me two hundred and fifty dollars in cash, and I agree to take him off your hands. You had better come at night, for the neighbours believe he is lost, and I couldn’t be responsible for what they would do to anybody they saw bringing him back.","Very respectfully, EBENEZER DORSET.","“Great pirates of Penzance!” says I; “of all the impudent—”","But I glanced at Bill, and hesitated. He had the most appealing look in his eyes I ever saw on the face of a dumb or a talking brute.","“Sam,” says he, “what’s two hundred and fifty dollars, after all? We’ve got the money. One more night of this kid will send me to a bed in Bedlam. Besides being a thorough gentleman, I think Mr. Dorset is a spendthrift for making us such a liberal offer. You ain’t going to let the chance go, are you?”","“Tell you the truth, Bill,” says I, “this little he ewe lamb has somewhat got on my nerves too. We’ll take him home, pay the ransom and make our get-away.”","We took him home that night. We got him to go by telling him that his father had bought a silver-mounted rifle and a pair of moccasins for him, and we were going to hunt bears the next day.","It was just twelve o’clock when we knocked at Ebenezer’s front door. Just at the moment when I should have been abstracting the fifteen hundred dollars from the box under the tree, according to the original proposition, Bill was counting out two hundred and fifty dollars into Dorset’s hand.","When the kid found out we were going to leave him at home he started up a howl like a calliope and fastened himself as tight as a leech to Bill’s leg. His father peeled him away gradually, like a porous plaster.","“How long can you hold him?” asks Bill.","“I’m not as strong as I used to be,” says old Dorset, “but I think I can promise you ten minutes.”","“Enough,” says Bill. “In ten minutes I shall cross the Central, Southern and Middle Western States, and be legging it trippingly for the Canadian border.”","And, as dark as it was, and as fat as Bill was, and as good a runner as I am, he was a good mile and a half out of Summit before I could catch up with him."],"glossary":{"philoprogenitiveness":"çocuk sevgisi","undeleterious":"zararsız (komik biçimde ağdalı bir sözcük)","lackadaisical":"isteksiz, ilgisiz","diatribe":"sert, acı eleştiri","forecloser":"haciz yapan kişi (borç yüzünden malı elinden alan)","tight":"cimri, eli sıkı","brake":"çalılık, sık ağaçlık","rubber":"boynunu uzatıp bakmak (argo)","dassent":"cesaret edemezsin (dare not)","pesky":"baş belası, sinir bozucu","hoss":"at (horse'un konuşma biçimi)","chawbacons":"köylü bozuntuları, görgüsüz taşralılar","peremptory":"kesin, emredici","decry":"kötülemek, küçümsemek","acceded":"razı oldu, kabul etti","reconnoitre":"keşif yapmak, çevreyi kollamak","calliope":"buharlı org (çok gürültülü bir müzik aleti)","graft":"kazançlı iş, hile ile para kazanma","dote":"göz bebeği yapmak, çok sevip şımartmak","plump":"pat diye, ağırlıkla"},"phrases":[{"en":"pull off","tr":"(zor bir işi) başarmak, becermek"},{"en":"put up a fight","tr":"direnmek, kıyasıya savaşmak"},{"en":"get on someone's nerves","tr":"sinirlerine dokunmak, canını sıkmak"},{"en":"catch up with","tr":"yetişmek, yakalamak"},{"en":"fall in with","tr":"(bir fikre) uymak, kabul etmek"},{"en":"come to your senses","tr":"aklı başına gelmek, kendine gelmek"},{"en":"have the time of your life","tr":"hayatının en güzel zamanını yaşamak"},{"en":"without batting an eye","tr":"gözünü bile kırpmadan, hiç istifini bozmadan"},{"en":"take a chance","tr":"şansını denemek, riske girmek"},{"en":"by and by","tr":"birazdan, bir süre sonra"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do Sam and Bill kidnap the boy?","options":["To teach him a lesson","To get ransom money from his father","Because he is lost","Because he asks them to"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"İkisi, çocuğun babası Ebenezer Dorset'ten fidye almak ister; dolandırıcılık planı için paraya ihtiyaçları vardır."},{"q":"What game does the boy play in the cave?","options":["Football with Bill","Cards with Sam","Indians, with himself as Red Chief","A quiet game of hide and seek"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Çocuk kızılderili oyunu oynar; kendisi 'Red Chief' olur, Bill'e ve Sam'e de esir rolü verir."},{"q":"In the 'Black Scout' game, what must Bill do?","options":["Cook breakfast","Be the boy's horse","Write the letter","Guard the cave"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Çocuk 'Kara İzci' olur ve Bill'e at olmasını söyler; Bill da dört ayak üzerinde onu taşır."},{"q":"What does Mr. Dorset say in his reply to the kidnappers?","options":["He will pay $1,500","He will pay $2,000","He will call the police","He will take the boy back if they pay him $250"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Dorset, oğlunu geri almak için fidye ödemek yerine karşı teklif yapar: ona 250 dolar ödenirse çocuğu geri alacaktır."},{"q":"How does the story end?","options":["The men return the boy and run away","The police arrest the men","The boy escapes alone","The men get the ransom and leave"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Adamlar çocuğu eve götürür, Dorset'e 250 dolar öder ve hemen kaçarlar."}],"words":4204,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-voice-on-the-wrong-tape","type":"story","title":"The Voice on the Wrong Tape","author":"Lantern Original","level":"B2","genre":"mystery","summaryTr":"Bir kasabanın ses arşivi için hazırlanan sergide, son feribot yolculuğuna ait kayıt yerini anlamsız bir sessizliğe bırakır. Ses teknisyeni Nessa, kayıp kaseti ararken bir hatıranın kim tarafından ve hangi amaçla değiştirilebileceğini sorgular.","paragraphs":["The first sound on the tape was a chair moving across a wooden floor. After that came a cough, a faint metallic knock, and almost twelve minutes of silence. Nessa removed her headphones and looked through the glass wall of the listening room. In the exhibition hall, a photograph showed Captain Fen standing beside the old river ferry. Beneath it, freshly printed letters promised visitors the sound of the ferry's final journey. They would hear its bell, its engine, and the captain saying goodbye. Instead, Nessa had spent twelve minutes listening to somebody breathe in a quiet room.","She checked the cassette label again. Number twenty-seven: Last ferry morning. It matched the catalogue, the box, and the list of recordings chosen for the exhibition. The tape itself was grey, with a white label and a small brown mark at one corner. Nessa had played it the previous afternoon. Then it had contained exactly what the catalogue promised, followed by an unexpected conversation she had not yet examined closely. Now the sounds of the river were gone. She pressed rewind, keeping her finger clear of the red recording button. Whatever had happened, she would not add another mistake.","The exhibition was due to open on Saturday. It was Thursday morning. Nessa had been hired for three days to prepare digital copies of recordings donated by local residents. The oldest tapes needed careful handling, so she had started by checking the equipment and making a list of damaged cases. Number twenty-seven had been marked as the main attraction. Its owner, Elliot Fen, was paying for much of the exhibition. His grandfather had run the ferry until a road bridge opened thirty years earlier. Elliot wanted visitors to hear a piece of the town's life that photographs could not preserve.","Clara Moss, the exhibition organiser, entered carrying two rolls of posters. Nessa played the beginning of the tape through a speaker. Clara frowned at the scrape of the chair. 'Are you sure you have the right side?' They listened to the other side, which was empty. Clara put the posters down. Her expression changed from irritation to alarm. 'Could the machine have erased it?' Nessa said that was possible only if someone had recorded over it. Simply playing a cassette could not replace river sounds with a quiet room. A person had been involved, even if the result was accidental.","Clara asked whether Nessa had already made a copy. She had not. The recorder's left channel had failed during testing, and she had spent the afternoon replacing a cable. She had played number twenty-seven only long enough to confirm its contents. At five, she had locked the original in the small steel cabinet with the other selected tapes. The cabinet key stayed in a labelled drawer at reception. This was a community archive, not a bank. Staff and volunteers borrowed it whenever they needed an item, signing a sheet that lay beside the drawer.","Four people had entered the building after Nessa left. Clara had worked on captions until seven. Elliot had delivered a model ferry shortly after six. Owen Price, the volunteer who had repaired the archive's cassette machines, had come to collect a tool bag. Salma, the cleaner, had stayed until half past eight. Nessa wrote their names in her notebook. Clara watched her. 'Please do not make this sound like a crime.' Nessa said they first needed to understand whether the wrong cassette had been put in the case. A simple mix-up was still possible, and easier to correct than an erased recording.","She began with the objects rather than the people. The plastic case was the one she remembered, with a crack across its lower corner. Inside lay a folded paper describing the recording. The cassette's label was handwritten in blue ink. Nessa had photographed each tape the day before to document its condition. She opened the photograph on her laptop and enlarged it. Yesterday's cassette had a small triangle cut from the label's left edge. The tape in front of her did not. There was also no brown mark in the photograph. The recording had not changed. The cassette had.","Clara sat down abruptly. 'Then somebody still has the original.' Nessa hoped so. She placed the substitute in a clear envelope and wrote the time on it. Then she searched the cabinet and all the boxes waiting to be catalogued. There were three other grey cassettes, but none had the damaged label shown in her photograph. One contained church music, one a birthday party, and one instructions for a language course. The missing tape had not simply fallen behind another case. By eleven, Nessa knew exactly what she was searching for, but not who had moved it.","Owen arrived with a replacement cable and listened to the news without surprise. He was a thin man in his thirties who usually spoke to machines before greeting people. 'I told you the key should not stay in that drawer,' he said to Clara. She answered that they had never lost anything before. Nessa asked when he had visited the previous evening. He said he arrived around six forty, went into the workshop, collected his tools, and left. He had not borrowed the key. The signing sheet supported him: the final entry was Clara's name at six ten.","When Nessa showed him the substitute cassette, he recognised the brown mark. It was a burn from the end of a hot tool. He had used that cassette to test old recorders because nobody wanted the original contents. The label had said Test three. Now a new label covered it. Owen carefully lifted one loose corner, without removing the whole thing, and showed Nessa the old writing beneath. The substitute had come from the archive's own workshop. That reduced the chance that a visitor had planned the theft in advance. It did not tell them who had taken it.","The workshop contained boxes of cables, a large table, and two recorders. One machine had a cracked battery cover. The other belonged to Owen and had a working microphone. Nessa asked whether he had tested it recently. 'Yesterday afternoon, before you arrived,' he said. 'I recorded the room for a while, then played it back.' He remembered Clara asking him to lower the volume because she was talking on the phone. The chair scrape might therefore be part of his test. The silent tape was not necessarily a recording of the person who replaced the original. Nessa crossed out one tempting idea.","Elliot came at noon in a dark coat, carrying a folder of family photographs. He was angry when Clara explained the problem. At first, he directed his anger towards Nessa, asking why she had not copied the recording immediately. She described the faulty cable and showed him the two cassette photographs. Elliot stopped speaking. He recognised the cut in the original label. His grandfather had done that to distinguish this tape from a similar one. 'The recording belongs to me,' he said. 'If it does not turn up today, I will report it missing.' Nessa told him that was his decision.","Asked about the previous evening, Elliot said he had arrived at six fifteen and stayed for about twenty minutes. Clara had helped him carry the model into the hall. He had looked at the display and left. Had he opened the cabinet? No. Had he listened to the tape before donating it? Not for several years. He remembered the ferry bell and his grandfather speaking. Nessa asked about the conversation after the farewell. Elliot looked puzzled. 'There is only engine noise after that.' She did not correct him yet. Her memory of the voices was brief and uncertain.","Salma was easier to find than Nessa expected. She worked at a nearby bakery during the day and recognised Clara's number when Nessa called. She had seen Elliot leave, then Owen. Clara had remained upstairs for a while before saying goodnight. Salma had cleaned the listening room last because somebody was using it earlier. Nessa asked who. 'I saw a coat through the glass,' Salma said. 'Dark blue or black. I did not look closely.' Both Clara and Elliot had worn dark coats. Salma could not give an exact time, and Nessa made sure not to turn her uncertainty into a fact.","Back in the hall, Nessa examined the listening room. Its glass wall faced the main entrance, but half of it was covered by a hanging exhibition banner. A person at the recorder could be seen only from certain angles. The chair had felt pads underneath its legs. It would not make the harsh scrape heard on the substitute. The workshop chairs had bare wooden feet, confirming Owen's explanation. Beside the recorder lay a stack of blank labels, a blue pen, and a ruler. Anyone preparing the exhibition could have made the false label without bringing materials from outside.","Nessa looked at the writing again. Last ferry morning appeared in neat capital letters, just as it did in the catalogue. The number twenty-seven was written beneath it. There was one small difference: the writer had added an apostrophe to Ferrys in a second line describing the ferry's bell. It was a reasonable correction of the old label's mistake. It suggested someone who cared about presentation even while hiding something. Clara corrected captions for a living. Elliot was a printer. Owen complained about unclear equipment labels. Nessa noted the detail without allowing it to choose a suspect for her.","She asked Clara to show her the exhibition files. The main caption described Captain Fen as the last person to guide the ferry across the river. A longer paragraph praised his devotion to the town. Next to it was a planned listening extract lasting three minutes and forty seconds. Nessa remembered that the voices she had heard began later than that. 'Who chose the end point?' she asked. Clara said Elliot had wanted a short piece for visitors. It ended after the farewell. There was no reason to include several minutes of engine noise. Her explanation sounded sensible, except that Nessa had heard words among those sounds.","At four, Owen returned to collect the broken cable. He glanced at the caption and made an unpleasant sound. 'Still the same story, then.' Nessa asked what he meant. His mother, Lena Price, had worked on the ferry. According to her, Captain Fen had not steered its final crossing. His eyesight had become poor, and Lena had taken the controls while he stood beside the passengers. 'She never got her name on anything,' Owen said. He had asked Clara to change the caption. Clara said a family memory was not enough evidence to contradict the existing records.","Here was a motive that seemed almost too clear. Owen wanted the exhibition to tell a different story. He knew the machines, owned the test cassette, and had entered the building after the original was locked away. If he had removed the main attraction, he could delay the opening or force Clara to change the display. Nessa asked why he had not mentioned the disagreement earlier. 'Because you would look at me exactly as you are doing now.' He folded the cable and put it in his bag. She realised that she had stopped asking questions and started arranging his answers into a familiar shape.","Instead of accusing him, Nessa asked to speak to his mother. Lena lived in a flat overlooking the river and agreed to a visit that evening. She was older than Nessa expected, with strong hands and a quiet way of speaking. On her kitchen wall hung a photograph of the ferry crew. Captain Fen stood at the centre. Lena stood beside the engine cover, half hidden by another man's shoulder. She pointed to herself without bitterness. 'There I am.' She did not claim to have owned the ferry or run its business. She had worked on it for twelve years.","Lena remembered the final morning clearly. A recorder had stood on a box near the front window. Captain Fen had welcomed the passengers and made his speech. Then Lena had taken the ferry across. Near the far bank, he had said something to her about the last rope. Nessa asked for the exact words. Lena shook her head. 'Memory is not a machine. I remember what happened. I cannot give you every sentence.' Nessa liked that answer more than a perfect quotation. Lena had no copy of the tape. She had not known it still existed until Owen mentioned the exhibition.","Nessa asked why Lena had not corrected the story before. She said that at first there had been nothing to correct. Everybody who worked on the ferry knew who had done what. Later, newspaper pieces became shorter and photographs were used without explanation. Captain Fen became the whole ferry. 'He was not a bad man,' she added. 'He paid me fairly. But he liked people looking at him.' Lena did not want the exhibition cancelled. She wanted the work described accurately. If the tape proved she was mistaken, she would accept that too. Nessa left with more questions and a smaller appetite for easy blame.","At home that night, Nessa reviewed the photograph of the original cassette. On the right spool was a narrow strip of red tape, used to attach a repaired section. Owen had told her he had never opened number twenty-seven because it played normally. Elliot remembered his grandfather repairing it after a jam. This meant the cassette could be identified even if somebody removed the label. Nessa sent the image to Clara and asked that nothing from the exhibition be thrown away. Clara replied immediately: Understood. Then a second message arrived asking whether the opening could still happen without the sound.","The question disturbed Nessa. Clara had spent the morning treating the tape as essential. Now she was considering its absence as a practical inconvenience. That might simply be sensible planning under pressure. Nessa knew organisers had to keep working even when something went wrong. Still, she wrote down the change. She also remembered Clara's first question: had Nessa already made a copy? Anyone would ask that, but the answer mattered differently to someone who wanted to recover the recording and someone who wanted certain words to disappear. The same sentence could point in opposite directions.","On Friday morning, Nessa asked each person to describe their evening again, without suggesting that earlier answers were wrong. Elliot repeated his times and produced a parking receipt ending at six thirty-eight. It did not prove every minute, but it supported his account of leaving early. Owen showed a message he had sent his partner from the bus stop at six fifty-two. Salma remembered that Clara said goodnight just after the radio news began in the kitchen. The news started at seven. Nessa now had a likely sequence, though she kept the word likely in front of it.","Clara said she had spent the period after Elliot left changing captions upstairs. She had used the cabinet key earlier to check a photograph number, which explained her signature at six ten. Nessa asked whether she had entered the listening room. 'Briefly. I wanted to hear whether the new speaker worked.' Which cassette had she played? Clara answered, 'The ferry tape, obviously.' It was the first time she had admitted listening to it that evening. When Nessa asked why she had not mentioned this before, Clara said nobody had asked specifically. That was true, but it was not reassuring.","According to Clara, the recording had sounded normal. She had stopped it after the captain's farewell, returned it to its case, and locked the cabinet. Nessa asked whether she had noticed a chair scrape at the start. Clara said no. If that account was accurate, the substitution had happened later. Only Owen and Salma were known to remain after Elliot left, and Clara herself stayed until seven. Nessa could not eliminate anybody on timing alone. She thanked Clara and went to the workshop, where she played Owen's other test cassettes. Each began with him speaking the date and naming the machine.","The substitute did not contain that spoken introduction. Its quiet room recording began abruptly with the chair moving. Owen listened and remembered that on Wednesday he had stopped the test, moved the cassette forward, and recorded again. The first part still ought to be on the tape. Nessa checked the tape counter and rewound completely. A few seconds before the scrape, at very low volume, was Owen saying, 'Second test, after lunch.' She had missed it because the machine had not been fully rewound when she first played it. The discovery settled one question and created another.","The false label could not have been on the test cassette while Owen was using it, because he remembered reading Test three. Therefore the substitution happened after his afternoon work. More importantly, the cassette had been left partly played, close to the chair scrape. Someone checking only its opening might believe it was blank. Nessa wondered whether the person who replaced the original had chosen the test cassette because it looked empty, without listening to all of it. That would fit a hurried decision better than a carefully prepared theft. She began looking for evidence of what had changed during Wednesday evening.","She found it in a wastepaper box under the caption desk. Clara had asked everyone to separate clean paper for reuse, so old drafts remained stacked rather than torn up. One draft of the ferry caption included a sentence absent from the final version: The last crossing was completed by the crew under Captain Fen's direction. Another named Lena Price as a crew member. A third returned to the claim that the captain himself guided the ferry across. Nessa arranged them by the printed times at the bottom. All three had been produced between six forty and six fifty-eight on Wednesday.","The drafts did not prove theft. They did prove that Clara had been reconsidering the story that evening. Nessa took them upstairs. Clara looked at the pages and sighed. Owen's complaint had made her try different versions, she said. She had rejected them because there was still no proof. Nessa asked why the most general version required more proof than the specific claim about Captain Fen steering. Clara answered that the existing caption had already been approved by Elliot. For the first time, she sounded defensive about the exhibition rather than the missing object.","Nessa asked to see the email approving the caption. Clara opened the shared project mailbox. Elliot's message contained several corrections, but also a clear sentence: If the archive finds an error, please let me know before printing. He had not demanded that his grandfather be described as the person at the controls. Clara had inferred that changing the story might upset the sponsor. When Nessa pointed this out, Clara said the situation was more complicated than one email. The building needed funding for its winter programme. Elliot had offered to help. Nessa could see the pressure, but pressure was not evidence of what he would actually do.","Downstairs, Salma arrived with the bakery's spare cardboard boxes. She saw Nessa examining the listening room and said she had remembered another detail. The person inside had been holding a white card close to the glass, perhaps reading it in the light. It had looked like a postcard from a distance. Nessa asked whether it might have been a cassette label. Salma said she could not tell. Nessa did not press her. Beside the recorder, however, the stack of blank labels had a missing corner where one label had been peeled away. That was ordinary enough in a busy archive, but the white card now had a possible purpose.","The decisive clue came from the model ferry. Elliot wanted to take it home if the exhibition could not open as promised. Nessa helped lift its clear cover and noticed a paper envelope beneath the wooden base. It contained the model's instructions, a small screwdriver, and a dark blue receipt. Elliot recognised the receipt. Clara had placed it there when he delivered the model, to keep it from getting lost. It was for a storage box she had bought that afternoon. The shop's product description said Four cassette cases, clear. Nessa looked at the new cases stacked beside the display.","Three cases were empty. The fourth was missing. This did not show where the original cassette had gone, but it suggested it might have been moved into a new case rather than carried loose. Nessa asked Clara about it. Clara said the fourth case had been cracked and thrown away. Nessa remembered her instruction that nothing should be discarded. Clara said she had thrown it out before receiving that message. Owen, who had unpacked the cases for her on Wednesday, quietly said they had all been sound. Clara answered too quickly that he must have missed the crack.","Nessa did not search Clara's bag or coat. Instead, she asked everyone to stop moving exhibition material until they had checked the remaining archive boxes together. Elliot agreed and said he would make the missing-property report after lunch if the tape was still absent. Clara looked towards the stairs. On the upper landing stood several boxes marked Winter programme. They contained display material that would not be opened for months. Nessa asked whether the spare cassette case could have been packed by mistake. Clara said she would check them herself. Nessa answered that it would be quicker if they all helped.","Clara remained still. Then she said, 'It is in the box with the school photographs.' Nobody spoke for a moment. Nessa asked what was in the box. 'The tape.' Clara sat on the bottom stair as if her legs had suddenly weakened. Elliot went upstairs, but Nessa asked him to wait while she fetched the box with him. Inside, between two folders of photographs, was a clear cassette case. The grey tape had a triangular cut in its label. Through the window above the right spool, Nessa could see the narrow red repair strip. It was number twenty-seven.","They played it together. The ferry bell rang, followed by the low engine and passengers talking. Captain Fen welcomed everyone and thanked the town for supporting his family. He spoke slowly, enjoying the occasion. After his farewell, footsteps crossed the deck. Then his voice came again, quieter now. 'Lena, take her over. I cannot see the far marker clearly today.' A woman answered, 'I have it.' The engine changed its note. Several minutes later, the captain thanked Lena for the smooth crossing and asked her to bring the final rope. Nothing in the conversation sounded secret or shameful.","Clara had heard those words on Wednesday. She admitted that she had played farther than the chosen extract because she wanted to check for useful background sound. The conversation contradicted the central caption. Elliot had just spoken enthusiastically about extending his support to other projects. Clara imagined him seeing the correction as an attack on his grandfather. She tried different captions, disliked each one, and decided to remove the tape until after the opening. The test cassette had offered a way to make the loss look like an old recording that had failed. She had assumed its quiet contents were blank.","'I was going to find it later,' she said. Elliot looked at her for a long time. 'And tell me what? That my grandfather's voice had come back?' Clara covered her face with her hands. Nessa asked why she had not simply explained the recording. Clara said she had been afraid of losing the funding, the winter programme, and her own position. Once she had made the substitution, every question had required another answer that was not quite true. Hiding the original among future displays had bought time. It had also made an honest return increasingly difficult.","Elliot did not offer immediate forgiveness. He said his donation had included an agreement that the original would be protected and returned after copying. Clara had broken that trust. He wanted a written account of what had happened and a meeting with the archive committee before deciding about further support. He would postpone reporting the cassette as missing because it was now recovered, but he would not pretend there had been an innocent mix-up. Nessa placed the original in a new envelope and wrote a receipt when she returned it to him after making two checked digital copies.","Owen stood beside the window, looking less satisfied than Nessa expected. He said he should have insisted that they interview his mother before printing anything. Clara apologised for dismissing Lena's account and for allowing suspicion to fall on him. He accepted that she had spoken but did not say everything was settled. Nessa thought this was fair. Recovering an object could happen in minutes. Repairing what people had said and assumed might take much longer. She also apologised to Owen for treating his anger as evidence. He nodded. 'It was a reason to ask,' he said. 'Not a reason to decide.'","They telephoned Lena and played the relevant section with Elliot's permission. She listened without interrupting. After the captain's thanks, she gave a small laugh. 'He always asked for that rope before we were properly stopped.' Elliot asked whether she would come to the exhibition and help correct the crew information. Lena said she would, provided nobody turned her into the only person who had done any work. There had been four people keeping the ferry running on the final day. She could name them all. Elliot found a pencil and began writing, asking her to spell one surname twice.","The opening moved from Saturday morning to Saturday afternoon. A short notice explained that the archive was correcting information and replacing a damaged display panel. It did not invent an equipment failure. Clara stepped back from handling original recordings while the committee reviewed the incident. Nessa set up a simple borrowing record with a named person responsible for each item. The cabinet key moved out of the public drawer. These changes would not make dishonesty impossible, but they would make objects easier to trace and uncertain memories less important. The archive could remain welcoming without treating every precaution as an insult.","On Saturday, the new caption described the final crossing as the work of a crew. Captain Fen still stood at the centre of the photograph, because that was where he had stood when it was taken. Beside it was a second photograph Lena had brought from home, showing the engine room with two workers inside. The listening extract continued beyond the captain's speech. Visitors could hear him asking for help, Lena answering, and the ferry reaching the bank. The longer recording did not make him smaller. It gave the scene more people and made the work easier to understand.","Nessa watched a child press the play button while his father read the caption. When the engine changed its sound, the child asked who was driving. His father pointed to Lena's name. A few steps away, Lena and Elliot were discussing a face in another photograph. Clara stood at the welcome desk, answering questions quietly. There would be a committee meeting on Monday and difficult decisions after it. For now, the original cassette was back with its owner, the copies had been checked, and the story on the wall matched the voices. Nessa closed her notebook and listened to the ferry arrive."],"glossary":{"spool":"kasetin içindeki bant makarası","caption":"fotoğraf veya sergi açıklaması","catalogue":"arşivdeki eserlerin kayıt listesi","extract":"bir kayıttan seçilmiş bölüm","substitution":"yerine başka bir şey koyma","marker":"nehirde yön gösteren işaret","pads":"keçe koruyucu parçalar","deck":"güverte"},"phrases":[{"en":"in advance","tr":"önceden"},{"en":"in front of","tr":"önünde"},{"en":"under pressure","tr":"baskı altında"},{"en":"a simple mix-up","tr":"basit bir karışıklık"},{"en":"without interrupting","tr":"sözünü kesmeden"},{"en":"rather than","tr":"... yerine"},{"en":"stepped back","tr":"görevden geri çekildi"},{"en":"sound like a crime","tr":"suç gibi görünmek, suç izlenimi vermek"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What first proves that the original cassette was replaced rather than erased?","options":["A difference in the printed exhibition dates","The damaged label visible in Nessa's earlier photograph","A message from Lena","The missing cabinet key"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Önceki fotoğraftaki etiketin üçgen kesik köşesi yeni kasette yoktur; fiziksel kaset değiştirilmiştir."},{"q":"Why is the quiet recording not reliable evidence of who made the substitution?","options":["It was recorded by Owen during an earlier equipment test","It contains several clearly identified voices","Nessa accidentally recorded it herself","It was made thirty years earlier on the ferry"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Odadaki sessiz kayıt, Owen'ın daha önce yaptığı cihaz testine aittir; kaseti değiştiren kişinin ses kaydı değildir."},{"q":"What assumption leads Clara to hide the original?","options":["Lena wants the whole exhibition cancelled","Owen intends to sell the cassette","Correcting the captain's role will make Elliot withdraw his support","The original is too damaged to play safely"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Clara, açıklamanın düzeltilmesinin bağışçıyı kızdırıp desteği kaybettireceğini varsayar; bunu Elliot'a sormaz."},{"q":"What does the recovered recording establish?","options":["Captain Fen never worked on the ferry","The final journey was cancelled","Elliot changed the recording himself","Lena operated the ferry during its final crossing"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Kaptan karşı kıyıdaki işareti net göremediği için Lena'dan feribotu kullanmasını ister ve sonra ona teşekkür eder."},{"q":"Which statement best describes the ending?","options":["The recording is recovered and corrected, while the breach of trust still needs review","All characters immediately forgive Clara","The exhibition removes every mention of Captain Fen","Nessa keeps the original cassette as evidence forever"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Kayıt sahibine döner ve sergi düzeltilir; ancak Clara'nın güveni zedeleyen davranışı kurul tarafından ayrıca ele alınacaktır."}],"words":4609,"length":"long"},{"id":"why-we-laugh","type":"article","title":"Why We Laugh: How Comic Stories Work","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"B2","genre":"comedy","summaryTr":"Kahkahanın ardındaki mekanizmayı inceleyen bu yazı; beklenti ile gerçek arasındaki uçurumu, komik zamanlamayı ve ironinin üç türünü Saki ve O. Henry'nin öyküleri üzerinden anlatıyor.","paragraphs":["Explain a joke and it dies. Anyone who has tried to describe why something is funny knows this small tragedy: the laughter itself lasts a fraction of a second, but the machinery behind it is surprisingly complicated. Nevertheless, if we look closely at comic fiction, the same few patterns appear again and again. Once we recognise them, we become better readers of stories such as Saki's \"The Open Window\" or O. Henry's \"The Ransom of Red Chief\", and we also learn something about how English behaves when it is trying to be playful.","## The gap between expectation and reality","Philosophers have tried to explain laughter for more than two thousand years, and no single theory has won universal agreement. For readers of fiction, however, the most useful idea is probably incongruity. According to this view, we laugh when something does not fit the pattern we were expecting. A joke creates a small, reasonable expectation in our minds and then breaks it, and the sudden gap between what we predicted and what actually happens can give rise to amusement. A short story can do the same thing across several pages instead of a single sentence.","Consider \"The Open Window\" by Saki, the pen name of Hector Hugh Munro, who was killed in the First World War in 1916. A nervous visitor named Framton Nuttel arrives at a country house hoping to calm his nerves. While he waits for the lady of the house, her young niece, Vera, tells him a haunting story about a family tragedy and a window that is left open every evening. We expect a ghost story, and for a while the text seems to offer one. The comedy comes from the discovery that Vera is not a messenger from the world of the dead but a brilliant improviser who enjoys deceiving strangers. Saki never explains the joke. He simply lets the distance between the visitor's terror and the reader's growing suspicion do the work.","## Three old explanations of laughter","Older theories help us to see why the same situation can be funny in different ways. Aristotle noted that comedy imitates people who are somewhat worse than average, but only in ways that are ridiculous rather than painful. Centuries later, the English philosopher Thomas Hobbes suggested that laughter expresses a sudden sense of superiority over other people, or over our own earlier selves. A third tradition, associated with Herbert Spencer and later with Sigmund Freud, treats laughter as a release of nervous energy: we laugh when a tension, often connected with something forbidden or frightening, is suddenly allowed to escape. Finally, the French philosopher Henri Bergson argued in his book Laughter (1900) that we laugh when a living person behaves like a machine, repeating the same rigid action even though the situation has changed.","Each of these ideas fits \"The Ransom of Red Chief\". Two small-time criminals kidnap a boy in order to demand money from his father, but the child is so energetic, noisy and violent that the kidnappers end up paying the father to take him back. Hobbes would point to our pleasure at seeing arrogant schemers defeated by a child. Spencer and Freud would remind us that many readers secretly enjoy a fantasy of total disorder in which no adult can control the situation. Bergson would notice that the criminals keep following their rigid plan long after reality has made it ridiculous.","## Timing on the page","We usually associate comic timing with stage performers, who can pause for exactly the right length of time before a punchline. A writer has no voice or face, yet the same control is possible through sentence structure. The simplest technique is delay: the writer holds back the surprising word until the end of the sentence, so that the reader has no chance to guess it in advance. Another technique is the rule of three. Two examples establish a pattern, and the third one breaks it. A further trick is contrast of length, in which a long, elegant, carefully balanced sentence is followed by a very short one that collapses everything the reader has just been told.","Understatement works in a similar way. When a narrator describes a disaster in calm, polite language, the gap between the event and the tone becomes funny in itself. Much of Saki's humour depends on this kind of polished, drawing-room politeness, while O. Henry often prefers a talkative narrator who inflates small matters with enormous vocabulary and then deflates them with a slangy phrase. In both cases, the effect is partly a matter of rhythm. Read a comic passage aloud, or even silently in your head, and you will often feel the beat before you understand it.","## Irony: saying one thing and meaning another","Irony is the engine inside most comic fiction, and it comes in three main forms. Verbal irony occurs when words mean the opposite of what they say, as when a narrator calls a terrible plan \"sensible\". Situational irony occurs when events turn out the reverse of what the characters intended: the kidnappers in O. Henry's story set out to make a profit and finish by paying to escape their victim. Dramatic irony occurs when the reader knows something that a character does not. In \"The Open Window\" we gradually understand that Vera is inventing her story, while poor Mr Nuttel remains completely convinced. Because we know more than he does, we feel like secret partners of the storyteller.","This partnership is important. Dramatic irony flatters the reader. It says, in effect, \"you are clever enough to see what this character cannot.\" Consequently, comic fiction depends on a very particular kind of trust between author and audience: the writer must give us enough clues to notice the joke without explaining it, and we must be willing to read between the lines.","## Why comedy deserves respect","Comic stories are sometimes treated as light entertainment, but they perform serious work. They deflate pretension, they allow us to look at fear, failure and embarrassment from a safe distance, and they build social bonds, since laughing together proves that two people share the same assumptions. A comic story also tests whether we are really paying attention. Missing one small detail can mean missing the entire joke.","For learners of English, humour is therefore one of the most demanding forms of reading, because it depends on tone, cultural knowledge and wordplay as well as vocabulary. However, it is also among the most rewarding. When a funny line finally clicks in a foreign language, it shows a depth of understanding that no vocabulary test can measure. Here is a practical tip: when you cannot see why a passage is funny, ask yourself what the writer wanted you to expect, and what you were given instead. The answer is almost always hidden in that gap."],"glossary":{"incongruity":"uyumsuzluk, beklenenle gerçekleşen arasındaki çelişki","irony":"ironi, söylenenle kastedilenin (ya da beklenenle olanın) birbirine zıt olması","understatement":"olduğundan az gösterme, hafife alarak anlatma","punchline":"fıkranın son vuruşu, komik asıl cümle","improviser":"doğaçlama yapan kişi","kidnap":"kaçırmak (fidye için)"},"phrases":[{"en":"give rise to","tr":"yol açmak, doğurmak"},{"en":"sense of superiority","tr":"üstünlük duygusu"},{"en":"the rule of three","tr":"üçlü kural (üçüncü öğenin beklentiyi bozması)"},{"en":"read between the lines","tr":"satır aralarını okumak, ima edileni anlamak"},{"en":"from a safe distance","tr":"güvenli bir mesafeden"},{"en":"set out to","tr":"bir amaçla yola çıkmak, -meyi amaçlamak"},{"en":"end up","tr":"sonunda ... durumunda kalmak"},{"en":"in effect","tr":"aslında, fiilen"},{"en":"a matter of","tr":"-e bağlı bir şey, ... meselesi"}],"quiz":[{"q":"According to the incongruity theory described in the article, why do we laugh?","options":["Because we feel nervous about forbidden subjects","Because something breaks the pattern we were expecting","Because a person behaves like a machine","Because we want to hide our embarrassment"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Uyumsuzluk (incongruity) kuramına göre beklediğimiz kalıp bozulduğunda, beklenenle gerçekleşen arasındaki boşluk bizi güldürür."},{"q":"What creates dramatic irony in Saki's \"The Open Window\"?","options":["The narrator speaks directly to the reader","Mr Nuttel secretly knows that Vera is a ghost","The reader gradually realises that Vera is inventing her story while Mr Nuttel believes it","The lady of the house tells two different versions of the same event"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Dramatik ironi, okurun karakterden daha fazlasını bilmesidir. Okur Vera'nın hikâyeyi uydurduğunu sezerken Bay Nuttel ona inanır."},{"q":"Which technique is described as holding back the surprising word until the end of the sentence?","options":["Delay","Understatement","Verbal irony","The rule of three"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Metin, şaşırtıcı sözcüğü cümlenin sonuna saklamayı 'delay' (geciktirme) olarak anlatır."},{"q":"What can we infer about the article's view of humour in a foreign language?","options":["It is too difficult for learners and should be avoided","It depends mainly on vocabulary, so a dictionary is enough","It is mostly universal and needs no cultural knowledge","It is demanding to understand but gives a deep sense of achievement"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Yazar mizahı hem zor (ton, kültür, kelime oyunu gerektirir) hem de çok tatmin edici bulur; bu yüzden doğru seçenek sonuncusudur."}],"words":1111,"length":"short"},{"id":"frankenstein","type":"summary","title":"Frankenstein","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C1","genre":"scifi","work":{"title":"Frankenstein","titleTr":"Frankenstein","author":"Mary Shelley","year":1818,"editionYear":1831},"source":{"name":"Mary Shelley — Frankenstein (Project Gutenberg; 1831 text)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/84","license":"Kaynak roman; metin Lantern English için yazılmış özgün özet ve incelemedir."},"summaryTr":"Mary Shelley'nin romanını baştan sona ele alan bu özgün İngilizce özet, Victor Frankenstein ile yarattığı varlığın çatışmasını izler; sorumluluk, dışlanma ve anlatıcıların güvenilirliğini inceler. 1831 metnine dayanır ve romanın sonunu açıklar.","paragraphs":["## 1. A Voyage Already in Danger","Mary Shelley's Frankenstein follows an ambitious student who brings a living being into existence and then refuses the relationship his achievement creates. This complete summary and reading review follows the novel's 1831 text, while recognising that the work first appeared in 1818. Victor Frankenstein is the creator; the creature receives no personal name. The narrative moves between their competing accounts of suffering and responsibility, beginning far from the laboratory. Before readers learn how the creature was made, they encounter a frozen landscape in which both creation and creator have almost reached the limits of survival.","Robert Walton, an English explorer, describes his northern expedition in letters to his sister, Margaret Saville. He hopes to discover an Arctic passage and contribute to scientific knowledge, but public benefit is entangled with his desire for exceptional achievement. He imagines the unknown region with an enthusiasm that outruns the evidence available to him. His letters also reveal loneliness. Although he commands a crew, he longs for an educated companion who can understand his ambitions and correct his judgement. The expedition therefore begins with two connected needs: to accomplish something extraordinary and to be recognised by someone capable of appreciating it.","While ice surrounds the ship, the sailors notice an unusually large figure driving a sledge across the distant surface. Soon afterwards, they rescue another traveller, exhausted and close to death. This man is Victor Frankenstein. Remarkably, he asks where the ship is going before agreeing to come aboard: even rescue matters to him chiefly as a means of continuing his pursuit. Walton nurses him and quickly develops an intense admiration for his intelligence and sensitivity. The reader, however, has already seen the material result of whatever Victor once hoped to achieve: a damaged man following another being through a landscape almost empty of human life.","When Walton speaks about the knowledge he hopes to gain, Victor recognises an ambition resembling his own. He offers his history as a warning. Yet the circumstances complicate that warning from the beginning. Walton regards Victor as the friend he has been seeking, so he is predisposed to admire the speaker before evaluating the story. Victor is also looking backwards through grief, choosing which incidents explain his ruin. His account may contain sincere regret and attempts to justify himself at the same time. Shelley invites the reader to listen sympathetically without surrendering independent judgement.","The novel's structure matters because information passes through several hands. Walton records Victor's narrative for Margaret; within it, Victor reproduces the creature's account of experiences he did not witness. Letters and other documents offer supporting material, but no detached narrator settles every disagreement. Reading becomes an exercise in weighing testimony. The Arctic frame also establishes a practical question that will return at the end: can a person recognise the cost of ambition early enough to change direction? Walton still has a crew to protect and a sister to return to. Victor's opportunity to choose differently belongs mostly to the past.","## 2. Affection, Education, and the Desire to Discover","Victor recalls a privileged childhood in Geneva. His father, Alphonse, is respected and affectionate; his mother, Caroline, has known poverty and responds generously to vulnerable people. In the 1831 version, Elizabeth Lavenza is the daughter of an Italian nobleman and a German mother. After her mother's death and her father's disappearance, she lives with a poor foster family in Italy until Caroline brings her into the Frankenstein household. Elizabeth is raised alongside Victor, although she is not his biological cousin. The family sometimes uses the language of cousinship, and Victor describes her with a troubling mixture of affection, admiration, and possession.","Another important companion is Henry Clerval, whose imaginative interests include heroic adventures, literature, and languages. Elizabeth is associated with domestic affection and the beauty of the natural world, while Victor increasingly wants to uncover the principles beneath appearances. These differences do not make his early life unhappy. On the contrary, his account emphasises the security he receives from family and friendship. This background makes his later abandonment of the creature especially striking. He understands, through experience, what patient care can provide, yet fails to offer even the beginnings of such care to the being wholly dependent on him.","As a boy, Victor becomes fascinated by older writers on natural philosophy and alchemy. Their promise of hidden powers appeals to his desire for discoveries beyond ordinary human limits. His father's brief dismissal of these books does little to explain their weaknesses, leaving curiosity without adequate guidance. Later encounters with modern explanations of electricity and natural processes redirect his interests. Looking back, Victor repeatedly presents educational accidents and chance meetings as decisive turns towards disaster. That retrospective pattern deserves attention: it can illuminate the circumstances that shaped him, but it can also make deliberate choices appear to have been unavoidable.","Before Victor leaves for university at Ingolstadt, Elizabeth contracts scarlet fever. Caroline nurses her, becomes ill herself, and dies. On her deathbed, she expresses the hope that Victor and Elizabeth will eventually marry. Her death interrupts a household that has seemed protected from severe loss. Victor mourns, postpones his departure, and then resumes his education. It would be too simple to explain his entire scientific ambition as an immediate response to bereavement, since that ambition already exists. Nevertheless, the loss gives particular emotional force to a project concerned with the boundary between living bodies and dead matter.","At university, Professor Krempe dismisses Victor's earlier studies harshly, while Professor Waldman offers a more generous account of scientific progress. Waldman acknowledges the ambitions of earlier thinkers while praising the practical achievements of modern chemistry. His encouragement helps Victor commit himself to intensive study. Victor makes rapid progress, becomes absorbed in the structure of living organisms, and begins investigating decay. He eventually believes he has discovered how to produce life. He does not disclose a reproducible method to Walton. The novel concentrates on the consequences of possessing this knowledge, rather than supplying a technical account of the procedure itself.","## 3. The Achievement Victor Cannot Bear","Victor begins constructing a human figure. Because working with tiny structures would be difficult, he makes the body much larger than an ordinary person's. He gathers materials through disturbing investigations of bodies and biological processes, withdrawing from company as his work advances. The important change is not simply that his experiment is unusual. His sense of proportion deteriorates: ordinary obligations become distractions, affection seems less urgent than completion, and physical exhaustion becomes an acceptable price. He imagines a new kind of being grateful to him for its existence, but gives little sustained thought to what that being might need once alive.","This imagined gratitude reveals a central imbalance. Victor pictures himself receiving recognition as a creator before he considers the experience of the created person. He has plans for an achievement, not for a relationship. Meanwhile, he neglects letters from home and loses the restorative contact with nature that previously gave him pleasure. The project takes over his days and nights until completion seems to promise release from every difficulty it has produced. Shelley makes the process feel less like confident mastery than narrowing attention: the closer Victor comes to his goal, the less of the surrounding world he allows himself to see.","On a November night, the creature begins to breathe and move. Victor immediately recoils from the appearance of the body he has assembled. Features that he selected individually do not create the beauty he expected, and animation intensifies his disgust. He retreats, sleeps briefly, and experiences a nightmare in which Elizabeth becomes associated with his dead mother. Waking, he sees the creature near his bed, attempting contact. Victor interprets its presence through terror and flees. The being's first moments of conscious life are therefore marked by the disappearance of the only person who knows how it came into existence.","Henry Clerval arrives in Ingolstadt soon afterwards, having obtained permission to pursue further study. Victor returns with him to the apartment and finds that the creature has gone. His relief does not lead him to search for it or consider its survival. Instead, the strain of his work and the shock of its result overwhelm him, and he falls seriously ill. Henry nurses him through a prolonged recovery, protecting him from unnecessary disturbance and gradually helping him reconnect with everyday life. The contrast is direct: Victor survives because another person undertakes the patient care that he has refused to provide.","During his recovery, Victor turns away from the scientific subjects that now provoke distress. He shares Henry's interest in languages and literature, receives news from Elizabeth, and begins to enjoy walks again. These quieter passages demonstrate that friendship and attention can restore someone whose identity has narrowed around a single obsession. Yet recovery also permits avoidance. Victor treats the creature's absence as if it had removed the underlying problem, although no arrangement has been made for its welfare or for the safety of others. His private horror remains private, and the consequences of his experiment develop beyond his knowledge.","## 4. William and Justine: Private Knowledge, Public Harm","A letter from Alphonse ends this fragile period of recovery. Victor's youngest brother, William, has been murdered near Geneva. Returning home, Victor reaches the city too late to enter its closed gates and walks near the place where the child died. During a storm, lightning reveals the creature. Victor immediately concludes that it killed William. At this point he has not heard a confession or seen the murder; his certainty comes from recognising the being and connecting its presence with the crime. The distinction between conviction and evidence matters, even though the creature will later confirm his conclusion.","Victor then learns that Justine Moritz, a young woman closely attached to the family, has been accused. A miniature portrait that William carried was discovered in her clothing. Other circumstances, including her movements during the night and her distressed behaviour, are interpreted against her. The reader sees how a plausible account can harden around incomplete evidence. Justine's confusion becomes a sign of guilt rather than a response to exhaustion and grief. Her previous kindness offers little protection once the court and public have organised the available facts into a story of criminal intention.","Elizabeth speaks in Justine's defence, insisting that the accusation contradicts the character she knows. Victor, however, does not reveal the experiment or the creature's existence. He believes his account would be dismissed as madness and would fail to save the accused woman. That concern is understandable within the world of the novel, but it does not remove the moral problem. He possesses information that nobody else can assess because he withholds it. His inward certainty that he is responsible produces intense suffering, yet suffering is not equivalent to taking action on another person's behalf.","Justine is convicted. Under religious pressure and fear of being denied spiritual comfort, she confesses to a crime she has not committed. She subsequently tells Elizabeth and Victor that the confession is false. Her execution completes the second death connected to William's murder. Shelley gives this episode consequences beyond Victor's personal anguish: a child has been killed, an innocent woman has been condemned, and legal and religious authorities have helped transform vulnerability into apparent guilt. Elizabeth's faith in the goodness of people is shaken. The family cannot simply resume its former understanding of the world.","Victor describes himself as the concealed cause of these disasters and struggles with despair. Seeking relief, he travels into the Alps, where immense natural scenes temporarily interrupt his inward torment. The mountains offer perspective without supplying a solution. On a glacier near Mont Blanc, the creature approaches him with extraordinary speed and strength. Victor responds with anger and threats, but the creature insists that its maker must hear its history. It argues that judgement without a hearing repeats the rejection it has already endured. Victor eventually agrees, and the narrative passes to a voice he had previously treated as incapable of deserving attention.","## 5. An Education from Outside the Door","The creature begins with bewildered sensations rather than a fully formed adult understanding. Light, darkness, hunger, cold, and sound arrive before it can organise them. It discovers food and shelter through trial and error, and learns that fire can provide warmth while also causing pain. These early experiences make its impressive body seem especially misleading: physical strength does not supply knowledge, language, or emotional security. When it approaches people, their fear and violence drive it away. Its education in human society therefore starts with a lesson that its own appearance can provoke punishment before it has done anything wrong.","Eventually, the creature finds a small shelter adjoining a cottage in Germany. Through a gap, it observes the De Lacey family: an elderly blind father, his son Felix, and his daughter Agatha. Their poverty initially puzzles it, as does the sadness beneath their affectionate behaviour. It secretly takes some food, then stops when it realises that doing so increases their hardship. Instead, it gathers wood for them at night. This change is ethically significant. Without direct instruction or the prospect of praise, it recognises another person's need and alters its own conduct to reduce harm.","By listening carefully, the creature begins connecting sounds with objects and actions. Language opens a world that physical observation alone could not explain. When Safie arrives at the cottage and receives lessons in French, the hidden listener benefits from the same teaching. Her presence also makes Felix happier, demonstrating the emotional force of companionship. As the creature learns to read and understand more complex speech, it discovers history, social ranks, property, government, and conflict. Knowledge enlarges its imagination, but it also clarifies its exclusion. It possesses neither family connections nor wealth nor a recognised place within any community.","The De Laceys themselves have suffered injustice. Felix helped Safie's father, a Turkish merchant, escape a death sentence in France that the narrative presents as influenced by prejudice and wealth. In consequence, the De Lacey family lost its position and was exiled. Safie's father encouraged Felix's affection for his daughter when it served the escape, then opposed their union. Safie eventually defied him and travelled to join Felix. The episode gives the creature examples of generosity, betrayal, and independent decision. A critical reading should also recognise that its portrayal of religion and women's freedom relies on sweeping cultural assumptions that readers need not accept.","The creature finds books, including Paradise Lost, Plutarch's Lives, and The Sorrows of Werter, which supply different models of human action and suffering. It reads with intense personal involvement, comparing its situation with beings who have origins, histories, or companions. Paradise Lost makes its isolation particularly painful: even a rejected figure seems to belong to a larger moral story. Papers discovered in the clothes it took from Victor reveal the circumstances of its creation and the maker's disgust. Reading therefore gives it both intellectual resources and documentary evidence that the person responsible for its life found that life repellent.","## 6. Rejection, Violence, and a Demand","The creature hopes that eloquence and good intentions can overcome the response to its appearance. It plans to approach the blind De Lacey while the others are absent, allowing the old man to judge its words before his family judges its body. Their conversation begins promisingly. De Lacey listens to an account of loneliness and offers sympathy, unaware that the strangers whose favour his visitor seeks are his own children. The creature becomes increasingly emotional, and the family's return interrupts the encounter before trust can develop. Felix sees an alarming figure beside his father and attacks it; the creature escapes without fighting back.","For a time, it considers trying again. It recognises that its approach may have been too sudden and hopes the old man's kindness could still provide a path towards acceptance. That possibility vanishes when it learns that the frightened family has left the cottage permanently. It burns the abandoned building in anger. The act marks a change from helping people secretly to destroying the place associated with its disappointed hopes. The family is absent when the fire is set. Its violence is already serious, but the distinction prevents this episode from being confused with a murder of the De Laceys.","Using information in Victor's papers, the creature travels towards Geneva to find its creator. During the journey, it rescues a girl who has fallen into a river. A man who sees it with her responds by shooting it, interpreting rescue as danger. The creature experiences this as another demonstration that kindness cannot overcome the judgement imposed on its body. Its wound heals, but resentment deepens. Shelley supplies strong reasons for understanding that resentment while continuing to show the creature making choices. Being treated unjustly explains its growing hatred; it does not establish that every later victim deserves punishment.","Near Geneva, the creature encounters William and imagines that a child might be young enough to accept it. Its attempt to force companionship on him immediately contradicts that hope. William struggles, insults its appearance, and identifies his family. On hearing the name Frankenstein, the creature recognises an opportunity to injure Victor through someone he loves. It strangles the boy and takes the miniature portrait. Later, finding Justine asleep, it places the portrait in her clothing. This is deliberate manipulation of evidence. The creature's account confirms both William's murder and its role in directing suspicion towards an innocent woman.","After completing its narrative, the creature demands that Victor create a female companion of the same kind. It promises that they will leave human society and live remotely in South America, surviving without harming people. The request combines a powerful appeal to loneliness with coercion: refusal will bring further suffering. Victor initially rejects it, then becomes persuaded that he owes some remedy to the being he abandoned. He also hopes that agreement will protect his family. His consent resolves the immediate confrontation without resolving the deeper uncertainties, including whether a second conscious being could reasonably be bound by an agreement made before her existence.","## 7. A Promise Withdrawn","Back in Geneva, Victor struggles to resume ordinary relationships while carrying the obligation he has accepted. Alphonse hopes that marriage to Elizabeth will ease his distress, but Victor feels unable to marry before completing the new creature. He arranges to travel to Britain, where he can consult scientific knowledge useful to the task. Henry accompanies him, delighted by opportunities for learning and acquaintance. Their contrasting responses to travel expose the narrowing of Victor's life. Henry sees people, landscapes, and possibilities; Victor sees delays, threats, and the work he is reluctant to perform.","The journey takes them through parts of continental Europe to England, and later north into Scotland. Victor eventually separates from Henry and chooses a remote island in the Orkneys for his secret work. The setting provides privacy but also intensifies the isolation that characterised the first experiment. This time he proceeds without the earlier confidence. The materials and actions disgust him, and the future he once treated as a space for glorious achievement becomes a field of possible disasters. He cannot know whether the promised withdrawal from humanity will occur or whether creating another being will multiply the danger.","Victor's doubts grow more specific. The female creature might reject the male, refuse the arrangement imposed on her, or develop desires neither existing party can predict. The pair might have children, bringing into existence a population humans could be unable to control. Some fears concern genuine uncertainty; others assume hostility from a being who has not yet lived. The scene therefore creates an ethical conflict rather than an easy answer. Keeping his promise would affect someone unable to consent to its terms, while breaking it would destroy the only hope he has offered to the creature already alive.","Seeing the creature watching through the window, Victor tears apart the unfinished female body. She has never been animated. The creature witnesses the destruction and later confronts him, treating the act as the deliberate cancellation of its future. Victor refuses to reconsider. Their exchange abandons the limited negotiation they previously achieved, and the creature threatens to appear on Victor's wedding night. Victor assumes that the threat concerns his own death. This interpretation fits his tendency to place himself at the centre of every conflict, although the creature has already demonstrated that it can hurt him by attacking others.","Victor takes the remains out to sea in a boat and disposes of them. After falling asleep, he is carried away and eventually lands in Ireland, where local people suspect him of a recent murder. The victim is Henry Clerval, whose body bears signs of strangulation. Confronted with his friend's corpse, Victor collapses and suffers another severe illness. The magistrate, Mr Kirwin, treats him with consideration, and Alphonse comes to support him. Evidence eventually establishes that Victor was elsewhere when Henry died, allowing his release. Legal innocence, however, offers little relief from his sense that the secret struggle has destroyed another person who cared for him.","## 8. The Wedding and the Pursuit","Victor returns towards Geneva with his father, burdened by Henry's death and the creature's threat. Elizabeth writes to ask whether he loves someone else, interpreting his distance through the ordinary possibilities available to her. She offers to release him from their expected marriage if it would make him unhappy. Her question exposes the cost of his secrecy: she tries to make a generous decision without access to the facts shaping her life. Victor assures her of his love and promises an explanation after the wedding. He postpones disclosure beyond the moment when it might help her judge the danger.","They marry and travel across the lake to spend the night at an inn in Evian. Victor carries weapons and expects an attack on himself. Elizabeth senses his fear but does not understand its source. Hoping to spare her the confrontation, he asks her to retire while he searches the building. Then he hears her scream. The creature has strangled her in their room. Victor's attempt at protection has left her alone with the danger he failed to identify. Seeing the creature at a window, he fires unsuccessfully; the murderer escapes, and the future promised throughout his childhood has been destroyed.","The loss overwhelms Alphonse, who dies soon afterwards. Victor undergoes a period of mental collapse and confinement before recovering enough to act. He eventually tells a magistrate about the creature and demands official assistance. The extraordinary story and the practical difficulty of capturing such a being prevent the response he wants. Victor then commits himself to pursuit. The role reversal is striking: the creator who once fled contact now organises his existence around finding the being he abandoned. Yet pursuit is still a form of fixation, and revenge offers him purpose without restoring any of the relationships he has lost.","The chase leads across great distances and into the north. The creature leaves messages and sometimes supplies that allow Victor to continue. These interventions make the pursuit a relationship of mutual dependence as well as hatred. The creature wants Victor to experience prolonged isolation and suffering; Victor needs the prospect of revenge to keep moving. Neither simply escapes the other. As their route enters increasingly hostile conditions, the external landscape comes to resemble the reduction of their emotional world. Food, movement, endurance, and the enemy ahead replace the wider society both have lost.","On the Arctic ice, Victor nearly catches up, but the breaking surface separates him from the creature. Stranded on a drifting piece of ice, he makes his way to Walton's ship, returning the narrative to its opening situation. The entire history has led to the exhausted stranger whom Walton admired before knowing his name. Victor asks Walton to continue the pursuit if he dies. His story thus functions in two directions: it warns against destructive ambition, while also attempting to recruit a new person into an unfinished act of revenge. The reader must assess the conflict between the lesson Victor claims to teach and the obligation he now tries to pass on.","## 9. What Walton Chooses, and What Remains Unknown","Walton's own expedition becomes increasingly dangerous as ice traps the ship and threatens the crew. Representatives of the sailors ask him to promise that, if a passage opens, he will return south instead of pressing forward. Their demand forces the question that Victor repeatedly avoided: how much can a leader ask other people to risk for an ambition they do not fully share? Victor intervenes with an eloquent speech urging perseverance and portraying retreat as shameful. The dying man who has described the cost of obsession can still make the language of heroic achievement sound irresistible.","Walton eventually agrees to return. His decision should not be confused with a complete emotional conversion. He remains disappointed and initially regards the crew's determination as a defeat imposed on him. Nevertheless, his action matters more than perfect acceptance. He recognises that he cannot justly drive unwilling people into further danger. When the ice opens, the ship turns towards home. Unlike Victor, Walton permits a relationship with other people to place an effective limit on his project. He loses the result he wanted, but preserves possibilities that the novel's central adversaries have progressively destroyed.","Victor weakens and dies aboard the ship. Before his death, he reflects on his conduct, acknowledges some duties towards the creature, and continues to defend his refusal to make a companion. His advice remains divided: he recommends a quieter happiness, then admits that another ambitious person might succeed where he failed. Walton mourns him deeply. The narrative does not provide a final speech that resolves every contradiction in Victor's character. His tenderness, intelligence, pride, suffering, and capacity for self-justification remain present together, even as his ability to act comes to an end.","Afterwards, Walton finds the creature beside Victor's body. It expresses grief and remorse, insisting that revenge has brought torment rather than satisfaction. Walton challenges this account, pointing out that remorse did not prevent repeated murder. The creature describes the deterioration of its earlier hopes into hatred, but also recognises the horror of what it has done. Its final appearance asks the reader to hold sympathy and condemnation together. A person may have been profoundly wronged and still be responsible for terrible wrongs. Neither fact has to erase the other.","The creature announces that it intends to travel farther north, build a funeral fire, and burn itself so that no remains will allow another being like it to be made. It then leaves the ship and disappears across the dark water on a piece of ice. Walton does not witness the promised fire or verify the creature's death. The ending therefore contains a declared intention and a final disappearance, rather than a confirmed account of suicide. That limit is consistent with the novel's larger method: the reader receives powerful testimony, but must distinguish what a speaker says from what the narrative actually shows.","## 10. Reading Responsibility beyond the Experiment","The central ethical failure in Frankenstein develops through the separation of technical achievement from continuing care. Victor asks whether he can create life and imagines the status that success will bring. He does not prepare to teach, protect, accompany, or listen to the life he produces. This makes the novel more demanding than a simple warning that knowledge is dangerous. Its scientists are not condemned merely for asking ambitious questions. The catastrophe unfolds when the capacity to act grows faster than the willingness to remain answerable for the consequences, especially once those consequences become frightening or inconvenient.","The creature's education complicates any easy division between a humane society and an inhuman outsider. Before committing murder, it learns sympathy by observing affection, discovers injustice through history, and attempts to help people. Its appearance repeatedly prevents those actions from being recognised. Yet the novel also resists turning exclusion into a complete excuse. William, Justine, Henry, and Elizabeth are individuals, not interchangeable representatives of the society that rejected it. By attacking them to punish Victor, the creature repeats the reduction of persons to instruments that marked its creator's original dream of grateful dependence.","Family relationships reveal another version of this problem. Victor benefits from the labour of people whose ambitions rarely occupy the centre of his account. Caroline nurses, Elizabeth comforts, Justine cares for children, and Henry tends the sick. Their work sustains the conditions in which his intellectual freedom becomes possible. The destruction of the unfinished companion extends the question to a woman denied even the chance to speak. Victor and the creature debate her purpose before she exists. A critical reading can recognise the real danger Victor fears while asking why her imagined independence appears chiefly as a threat to plans made by men.","The competing narratives also make responsibility a matter of language. Victor repeatedly describes himself as driven by fate, while the creature describes its violence as the outcome of rejection. Both explanations contain evidence; both can become ways to reduce personal agency. Walton's admiration supplies another pressure on judgement, encouraging the reader to see Victor through the eyes of a devoted friend. The task is neither to dismiss every narrator nor to accept the most moving speech. It is to notice omissions, compare accounts with actions, and ask whose experience remains outside the speaker's preferred explanation.","Frankenstein leaves its readers with an unresolved but productive tension between understanding and accountability. Its most disturbing figure can speak with intelligence and feel remorse; its gifted, affectionate protagonist can abandon obligations and misunderstand those closest to him. The novel's emotional power depends on preserving that complexity. Walton offers a limited alternative because he changes his conduct while still feeling disappointed. He does not repair Victor's losses or settle the creature's future, but he stops adding unwilling companions to the cost of his ambition. Across the larger tragedy, that imperfect decision gives practical weight to the possibility of learning."],"glossary":{"creature":"Victor'un yarattığı, özel adı olmayan varlık","creator":"yaratıcı; burada Victor Frankenstein","frame":"ana öyküyü çevreleyen dış anlatı","animation":"canlandırma, hayat verme","miniature":"küçük boyutlu portre","testimony":"anlatılan olaylara ilişkin tanıklık","agency":"kendi kararlarını verme ve eylemde bulunma gücü","answerable":"hesap vermekle yükümlü","companion":"yoldaş, hayat arkadaşı","natural":"doğal; natural philosophy: doğa felsefesi","retire":"burada: dinlenmek üzere odasına çekilmek","condemned":"mahkûm edilmiş; kınanmış, suçlanmış"},"phrases":[{"en":"weighing testimony","tr":"tanıklıkları değerlendirip tartmak"},{"en":"surrendering independent judgement","tr":"bağımsız yargılama yetisini bırakmak"},{"en":"taking action on another person's behalf","tr":"başka birinin adına harekete geçmek"},{"en":"through trial and error","tr":"deneme yanılma yoluyla"},{"en":"the prospect of praise","tr":"övgü alma beklentisi"},{"en":"bound by an agreement","tr":"bir anlaşmayla bağlı, yükümlü"},{"en":"hold sympathy and condemnation together","tr":"sempatiyi ve kınamayı bir arada tutmak"},{"en":"remain answerable for the consequences","tr":"sonuçlardan sorumlu kalmak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"How is Elizabeth connected to Victor's family in the 1831 version followed here?","options":["She joins the household after Victor meets her at university.","She is Victor's biological cousin, raised elsewhere until adulthood.","She is brought into the household as a child and raised alongside Victor.","She is Henry Clerval's sister and visits Geneva during his illness."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"1831 sürümünde Elizabeth, İtalya'daki yoksul koruyucu ailesinden Frankenstein ailesine alınır. Victor'la büyür; kuzen diye hitap edilse de biyolojik kuzeni değildir."},{"q":"What does the creature's decision to stop taking food from the De Laceys show?","options":["It recognises their hardship and changes its behaviour to reduce harm.","It has already decided that all people deserve to suffer.","It expects the family to reward it publicly for its generosity.","It no longer needs food because it has mastered the use of fire."],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Yaratık, yiyecek almasının ailenin yoksulluğunu ağırlaştırdığını anlar; bundan vazgeçer ve gizlice odun toplar. Bu davranış, cinayetlerden önce geliştirdiği empatiyi gösterir."},{"q":"Why does Victor destroy the unfinished female creature?","options":["Henry discovers the experiment and orders him to abandon it.","He learns that the original creature has already left for South America.","Elizabeth demands that he complete their marriage before continuing.","He fears consequences that neither his promise nor the creature's assurances can control."],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Victor yeni varlığın anlaşmayı kabul etmeyebileceğinden, eşini reddedebileceğinden veya üreyerek yeni tehlikeler doğurabileceğinden kaygılanır. Henüz canlandırılmamış bedeni parçalar."},{"q":"What makes Walton's final decision different from Victor's pattern of conduct?","options":["He persuades the sailors to accept every danger as the price of discovery.","He allows his responsibility towards unwilling crew members to limit his ambition.","He completes Victor's pursuit before returning to his scientific expedition.","He gives up exploration because he no longer feels any disappointment."],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Walton hayal kırıklığını sürdürse de tayfanın istemediği bir tehlikeye zorlanamayacağını kabul eder ve geri döner. Değişim kusursuz duygusal kabullenişte değil, aldığı karardadır."},{"q":"What can readers confidently say about the creature at the end?","options":["Walton watches it build a fire and confirms that it dies.","It promises to live peacefully with a new companion in South America.","It announces an intention to burn itself and disappears; its death is not witnessed.","The returning sailors capture it and bring it to trial in England."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Yaratık kendisini yakmayı planladığını söyler ve buz parçasıyla karanlıkta uzaklaşır. Walton ateşe ya da ölüme tanık olmaz; plan ile doğrulanmış olay ayrılmalıdır."}],"words":4929,"length":"long"},{"id":"pride-class-and-irony","type":"article","title":"Pride, Class and the Cruel Little Irony","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C1","genre":"drama","summaryTr":"Gerçekçi kısa öyküde gurur, sınıf ve ironi nasıl işler? Yazı, Maupassant'ın 'The Necklace' öyküsü üzerinden sıradan insanların trajedisini, anlatıcının suskunluğunu ve Çehov'la karşıtlığı inceliyor.","paragraphs":["A borrowed necklace, a single glittering evening and ten years of drudgery: it sounds like the stuff of melodrama, and yet Guy de Maupassant's \"The Necklace\" (1884) is told with such restraint that its cruelty arrives almost without a sound. The narrator does not weep for Mathilde Loisel, nor does he scold her. He simply records, in a clear and measured voice, what happens when a woman who cannot afford to look wealthy decides that appearances are worth any price. Readers who have not yet read the story may wish to do so first, since this essay discusses its ending.","## Ordinary lives, serious stakes","In a literary context, the word drama does not refer only to plays. In short fiction it denotes stories in which ordinary people face moral or emotional crises that are treated with complete seriousness, without the escapism of adventure or the exaggeration of horror. Such narratives owe a great deal to nineteenth-century realism, a movement that insisted that the lives of clerks, housewives and small tradesmen deserved the same attention that earlier literature had reserved for kings and heroes. Maupassant (1850 to 1893) was formed in this tradition. He learned his craft under Gustave Flaubert, whose insistence on the exact word and the impersonal narrator was to leave a lasting mark on his pupil.","The twentieth-century playwright Arthur Miller made a related argument in his essay \"Tragedy and the Common Man\" (1949). In his view, the ordinary person is as fit a subject for tragedy as any king, since what matters is not rank but the intensity with which an individual defends his sense of dignity. That perspective helps to explain why stories of this kind can feel weighty even though their settings are cramped apartments and office corridors.","## The costume of class","In \"The Necklace\", class is less a fixed essence than a performance. Mathilde, the wife of a minor government clerk, feels that she was born for luxury and has been cheated by fate. When her husband obtains an invitation to a ministerial reception, she is miserable at having nothing suitable to wear, and so she borrows a diamond necklace from a wealthy friend. For one night the costume works: she is the prettiest woman at the party, admired and flattered. That success is exactly the problem. For a brief moment, social standing is revealed as a matter of clothing, jewellery and confidence, and the story invites us to wonder how much of rank is merely stage design.","O. Henry, writing in a very different register, treated money and status in a comparable way. In \"The Gift of the Magi\", poverty is not a moral failing but a constraint that shapes every choice the young couple makes. The comparison is instructive: Maupassant is severe with illusion, whereas O. Henry is tender about sacrifice, yet both authors understand that the economic pressures of urban life determine even the smallest decisions.","## Pride as the engine of plot","When Mathilde loses the necklace, she and her husband could simply confess to its owner and ask for time. Instead, they decide to conceal the loss, buy a replacement at a ruinous price and spend a decade paying off the debt. What drives this choice is not stupidity but pride: she cannot bear to appear careless, or poor, in front of someone she wished to impress. In the vocabulary of classical tragedy, pride is the flaw that turns an accident into a catastrophe, and in Maupassant's hands that flaw is entirely recognisable. We do not need to be Mathilde to understand why admitting a mistake can feel more dangerous than paying for it.","It is worth noting that pride has two faces in this story. The quality that ruins Mathilde also grants her a kind of dignity in the years that follow, when she learns the hardships of manual labour and faces her debt without complaint. The narrator remains ambiguous about whether this transformation is a punishment or an achievement, and one might argue that the emotional force of the story comes precisely from that uncertainty.","## Irony without commentary","The final revelation, that the borrowed necklace was almost worthless, arrives in the last lines of the story, and Maupassant does not comment on it. There is no speech about fate, no moral summary. The irony is situational in the strictest sense: the sacrifice was made for something that never had the value attributed to it, so ten years of labour purchased what was, in effect, a copy of a copy. Because the narrator refuses to explain the reversal, the reader must confront its significance alone. This restraint is one of the hallmarks of realist short fiction, whose authors trust events, rather than editorial voices, to carry meaning.","The Russian writer Anton Chekhov (1860 to 1904) developed this restraint in a different direction. Where Maupassant tends to build towards a decisive turn, Chekhov often ended his stories without resolution, leaving characters at a moment of unfinished understanding. Comparing the two clarifies a basic choice in dramatic short fiction: whether an ending should deliver a verdict or merely pose an uncomfortable question.","## What the story still asks of us","Stories like these endure because they combine the particular with the universal. The details, a ball gown, a rented necklace, a clerk's salary, belong to nineteenth-century Paris, but the psychology they expose does not. In an age when a flattering public image has never been easier to produce, the temptation to pay almost any price to avoid looking diminished has hardly disappeared. The best dramatic fiction does not tell us what to feel about Mathilde. It presents her case with enough clarity that we are forced to decide whether we are judging her or ourselves."],"glossary":{"realism":"gerçekçilik; gündelik hayatı olduğu gibi yansıtmayı amaçlayan edebi akım","melodrama":"melodram; abartılı ve aşırı duygusal anlatım","restraint":"ölçülülük, kendini tutma","clerk":"memur, katip","drudgery":"yorucu ve sıkıcı angarya iş","situational irony":"durum ironisi; beklenenin tam tersi bir sonucun ortaya çıkması","ruinous":"yıkıcı, mahveden"},"phrases":[{"en":"owe a great deal to","tr":"-e çok şey borçlu olmak"},{"en":"leave a lasting mark on","tr":"-de kalıcı iz bırakmak"},{"en":"in the strictest sense","tr":"kelimenin tam anlamıyla, en katı anlamda"},{"en":"it is worth noting that","tr":"şunu belirtmekte fayda var ki"},{"en":"hallmarks of","tr":"-in ayırt edici özellikleri"},{"en":"a matter of","tr":"-e bağlı bir konu, ... meselesi"},{"en":"in front of","tr":"-in önünde, -in yanında"},{"en":"in effect","tr":"aslında, fiilen"},{"en":"carry meaning","tr":"anlam taşımak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What principle of nineteenth-century realism does the article emphasise?","options":["Stories should always end with a moral lesson","Only people of high rank are suitable subjects for serious literature","The lives of ordinary people deserve the same serious attention once given to kings and heroes","Narrators should explain the meaning of events in detail"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Gerçekçilik, memurların, ev kadınlarının ve küçük esnafın hayatlarının da krallar ve kahramanlar kadar ciddi ilgiyi hak ettiğini savunur."},{"q":"Why do Mathilde and her husband conceal the loss of the necklace?","options":["Her pride makes her unable to bear appearing careless or poor","The owner has told them that she will not forgive the loss","They plan to sell the replacement for profit","They believe the necklace is worthless"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Karar budalalıktan değil gururdan kaynaklanır: Mathilde, özellikle etkilemek istediği birinin önünde dikkatsiz veya yoksul görünmeye katlanamaz."},{"q":"What does the article mean by saying that pride 'has two faces' in the story?","options":["It is admired by the narrator but condemned by the husband","It ruins Mathilde but also gives her a kind of dignity later on","It is shown in both the rich and the poor characters equally","It appears at the beginning and disappears by the end"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Mathilde'i mahveden aynı özellik, sonraki yıllarda ona bir tür onur da kazandırır; anlatıcı bunun ceza mı başarı mı olduğunu açık bırakır."},{"q":"Why does the final revelation of the story have such force, according to the article?","options":["A new character explains the moral at length","The narrator admits that Mathilde was wrong","Chekhov's influence makes the ending ambiguous and unresolved","The narrator offers no commentary and leaves the reader to face its significance"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Maupassant sonuç hakkında yorum yapmaz; anlatıcı açıklamayı reddettiği için okur sonucun anlamıyla tek başına yüzleşir."}],"words":926,"length":"short"},{"id":"suspicion-is-not-evidence","type":"article","title":"Suspicion Is Not Evidence","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C1","genre":"mystery","summaryTr":"Bir gizem öyküsünde şüphe uyandıran ayrıntı ile bir iddiayı gerçekten destekleyen kanıt aynı şey değildir. Bu yazı, özgün bir kayıp çizim örneği üzerinden çıkarım, zaman çizelgesi, yanlış iz ve adil çözüm arasındaki ilişkiyi inceliyor.","paragraphs":["## What a clue actually supports","A clue becomes useful when we can state what it supports without quietly adding what we hope it proves. A muddy shoe may establish that someone walked through wet ground. It does not, by itself, establish which patch of ground, when the walk occurred, or whether the walker stole anything. Mystery fiction often draws energy from the distance between those claims. A detective sees an object; a suspect offers an explanation; the reader chooses how much weight to give each. The pleasure deepens when the story rewards precise distinctions rather than treating every suggestive detail as a direct route to guilt.","Consider an invented case involving a missing garden design. A local competition winner has lent the original drawing to a small gallery. On the morning of the prize ceremony, the frame contains a colour copy. The paper looks similar, but the original had two small pinholes near its upper edge. Yesterday's condition photograph shows them clearly; today's sheet has printed dots in the same positions. This detail supports a narrow but important conclusion: the object has been substituted. It does not yet identify the person responsible, establish a motive, or show whether the original remains in the building.","That narrow conclusion is more valuable than a dramatic accusation because it defines the next questions. Who could obtain a copy? Who could open the frame? When was the original last observed directly? Each question calls for different evidence. A person may have access to the digital image without access to the gallery. Another may carry a frame key but have no opportunity to use it unnoticed. The investigation becomes a process of connecting constraints. Its reasoning remains visible because each new claim grows from an earlier, limited finding rather than from the detective's unexplained confidence.","A useful reading habit is to separate observation, inference, and speculation. The printed dots are an observation. The conclusion that this sheet is a copy is an inference supported by those dots and the earlier photograph. The idea that a jealous competitor made the substitution is speculation until further evidence connects that person to the act. Stories may deliberately blur these categories through an overconfident narrator or an anxious witness. Readers need not avoid guessing; guessing is part of the experience. But keeping track of the categories allows a later reversal to feel like a correction of reasoning rather than a rule invented at the end.","## Opportunity needs a reliable sequence","A timeline is persuasive only to the extent that its time markers are dependable. Suppose a witness says the gallery assistant left at six because a clock showed that hour. If the clock has stopped, the apparent precision is worthless. Yet exposing the broken clock does not prove that the assistant stayed late. It removes one piece of support for an earlier claim. This distinction matters because a mystery can become unfair when every weakened alibi is treated as positive evidence of guilt. Not knowing where someone was is different from knowing that they stood beside the open frame.","In the garden case, an electronic ticket might place a delivery driver at the building's entrance at six twelve. It does not automatically show when the driver entered the exhibition room or left the property. A camera could establish those movements more directly, but only within its field of view. The narrative need not turn into a technical manual. It should simply respect the limits it has introduced. If the camera misses a side door, that limitation should be available before the solution relies on it. Otherwise, the reader has been encouraged to reason from a completeness that never existed.","Relative order can sometimes be stronger than an exact time. A volunteer remembers seeing the original drawing after a large parcel was unpacked. The parcel contained a distinctive orange display cloth visible in the volunteer's photograph. Even if the photograph's clock setting is wrong, the sequence may still be clear: unpacking came first, then the photograph, then the substitution. This can narrow the investigation without pretending to know the minute. A well constructed mystery distinguishes between certainty about order and certainty about duration. They answer different questions and should not be exchanged simply because one produces a cleaner timetable.","Witnesses also remember events through their purposes. A cleaner may notice whether chairs block a doorway while overlooking who sits in them. A delivery driver may remember a signature and forget the room's artwork. Such differences can make testimony both limited and useful. The writer does not need to make every witness deceptive or astonishingly observant. Ordinary attention provides its own pattern of gaps. When a detective asks a witness to reconstruct a task rather than recite an exact minute, a new detail can emerge naturally. The discovery follows from how the person encountered the scene, not from a sudden gift of perfect memory.","## Motive is a question, not a verdict","Motive makes a suspect narratively interesting because it gives an action a possible purpose. A disappointed competitor might benefit from delaying the prize ceremony. A gallery organiser might fear that the original drawing exposes an error in the printed catalogue. An owner might regret lending a valuable object. These possibilities help decide whom to question and what to ask. They do not establish that anyone acted. A story becomes predictable when the angriest person is automatically the criminal; it becomes equally mechanical when the angriest person must always be innocent because experienced readers expect the obvious suspect to be cleared.","The most productive motives connect a possible action to a particular pressure. If the organiser has approved an altered version of the winning design, the original might expose that decision. Removing it would buy time before the ceremony. This explanation is more specific than saying that the organiser is ambitious. It also generates testable questions. Did the organiser know about the discrepancy? When did that knowledge arrive? Would hiding the original actually delay discovery? A motive should fit the method and timing, not merely provide a dark adjective that makes a character sound capable of wrongdoing.","Multiple motives can coexist without making the solution arbitrary. The competitor may resent the winner and still report a clue that helps recover the drawing. The owner may want the work returned but refuse to interrupt the ceremony. These choices reveal the distance between feeling and action. They also protect secondary characters from becoming disposable containers for suspicion. Once cleared, a suspect can remain angry, proud, or unreliable about unrelated matters. Innocence of the central act does not require a complete moral transformation. The mystery resolves a specific question; it need not divide the cast into entirely admirable and entirely corrupt people.","A confession needs similar care. If the organiser eventually admits making the substitution, the admission should explain the evidence already available rather than replace it. The recovered original, a printing record, and a documented opportunity can support the account. A confession that introduces an unknown passage, an unseen accomplice, and a new motive in its final paragraph asks the reader to accept a different story. The emotional explanation may arrive late, but the practical possibility should already exist. Surprise is strongest when it rearranges familiar facts into a shape the reader could have recognised.","## A fair wrong turn teaches the reader something","A misleading clue can be fair when the observed fact remains true after its first interpretation fails. Imagine that blue dust appears on the competitor's sleeve and on the back of the empty frame. The detective initially links the two. Later, both traces turn out to come from chalk used to mark positions on the gallery wall. The competitor helped hang several displays. The dust was real; the inference was too narrow. This wrong turn can clarify the investigation by showing that contact with shared materials does not uniquely connect someone to the substitution.","The alternative explanation should have roots in the story. If blue chalk appears for the first time during the final reveal, the writer has concealed the information needed to assess the clue. A brief earlier scene showing a volunteer mark the wall would make the later correction available. The reader might overlook it, which is fair, but could return and see that it was there. This is the difference between directing attention and withholding the rules. Mystery writing can encourage a mistaken interpretation while preserving the evidence required to challenge it.","Wrong turns should also change the detective's behaviour. After overvaluing the dust, the investigator might ask whether another apparently distinctive object was shared. That question could lead to the gallery's label printer, which everyone used, and then to a print log that distinguishes separate jobs. The failed inference has produced a better question. Without such consequences, misleading clues can feel like delays inserted merely to lengthen the plot. A useful detour deepens understanding of the setting, exposes a character's bias, or changes the method by which the investigation proceeds.","Bias becomes especially interesting when it arises from a sympathetic concern. The detective may distrust the wealthy owner because the gallery depends on that person's funding. That dependence is relevant to the organiser's fear, but it does not prove that the owner threatened anyone. Reading an actual message might reveal that the owner invited factual corrections. The detective must then distinguish pressure that was expressed from pressure another person imagined. This does not make unequal power disappear. It prevents a plausible account of the institution from becoming an unsupported claim about one individual's conduct.","## The solution should close the right questions","A satisfying solution explains the central act with enough precision to account for the strongest clues. In the garden case, we should learn who replaced the drawing, how they accessed the frame, what made the substitution possible at that time, and where the original went. We do not necessarily need a biography of every witness or a reason for every accidental noise. Completeness is selective. The ending should settle the questions on which the investigation depended while leaving ordinary background details free to remain ordinary. Explaining everything can be as artificial as explaining too little.","The solution should also distinguish proof from remaining uncertainty. Perhaps the organiser admits intending to return the original after the ceremony. Unless there is supporting evidence, the detective can report that intention as a claim rather than a fact. Recovering the drawing does not establish what would have happened without discovery. This restraint gives the ending intellectual honesty. It also prevents a sympathetic motive from erasing the act itself. We may understand why the organiser panicked while still recognising the broken agreement, the false accusation directed at the competitor, and the owner's loss of trust.","Consequences provide another form of closure. The gallery might correct its catalogue, return the drawing, and change how borrowed work is recorded. The organiser may step away from handling original objects while the board reviews the incident. These actions do not need to become a long administrative appendix. A few concrete details show that discovery alters the world of the story. If everyone resumes work exactly as before, the mystery risks becoming an isolated trick. A changed label or a signed receipt can make the resolution visible while leaving forgiveness and institutional decisions appropriately unfinished.","Reading a mystery closely means enjoying possibilities while asking what each one rests on. Follow the difference between a missing fact and a contradiction, between an opportunity and an action, between a motive and a demonstrated choice. Notice whether a wrong turn preserves its underlying observations and whether the solution returns to details already present. The detective's most valuable skill may be the willingness to reduce a confident claim to the smaller statement the evidence supports. That reduction does not weaken suspense. It makes the eventual conclusion feel earned, because the story has shown how uncertainty was narrowed rather than merely announcing that it has ended."],"glossary":{"inference":"kanıttan çıkarılan sonuç","speculation":"henüz kanıtlanmamış varsayım","alibi":"olay sırasında başka yerde bulunma iddiası","discrepancy":"uyuşmazlık, farklılık","testimony":"tanık anlatımı","detour":"soruşturmada dolaylı veya yanlış yola sapma","restraint":"kesin hüküm vermede ölçülülük"},"phrases":[{"en":"by itself","tr":"tek başına"},{"en":"keeping track of","tr":"izini sürmek, takibini yapmak"},{"en":"to the extent that","tr":"... ölçüsünde"},{"en":"field of view","tr":"görüş alanı"},{"en":"buy time","tr":"zaman kazanmak"},{"en":"positive evidence","tr":"bir iddiayı doğrudan destekleyen kanıt"},{"en":"rather than","tr":"... yerine"},{"en":"rests on","tr":"dayanır"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the printed dots on the replacement drawing establish?","options":["The identity of the person who made the substitution","That the displayed sheet is a copy rather than the original","That the organiser intended to sell the original","That every witness has lied"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Basılı noktalar ile önceki fotoğraftaki gerçek iğne delikleri karşılaştırıldığında yalnızca nesnenin kopyayla değiştirildiği anlaşılır."},{"q":"What follows when a clock used to support an alibi is found to have stopped?","options":["The suspect is proved guilty","The witness must have invented the whole visit","One source of support for the timing is weakened","The substitution must have happened before six"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Saatin bozuk olması zaman iddiasının dayanağını zayıflatır; tek başına suçluluğu kanıtlamaz."},{"q":"Why should a motive generate testable questions?","options":["To connect a possible purpose to the method, knowledge and timing","To avoid examining physical evidence","To ensure the angriest character is guilty","To make every suspect equally responsible"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"İyi bir güdü açıklaması kişinin ne bildiğini, ne zaman bildiğini ve seçilen yöntemin amaca uyup uymadığını araştırmaya yöneltir."},{"q":"What makes the blue dust a fair misleading clue?","options":["The dust is later said never to have existed","Its alternative source is introduced only in the final sentence","It proves contact with the original drawing","The observation remains true, while an established shared source corrects the inference"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Mavi toz gerçektir; öyküde önceden görülen ortak tebeşir kaynağı, yanlış yorumun düzeltilmesini sağlar."},{"q":"Why should the organiser's claimed intention to return the drawing remain a claim?","options":["Intentions never matter in fiction","Without support, recovery does not prove what would have happened later","The organiser cannot remember any events","Every confession must be rejected"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Çizimin bulunması, olay açığa çıkmasa failin ne yapacağını kanıtlamaz; desteklenmeyen niyet beyanı kesin olgu sayılmaz."}],"words":1954,"length":"medium"},{"id":"last-crossing-of-greyhaven","type":"story","title":"The Last Crossing of Greyhaven","author":"Lantern Original","level":"C1","genre":"adventure","summaryTr":"Kapanmak üzere olan bir kara yolunun ucundaki eski kıyı istasyonundan on sekiz arşiv kutusunu çıkarmaya giden Mara, listede olmayan defterlerle ve baskı kalıplarıyla karşılaşır. Plan bozulup saatler daraldığında neyi taşıyacağına, geride kalanların hesabını kimin vereceğine karar vermesi gerekir.","paragraphs":["## 1. Eighteen cases","The transport sheet allowed three lines for difficulties. Mara had filled the first with 'restricted access', the second with 'fragile material', and the third with 'departure subject to crossing control'. There was no fourth line, so she had stopped thinking in categories. She checked the van's fuel, the straps, the weatherproof containers, and the telephone numbers printed beneath the regional library's logo. In the passenger seat, Nessa was eating a pear over a folded map. She had removed the map from its plastic cover because the cover made a useful plate.","'You know we have a table at the office,' Mara said. Nessa nodded, inspecting a bruise on the fruit. 'I also know you would have left without me.' This was probably true. Mara had coordinated the transfer for six weeks, and the final journey had acquired the strange importance of a last sentence. Eighteen cases from the former coastal observation station at Greyhaven, delivered to the regional library before the access road closed for rebuilding. Each case had a number, a destination shelf, and a weight. She wanted the day to end with eighteen ticks.","Greyhaven was not an island, although people across the bay called it one. A low strip of land joined it to the mainland, and a controlled road followed that strip between water and grass. Beyond it rose a broad, uneven headland with a few houses, a boatyard, and the station. The road was due to close for the winter after an inspection that afternoon. Residents had already made their own arrangements; some had moved temporarily, while others would use the licensed passenger boat. The archive was among the last loads authorised to leave by road.","At the crossing office, the controller leaned through the van window and read their permit. 'Outbound by three forty,' she said. 'That is the present limit, not a guarantee. If we change it, Ruth at the station receives the message.' Mara repeated the time and the contact name. The woman looked satisfied and lifted the barrier. As they drove onto the narrow road, the van's reflection broke into pieces in the channels beside it. Nessa put the pear core into a paper bag and began watching the water.","Mara had grown up inland, where a road generally remained a road until someone dug it up. Here, it seemed more like an agreement renewed each morning. She kept her eyes on the marked lane and drove without speaking. Far ahead, a man on a bicycle waited at the headland checkpoint. He had a yellow waterproof folded over the handlebars. As they approached, he raised one hand, then pointed them towards the station. A small white building stood against the grey slope, its windows reflecting a brighter sky than the one above them.","Ruth met them at the station door with a ring of keys. She had been its caretaker, receptionist, cleaner, and unofficial guide at different times, sometimes all on the same day. Her retirement was scheduled for the following week. Beside her stood Kit, a seasonal harbour worker hired to help with the transfer. He had brought work gloves and a notebook no larger than his palm. Mara liked that. People who wrote things down were usually easier to work with than people who assured her they would remember.","Inside, eighteen archive cases stood along the corridor, exactly as promised. Nessa, the library conservator, checked the seals while Mara matched the numbers to her sheet. Through an open doorway she could see a room without furniture. Pale rectangles marked the walls where charts had hung. A clock still worked above the doorway, and its ticking seemed unnecessarily loud. Ruth noticed her looking. 'We thought about taking it down,' she said, 'but then nobody would know when to leave.' Kit laughed, though Ruth had not quite made a joke.","Case eighteen contained photographs of the station staff. The packing list described them as institutional records, but the picture visible through the protective sleeve showed four people eating chips beside a car. One had an arm over another's shoulder. Mara closed the lid carefully. She had moved collections before: court registers, theatre costumes, a cabinet of shells. Objects acquired an official dignity when they entered her paperwork. Sometimes she forgot that they had existed before the descriptions. Nessa checked the last seal and said, 'All right. Now we need Ruth's index.'","## 2. The things outside the list","Ruth's index occupied two exercise books with different coloured covers. Neither appeared on the transport sheet. Nessa had expected them because Ruth had mentioned them in a telephone conversation, but the catalogue team had understood 'index' to mean the typed list already included in the files. Ruth opened the red book. Its pages contained dates, nicknames, short accounts of arguments, and arrows connecting photographs to people whose formal names had never reached the station register. Without it, the man holding a broken umbrella in photograph sixty-one would remain 'unidentified visitor'.","'He was the electrician,' Ruth said. 'The umbrella belonged to the director. That is why everyone was laughing.' Nessa asked whether the books could travel with the collection. Ruth hesitated. 'Most of the writing is mine. Some entries are things people told me privately. I marked those pages.' She showed them small slips of green paper. Nessa proposed a temporary restriction until Ruth could review those sections with the library. She wrote the condition on a separate form and gave Ruth a copy. Mara added 'two notebooks, restricted pending review' beneath case eighteen.","It was a reasonable addition. It weighed almost nothing. Yet Mara felt the outline of the job begin to soften. 'Is there anything else the catalogue team missed?' she asked. Ruth looked towards the back window. Beyond the garden, a track descended to a low shed with a corrugated roof. 'Elian has the printing plates,' she said. 'I thought you knew.' Mara checked the list, though she knew already that there were no printing plates on it. The only metal object described was a brass nameplate, wrapped inside case three.","Kit looked up from a container handle he was tightening. 'Elian came to the planning meeting.' Ruth nodded. 'He brought photographs. Someone said the library would be interested.' Interest was not a transport instruction, but Mara could already imagine how the difference had disappeared between the meeting and this morning. She asked how many plates there were. Ruth said twelve, perhaps fourteen, and that some were quite large. Nessa asked what had been printed from them. 'The harbour notices,' Ruth answered. 'And the local maps. Before everything came from the town.'","Mara looked at the clock. It was half past ten. Loading the cases would take less than an hour; the road was twenty minutes away. There was time to inspect the plates, establish ownership, and decide whether another transfer should be arranged. She made that distinction aloud. Ruth listened without interruption, then said, 'Of course.' The polite answer made Mara feel harsher than she had intended. She asked Kit to stay with the van and the sealed cases. Nessa took a notebook, and the three women went down the track.","The shed door stood open. Inside, Elian was wrapping a flat object in an old cotton sheet. He was the cyclist from the checkpoint, older than Mara had thought, with grey hair cut close to his scalp and ink permanently lodged beside one thumbnail. The room smelled of oil and paper. A heavy printing press stood against the wall, its wheel taller than a bicycle wheel. Shelves held type, tools, and wooden trays. Elian saw Mara glance at the press and smiled. 'You can stop calculating. That stays here.'","His family had once printed notices for the station and the harbour. He still used the press for small commissions, and his house stood safely on the upper lane. He was not leaving Greyhaven. What he wanted moved were the old plates and their sample sheets, which belonged to the station under an arrangement nobody had yet located in writing. He had kept them because the station's store was damp. Now the store was empty and the collection was leaving. He wanted the plates to remain with the records that explained them.","Nessa examined one plate without touching its engraved face. It showed the headland as a series of lines: road, shore, footpaths, buildings. The station appeared solid and central. The print shop was a tiny rectangle near the edge. Elian laid a paper impression beside it. Beneath the station's formal title, someone had written local names in pencil. 'The printed version and the version people used,' he said. Mara recognised the track they had just walked, though on the official sheet it ended before reaching the shed. Beyond that point, the map was blank.","## 3. A different kind of weight","Elian had expected a larger vehicle. He had also expected a conversation about access to the collection once it left. Neither expectation seemed unreasonable, but together they made Mara feel that the van had arrived carrying the wrong promise. She explained the library's reading room arrangements as far as she knew them. Nessa added that the material would first be assessed and catalogued. Elian listened, then asked what 'first' meant in months. Neither woman could give him an honest number. Outside, a loose length of wire tapped against the shed roof.","'I am not asking for a display with my name on it,' he said. 'I am asking whether Ruth can find a page when someone rings her.' Ruth stood beside the open door, her hands deep in her cardigan pockets. She had answered questions about Greyhaven for thirty years. Transferring the archive would relieve her of unpaid work, but it would also move the answers beyond the reach of the people who still called. Mara had understood access as a road problem. Here it was becoming a question about what happened after arrival.","Nessa suggested a practical first step. The notebooks could be photographed after Ruth's private entries were separated, and a working copy returned to the local reading room. The plates could be catalogued alongside their paper impressions. She did not promise an exhibition or an immediate public website. She wrote down the actions she could actually undertake and put her own name beside them. Elian read the page twice. 'That sounds smaller than what they said at the meeting,' he observed. 'It is smaller,' Nessa replied. 'It is also something I can do.'","There were twelve plates. Six were small enough to fit into an empty container, but the others required flat support. Together with their protective boards, they were heavier than Mara wanted to guess at. Elian had a scale used for paper deliveries. They weighed the wrapped groups and recorded the figures. The van could carry the complete load within its permitted limit, and there was space along one side if the archive cases were rearranged. Mara telephoned her manager, explained the ownership uncertainty, and obtained approval for temporary deposit rather than permanent acquisition.","Ruth produced a station ledger showing that the plates had been paid for from its printing account. That did not settle every possible question, but it supported taking them into documented temporary care. Nessa photographed the relevant page and prepared a receipt naming Elian as their present custodian. Mara wrote the additional weight on her sheet. It extended beyond the little box provided. For the first time that morning, she found the untidy margin reassuring. A number outside a box was still a number; an unwritten assumption was harder to carry responsibly.","While Nessa packed the smaller plates, Elian showed Mara a narrow green volume. It contained the corrections made to successive map printings. A footpath had moved because a wall fell. A landing had changed its name because boats stopped using the older one. Each alteration included a date and, sometimes, a short explanation. The book belonged with the plates, he said, but he wanted a copy. Mara added it to the receipt. She also noticed a photograph tucked inside the cover: a young woman beside the press, laughing at something beyond the frame.","Elian saw her pause. 'My sister. She ran this place before I did.' He removed the photograph and put it in his shirt pocket. Mara was grateful that he had made the decision himself. There was a particular discomfort in watching people decide, under the pressure of a collection visit, which parts of their lives counted as personal and which might become useful history. She had no wish to turn his hesitation into a donation. Nessa finished the final wrapper and asked him to check the written description of every item.","By noon, the plates were ready for the van. Kit arrived with the first of two low trolleys, sent by Ruth to help. They loaded only what each trolley was designed to carry, leaving the remaining groups on the shed's sturdy table. The route uphill was short but steep, and progress required more patience than strength. Halfway to the station, Mara stopped to change hands. From there, she could see the crossing road and the mainland roofs beyond it. The distance looked manageable. She would remember that impression later with considerable irritation.","## 4. The road closes early","They had loaded half the archive when Kit noticed liquid beneath the van. He did not touch it. He called Mara over, and she saw a thin dark line spreading from inside the front wheel. Nothing in her transport training authorised her to diagnose it by appearance. She rang the fleet service, described the position, and sent photographs. The mechanic asked several questions, then told her not to drive. A recovery vehicle would have to come from the mainland. Mara looked at the remaining cases in the corridor and asked for an arrival estimate.","The answer was at least two hours. She called the crossing office before she called her manager. The controller remembered the van and sounded distracted. An inspection team was reporting deterioration along the road edge. All authorised traffic was being brought forward. Outbound vehicles already on the headland would be directed across by one thirty; after that the barrier would close until the engineers decided otherwise. The controller could not admit a recovery truck on the chance that it might return before the closure. The van would have to remain at the station.","For a few seconds, Mara watched Kit tighten the cap on his water bottle. She could hear herself explaining that they had a permit, that the transfer had been booked for weeks, that the cases were loaded. None of those statements changed a damaged road. She stopped before saying the third. 'Understood,' she told the controller. 'We will not attempt to move the van. Can you confirm the passenger boat arrangements?' The woman gave her the harbour office number and asked her to keep the station telephone free for further instructions.","The boat was still operating between the sheltered inner landing and the mainland harbour. Its skipper, Ivo, had one scheduled afternoon departure and could take the team, subject to the harbour's conditions. Cargo required separate discussion. Mara knew him from the backup section of her plan, which had consisted of his telephone number and the word 'possible'. When he answered, she wished she had asked more useful questions six weeks earlier. He listened to the list, then asked for weights, dimensions, passenger numbers, and the exact loading point. She could answer only three of those immediately.","The station's nearest landing was no longer in use for passenger boarding. The authorised inner landing lay on the other side of the headland. A service lane reached it, but the van was the only available vehicle large enough for the cases. Ivo could not simply bring the boat to the beach below the station. He explained this without apology, and Mara found that she preferred his certainty to encouraging possibilities. He would speak to the harbour supervisor about additional cargo. She should send him the figures and wait for a definite answer.","Nessa was standing beside case nine when Mara returned to the corridor. 'We have a road problem,' Mara said. 'And a vehicle problem. They have unfortunately decided to cooperate.' Kit gave a brief laugh. Ruth did not. She asked whether everyone was safe, and Mara said yes. The cases were dry, the building remained secure, and the road closure did not mean the headland itself was being evacuated. Those facts helped settle the room. They were facing a failed transport plan, not a command to abandon judgement.","Ruth made sandwiches while Mara called the library. Her manager first suggested hiring another van, then understood the closure and stopped suggesting things from a distance. He authorised secure temporary storage at the station if the material could not travel. The collection could be moved later by a properly arranged boat service. That option was real, and Mara wrote it at the top of a fresh page. It prevented every later choice from becoming a false contest between immediate removal and total loss. The station would still have locks and a roof tomorrow.","Yet some material needed attention soon. Nessa had identified damp inside three cases despite their recent packing. The plates and their paper samples were now out of Elian's controlled workshop, though they could return there if necessary. The library had prepared a treatment space and staff for the following day. Moving the vulnerable items would be useful; moving everything merely to complete Mara's sheet would not. She crossed out the word 'complete' in her private notes and replaced it with 'appropriate'. It was an ungainly word, but it asked a better question.","## 5. What can travel","Ivo called at one fifteen. The harbour had authorised a cargo allocation on the afternoon boat, but only within a defined weight and deck arrangement. Four passengers could accompany it. Elian would stay in Greyhaven, as planned, and would help until loading. The boat had sufficient capacity for the selected load; the limiting issue was the combination of size, secure stowage, and the time available to bring it down. Ivo would inspect everything before accepting it. Departure was scheduled for five twenty from the inner landing. He would contact them if conditions changed.","Mara repeated the details while Kit wrote them on the whiteboard. They had four hours, two trolleys, several sets of hands, and a route nobody in the room had yet checked for this load. Elian knew a pedestrian way shorter than the service lane. Part of it followed the old tramway used by the print shop. There were gates, a narrow bridge over a drainage ditch, and a section where the trolleys would need to be unloaded. It was possible, he said, but possible was not the same as quick. Mara asked him to walk it with Kit first.","While they were gone, Nessa opened the packing records. The eighteen cases had been arranged by subject, not by urgency or whether another copy existed. Six held printed reports already digitised and available at the library. Four held unique registers. Three contained the damp material. The others held photographs, correspondence, and equipment records. The plates were a separate addition, requiring careful support. Mara could see why the original tidy numbering was now unhelpful. Case one was first because its subject began earliest in the catalogue, not because it should move first.","They made three groups: material needing prompt attention, unique material ready to travel, and stable material that could remain securely until a later collection. Nessa explained each decision, and Ruth checked whether a supposedly duplicate report contained handwritten additions. Twice, it did. Those pages were recorded and moved into a suitable protective folder with a cross-reference left behind. Nothing was discarded. The aim was to reduce the immediate load without breaking the connections that made the papers understandable. Mara found herself listening to Nessa's descriptions with the concentration she usually reserved for road instructions.","The map plates caused the longest discussion. They were heavy, awkward, and stable in Elian's workshop. Taking them today would consume space that could carry more papers. Elian had not yet returned, so Mara resisted the temptation to make the decision for him. They could propose leaving the plates under the temporary deposit agreement until a dedicated transfer was arranged. Their paper impressions and correction book could accompany the archive now. The objects would remain connected through their records even while they occupied different places. Nessa wrote a clear proposal instead of quietly removing them from the list.","Ruth sat at the table, turning the red index book over in her hands. 'It feels as though we are choosing which things matter,' she said. Nessa put down her pencil. 'We are choosing which things need this journey today.' She showed Ruth the future collection column, including dates by which the library would contact Elian and the station owner. A column could become another empty promise, Mara knew. She added the name of the person responsible for each action, including her own. Then she sent the revised plan to her manager and requested written acknowledgement.","Kit and Elian returned shortly before two. The shorter route was passable, but a gate beside the boatyard was locked. Elian knew the owner and had already arranged to borrow the key. The drainage bridge was sound and open to pedestrians, but too narrow for the wide trolley. They would carry individual containers across and reload on the other side. Kit had measured the opening rather than relying on his impression. He drew the difficult section on the whiteboard. For the first time since the phone call, the afternoon had a shape they could discuss.","Elian accepted the proposal about the plates after reading it in silence. He asked for one change: the library should collect them from his workshop, not expect him to bring them to a landing. Mara added that condition. He took the receipt, folded it carefully, and said he would help return the plates before joining the carrying team. Their combined load was now smaller, properly documented, and still substantial. Mara sent the final weights and dimensions to Ivo. His reply arrived while they were repacking: 'Received. Bring only this list. Call before leaving the station.'","## 6. The borrowed key","Mara rang Ivo from the station doorway at twenty-five past two, because he had asked her to and because his instruction was the only fixed point the afternoon had so far produced. 'We are leaving now,' she said. 'Then I shall see you when I see you,' said Ivo. 'Mind the bridge. People forget the bridge until they are standing on it.' She had not mentioned a bridge. She decided that this was not a puzzle worth spending battery on.","The nine cases from the van came out first. The van had been loaded in numerical order, which had seemed sensible at nine o'clock and now meant that the day's two groups were shuffled together like cards. Three of the nine were going, and seven more were waiting in the corridor. Kit marked all ten on the end with a yellow cross of chalk, a sign that meant nothing to anyone outside the five of them, and Mara noticed that she trusted it more than the printed numbers. The six that were staying went back beside the two that had never left. Nessa covered all eight with plastic sheet, tucked a card under each giving the number, the contents and Mara's name, and stood back like someone leaving a ward for the night.","Ruth locked the station door and tried the handle twice. Through the glass, the clock above the doorway went on ticking over the shapes under their sheets. She did not look at it again, and Mara, who noticed, understood that this was a decision and not an oversight.","The old tramway began behind the shed, where the track from the station ran out of surface and became cinders. Two iron rails still lay in it, worn bright on top and brown at the sides, and for the first hundred metres the wheels of both trolleys wanted to follow them. Kit had walked the route out and back that afternoon and took command of it without being asked. He counted aloud in fives. At two hundred paces everyone stopped. At four hundred they stopped again, whether or not anyone needed to. 'Rest before you need it,' he said. 'It's cheaper.' He said it like a proverb, and by the third stop Mara was prepared to treat it as one.","Elian caught them up at the first bend, wheeling his bicycle. The plates were back under their sheets in the shed, he reported, and the shed was padlocked, which was more than could be said for it in forty years. He took the case Mara had insisted on carrying out of her hands without negotiation, and she let him, although her fingers stayed curled around the shape of the handle for some time afterwards.","At the second rest nobody sat on a case; that was Nessa's single rule. Ruth sat on a boundary stone instead, with the red notebook in a bag across her chest and her chin lifted towards the water. 'Thirty years I have known this path,' she said, 'and I never saw it closed. I never saw it formally opened, either.' Elian, who had pushed barrows down it as a boy, pointed along the cinders. 'Paper came up this way. Paper, then ink, then my grandfather's complaints. The tram stopped the year somebody asked what it was for.' Nobody laughed, but Kit wrote it down.","Where the boatyard wall closed in on their left, the route stopped being a route and became a set of sensations: the trolley strap across Mara's shoulder, the cold of a handle through her glove, the dull arithmetic of another two hundred. It ended at a double gate of green planks, chained and padlocked, and leadership passed to Elian without announcement. 'This is where I become useful,' he said. He took from his pocket a key on a loop of tarred twine and a note the yard's owner had left that morning, written in capitals. KEEP IT LOCKED. NOTHING NEAR THE CRADLES. THE KEY COMES BACK TO THE HOUSE, NOT THE OFFICE. 'She is a reasonable woman,' he said, 'who dislikes being surprised.' He opened the padlock, held the gate while the trolleys went through and, when the last of them was in, threaded the chain from the inside and snapped the lock shut again, because the note said so. Then he put the key back in the pocket where he kept his sister's photograph.","The yard smelled of paint and cold iron. Hulls stood on their cradles like patient animals with the sea taken away. Elian led them along a line of whitewashed stones that kept clear of the winch cables, and nobody spoke until they reached the far fence, where the ground fell into a drainage ditch and a single bridge of plank and concrete crossed it, with an iron handrail on one side only.","Kit measured it again, as if the number might have changed in his absence. 'Seventy-one centimetres,' he said. 'The wide trolley is seventy-eight. I would have preferred to be wrong.' The trolleys went over first, empty and folded, like stretchers. Then Nessa took charge of the bridge as naturally as she had taken charge of the seals. One case at a time; one person on the bridge; the receiver on the far side with both hands free before the carrier set off. Nobody was to speak to the carrier. 'Not because it is dramatic,' she said. 'Because the only thing that can go wrong is somebody turning their head.'","At the far end Kit read each chalk cross, then the number beneath it, and wrote the number down. Mara carried number thirteen herself, one of the damp three, eighteen kilograms of paper that smelled of cellars. Halfway across she made the mistake of looking up. Beyond the fence, beyond the quay lane and the water, the mainland roofs sat in a tidy row. The distance looked manageable. She noted, with something close to gratitude, that the irritation she had been promised had arrived exactly on time.","It took thirty-five minutes. Ruth went over with the notebooks and the folders, Nessa last, and when the final case had come off the bridge Kit read the count aloud: ten crosses, ten numbers, no gaps. Mara wrote it on her sheet. Nobody cheered. Kit's knuckles had gone raw, so along the quay lane Mara took the front and counted in fives, because it was cheaper. It was twenty past four when the lane ended at the water.","## 7. Contents, not numbers","The inner landing was not a place anyone would have chosen to hurry. A granite quay curved round a pool of dark water like a hand cupped to keep something in, and Ivo's boat lay against a pontoon at the bottom of a ramp: a stubby white launch with a green wheelhouse and the name Gannet in plain letters. The tide was out. At high water the ramp was a gentle slope; now it dropped to the pontoon at the angle of a ladder, its boards slick and green.","Kit looked down it and said something that his notebook did not record. The trolleys had been the plan: down the ramp, along the pontoon, alongside the boat. The ramp had been built for a different tide. Ivo, who stood on the pontoon in a navy hat with a clipboard under his arm, offered no opinion. He pointed instead to a hand davit bolted to the edge of the quay, a rusted arm with a crank, used for lobster pots and outboard engines. Kit, to Mara's lasting gratitude, knew exactly how it worked.","'Each case goes into the sling and is weighed in the air,' said Ivo, who had already shackled a brass-faced hook scale into the davit's line. 'Then it comes down to me. You read me the number; I read you the weight.' He was a broad man in his sixties, whose timetable was set by the water and not by anybody's anxiety. 'The limit is two hundred kilograms on that foredeck, passengers not included. It is not an opinion.' Mara took the list from her folder. It said a hundred and ninety-four, a figure he had accepted that afternoon in a single short message.","The scale, older than Mara, took each case with a small shudder and settled. Kit read the chalk and the number, Mara found the line on the list, and Ivo said the weight. Eighteen, said the list, and Mara thought of her shoulder; twenty-one, said Ivo. Sixteen; seventeen. At the end the figure on his pad was two hundred and seven. The three damp cases had drunk eleven kilograms between them since the packers' scales, and straps and polythene had added the rest.","'Seven over,' said Ivo, not unkindly. 'I can take a hundred and ninety-four on a list I have accepted, or two hundred on a deck I have measured. I cannot take two hundred and seven on a hope.' Mara heard herself begin: the permit, the six weeks, the bridge. She stopped at the first clause, as she had stopped that morning with the controller. None of it altered a deck rating.","She looked at the sheet. It held numbers, destination shelves and weights. It could not tell her what any case was for. 'Which seven kilos?' asked Kit. The sheet said what the packers had believed six weeks ago, and the catalogue team had believed, in the same spirit, that an index was a typed list. 'Nessa,' Mara said, 'I need what is in them. Not what they are called.' Nessa already had a thumbnail under the first seal. 'Ten minutes,' she said. 'We have thirty,' said Ivo, 'and the bar will not wait for a tidy list.' Mara could hear the minutes she did not have being spent. She said, 'Go on.'","They did it on the pontoon, as the bridge had taught them: one case at a time, one voice. Kit read the number; Mara said what her sheet promised; Nessa lifted the lid a hand's width and said what was there. A register, as promised. Photographs in sleeves, as promised. A bundle of correspondence tied with grey tape. Time after time the lid agreed with the list, and each time Mara drew a tick and felt the pen slow in her hand, because a tick written in a hurry was only a belief with a mark beside it.","Case fourteen was listed as a register, 1921 to 1934, unique, twenty-three kilograms. Nessa raised the lid and stopped. The register lay there in its tissue, but beneath it was a thick layer of printed bulletins, laid flat to stop the volume sliding. 'Duplicates,' she said, fanning a corner. 'The annual bulletins, the same as in cases two and seven, and those are staying. Five kilos at least. Someone wanted it not to rattle.' 'Thirty years of lists,' said Ivo, 'and not one of them has been wrong in a convenient direction. Take it as a gift, and do not expect another.'","The bulletins rose alone in the sling: five kilograms exactly, a tidy number that belonged to nobody. Nessa folded her scarf into the gap so that the register could not slide, and for the first time that day Mara saw her look undignified in the service of the collection. 'Two hundred and two,' said Ivo. 'Two over.'","The impressions had been the last addition to the list: twelve sheets of stiff card in a grey folder, four kilograms, written in Mara's margin outside the box. She did not decide. She went to where Elian was coiling a line that nobody had asked him to coil. 'They were meant to travel with the archive,' she said. 'That is what we agreed. I can leave them with you instead, until the plates go.' He looked at the folder for a long time, reading it twice, as he had read Nessa's page. 'They were printed in my shop,' he said. 'They can wait for their plates. It will do them good to be together.'","Ivo took Mara's pen, which until then had been used only for ticks. He struck out one line, corrected the weight of case fourteen, wrote a new total of a hundred and ninety-eight, and signed beneath it with the time: sixteen fifty-eight. 'Now it is a list I can carry,' he said. 'Two kilograms is not a margin. It is a rounding error. But it is an honest one.' The bulletins went into the harbour's parcel cage. Freight crossed on Thursdays in the winter, he said, every other week; the next was the twelfth, if the supervisor found deck space for plates. Nessa added a line to Elian's receipt: twelve impressions, held by Elian, to travel with the plates. The date was left blank. Mara looked at the blank for a moment and wrote her own name beside it.","## 8. The short crossing","Ivo stowed the cargo as though he were laying a table. The heaviest cases went amidships and low, against the wheelhouse, where the boat's weight already lived. The three damp ones went on slatted duckboards in the lee, where spray could not reach and air could, under a tarpaulin that Nessa lifted every few minutes. Everything was strapped. 'If I can see it move, it is not stowed,' he said. 'If I cannot see it, it is not checked. Either way it is my problem.' Mara signed the manifest where he tapped, and he countersigned. It was strange how little the signatures weighed.","By then the quay had acquired an audience. Three people had walked down from the upper lane, which was what one did for the last boat when the road was closing for the winter: a man with a spaniel, a woman carrying a flask under her arm like a baton, a boy on the wall with his heels knocking. They did not look at the cases. They looked at Ruth. 'Is it true the photographs are going?' the woman asked. 'Some of them,' said Ruth. 'The ones that need a drier room than ours. The rest are staying under sheets, and the books are going to be copied.' 'And who tells the library who is in the lifeboat picture?' 'You ring me,' said Ruth. 'Not the station. After next week that number rings in an empty corridor.' She took the boy's chalk and wrote her own number on the slate beside the ramp, in the slanted figures of someone who had written the station's on forms for thirty years and had never once been asked to write her own.","Elian stood at the head of the ramp with the yard key in his hand. Ruth took a second key from her ring and held it a moment longer than she needed to. 'The corridor light is on a timer,' she said. 'The clock wants winding on Fridays.' 'Then I shall look in on Fridays,' said Elian. 'I do not trust dates that are not written down, but I can trust a Friday.' Mara, who ought to have objected on the library's behalf, wrote the key on the receipt with her name beside it. It was not strictly within her authority, and she knew exactly whom she would have to tell, and how soon.","At twenty past five, to the minute, Ivo took in the stern line himself. He had not looked at a clock. He had looked at the water against the piles, then at the sky over the bar, then, briefly, at Mara, as if confirming that the list and the person carrying it were the same. Elian stood on the quay with his hands in his pockets, the way people stand when they have decided not to wave. Then he waved.","The crossing took twenty-five minutes. The launch slid out of the pool, past the last cradle and the harbour wall, and met the swell at the bar, which lifted it and set it down again with long, slow indifference. Mara found a place where her back could rest against a case, remembered Nessa's rule, and stood instead, holding a rail that was still cold from the afternoon. The light was going the colour of slate.","Ruth sat in the lee of the wheelhouse with the red notebook on her knees and did not open it. Nessa lowered herself beside her, took from her coat a pear that was the worse for a day of being carried, and cut it in two with a conservator's scalpel. 'You carry a scalpel for fruit,' said Ruth. 'I carry a scalpel for everything,' said Nessa. 'The fruit is the least of it.' Then she took a card from her notebook, wrote on it, and handed it over. It was a list, not a promise. A working copy of both notebooks, with the marked pages withheld, in the Greyhaven reading room by the thirtieth of November. Ruth's review of every withheld page at the library tomorrow at ten. The plate impressions photographed by the fifteenth of December, and a print left with Elian. Nessa's name beside each. Ruth read it twice.","'Put my number on it,' she said, 'not the station's. The people who ring will still be there after the desk has gone.' She turned the red book over. 'They told me those things because it was me. I should like to be asked about every one of them before anybody else decides they are history.' Nessa wrote Ruth's name and home number under her own. 'Then it is a condition,' she said, 'not a favour.'","Kit had stopped writing. 'What do you put,' he asked Mara, 'for the ones that stayed?' 'A reason, a name and a date,' she said. 'The reason has to fit in one sentence. If I cannot say it to someone who was not there, it is not a decision. It is a mood.' 'And if the date slips?' 'Then the name is the person who has to explain why.' He wrote that down too. She watched him do it and, for the first time that day, felt the ordinary pressure of being quoted.","Halfway across she turned. Greyhaven lay behind them as a long dark shape without edges, the road a faint pale thread beside it, the station a smaller darkness on the slope. Then a window lit near the shed, which was Elian's lamp, and a minute later another on the hill: the corridor light, on the timer Ruth had set with the embarrassed air of someone confessing to a superstition. It shone on eight covered cases and a clock that still knew the time. 'It costs a few pence a night,' Ruth said, to nobody. 'Somebody should find it lit.'","## 9. Ticks and names","Mara's manager had come to the harbour in person, having stopped suggesting things from a distance. He stood on the quay in a coat too good for the weather and shook Ivo's hand as though something had been agreed, which, in a way, it had. Behind him the library's own van waited with its back doors open, and beside it a registrar who had stayed late and wore the look of a person who had been told to expect a few cases. Kit tore a page from his notebook before he went to catch the last bus: the bridge count, ten numbers in a column, signed. It was the first receipt a seasonal harbour worker had ever given her.","In the loading bay, under lights that hid nothing, the registrar read the list aloud while Nessa lifted the lids, and ten cases agreed with ten lines. Mara had expected relief. What she felt was closer to the pleasant dullness of a sum that comes out right. Nobody around her used the word 'approximately'.","The treatment room had been made ready for the next morning, but two conservators came back from their dinners with their coats still on, and within the hour the damp cases lay open on the long table: interleaving tissue, blotters, a fan on its lowest setting. Two of the three would be dry and flat within the week. In case thirteen, the one Mara had carried over the bridge, the last forty pages of a ledger had fused into a single grey block. 'It was like this before it left the station,' Nessa said, which was probably true, and which Mara was grateful to be told. She would know in a month, she added, and wrote the date on the folder. From case three she lifted a brass nameplate that had left a green bloom on its wrapping, and read it aloud: GREYHAVEN COASTAL OBSERVATION STATION and, beneath, in smaller letters, ENQUIRIES TO THE CARETAKER. Ruth, on a stool by the radiator with a mug of tea she was not drinking, laughed once, quietly, and then stopped.","At half past eight Ruth and Nessa sat down with the two notebooks and a pencil and began to decide, page by page, which of them were Ruth's to give. They would not finish tonight; the card said ten o'clock tomorrow. It was the one job of the day that nobody tried to hurry.","Mara was alone with her transport sheet at nine. It looked the way an exercise book looks the evening before it is marked: careful columns, a blue pen, the optimism of the first page. Eighteen lines. She counted the ticks twice, which she had not needed to do in years. Ten. Beside the other eight she did not leave a gap. Cases one, two, four, seven, nine and twelve: printed reports, already digitised, under sheets in the station corridor, cards in place. Cases six and fifteen: equipment records, the same. For all eight the reason was written out in a line, the owner was Mara, and the day was the twelfth of November, a Thursday, on Ivo's freight crossing. She had chosen it because Ruth would still hold the keys and could sign for whatever left the building. Ruth's last day was the Friday.","Below the line, in the untidy margin she had once found reassuring, she wrote the rest. Two notebooks, restricted pending review: Nessa, with Ruth, ten o'clock tomorrow. Five kilograms of bulletins in the parcel cage: Ivo, the twelfth. Twelve plates and twelve impressions in Elian's workshop, receipt signed: the library to collect, Mara for transport, Nessa for the condition report, the twelfth. The fused ledger: Nessa, a month. Working copies for the Greyhaven reading room: Nessa, the thirtieth. The van, with its flat wheel in the station yard: the fleet service, when the engineers reopened the road, and Mara until it was gone.","Ten ticks and eight names, and at the foot of the page six lines that had not existed that morning. It was not the sentence she had wanted when she imagined eighteen ticks. It was longer than that, and every part of it was somebody's. She found, rather to her surprise, that she could read it aloud to a stranger without lowering her voice.","She rang Elian first, because he had said he did not trust dates that were not written down, and she read him the twelfth, ten o'clock, the workshop, the receipt. 'And the green book?' he asked. 'Photographed this week. Your copy comes over with us on the twelfth.' She could hear the shed behind him: wood, a settling radiator. 'I shall bring my sister's photograph out of my pocket that morning,' he said. 'Not to keep. To be copied, if somebody asks nicely. I will decide on the day.' 'That is yours to decide,' said Mara. 'Yes,' said Elian. 'I thought you would say that.'","Then she rang the harbour office, where Ivo answered on the second ring. The backup section of her plan had once consisted of his number and the word 'possible'. She read him what she had now: a date, a deck, a weight, a name beside each. 'The supervisor gave you the slot the moment I asked,' he said. 'Thursday the twelfth, ten o'clock, freight deck. Send me the list. When I have approved it, bring only what is on it, and call before you leave the station.'"],"glossary":{"case":"arşiv kutusu (belgeleri taşımak için kapaklı koruma kutusu)","plate":"baskı kalıbı (baskı için işlenmiş metal levha)","impression":"kalıbın kâğıda alınmış baskı örneği","register":"resmî kayıt defteri","landing":"tekneye biniş ve yükleme yeri, iskele","headland":"denize doğru uzanan yüksek kara parçası, burun","tramway":"eskiden yük taşıyan ray hattı","stowage":"yükün tekneye güvenle yerleştirilmesi","allocation":"ayrılan kapasite, tahsis","custodian":"korumaktan sorumlu kişi","bar":"liman ağzındaki kum seti","lee":"rüzgârdan korunan taraf","tick":"saatin tik sesi; kutucuğa konan onay işareti","sling":"yük kaldırmak için halat veya kanvas askı","davit":"tekne ve yük indirmeye yarayan küçük vinç kolu"},"phrases":[{"en":"subject to","tr":"bir koşula bağlı olarak, ...'a tabi"},{"en":"on the chance that","tr":"olabileceği ihtimaline karşı, umuduyla"},{"en":"took charge of","tr":"sorumluluğunu üstlendi, yönetimi ele aldı"},{"en":"in the same spirit","tr":"aynı anlayışla, aynı niyetle"},{"en":"in the service of","tr":"bir şeyin hizmetinde, uğrunda"},{"en":"to the minute","tr":"tam zamanında, dakikasına kadar"},{"en":"look in on","tr":"kısaca uğrayıp bakmak, kontrol etmek"},{"en":"in person","tr":"bizzat, şahsen"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do Ruth's two notebooks matter to the collection?","options":["They list the weight of every case for the boat","They are the only record of the road closure","They name people and explain local context that the formal records leave out","They prove that Elian owns the printing plates"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Ruth'un defterleri, resmî kayıtlarda kimliği belirsiz kalan kişileri ve olayların yerel bağlamını açıklar; arşivin anlaşılması bu defterlere bağlıdır."},{"q":"What stops the team from finishing the job by road?","options":["The van leaks and the controller brings the closure forward","Ruth refuses to lock the station","The library cancels the transfer","Nessa decides the cases are too damp to travel"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Van arızalanır ve yol denetimi kapanışı öne çeker; kurtarma aracı da gelemediği için ekip tekneyle taşıma planına yönelir."},{"q":"Why are the cases carried over the drainage bridge one at a time?","options":["Ivo has asked for every case to be counted twice","The yard's owner allows only one person through at a time","The cinders are too soft for the trolleys","The bridge is too narrow for the wide trolley, and Nessa wants one careful carrier on it at a time"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Köprü geniş el arabası için fazla dardır; Nessa da köprüde aynı anda yalnızca tek bir dikkatli taşıyıcı olmasını ister, bu yüzden kutular tek tek geçirilir."},{"q":"How does Mara bring the load under Ivo's two-hundred-kilogram limit?","options":["She persuades Ivo to accept a few extra kilograms","She removes duplicate bulletins from one case and, with Elian's agreement, leaves the impressions with him","She leaves a passenger on the quay","She sends case fourteen back to the station"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Kaptanın sınırı 200 kg'dır. Kutuları gerçek içerikle karşılaştıran ekip, kutu 14'ten çift nüshaları çıkarır ve Elian'ın onayıyla baskı örneklerini ona bırakır; yük 198 kg'a iner."},{"q":"How does Mara's final sheet differ from the 'eighteen ticks' she wanted at the start?","options":["It shows eighteen ticks after a second crossing that night","It shows no ticks, because the transport sheet was damaged","It shows ten ticks, and every other item has a named owner, a reason and a date","It shows fourteen ticks, with the plates counted as extra cases"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Sayfada on onay işareti vardır; geri kalan her kalemin gerekçesi, sorumlusu ve tarihi yazılmıştır. Eksiksiz liste hedefi, hesabı verilebilir bir kayda dönüşür."}],"words":7726,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-metamorphosis","type":"summary","title":"The Metamorphosis","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C1","genre":"drama","work":{"title":"The Metamorphosis","titleTr":"Dönüşüm","author":"Franz Kafka","year":1915},"source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #22367 — Die Verwandlung, German original","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/22367","license":"Kaynak eser; metin Lantern English için yazılmış özgün özet ve incelemedir."},"summaryTr":"Gregor Samsa'nın açıklanmayan dönüşümü üzerinden aile, emek ve insanın değeri: olay örgüsünü, finali ve farklı yorumları ele alan özgün İngilizce okuma rehberi.","paragraphs":["## 1. An Impossible Event in an Ordinary Home","Franz Kafka's The Metamorphosis, first published in 1915, begins with an impossible physical change and follows its disturbingly practical consequences. Gregor Samsa, a travelling salesman, discovers that his body has become that of an enormous, repulsive creature. The narrative never supplies a cause, a cure, or a scientific explanation. This guide discusses the complete story, including its ending. Its account and interpretations are written independently in English; they are not passages taken from an English translation. The central question is less how the transformation happened than what happens to a person whose familiar place in the world abruptly disappears.","It is tempting to give Gregor a precise animal identity, but that closes a question the work leaves open. Kafka's German designation suggests vermin, something regarded as disgusting and unwelcome, without securely identifying a biological species. Gregor has an unfamiliar hard body and numerous small legs, yet these details do not establish that he is certainly a beetle or a cockroach. His uncertainty about his own movements matters more than an accurate drawing could. The reader encounters a conscious person struggling with a body that other people cannot accept, rather than a creature presented for calm scientific inspection.","The setting remains recognisable. There are doors, furniture, railway connections, household expenses, employers, and meals. These ordinary arrangements do not pause in the face of Gregor's extraordinary condition. Indeed, their continued operation produces much of the story's pressure. A missed train can seem more urgent to him than the collapse of his physical identity. This mismatch can be darkly funny, although the humour never removes the distress. Readers are invited to notice how thoroughly everyday obligations have organised his thinking before the first page begins. The change exposes that organisation by making obedience almost physically impossible.","Gregor lives with his parents and his younger sister, Grete. His earnings have supported the household, and his father's financial difficulties have helped bind him to an exhausting occupation. He imagines a future in which the debt will be repaid and he can leave the job. Until then, he treats his own dissatisfaction as something to postpone. His affection for Grete includes an unannounced ambition to support her musical education. These wishes reveal an inner life larger than his employment, but they also show how habitually he expresses love through responsibility for somebody else's future.","A useful reading method is to keep three questions separate. What physically happens? What does Gregor believe is happening? What judgement does the reader make about it? The narrator gives unusually close access to Gregor's thoughts, but closeness does not make every assumption he holds correct. He can misread his family's needs, excuse injuries, or underestimate his own distress. At the same time, the family's inability to understand his speech does not erase the consciousness the reader continues to encounter. The resulting imbalance of knowledge makes apparently simple household decisions ethically complicated throughout the novella.","## 2. The Morning of the Transformation","On the first morning Gregor tries to get out of bed, but the body that once performed familiar actions no longer obeys him. Turning over, coordinating his legs, and reaching the floor become difficult projects. He worries about railway timetables and the employer's reaction to his absence. His thoughts repeatedly return to work even while his immediate experience should make work irrelevant. This is not evidence that he finds the transformation normal. Rather, the normal rules of his life continue to supply the categories through which he tries to manage an event those rules cannot explain.","His family notices that he has not left. They speak to him through the locked doors of his room, initially treating the delay as a manageable problem. Gregor answers, but his voice has changed. He can still formulate meaningful intentions, while the people outside increasingly hear sounds they cannot interpret. This separation between thought and audible speech is essential: Gregor has not simply become mentally absent. He remains concerned about the responses his words will produce, although he has lost the ordinary means of checking that others receive the meaning he intends.","A senior employee from the firm arrives at the apartment to investigate. His presence brings workplace authority directly into the domestic space. Rather than allowing Gregor privacy, the visitor treats his failure to appear as grounds for suspicion and criticism. Gregor attempts to defend his reliability and reassure everyone. The more urgently he explains, however, the less his explanation can achieve. Kafka builds a painful situation in which effort and success move apart. Gregor behaves like someone who believes that sufficient cooperation will restore trust, but the basic conditions necessary for cooperation have already broken down.","Eventually Gregor manages to unlock the door with his mouth. The action requires enormous effort and causes injury, yet he approaches it as a necessary step towards satisfying the people outside. When they see him, their reactions are fear and shock. His mother is overwhelmed, the visitor retreats, and Gregor's attempt to follow and explain only increases the alarm. The scene makes a distinction between intention and effect: his wish to prevent misunderstanding produces behaviour that others experience as threatening. Readers know the wish behind the movement; the witnesses in the apartment do not.","Gregor's father forces him back towards his room. The entrance becomes a physical obstacle because Gregor cannot move through it quickly or easily in his altered body. He is injured as he is driven inside. The door closes, establishing the arrangement that will govern much of the remaining story: the family occupies the shared home, while Gregor becomes its concealed inhabitant. No official decision has declared that he no longer belongs. Exclusion takes shape through movement, panic, force, and the practical use of a door. This gives the domestic setting a disturbing institutional quality.","The opening also changes the reader's expectations about narrative time. A short period of an ordinary morning expands into an extended sequence of calculations and failed movements. Gregor's attention makes the smallest physical difficulty substantial. His employer, meanwhile, treats the same passing minutes as evidence against him. Time therefore has competing meanings: bodily adjustment takes as long as it takes, while commercial obligation demands punctuality regardless of circumstances. The story does not need an explicit argument about productivity to show the cruelty that can emerge when one measure of time overrides every other.","## 3. Care, Appetite, and Uncertain Adaptation","Grete initially takes responsibility for feeding Gregor and tending his room. Her care involves observation and experimentation because he cannot explain his needs. Food that once appealed to him no longer does, while neglected or decaying food becomes attractive. Grete offers alternatives and notices what he eats. These actions matter because they recognise that his preferences still deserve attention, even though those preferences now seem strange. Early care is therefore neither a complete restoration of intimacy nor mere mechanical maintenance. It is an imperfect effort to respond to a living being under unfamiliar conditions.","Gregor tries to reduce the discomfort his appearance causes. He hides beneath the sofa and uses a covering to keep himself out of sight when Grete enters. From his perspective, concealment is considerate. From another perspective, it trains both siblings to organise their encounters around the assumption that his visible presence is intolerable. The same action can preserve a relationship and deepen his disappearance within it. This ambiguity is typical of the novella: a practical accommodation may contain affection, shame, and submission at once, without any single motive fully explaining what occurs.","The family has to reconsider its finances. Gregor learns that his father has retained some resources from the earlier business failure and that not all of Gregor's earnings have been spent. He has not previously understood the household's situation in full. This discovery should not be exaggerated into proof that the family could easily have lived without his work. Savings are limited, and the future remains uncertain. Nevertheless, it unsettles his picture of himself as the only barrier against immediate disaster. His self-sacrifice has rested partly on information and assumptions that he never fully examined.","The transformation also changes what Gregor can enjoy. He learns to climb on walls and across the ceiling, finding forms of movement unavailable to his former body. These moments complicate any account in which everything after the change is simply loss. There is adaptation, sensation, and temporary relief within his confinement. Yet new bodily pleasures coexist with memories and attachments that remain recognisably human. The story refuses a clean division in which a human mind occupies an entirely alien machine. Body and consciousness influence one another, making his condition unstable rather than neatly separable.","Grete's growing expertise gives her a new position within the family. Because she attends to Gregor, she can speak with authority about what he supposedly needs. That authority is understandable, but it is not identical to direct knowledge of his wishes. Nobody has solved the communication problem. A reader can appreciate her labour while still questioning decisions made on his behalf. The distinction matters beyond this particular household: caring for someone creates responsibilities, but it does not automatically grant perfect access to their inner experience. Kafka makes that gap visible without providing an easy alternative arrangement.","At this stage, the family has established routines without reaching understanding. Feeding, cleaning, and listening through doors allow life to continue, but continuation should not be mistaken for a settled response. Gregor measures success partly by how little trouble he causes. Grete measures needs through behaviour she can observe. The parents react through fear, tenderness, avoidance, and concern about money. These different standards rarely meet in a shared conversation. Because the ordinary channel of speech is unavailable, assumptions can harden without being challenged by the person whose life those assumptions most directly determine.","## 4. The Room and the Apple Wound","A disagreement over Gregor's furniture exposes the conflict between his present body and his former identity. Grete thinks that removing furniture will give him more space to crawl. His mother fears that emptying the room would imply abandoning hope of his recovery and would erase the familiar surroundings of his human life. Both positions have a kind of logic. More open space might make movement easier; retained objects might sustain continuity. The difficulty is that Gregor cannot participate in the discussion through ordinary speech, although he is the person who must inhabit its consequences.","Gregor becomes distressed by the removal of possessions. He clings to a framed picture of a woman in fur, protecting an object associated with his previous room and habits. The picture has prompted many interpretations, including readings about desire, ownership, and the remnants of private identity. The secure narrative fact is simpler: he actively resists losing it. A careful interpretation begins with that action before deciding what the object symbolises. Its importance lies partly in being something he chooses to preserve when other people are deciding which elements of his former world still matter.","His mother sees him and collapses. Confusion follows, and when his father returns, he interprets the situation as a threat caused by Gregor. The father attacks him by throwing apples. One becomes lodged in Gregor's body, leaving a severe wound that never properly heals. This is a decisive change in the story's physical conditions. Gregor's later weakness is not merely a natural effect of being transformed. Violence by a member of his family contributes directly to his deterioration. Any account that treats his decline as wholly inevitable leaves out this concrete responsibility.","The apple remains associated with injury over time. It is not just an object used during a brief outburst that the story then forgets. Gregor carries the consequences into subsequent days, and his mobility is reduced. This continuity gives the violence a duration beyond the father's anger. Readers may connect the apple with religious stories or other symbolic traditions, but such associations remain interpretations. The novella does not provide a single official symbolic explanation. The bodily fact should remain visible beneath every larger reading: a vulnerable family member has been wounded and continues living with the damage.","After the attack, the family makes a limited adjustment. Gregor is sometimes allowed to hear or observe the household through an open door in the evening. This can resemble a form of inclusion, but it also underlines how far ordinary membership has been reduced. He watches from a distance while the others occupy the shared room. Contact depends on their arrangements and tolerance. The door is no longer simply shut, yet the distribution of power remains unequal. Small permissions can offer real comfort while still falling far short of the relationship Gregor remembers and desires.","The room now carries several meanings simultaneously. It is a refuge from frightened reactions, a prison, a place of bodily experimentation, and a store of memories. Its changing contents register changes in Gregor's social position. Instead of assigning it one fixed symbolic meaning, track how its use develops. Who enters freely? Who must hide? Who decides what stays? What is placed inside because it is unwanted elsewhere? These concrete questions connect the physical setting to the larger issue of belonging, while keeping the interpretation accountable to observable details in the work.","## 5. A Household Reorganised around Work","Gregor's parents and sister begin earning money. His father takes employment involving a uniform, his mother does sewing, and Grete works while also studying skills that may improve her prospects. Their activity reveals capacities that Gregor's earlier account of family dependence had not fully recognised. It also leaves them tired. The story presents neither effortless liberation nor pure victimhood. The household becomes more economically active at the same time that its care for Gregor deteriorates. Independence for some family members and abandonment for another develop within the same changing arrangement.","The father's uniform becomes a striking visual detail. He wears the signs of his new employment into the home, where exhaustion and authority coexist. One possible reading is that outside discipline strengthens his domestic role, while also revealing that he remains subordinate in another hierarchy. He can dominate Gregor and still be a worker answerable to others. This prevents a simple contrast between a powerful father and a powerless employee. Power is distributed unevenly across relationships, and someone subject to pressure in one setting may pass pressure down to someone more vulnerable in another.","As Grete's responsibilities increase, the early attentiveness of her care fades. Food and cleaning become more hurried, and Gregor's room accumulates dirt and unwanted objects. A cleaning woman deals with him with a rough familiarity that differs from the family's fearful sensitivity. Her response suggests that disgust is not the only possible reaction to his appearance, although her manner is not a model of tenderness. Different people can encounter the same body in different ways. The family response is therefore emotionally powerful without being the only imaginable human response the text permits.","The family takes in three lodgers, bringing paying outsiders into the apartment. Their expectations affect how rooms are used and how the household behaves. Gregor must be kept out of sight, while objects displaced by the new arrangement end up in his room. The logic of income extends further into the home: space is allocated according to what can attract payment, and the inhabitant who cannot contribute becomes easier to treat as an inconvenience. This is an interpretation of the arrangement, grounded in the actual redistribution of rooms, attention, and labour.","It would be too simple to say that the family stops caring solely because money matters. Fear, exhaustion, shame, disappointment, and uncertainty also shape their conduct. Yet economic language is unusually persistent in Gregor's own thinking and in the household's organisation. The novella allows readers to examine how material strain can narrow sympathy without claiming that strain makes cruelty unavoidable or innocent. Explaining pressure and excusing conduct are different tasks. A responsible reading can acknowledge the family's difficult circumstances while still judging the violence, neglect, and withdrawal of recognition that Gregor experiences.","The change also tests the idea of reciprocity. Gregor has worked for the others, so readers may expect the others to care for him in return. That expectation exposes their failures, but it contains a further problem: would he deserve care only because he had previously earned it? The novella can push beyond an exchange of services towards a more demanding question about human worth. If protection depends entirely on past or future usefulness, the most vulnerable remain at risk. Gregor's history makes the injustice especially visible, but it need not be the sole basis for recognising it.","## 6. The Violin and the Final Rejection","One evening Grete plays the violin while the lodgers are present. Gregor is deeply moved by the music and comes out of his room. His response recalls the ambitions he once held for Grete: he wanted to help her develop her talent through professional study. Music draws him towards a form of connection that the household's practical routines have failed to preserve. The scene does not prove that art can solve his condition. Instead, it briefly reveals the continuing force of feeling and aspiration within someone increasingly treated as incapable of meaningful human attachment.","Gregor imagines bringing Grete closer and communicating his plan for her future. His thoughts are affectionate, but they are also complicated by possessiveness. He pictures an intimacy arranged around his own room and desires, while she has a separate life and cannot hear his explanation. Readers need not choose between seeing him as wholly selfless and condemning his wish. The scene reveals how dependence, love, memory, and a desire for control can overlap. His intentions deserve attention, but Grete's imagined agreement should not be confused with anything she actually expresses.","The lodgers notice Gregor. Their reaction turns his appearance into a dispute about the conditions of their stay, further humiliating and alarming the family. After they withdraw, Grete argues that the household cannot continue living with the creature. Her position marks the reversal of her earlier role as his most active carer. She separates the being in the room from the brother she remembers, and treats its departure as necessary for the family's survival. The decisive injury here is not physical. Gregor hears the person closest to him deny the continuity of his identity.","Grete's change should be read across the story rather than reduced to a sudden discovery of a secretly cruel character. She has provided care, assumed work, and lived through fear and exhaustion. None of this makes her final rejection harmless. It makes the rejection part of a process through which responsibility becomes resentment and uncertainty becomes a firm exclusionary judgement. The novella allows the reader to understand how she arrives there while still confronting what her judgement does. Understanding a character's motives does not require accepting the moral conclusion that character reaches.","Gregor returns slowly to his room. He has heard that his continued presence is considered unbearable, and he accepts the idea that he should disappear. During the night he dies. His death follows lasting injury, declining nourishment, isolation, and the final collapse of his hope of belonging. The narrative does not present a medical diagnosis, so it is inaccurate to identify one exclusive physical cause with certainty. Nor does it show a deliberate violent act by which he kills himself. What it does show is a life weakened across several connected bodily and emotional dimensions.","His final thoughts retain affection for his family. This makes the ending especially painful because his acceptance may reflect the same habits that governed him before the transformation: he continues measuring his own existence by its consequences for others. A reader might interpret his withdrawal as a last act of love, an internalisation of rejection, or both. These readings remain open to argument. The facts do not require us to celebrate his disappearance as the correct solution. His agreement with the family's judgement is part of the tragedy, rather than proof that the judgement is just.","## 7. Death, Relief, and the Journey Outside","The cleaning woman discovers Gregor's body in the morning and informs the family. Their response includes relief. The lodgers are dismissed, and the household begins to imagine a future no longer organised around concealing or supporting Gregor. This turn can shock readers who expect death to restore a lost person's value through grief. Kafka offers no extended ceremony in which Gregor's sacrifices are publicly recognised. The practical world continues to move, and the speed of that movement makes his previous isolation visible from another angle: even his absence is quickly absorbed into planning.","The parents and Grete leave the apartment together and take a tram journey into the open air. They discuss their employment and the possibility of moving to a smaller, more suitable home. The enclosed rooms give way to space and movement. For the surviving family members, this is a release into possibility. For the reader who has inhabited Gregor's restricted experience, the same release may feel morally disturbing. The ending holds these perspectives together. A brighter setting and hopeful conversation do not automatically turn everything preceding them into a necessary preparation for happiness.","The parents also notice Grete's physical maturity and begin to think about her future marriage. Her development offers another meaning of transformation within the work. Gregor's body has changed catastrophically; Grete has moved towards adult possibilities in ways her parents now recognise. The parallel should not erase their differences. She has not undergone the same kind of change, and her future remains imagined rather than narrated. Still, the ending transfers attention from the son's impossible body to the daughter's socially promising one, inviting questions about how the family assigns value to each.","One interpretation sees the final journey as evidence that Gregor had prevented the others from developing. Their new employment and plans can support part of that argument. Yet describing his death as beneficial risks adopting the household's perspective without examining its cost. An alternative reading stresses that families can adapt while also failing the person whose crisis forced the adaptation. These possibilities are not mutually exclusive. The novella can show genuine capacities emerging and genuine cruelty occurring in the same process. Literary complexity often depends on refusing to let one true observation cancel another.","The ending also changes the narrative's access to consciousness. For most of the work, Gregor's thoughts give the reader a connection unavailable to his family. After his death, that centre of awareness is gone, and the narrative follows the survivors. The shift creates a formal experience of exclusion: the person whose inner life filled the story no longer contributes to its future. Readers carry memories that the family's plans do not fully acknowledge. That difference between what a narrative moves past and what a reader remembers helps explain why the final scene can remain unsettling long after it ends.","## 8. Work, Identity, and the Limits of an Explanation","A reading centred on labour begins with Gregor's habit of treating exhaustion as obligation. Before his transformation, he is already travelling according to other people's schedules and deferring his own wishes. Afterwards, the family increasingly judges his place through what he can no longer do. The physical transformation can therefore be understood as making an existing form of alienation visible. This does not mean that the creature is merely a code for an unhappy worker. The bodily discomfort, altered appetite, and unpredictable movements have their own narrative reality and should not disappear into an abstract formula.","Illness and disability provide another productive lens. Gregor suddenly needs support, cannot use familiar spaces easily, and faces decisions made without effective communication with him. These conditions invite questions about access, dignity, and the burden assigned to carers. The analogy has limits: real disabled people are not transformed vermin, and their lives should not be treated as versions of Gregor's fate. A thoughtful comparison examines the household's responses rather than treating his fictional body as a definition of disability. The relevant issue is how dependence changes the recognition someone receives from other people.","A family reading considers the uneven distribution of duty. Gregor takes pride in supporting the others, but his central role may also prevent him from seeing them clearly. His father is more capable than he assumes, and Grete has desires beyond the future he privately plans for her. This complicates the picture of an entirely generous provider surrounded by entirely selfish dependants. Nevertheless, complexity does not distribute responsibility equally for every action. Gregor's limitations as a brother or son do not justify the attack with apples or the progressive denial of his membership in the family.","The absence of an explanation is itself a structural choice. A story that revealed a curse, a hidden experiment, or a moral punishment could direct readers towards solving or reversing the event. Kafka supplies none of these. The reader must live with uncertainty while attending to consequences that remain concrete. This produces a different kind of question: when the cause of suffering is unknown, what responsibilities still exist? The family does not need a complete account of the transformation to observe hunger, pain, fear, or attachment. Knowledge may be incomplete without care becoming meaningless.","Avoid claiming that one interpretation unlocks every detail. An apple may recall punishment, but it is also something a frightened and angry father can throw. The room may represent confinement, but it also contains furniture Gregor values. The violin may suggest artistic transcendence, yet it is played within a household negotiating its finances and reputation. Strong analysis keeps several levels in contact. It makes a claim, points to a detail, explains the connection, and acknowledges what the claim does not settle. This method preserves the novella's ambiguity while still allowing a clear, defensible argument.","## 9. Narrative Voice and a Language Reading Practice","The narration stays remarkably composed in the presence of an impossible event. This controlled tone encourages the reader to spend time on practical details rather than escape into spectacle. Gregor's calculations about trains or door handles receive serious attention, even when the mismatch between problem and response is absurd. The effect depends on precision rather than explicit emotional cues. Readers are not repeatedly told that the situation is terrible; they encounter a sequence in which familiar habits continue after the conditions that once supported them have vanished. The calm surface makes that persistence easier to notice.","Close third-person narration gives access to Gregor without turning the work into his direct testimony. We learn what he thinks, remembers, fears, and imagines, while also seeing consequences he cannot fully control. This form is useful for studying the difference between a character's belief and a narrative fact. When Gregor assumes that others will respond well if he explains himself, the belief is sincere but the expected response does not follow. When he imagines Grete's delight at his educational plans, the imagined reaction remains his construction. Grammatical access to a thought is not evidence of its accuracy.","For English practice, distinguish expressions that report events from expressions that frame interpretation. A sentence such as 'The father throws apples' describes an action. A sentence such as 'The attack may reveal fear turning into authority' offers a reading of that action. Words such as 'may', 'suggests', and 'can be understood as' are not signs of weak thinking when the evidence permits more than one conclusion. They make the strength of a claim visible. Practise revising an absolute statement into a precise one without removing its main argument or making every sentence uncertain.","Another useful exercise is to follow agency through your own summary. Compare 'Gregor is injured' with 'His father injures Gregor'. Both can be accurate, but they direct attention differently. The first highlights the condition; the second identifies responsibility. Neither grammatical form is always preferable. Choose according to the question you are examining, and notice when passive wording hides an actor your argument needs to name. This exercise is especially valuable in a work where practical descriptions can make exclusion appear to happen by itself, even though particular people perform the actions that produce it.","Keep a small vocabulary record organised by relationships rather than isolated definitions. Place 'duty', 'obligation', 'dependence', and 'reciprocity' together, then explain how their meanings differ in Gregor's situation. Duty concerns a perceived responsibility; dependence concerns reliance; reciprocity concerns something returned between parties. They overlap without being interchangeable. Write an original sentence for each, using evidence from the guide rather than imitating the novelist's wording. The aim is to build language that can carry distinctions. Advanced reading develops when a learner can explain why two plausible descriptions are not quite saying the same thing.","## 10. Questions to Carry into a Full Reading","Before beginning the novella itself, draw a simple map of the apartment as you understand it, leaving uncertain details open. Mark the doors and note how access changes. After each major event, add one sentence about who controls movement. This is not an exercise in producing a perfect architectural plan. It trains attention towards the connection between space and relationship. Gregor's exclusion becomes easier to analyse when you can describe actual restrictions rather than relying only on broad words such as loneliness. Concrete observations give an interpretation something the reader can examine and dispute.","Next, create two columns for Grete. In one, record actions that express care; in the other, record actions or judgements that distance her from Gregor. Do not force every detail into only one column. Removing furniture, for example, can respond to a bodily need while threatening continuity with a former identity. If an action belongs in both columns, explain why. This prevents a character study from becoming a search for a single flattering or hostile adjective. It also makes change visible as a process involving repeated decisions, increasing pressure, and altered assumptions.","Try a short argument about the ending. Start with a claim about the tram journey, then support it with two details from the final sequence and one earlier detail. Include an alternative reading that a reasonable reader might defend. Finally, explain why your argument remains persuasive despite that alternative, or revise it if the counterargument exposes a weakness. The task is not to guess a hidden answer possessed by the author. It is to practise accountable interpretation: your conclusion should be connected to the work, clear about its assumptions, and open to revision when the evidence requires it.","For discussion, ask whether Gregor's inner life changes as much as his body. Evidence of continuity includes his concern for the family and his response to Grete's music. Evidence of change includes altered appetite, new pleasures of movement, and shifts in what his room means to him. Neither list alone settles the issue. Consider whether identity must remain unchanged to deserve recognition. This question moves beyond deciding how human he still is towards examining why observers make humanity depend on familiar appearance, understandable speech, productive work, or behaviour that causes them no discomfort.","A final reflection can return to the story's unanswered cause. Write a paragraph explaining what would change if the transformation received a complete explanation at the end. Would blame become easier to assign? Would the household's decisions seem less important? Would a cure turn care into a temporary investment in recovery? These are hypothetical questions about narrative design, not missing scenes to invent for Kafka. They help clarify what the existing work achieves by refusing closure on one level while bringing Gregor's life to a definite end. The mystery persists; the consequences have already occurred."],"glossary":{"vermin":"istenmeyen, iğrenç sayılan zararlı canlı","lodgers":"evde ücret karşılığı kalan kiracılar","reciprocity":"karşılıklılık, karşılıklı sorumluluk","agency":"eyleme gücü ve sorumluluğu","alienation":"kişinin kendine veya çevresine yabancılaşması","uniform":"görev giysisi, üniforma","recognition":"kişiliğinin ve değerinin tanınması","confinement":"kapalı yerde tutulma","accountable":"hesap verebilir; burada: kanıta dayalı, gerekçelendirilebilir","transcendence":"gündelik sınırların aşılması"},"phrases":[{"en":"in the face of","tr":"karşısında"},{"en":"takes responsibility for","tr":"sorumluluğunu üstlenir"},{"en":"on his behalf","tr":"onun adına"},{"en":"at the same time","tr":"aynı zamanda"},{"en":"in return","tr":"karşılığında"},{"en":"grounds for suspicion","tr":"şüphe için gerekçe"},{"en":"open to revision","tr":"yeniden değerlendirmeye açık"},{"en":"assigns value to","tr":"değer biçer"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the novella establish about the cause of Gregor's transformation?","options":["His employer deliberately causes it.","It results from a clearly identified illness.","Its cause is never explained.","Grete discovers a family curse."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Anlatı dönüşümün nedenini açıklamaz; rehber de bir neden veya kesin hayvan türü uydurmaz."},{"q":"Why is the furniture dispute important?","options":["It brings Gregor's present bodily needs into conflict with links to his former life.","It proves that Gregor wants to sell the apartment.","It reveals that Grete has never entered his room.","It settles the question of how to cure him."],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Boş alan hareketi kolaylaştırabilir; eşyaların korunması ise eski kimliğiyle bağını sürdürebilir."},{"q":"Which description best captures Grete's changing role?","options":["She rejects Gregor immediately and never helps him.","She initially cares for him but eventually demands his removal.","She understands his speech throughout the story.","She leaves the household before his transformation."],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Grete başlangıçta yiyecek ve temizlikle ilgilenir; süreç içinde bakımı zayıflar ve sonunda Gregor'u reddeder."},{"q":"What does the guide identify as a consequence of the father's apple attack?","options":["Gregor regains his human voice.","The lodgers leave that same morning.","Grete stops playing music permanently.","Gregor suffers a lasting wound that reduces his mobility."],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Vücudunda kalan elma kalıcı yaraya ve hareketlerinin kısıtlanmasına yol açar; sonraki zayıflamasında aile içi şiddetin payı vardır."},{"q":"Why can the family's final tram journey feel unsettling?","options":["It reveals that Gregor is secretly alive.","It restores the original household arrangement.","Their hopeful plans follow Gregor's death without fully acknowledging his suffering.","It provides the missing explanation for the transformation."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Ailenin gelecek umudu Gregor'un ölümüyle yan yana gelir; okur onun acısını hatırlarken aile yeni planlara yönelir."}],"words":5183,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-pleasure-of-fear","type":"article","title":"The Pleasure of Fear: Why We Choose to Be Frightened","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C1","genre":"horror","summaryTr":"İnsanlar neden korku hikâyelerini isteyerek okur? Yazı; Aristoteles'in katarsisinden Freud'un 'tekinsiz' kavramına, Poe'nun güvenilmez anlatıcısından Jacobs'un ima gücüne uzanan bir yolculuk sunuyor.","paragraphs":["Nobody forces us to read horror. We choose it, usually in the evening, preferably with the lights turned low, and we are rewarded with a racing pulse and a restless night. If fear is an unpleasant emotion, shaped by evolution to make us flee from danger, why do we seek it out for entertainment? Philosophers call this puzzle the paradox of horror, and it is considerably older than the genre itself.","## The paradox of safe danger","Aristotle already observed in the Poetics that audiences of tragedy experience pity and fear, and he suggested that the experience ends in catharsis, a purging or clarification of those emotions. Whatever precisely he meant, and scholars still debate the term, the idea has proved remarkably durable: we may turn to frightening stories in order to discharge anxieties that everyday life gives us no opportunity to express. Modern psychologists offer a complementary explanation. Fear in fiction produces the physical symptoms of arousal, such as a faster heartbeat, tense muscles and heightened attention, but because the reader knows that he is safe, the brain can interpret that arousal as excitement rather than terror. When the story ends, the relief that follows may be intensely pleasurable. Some researchers describe this process as excitation transfer. A roller coaster works on the same principle: the danger is real enough to thrill and unreal enough to enjoy.","## The familiar made strange","In 1919 Sigmund Freud published an essay on the uncanny (in German, das Unheimliche), the peculiar dread we feel when something familiar becomes disturbingly strange. His examples included doubles, dolls that seem to be alive, and the return of things we believed we had left behind. The concept explains why so much of the best horror is domestic. The most chilling settings are not remote castles but ordinary rooms, family dinners and well-known faces that have changed in some small, terrible way.","W. W. Jacobs's \"The Monkey's Paw\" (1902) is a model of this strategy. Its setting is a comfortable English house on a stormy night, where a father, a mother and their grown-up son sit by the fire, playing chess and talking. The supernatural enters in the form of a withered object brought home by a visiting sergeant-major, and what follows is horrifying precisely because the family's desires are so ordinary and so understandable. Jacobs was better known as a humorous writer about sailors and dockside life, and perhaps that experience taught him how thin the line between comedy and dread can be. He understood, at any rate, that the reader's sympathy is the true source of fear: we are frightened for the family, not by the paw.","## A mind we cannot trust","Another resource of horror is the unreliable narrator, a storyteller whose account the reader has reason to doubt. The term was popularised by the critic Wayne C. Booth in The Rhetoric of Fiction (1961), but the technique is far older. Edgar Allan Poe's \"The Tell-Tale Heart\" (1843) remains one of the most economical examples ever written. The narrator opens by insisting that he is not mad, and then proceeds to describe, with painstaking pride in his own calmness, how he murdered an old man whose pale eye disturbed him. The more he argues that a madman could never have acted so cautiously, the more convincingly he demonstrates the opposite.","The effect depends on a gap between what the narrator says and what the reader understands, a kind of dramatic irony turned inward. We are placed inside a consciousness and forced to witness its distortions from within. This is more disturbing than any external monster, because it suggests that the instrument we use to understand reality, the mind, can fail. First-person narration also removes the safety of distance: the speaker's obsession becomes the lens through which everything is seen, and we must struggle to detect what is truly happening beneath his account. Even the sound that finally destroys him, the beating of a heart, may exist only in his guilty imagination, or may be the throbbing of his own pulse; the text never tells us which.","## What we do not see","Horror writers are frequently advised, and frequently ignore the advice, that suggestion is more powerful than display. H. P. Lovecraft, in his survey Supernatural Horror in Literature (written in the 1920s), argued that the oldest and strongest kind of fear is fear of the unknown, a claim that later writers have often treated as a rule of craft. By withholding the details of the threat, the author forces the reader's imagination to supply the details, and the imagination, being personal, will usually find the most disturbing possibility available. In \"The Monkey's Paw\" the climax takes place largely in darkness, behind a closed door, and the story closes on nothing more than an empty road.","## Fear as a mirror of its age","Horror also reflects the anxieties of the culture that produces it. The Gothic novel emerged in the eighteenth century, and Horace Walpole's The Castle of Otranto (1764) is conventionally regarded as its first example. Mary Shelley conceived Frankenstein in the summer of 1816, when a group of friends, among them Lord Byron, challenged one another to invent ghost stories beside Lake Geneva; the novel, published in 1818, was to give imaginative form to fears about science and responsibility. Later horror translated dread of disease, war and social upheaval into monsters of every kind. We should be cautious about simple one-to-one allegories, but the pattern is persistent: what a society cannot discuss directly, it often learns to whisper as a ghost story.","In the end, the pleasure of horror may lie in a paradoxical form of control. By choosing fear, rehearsing it and surviving it to the final page, we practise facing the unknown on terms that we have set ourselves. The lights come back on, the book closes, and the world looks, for a moment at least, slightly more manageable."],"glossary":{"catharsis":"katarsis, duygusal arınma","uncanny":"tekinsiz; tanıdık olanın ürkütücü biçimde yabancılaşması","unreliable narrator":"güvenilmez anlatıcı","gothic":"Gotik; karanlık, ürpertici ortamlar ve doğaüstü öğelerle ilişkili edebi tür","withered":"kurumuş, büzüşmüş","sergeant-major":"başçavuş","arousal":"uyarılma, fizyolojik heyecan hali"},"phrases":[{"en":"the paradox of horror","tr":"korku paradoksu (hoşlanmadığımız bir duyguyu isteyerek aramamız)"},{"en":"discharge anxieties","tr":"kaygıları boşaltmak, kaygılardan arınmak"},{"en":"heightened attention","tr":"artmış dikkat"},{"en":"in the form of","tr":"... biçiminde, ... şeklinde"},{"en":"precisely because","tr":"tam da ... olduğu için"},{"en":"a gap between","tr":"... arasındaki boşluk, uyumsuzluk"},{"en":"from within","tr":"içeriden"},{"en":"give imaginative form to","tr":"hayal gücüyle biçimlendirmek, ... e hayali bir biçim vermek"},{"en":"supply the details","tr":"ayrıntıları tamamlamak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is 'the paradox of horror' as it is presented in the article?","options":["Horror stories are frightening even when they have happy endings","People deliberately seek out an emotion that is normally unpleasant","The best horror writers are usually comic writers","Fear is stronger in fiction than in real life"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Paradoks, normalde hoş olmayan bir duygu olan korkuyu eğlence olarak isteyerek aramamızdır."},{"q":"Why is \"The Monkey's Paw\" described as horrifying 'precisely because' of the family's ordinary desires?","options":["The paw is described in great physical detail","The family is rich and powerful","The reader feels sympathy for the family, so their fate becomes frightening","The story is set in a remote castle"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Aile sıradan ve anlaşılır arzular taşıdığı için okur onlarla özdeşleşir; korku, ailenin başına gelenlerden doğar."},{"q":"What makes the narrator of \"The Tell-Tale Heart\" unreliable?","options":["He tells the story many years after the events","He changes the order of events on purpose","He forgets important details of the crime","His claim to be sane is contradicted by the behaviour he describes"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Anlatıcı akıl sağlığının yerinde olduğunu ısrarla savunur; ancak anlattığı davranışlar bunun tersini gösterir."},{"q":"According to the article, why do many writers prefer suggestion to display?","options":["Readers' imaginations tend to produce more disturbing images than the author could describe","Detailed descriptions are too expensive to publish","Suggestion is easier for foreign readers to understand","Readers dislike stories with too many characters"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Tehdidin ayrıntıları saklandığında okurun hayal gücü kişisel olarak en rahatsız edici ihtimali kendisi üretir."}],"words":960,"length":"short"},{"id":"the-puzzle-with-rules","type":"article","title":"The Puzzle with Rules: How Detective Fiction Learned to Play Fair","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C1","genre":"mystery","summaryTr":"Dedektif öyküsünün Poe ile doğuşunu, Sherlock Holmes'un yükselişini ve okurla yazar arasındaki 'adil oyun' sözleşmesini inceleyen bir deneme.","paragraphs":["Every detective story contains a small act of aggression against the reader. Somewhere in the first few pages, the author hides the truth, and for the remainder of the book dares us to find it. What makes the dare acceptable, even delicious, is an unwritten agreement: the author may mislead, but must not cheat. The history of mystery fiction is, to a large extent, the history of how that agreement was negotiated.","## An invention of the modern city","Crime stories are ancient, but the detective story, in which the interest lies less in the crime itself than in the reasoning that uncovers it, is a surprisingly recent invention. Most scholars date its emergence to 1841, when Edgar Allan Poe published \"The Murders in the Rue Morgue\" and introduced C. Auguste Dupin, an eccentric Parisian amateur who solves an apparently impossible killing through observation and logic. Poe himself described such narratives as tales of ratiocination, a term that foregrounds the intellectual process rather than the violence. It may be no coincidence that the form appeared when large cities, anonymous crowds and newly organised police forces (London's Metropolitan Police had been founded in 1829) were transforming how people thought about order and risk.","A generation later, Wilkie Collins's novel The Moonstone (1868), built around a stolen Indian diamond, demonstrated that the puzzle could sustain a full-length narrative with several narrators and a professional investigator, Sergeant Cuff. Many critics regard it as the first English detective novel, although such judgements remain open to debate.","## Holmes and the cult of method","It was Arthur Conan Doyle, however, who turned the detective into a popular hero. Sherlock Holmes first appeared in the novel A Study in Scarlet (1887), but his true celebrity began in 1891, when The Strand Magazine started to publish short stories about him. Doyle, a trained physician, is said to have modelled Holmes's analytical manner partly on one of his Edinburgh teachers, Joseph Bell, who was famous for deducing details about patients from minute observations. The short-story format suited the character perfectly: a self-contained puzzle, a dazzling explanation and a satisfying conclusion, all within a single sitting.","\"The Red-Headed League\" (1891) shows the formula at its most elegant. The premise is deliberately absurd: a London pawnbroker is offered well-paid work that requires nothing of him except that he copy out an encyclopaedia, and the offer is open only to men with red hair. The reader is invited to ask what could possibly lie behind so strange an arrangement, and the pleasure of the story consists in discovering that the absurdity conceals a perfectly rational design. Holmes's solution, moreover, rests on small physical and circumstantial details that have been set before us in plain sight, though we did not grasp their significance at the time.","## A contract with the reader","As the genre matured, writers and critics began to ask what obligations the author owed the audience. The so-called Golden Age of the puzzle mystery, roughly the 1920s and 1930s, produced several attempts to codify the rules. In 1928 the American writer S. S. Van Dine, the pen name of Willard Huntington Wright, published a list of twenty rules, and in 1929 the English priest and crime novelist Ronald Knox proposed ten commandments, among them the requirement that no accident should ever help the detective. In 1930 a group of British authors, including Agatha Christie and Dorothy L. Sayers, founded the Detection Club, whose members took a playfully solemn oath to solve their fictional crimes by wit rather than by supernatural help, coincidence or other cheap devices.","The principle behind all these codes was fair play: every clue available to the detective should also be available to the reader. Agatha Christie tested the limits of that principle in The Murder of Roger Ackroyd (1926), whose solution depends on a bold manipulation of the way the story is narrated. Some critics accused her of breaking the contract; others replied that she had honoured its letter while exploiting the reader's unexamined assumptions. The argument has never been settled, which is perhaps the best evidence that the contract genuinely matters.","## Two stories in one","In an essay published in 1966, the structuralist critic Tzvetan Todorov observed that a detective novel always contains two stories: the story of the crime, which is complete but hidden, and the story of the investigation, which is visible and which gradually reconstructs the first. Skilful authors exploit the tension between these two timelines. The first story ends before the book begins; the second ends only on the final page. Much of the reader's pleasure derives from their slow convergence, and from the moment when a detail that seemed irrelevant is suddenly revealed as the hinge of the whole structure.","The red herring, a false clue deliberately planted to lead the reader astray, belongs to the same art of controlled misdirection. It is legitimate precisely because it is built from genuine information that the reader chooses to interpret wrongly. The author lies by arrangement, never by assertion, and that distinction is the moral foundation of the entire genre.","## Why we keep solving","Psychologists would probably say that mysteries satisfy a deep human appetite for closure. An unexplained event produces discomfort; an explanation relieves it. The detective story intensifies this cycle by delaying relief, and by promising that the universe, however disordered it appears in the first chapter, is ultimately intelligible. It is tempting to connect the popularity of the Golden Age, which flourished between two world wars, with a society anxious to believe that disorder could be traced, named and removed.","Not everyone was persuaded by this comforting vision. In his essay \"The Simple Art of Murder\" (1944), Raymond Chandler argued that the polite drawing-room puzzle was artificial, and he championed the harder tradition of Dashiell Hammett, in which violence grows out of corruption in the street rather than from a clever plot. The tension between the cerebral puzzle and the morally ambiguous city continues to animate crime fiction today. What remains constant is the bargain at the heart of the form: the author promises a solution, the reader promises to play, and both agree, in the end, to be surprised."],"glossary":{"ratiocination":"akıl yürütme, mantıksal çıkarım","red herring":"yanıltıcı ipucu, dikkat dağıtan sahte iz","pawnbroker":"rehinci, ödünç karşılığı eşya alan tefeci","deduce":"çıkarım yapmak, akıl yürüterek sonuca varmak","codify":"kurallara bağlamak, sistemleştirmek","closure":"kapanış, belirsizliğin giderilmesi duygusu","misdirection":"yanlış yöne çekme, dikkati başka yere çevirme"},"phrases":[{"en":"to a large extent","tr":"büyük ölçüde"},{"en":"open to debate","tr":"tartışmaya açık, tartışmalı"},{"en":"in plain sight","tr":"gözün önünde, açıkça ortada"},{"en":"lead the reader astray","tr":"okuru yanlış yola sürüklemek"},{"en":"it may be no coincidence that","tr":"belki de tesadüf değildir ki"},{"en":"fair play","tr":"adil oyun, kurallara uygun davranış"},{"en":"grows out of","tr":"-den doğmak, -den kaynaklanmak"},{"en":"the hinge of","tr":"-in dayanak noktası, kilit nokta"},{"en":"derives from","tr":"-den kaynaklanır, -e dayanır"}],"quiz":[{"q":"According to the article, what distinguishes the detective story from earlier crime stories?","options":["It is set in large modern cities","It focuses on the reasoning that uncovers the crime","It always features a professional police officer","It avoids violence entirely"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Makale, dedektif öyküsünde ilginin suçun kendisinden çok onu açığa çıkaran akıl yürütmede olduğunu vurgular."},{"q":"What does the principle of 'fair play' require of the author?","options":["That the detective must be an amateur","That the criminal is caught by accident","That every clue the detective has is also available to the reader","That the narrator never describes the crime"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Adil oyun ilkesi, dedektifin elindeki her ipucunun okurun da elinde olması demektir."},{"q":"In Todorov's view, what are the 'two stories' contained in a detective novel?","options":["The story of the crime and the story of the investigation","The story of the detective and the story of his assistant","The story of the victim and the story of the police","The story of the clues and the story of the reader"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Todorov'a göre biri gizli kalan suç öyküsü, diğeri bu öyküyü adım adım yeniden kuran soruşturma öyküsüdür."},{"q":"Why did Raymond Chandler criticise the Golden Age puzzle?","options":["He believed it contained too much violence","He found it too difficult for ordinary readers","He thought it was dishonest about the identity of the criminal","He considered it artificial compared with a more realistic, harder tradition"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Chandler nazik salon bulmacasını yapay bulmuş ve sokaktaki yozlaşmayı anlatan, daha sert ve gerçekçi Hammett geleneğini savunmuştur."}],"words":999,"length":"short"},{"id":"the-red-headed-league","type":"story","title":"The Red-Headed League","author":"Arthur Conan Doyle","year":1891,"level":"C1","genre":"mystery","source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #1661","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1661","license":"Public domain"},"summaryTr":"Kızıl saçlı bir rehinci, gazetede gördüğü ilanla haftada dört sterlin kazandıran tuhaf bir işe girer; sonra o iş bir sabah esrarengiz biçimde sona erer. Sherlock Holmes bu garip dernek hikâyesinin ardındaki gerçeği ortaya çıkarmaya girişir.","paragraphs":["I had called upon my friend, Mr. Sherlock Holmes, one day in the autumn of last year and found him in deep conversation with a very stout, florid-faced, elderly gentleman with fiery red hair. With an apology for my intrusion, I was about to withdraw when Holmes pulled me abruptly into the room and closed the door behind me.","“You could not possibly have come at a better time, my dear Watson,” he said cordially.","“I was afraid that you were engaged.”","“So I am. Very much so.”","“Then I can wait in the next room.”","“Not at all. This gentleman, Mr. Wilson, has been my partner and helper in many of my most successful cases, and I have no doubt that he will be of the utmost use to me in yours also.”","The stout gentleman half rose from his chair and gave a bob of greeting, with a quick little questioning glance from his small fat-encircled eyes.","“Try the settee,” said Holmes, relapsing into his armchair and putting his fingertips together, as was his custom when in judicial moods. “I know, my dear Watson, that you share my love of all that is bizarre and outside the conventions and humdrum routine of everyday life. You have shown your relish for it by the enthusiasm which has prompted you to chronicle, and, if you will excuse my saying so, somewhat to embellish so many of my own little adventures.”","“Your cases have indeed been of the greatest interest to me,” I observed.","“You will remember that I remarked the other day, just before we went into the very simple problem presented by Miss Mary Sutherland, that for strange effects and extraordinary combinations we must go to life itself, which is always far more daring than any effort of the imagination.”","“A proposition which I took the liberty of doubting.”","“You did, Doctor, but none the less you must come round to my view, for otherwise I shall keep on piling fact upon fact on you until your reason breaks down under them and acknowledges me to be right. Now, Mr. Jabez Wilson here has been good enough to call upon me this morning, and to begin a narrative which promises to be one of the most singular which I have listened to for some time. You have heard me remark that the strangest and most unique things are very often connected not with the larger but with the smaller crimes, and occasionally, indeed, where there is room for doubt whether any positive crime has been committed. As far as I have heard, it is impossible for me to say whether the present case is an instance of crime or not, but the course of events is certainly among the most singular that I have ever listened to. Perhaps, Mr. Wilson, you would have the great kindness to recommence your narrative. I ask you not merely because my friend Dr. Watson has not heard the opening part but also because the peculiar nature of the story makes me anxious to have every possible detail from your lips. As a rule, when I have heard some slight indication of the course of events, I am able to guide myself by the thousands of other similar cases which occur to my memory. In the present instance I am forced to admit that the facts are, to the best of my belief, unique.”","The portly client puffed out his chest with an appearance of some little pride and pulled a dirty and wrinkled newspaper from the inside pocket of his greatcoat. As he glanced down the advertisement column, with his head thrust forward and the paper flattened out upon his knee, I took a good look at the man and endeavoured, after the fashion of my companion, to read the indications which might be presented by his dress or appearance.","I did not gain very much, however, by my inspection. Our visitor bore every mark of being an average commonplace British tradesman, obese, pompous, and slow. He wore rather baggy grey shepherd’s check trousers, a not over-clean black frock-coat, unbuttoned in the front, and a drab waistcoat with a heavy brassy Albert chain, and a square pierced bit of metal dangling down as an ornament. A frayed top-hat and a faded brown overcoat with a wrinkled velvet collar lay upon a chair beside him. Altogether, look as I would, there was nothing remarkable about the man save his blazing red head, and the expression of extreme chagrin and discontent upon his features.","Sherlock Holmes’ quick eye took in my occupation, and he shook his head with a smile as he noticed my questioning glances. “Beyond the obvious facts that he has at some time done manual labour, that he takes snuff, that he is a Freemason, that he has been in China, and that he has done a considerable amount of writing lately, I can deduce nothing else.”","Mr. Jabez Wilson started up in his chair, with his forefinger upon the paper, but his eyes upon my companion.","“How, in the name of good-fortune, did you know all that, Mr. Holmes?” he asked. “How did you know, for example, that I did manual labour. It’s as true as gospel, for I began as a ship’s carpenter.”","“Your hands, my dear sir. Your right hand is quite a size larger than your left. You have worked with it, and the muscles are more developed.”","“Well, the snuff, then, and the Freemasonry?”","“I won’t insult your intelligence by telling you how I read that, especially as, rather against the strict rules of your order, you use an arc-and-compass breastpin.”","“Ah, of course, I forgot that. But the writing?”","“What else can be indicated by that right cuff so very shiny for five inches, and the left one with the smooth patch near the elbow where you rest it upon the desk?”","“Well, but China?”","“The fish that you have tattooed immediately above your right wrist could only have been done in China. I have made a small study of tattoo marks and have even contributed to the literature of the subject. That trick of staining the fishes’ scales of a delicate pink is quite peculiar to China. When, in addition, I see a Chinese coin hanging from your watch-chain, the matter becomes even more simple.”","Mr. Jabez Wilson laughed heavily. “Well, I never!” said he. “I thought at first that you had done something clever, but I see that there was nothing in it after all.”","“I begin to think, Watson,” said Holmes, “that I make a mistake in explaining. ‘Omne ignotum pro magnifico,’ you know, and my poor little reputation, such as it is, will suffer shipwreck if I am so candid. Can you not find the advertisement, Mr. Wilson?”","“Yes, I have got it now,” he answered with his thick red finger planted halfway down the column. “Here it is. This is what began it all. You just read it for yourself, sir.”","I took the paper from him and read as follows:","“TO THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE: On account of the bequest of the late Ezekiah Hopkins, of Lebanon, Pennsylvania, U.S.A., there is now another vacancy open which entitles a member of the League to a salary of £4 a week for purely nominal services. All red-headed men who are sound in body and mind and above the age of twenty-one years, are eligible. Apply in person on Monday, at eleven o’clock, to Duncan Ross, at the offices of the League, 7 Pope’s Court, Fleet Street.”","“What on earth does this mean?” I ejaculated after I had twice read over the extraordinary announcement.","Holmes chuckled and wriggled in his chair, as was his habit when in high spirits. “It is a little off the beaten track, isn’t it?” said he. “And now, Mr. Wilson, off you go at scratch and tell us all about yourself, your household, and the effect which this advertisement had upon your fortunes. You will first make a note, Doctor, of the paper and the date.”","“It is The Morning Chronicle of April 27, 1890. Just two months ago.”","“Very good. Now, Mr. Wilson?”","“Well, it is just as I have been telling you, Mr. Sherlock Holmes,” said Jabez Wilson, mopping his forehead; “I have a small pawnbroker’s business at Coburg Square, near the City. It’s not a very large affair, and of late years it has not done more than just give me a living. I used to be able to keep two assistants, but now I only keep one; and I would have a job to pay him but that he is willing to come for half wages so as to learn the business.”","“What is the name of this obliging youth?” asked Sherlock Holmes.","“His name is Vincent Spaulding, and he’s not such a youth, either. It’s hard to say his age. I should not wish a smarter assistant, Mr. Holmes; and I know very well that he could better himself and earn twice what I am able to give him. But, after all, if he is satisfied, why should I put ideas in his head?”","“Why, indeed? You seem most fortunate in having an employé who comes under the full market price. It is not a common experience among employers in this age. I don’t know that your assistant is not as remarkable as your advertisement.”","“Oh, he has his faults, too,” said Mr. Wilson. “Never was such a fellow for photography. Snapping away with a camera when he ought to be improving his mind, and then diving down into the cellar like a rabbit into its hole to develop his pictures. That is his main fault, but on the whole he’s a good worker. There’s no vice in him.”","“He is still with you, I presume?”","“Yes, sir. He and a girl of fourteen, who does a bit of simple cooking and keeps the place clean—that’s all I have in the house, for I am a widower and never had any family. We live very quietly, sir, the three of us; and we keep a roof over our heads and pay our debts, if we do nothing more.","“The first thing that put us out was that advertisement. Spaulding, he came down into the office just this day eight weeks, with this very paper in his hand, and he says:","“‘I wish to the Lord, Mr. Wilson, that I was a red-headed man.’","“‘Why that?’ I asks.","“‘Why,’ says he, ‘here’s another vacancy on the League of the Red-headed Men. It’s worth quite a little fortune to any man who gets it, and I understand that there are more vacancies than there are men, so that the trustees are at their wits’ end what to do with the money. If my hair would only change colour, here’s a nice little crib all ready for me to step into.’","“‘Why, what is it, then?’ I asked. You see, Mr. Holmes, I am a very stay-at-home man, and as my business came to me instead of my having to go to it, I was often weeks on end without putting my foot over the door-mat. In that way I didn’t know much of what was going on outside, and I was always glad of a bit of news.","“‘Have you never heard of the League of the Red-headed Men?’ he asked with his eyes open.","“‘Never.’","“‘Why, I wonder at that, for you are eligible yourself for one of the vacancies.’","“‘And what are they worth?’ I asked.","“‘Oh, merely a couple of hundred a year, but the work is slight, and it need not interfere very much with one’s other occupations.’","“Well, you can easily think that that made me prick up my ears, for the business has not been over good for some years, and an extra couple of hundred would have been very handy.","“‘Tell me all about it,’ said I.","“‘Well,’ said he, showing me the advertisement, ‘you can see for yourself that the League has a vacancy, and there is the address where you should apply for particulars. As far as I can make out, the League was founded by an American millionaire, Ezekiah Hopkins, who was very peculiar in his ways. He was himself red-headed, and he had a great sympathy for all red-headed men; so, when he died, it was found that he had left his enormous fortune in the hands of trustees, with instructions to apply the interest to the providing of easy berths to men whose hair is of that colour. From all I hear it is splendid pay and very little to do.’","“‘But,’ said I, ‘there would be millions of red-headed men who would apply.’","“‘Not so many as you might think,’ he answered. ‘You see it is really confined to Londoners, and to grown men. This American had started from London when he was young, and he wanted to do the old town a good turn. Then, again, I have heard it is no use your applying if your hair is light red, or dark red, or anything but real bright, blazing, fiery red. Now, if you cared to apply, Mr. Wilson, you would just walk in; but perhaps it would hardly be worth your while to put yourself out of the way for the sake of a few hundred pounds.’","“Now, it is a fact, gentlemen, as you may see for yourselves, that my hair is of a very full and rich tint, so that it seemed to me that if there was to be any competition in the matter I stood as good a chance as any man that I had ever met. Vincent Spaulding seemed to know so much about it that I thought he might prove useful, so I just ordered him to put up the shutters for the day and to come right away with me. He was very willing to have a holiday, so we shut the business up and started off for the address that was given us in the advertisement.","“I never hope to see such a sight as that again, Mr. Holmes. From north, south, east, and west every man who had a shade of red in his hair had tramped into the city to answer the advertisement. Fleet Street was choked with red-headed folk, and Pope’s Court looked like a coster’s orange barrow. I should not have thought there were so many in the whole country as were brought together by that single advertisement. Every shade of colour they were—straw, lemon, orange, brick, Irish-setter, liver, clay; but, as Spaulding said, there were not many who had the real vivid flame-coloured tint. When I saw how many were waiting, I would have given it up in despair; but Spaulding would not hear of it. How he did it I could not imagine, but he pushed and pulled and butted until he got me through the crowd, and right up to the steps which led to the office. There was a double stream upon the stair, some going up in hope, and some coming back dejected; but we wedged in as well as we could and soon found ourselves in the office.”","“Your experience has been a most entertaining one,” remarked Holmes as his client paused and refreshed his memory with a huge pinch of snuff. “Pray continue your very interesting statement.”","“There was nothing in the office but a couple of wooden chairs and a deal table, behind which sat a small man with a head that was even redder than mine. He said a few words to each candidate as he came up, and then he always managed to find some fault in them which would disqualify them. Getting a vacancy did not seem to be such a very easy matter, after all. However, when our turn came the little man was much more favourable to me than to any of the others, and he closed the door as we entered, so that he might have a private word with us.","“‘This is Mr. Jabez Wilson,’ said my assistant, ‘and he is willing to fill a vacancy in the League.’","“‘And he is admirably suited for it,’ the other answered. ‘He has every requirement. I cannot recall when I have seen anything so fine.’ He took a step backward, cocked his head on one side, and gazed at my hair until I felt quite bashful. Then suddenly he plunged forward, wrung my hand, and congratulated me warmly on my success.","“‘It would be injustice to hesitate,’ said he. ‘You will, however, I am sure, excuse me for taking an obvious precaution.’ With that he seized my hair in both his hands, and tugged until I yelled with the pain. ‘There is water in your eyes,’ said he as he released me. ‘I perceive that all is as it should be. But we have to be careful, for we have twice been deceived by wigs and once by paint. I could tell you tales of cobbler’s wax which would disgust you with human nature.’ He stepped over to the window and shouted through it at the top of his voice that the vacancy was filled. A groan of disappointment came up from below, and the folk all trooped away in different directions until there was not a red-head to be seen except my own and that of the manager.","“‘My name,’ said he, ‘is Mr. Duncan Ross, and I am myself one of the pensioners upon the fund left by our noble benefactor. Are you a married man, Mr. Wilson? Have you a family?’","“I answered that I had not.","“His face fell immediately.","“‘Dear me!’ he said gravely, ‘that is very serious indeed! I am sorry to hear you say that. The fund was, of course, for the propagation and spread of the red-heads as well as for their maintenance. It is exceedingly unfortunate that you should be a bachelor.’","“My face lengthened at this, Mr. Holmes, for I thought that I was not to have the vacancy after all; but after thinking it over for a few minutes he said that it would be all right.","“‘In the case of another,’ said he, ‘the objection might be fatal, but we must stretch a point in favour of a man with such a head of hair as yours. When shall you be able to enter upon your new duties?’","“‘Well, it is a little awkward, for I have a business already,’ said I.","“‘Oh, never mind about that, Mr. Wilson!’ said Vincent Spaulding. ‘I should be able to look after that for you.’","“‘What would be the hours?’ I asked.","“‘Ten to two.’","“Now a pawnbroker’s business is mostly done of an evening, Mr. Holmes, especially Thursday and Friday evening, which is just before pay-day; so it would suit me very well to earn a little in the mornings. Besides, I knew that my assistant was a good man, and that he would see to anything that turned up.","“‘That would suit me very well,’ said I. ‘And the pay?’","“‘Is £4 a week.’","“‘And the work?’","“‘Is purely nominal.’","“‘What do you call purely nominal?’","“‘Well, you have to be in the office, or at least in the building, the whole time. If you leave, you forfeit your whole position forever. The will is very clear upon that point. You don’t comply with the conditions if you budge from the office during that time.’","“‘It’s only four hours a day, and I should not think of leaving,’ said I.","“‘No excuse will avail,’ said Mr. Duncan Ross; ‘neither sickness nor business nor anything else. There you must stay, or you lose your billet.’","“‘And the work?’","“‘Is to copy out the Encyclopædia Britannica. There is the first volume of it in that press. You must find your own ink, pens, and blotting-paper, but we provide this table and chair. Will you be ready to-morrow?’","“‘Certainly,’ I answered.","“‘Then, good-bye, Mr. Jabez Wilson, and let me congratulate you once more on the important position which you have been fortunate enough to gain.’ He bowed me out of the room and I went home with my assistant, hardly knowing what to say or do, I was so pleased at my own good fortune.","“Well, I thought over the matter all day, and by evening I was in low spirits again; for I had quite persuaded myself that the whole affair must be some great hoax or fraud, though what its object might be I could not imagine. It seemed altogether past belief that anyone could make such a will, or that they would pay such a sum for doing anything so simple as copying out the Encyclopædia Britannica. Vincent Spaulding did what he could to cheer me up, but by bedtime I had reasoned myself out of the whole thing. However, in the morning I determined to have a look at it anyhow, so I bought a penny bottle of ink, and with a quill-pen, and seven sheets of foolscap paper, I started off for Pope’s Court.","“Well, to my surprise and delight, everything was as right as possible. The table was set out ready for me, and Mr. Duncan Ross was there to see that I got fairly to work. He started me off upon the letter A, and then he left me; but he would drop in from time to time to see that all was right with me. At two o’clock he bade me good-day, complimented me upon the amount that I had written, and locked the door of the office after me.","“This went on day after day, Mr. Holmes, and on Saturday the manager came in and planked down four golden sovereigns for my week’s work. It was the same next week, and the same the week after. Every morning I was there at ten, and every afternoon I left at two. By degrees Mr. Duncan Ross took to coming in only once of a morning, and then, after a time, he did not come in at all. Still, of course, I never dared to leave the room for an instant, for I was not sure when he might come, and the billet was such a good one, and suited me so well, that I would not risk the loss of it.","“Eight weeks passed away like this, and I had written about Abbots and Archery and Armour and Architecture and Attica, and hoped with diligence that I might get on to the B’s before very long. It cost me something in foolscap, and I had pretty nearly filled a shelf with my writings. And then suddenly the whole business came to an end.”","“To an end?”","“Yes, sir. And no later than this morning. I went to my work as usual at ten o’clock, but the door was shut and locked, with a little square of cardboard hammered on to the middle of the panel with a tack. Here it is, and you can read for yourself.”","He held up a piece of white cardboard about the size of a sheet of note-paper. It read in this fashion:","“THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE IS DISSOLVED. October 9, 1890.”","Sherlock Holmes and I surveyed this curt announcement and the rueful face behind it, until the comical side of the affair so completely overtopped every other consideration that we both burst out into a roar of laughter.","“I cannot see that there is anything very funny,” cried our client, flushing up to the roots of his flaming head. “If you can do nothing better than laugh at me, I can go elsewhere.”","“No, no,” cried Holmes, shoving him back into the chair from which he had half risen. “I really wouldn’t miss your case for the world. It is most refreshingly unusual. But there is, if you will excuse my saying so, something just a little funny about it. Pray what steps did you take when you found the card upon the door?”","“I was staggered, sir. I did not know what to do. Then I called at the offices round, but none of them seemed to know anything about it. Finally, I went to the landlord, who is an accountant living on the ground floor, and I asked him if he could tell me what had become of the Red-headed League. He said that he had never heard of any such body. Then I asked him who Mr. Duncan Ross was. He answered that the name was new to him.","“‘Well,’ said I, ‘the gentleman at No. 4.’","“‘What, the red-headed man?’","“‘Yes.’","“‘Oh,’ said he, ‘his name was William Morris. He was a solicitor and was using my room as a temporary convenience until his new premises were ready. He moved out yesterday.’","“‘Where could I find him?’","“‘Oh, at his new offices. He did tell me the address. Yes, 17 King Edward Street, near St. Paul’s.’","“I started off, Mr. Holmes, but when I got to that address it was a manufactory of artificial knee-caps, and no one in it had ever heard of either Mr. William Morris or Mr. Duncan Ross.”","“And what did you do then?” asked Holmes.","“I went home to Saxe-Coburg Square, and I took the advice of my assistant. But he could not help me in any way. He could only say that if I waited I should hear by post. But that was not quite good enough, Mr. Holmes. I did not wish to lose such a place without a struggle, so, as I had heard that you were good enough to give advice to poor folk who were in need of it, I came right away to you.”","“And you did very wisely,” said Holmes. “Your case is an exceedingly remarkable one, and I shall be happy to look into it. From what you have told me I think that it is possible that graver issues hang from it than might at first sight appear.”","“Grave enough!” said Mr. Jabez Wilson. “Why, I have lost four pound a week.”","“As far as you are personally concerned,” remarked Holmes, “I do not see that you have any grievance against this extraordinary league. On the contrary, you are, as I understand, richer by some £30, to say nothing of the minute knowledge which you have gained on every subject which comes under the letter A. You have lost nothing by them.”","“No, sir. But I want to find out about them, and who they are, and what their object was in playing this prank—if it was a prank—upon me. It was a pretty expensive joke for them, for it cost them two and thirty pounds.”","“We shall endeavour to clear up these points for you. And, first, one or two questions, Mr. Wilson. This assistant of yours who first called your attention to the advertisement—how long had he been with you?”","“About a month then.”","“How did he come?”","“In answer to an advertisement.”","“Was he the only applicant?”","“No, I had a dozen.”","“Why did you pick him?”","“Because he was handy and would come cheap.”","“At half wages, in fact.”","“Yes.”","“What is he like, this Vincent Spaulding?”","“Small, stout-built, very quick in his ways, no hair on his face, though he’s not short of thirty. Has a white splash of acid upon his forehead.”","Holmes sat up in his chair in considerable excitement. “I thought as much,” said he. “Have you ever observed that his ears are pierced for earrings?”","“Yes, sir. He told me that a gipsy had done it for him when he was a lad.”","“Hum!” said Holmes, sinking back in deep thought. “He is still with you?”","“Oh, yes, sir; I have only just left him.”","“And has your business been attended to in your absence?”","“Nothing to complain of, sir. There’s never very much to do of a morning.”","“That will do, Mr. Wilson. I shall be happy to give you an opinion upon the subject in the course of a day or two. To-day is Saturday, and I hope that by Monday we may come to a conclusion.”","“Well, Watson,” said Holmes when our visitor had left us, “what do you make of it all?”","“I make nothing of it,” I answered frankly. “It is a most mysterious business.”","“As a rule,” said Holmes, “the more bizarre a thing is the less mysterious it proves to be. It is your commonplace, featureless crimes which are really puzzling, just as a commonplace face is the most difficult to identify. But I must be prompt over this matter.”","“What are you going to do, then?” I asked.","“To smoke,” he answered. “It is quite a three pipe problem, and I beg that you won’t speak to me for fifty minutes.” He curled himself up in his chair, with his thin knees drawn up to his hawk-like nose, and there he sat with his eyes closed and his black clay pipe thrusting out like the bill of some strange bird. I had come to the conclusion that he had dropped asleep, and indeed was nodding myself, when he suddenly sprang out of his chair with the gesture of a man who has made up his mind and put his pipe down upon the mantelpiece.","“Sarasate plays at the St. James’s Hall this afternoon,” he remarked. “What do you think, Watson? Could your patients spare you for a few hours?”","“I have nothing to do to-day. My practice is never very absorbing.”","“Then put on your hat and come. I am going through the City first, and we can have some lunch on the way. I observe that there is a good deal of German music on the programme, which is rather more to my taste than Italian or French. It is introspective, and I want to introspect. Come along!”","We travelled by the Underground as far as Aldersgate; and a short walk took us to Saxe-Coburg Square, the scene of the singular story which we had listened to in the morning. It was a poky, little, shabby-genteel place, where four lines of dingy two-storied brick houses looked out into a small railed-in enclosure, where a lawn of weedy grass and a few clumps of faded laurel bushes made a hard fight against a smoke-laden and uncongenial atmosphere. Three gilt balls and a brown board with “JABEZ WILSON” in white letters, upon a corner house, announced the place where our red-headed client carried on his business. Sherlock Holmes stopped in front of it with his head on one side and looked it all over, with his eyes shining brightly between puckered lids. Then he walked slowly up the street, and then down again to the corner, still looking keenly at the houses. Finally he returned to the pawnbroker’s, and, having thumped vigorously upon the pavement with his stick two or three times, he went up to the door and knocked. It was instantly opened by a bright-looking, clean-shaven young fellow, who asked him to step in.","“Thank you,” said Holmes, “I only wished to ask you how you would go from here to the Strand.”","“Third right, fourth left,” answered the assistant promptly, closing the door.","“Smart fellow, that,” observed Holmes as we walked away. “He is, in my judgment, the fourth smartest man in London, and for daring I am not sure that he has not a claim to be third. I have known something of him before.”","“Evidently,” said I, “Mr. Wilson’s assistant counts for a good deal in this mystery of the Red-headed League. I am sure that you inquired your way merely in order that you might see him.”","“Not him.”","“What then?”","“The knees of his trousers.”","“And what did you see?”","“What I expected to see.”","“Why did you beat the pavement?”","“My dear doctor, this is a time for observation, not for talk. We are spies in an enemy’s country. We know something of Saxe-Coburg Square. Let us now explore the parts which lie behind it.”","The road in which we found ourselves as we turned round the corner from the retired Saxe-Coburg Square presented as great a contrast to it as the front of a picture does to the back. It was one of the main arteries which conveyed the traffic of the City to the north and west. The roadway was blocked with the immense stream of commerce flowing in a double tide inward and outward, while the footpaths were black with the hurrying swarm of pedestrians. It was difficult to realise as we looked at the line of fine shops and stately business premises that they really abutted on the other side upon the faded and stagnant square which we had just quitted.","“Let me see,” said Holmes, standing at the corner and glancing along the line, “I should like just to remember the order of the houses here. It is a hobby of mine to have an exact knowledge of London. There is Mortimer’s, the tobacconist, the little newspaper shop, the Coburg branch of the City and Suburban Bank, the Vegetarian Restaurant, and McFarlane’s carriage-building depot. That carries us right on to the other block. And now, Doctor, we’ve done our work, so it’s time we had some play. A sandwich and a cup of coffee, and then off to violin-land, where all is sweetness and delicacy and harmony, and there are no red-headed clients to vex us with their conundrums.”","My friend was an enthusiastic musician, being himself not only a very capable performer but a composer of no ordinary merit. All the afternoon he sat in the stalls wrapped in the most perfect happiness, gently waving his long, thin fingers in time to the music, while his gently smiling face and his languid, dreamy eyes were as unlike those of Holmes the sleuth-hound, Holmes the relentless, keen-witted, ready-handed criminal agent, as it was possible to conceive. In his singular character the dual nature alternately asserted itself, and his extreme exactness and astuteness represented, as I have often thought, the reaction against the poetic and contemplative mood which occasionally predominated in him. The swing of his nature took him from extreme languor to devouring energy; and, as I knew well, he was never so truly formidable as when, for days on end, he had been lounging in his armchair amid his improvisations and his black-letter editions. Then it was that the lust of the chase would suddenly come upon him, and that his brilliant reasoning power would rise to the level of intuition, until those who were unacquainted with his methods would look askance at him as on a man whose knowledge was not that of other mortals. When I saw him that afternoon so enwrapped in the music at St. James’s Hall I felt that an evil time might be coming upon those whom he had set himself to hunt down.","“You want to go home, no doubt, Doctor,” he remarked as we emerged.","“Yes, it would be as well.”","“And I have some business to do which will take some hours. This business at Coburg Square is serious.”","“Why serious?”","“A considerable crime is in contemplation. I have every reason to believe that we shall be in time to stop it. But to-day being Saturday rather complicates matters. I shall want your help to-night.”","“At what time?”","“Ten will be early enough.”","“I shall be at Baker Street at ten.”","“Very well. And, I say, Doctor, there may be some little danger, so kindly put your army revolver in your pocket.” He waved his hand, turned on his heel, and disappeared in an instant among the crowd.","I trust that I am not more dense than my neighbours, but I was always oppressed with a sense of my own stupidity in my dealings with Sherlock Holmes. Here I had heard what he had heard, I had seen what he had seen, and yet from his words it was evident that he saw clearly not only what had happened but what was about to happen, while to me the whole business was still confused and grotesque. As I drove home to my house in Kensington I thought over it all, from the extraordinary story of the red-headed copier of the Encyclopædia down to the visit to Saxe-Coburg Square, and the ominous words with which he had parted from me. What was this nocturnal expedition, and why should I go armed? Where were we going, and what were we to do? I had the hint from Holmes that this smooth-faced pawnbroker’s assistant was a formidable man—a man who might play a deep game. I tried to puzzle it out, but gave it up in despair and set the matter aside until night should bring an explanation.","It was a quarter-past nine when I started from home and made my way across the Park, and so through Oxford Street to Baker Street. Two hansoms were standing at the door, and as I entered the passage I heard the sound of voices from above. On entering his room, I found Holmes in animated conversation with two men, one of whom I recognised as Peter Jones, the official police agent, while the other was a long, thin, sad-faced man, with a very shiny hat and oppressively respectable frock-coat.","“Ha! Our party is complete,” said Holmes, buttoning up his pea-jacket and taking his heavy hunting crop from the rack. “Watson, I think you know Mr. Jones, of Scotland Yard? Let me introduce you to Mr. Merryweather, who is to be our companion in to-night’s adventure.”","“We’re hunting in couples again, Doctor, you see,” said Jones in his consequential way. “Our friend here is a wonderful man for starting a chase. All he wants is an old dog to help him to do the running down.”","“I hope a wild goose may not prove to be the end of our chase,” observed Mr. Merryweather gloomily.","“You may place considerable confidence in Mr. Holmes, sir,” said the police agent loftily. “He has his own little methods, which are, if he won’t mind my saying so, just a little too theoretical and fantastic, but he has the makings of a detective in him. It is not too much to say that once or twice, as in that business of the Sholto murder and the Agra treasure, he has been more nearly correct than the official force.”","“Oh, if you say so, Mr. Jones, it is all right,” said the stranger with deference. “Still, I confess that I miss my rubber. It is the first Saturday night for seven-and-twenty years that I have not had my rubber.”","“I think you will find,” said Sherlock Holmes, “that you will play for a higher stake to-night than you have ever done yet, and that the play will be more exciting. For you, Mr. Merryweather, the stake will be some £30,000; and for you, Jones, it will be the man upon whom you wish to lay your hands.”","“John Clay, the murderer, thief, smasher, and forger. He’s a young man, Mr. Merryweather, but he is at the head of his profession, and I would rather have my bracelets on him than on any criminal in London. He’s a remarkable man, is young John Clay. His grandfather was a royal duke, and he himself has been to Eton and Oxford. His brain is as cunning as his fingers, and though we meet signs of him at every turn, we never know where to find the man himself. He’ll crack a crib in Scotland one week, and be raising money to build an orphanage in Cornwall the next. I’ve been on his track for years and have never set eyes on him yet.”","“I hope that I may have the pleasure of introducing you to-night. I’ve had one or two little turns also with Mr. John Clay, and I agree with you that he is at the head of his profession. It is past ten, however, and quite time that we started. If you two will take the first hansom, Watson and I will follow in the second.”","Sherlock Holmes was not very communicative during the long drive and lay back in the cab humming the tunes which he had heard in the afternoon. We rattled through an endless labyrinth of gas-lit streets until we emerged into Farrington Street.","“We are close there now,” my friend remarked. “This fellow Merryweather is a bank director, and personally interested in the matter. I thought it as well to have Jones with us also. He is not a bad fellow, though an absolute imbecile in his profession. He has one positive virtue. He is as brave as a bulldog and as tenacious as a lobster if he gets his claws upon anyone. Here we are, and they are waiting for us.”","We had reached the same crowded thoroughfare in which we had found ourselves in the morning. Our cabs were dismissed, and, following the guidance of Mr. Merryweather, we passed down a narrow passage and through a side door, which he opened for us. Within there was a small corridor, which ended in a very massive iron gate. This also was opened, and led down a flight of winding stone steps, which terminated at another formidable gate. Mr. Merryweather stopped to light a lantern, and then conducted us down a dark, earth-smelling passage, and so, after opening a third door, into a huge vault or cellar, which was piled all round with crates and massive boxes.","“You are not very vulnerable from above,” Holmes remarked as he held up the lantern and gazed about him.","“Nor from below,” said Mr. Merryweather, striking his stick upon the flags which lined the floor. “Why, dear me, it sounds quite hollow!” he remarked, looking up in surprise.","“I must really ask you to be a little more quiet!” said Holmes severely. “You have already imperilled the whole success of our expedition. Might I beg that you would have the goodness to sit down upon one of those boxes, and not to interfere?”","The solemn Mr. Merryweather perched himself upon a crate, with a very injured expression upon his face, while Holmes fell upon his knees upon the floor and, with the lantern and a magnifying lens, began to examine minutely the cracks between the stones. A few seconds sufficed to satisfy him, for he sprang to his feet again and put his glass in his pocket.","“We have at least an hour before us,” he remarked, “for they can hardly take any steps until the good pawnbroker is safely in bed. Then they will not lose a minute, for the sooner they do their work the longer time they will have for their escape. We are at present, Doctor—as no doubt you have divined—in the cellar of the City branch of one of the principal London banks. Mr. Merryweather is the chairman of directors, and he will explain to you that there are reasons why the more daring criminals of London should take a considerable interest in this cellar at present.”","“It is our French gold,” whispered the director. “We have had several warnings that an attempt might be made upon it.”","“Your French gold?”","“Yes. We had occasion some months ago to strengthen our resources and borrowed for that purpose 30,000 napoleons from the Bank of France. It has become known that we have never had occasion to unpack the money, and that it is still lying in our cellar. The crate upon which I sit contains 2,000 napoleons packed between layers of lead foil. Our reserve of bullion is much larger at present than is usually kept in a single branch office, and the directors have had misgivings upon the subject.”","“Which were very well justified,” observed Holmes. “And now it is time that we arranged our little plans. I expect that within an hour matters will come to a head. In the meantime Mr. Merryweather, we must put the screen over that dark lantern.”","“And sit in the dark?”","“I am afraid so. I had brought a pack of cards in my pocket, and I thought that, as we were a partie carrée, you might have your rubber after all. But I see that the enemy’s preparations have gone so far that we cannot risk the presence of a light. And, first of all, we must choose our positions. These are daring men, and though we shall take them at a disadvantage, they may do us some harm unless we are careful. I shall stand behind this crate, and do you conceal yourselves behind those. Then, when I flash a light upon them, close in swiftly. If they fire, Watson, have no compunction about shooting them down.”","I placed my revolver, cocked, upon the top of the wooden case behind which I crouched. Holmes shot the slide across the front of his lantern and left us in pitch darkness—such an absolute darkness as I have never before experienced. The smell of hot metal remained to assure us that the light was still there, ready to flash out at a moment’s notice. To me, with my nerves worked up to a pitch of expectancy, there was something depressing and subduing in the sudden gloom, and in the cold dank air of the vault.","“They have but one retreat,” whispered Holmes. “That is back through the house into Saxe-Coburg Square. I hope that you have done what I asked you, Jones?”","“I have an inspector and two officers waiting at the front door.”","“Then we have stopped all the holes. And now we must be silent and wait.”","What a time it seemed! From comparing notes afterwards it was but an hour and a quarter, yet it appeared to me that the night must have almost gone, and the dawn be breaking above us. My limbs were weary and stiff, for I feared to change my position; yet my nerves were worked up to the highest pitch of tension, and my hearing was so acute that I could not only hear the gentle breathing of my companions, but I could distinguish the deeper, heavier in-breath of the bulky Jones from the thin, sighing note of the bank director. From my position I could look over the case in the direction of the floor. Suddenly my eyes caught the glint of a light.","At first it was but a lurid spark upon the stone pavement. Then it lengthened out until it became a yellow line, and then, without any warning or sound, a gash seemed to open and a hand appeared, a white, almost womanly hand, which felt about in the centre of the little area of light. For a minute or more the hand, with its writhing fingers, protruded out of the floor. Then it was withdrawn as suddenly as it appeared, and all was dark again save the single lurid spark which marked a chink between the stones.","Its disappearance, however, was but momentary. With a rending, tearing sound, one of the broad, white stones turned over upon its side and left a square, gaping hole, through which streamed the light of a lantern. Over the edge there peeped a clean-cut, boyish face, which looked keenly about it, and then, with a hand on either side of the aperture, drew itself shoulder-high and waist-high, until one knee rested upon the edge. In another instant he stood at the side of the hole and was hauling after him a companion, lithe and small like himself, with a pale face and a shock of very red hair.","“It’s all clear,” he whispered. “Have you the chisel and the bags? Great Scott! Jump, Archie, jump, and I’ll swing for it!”","Sherlock Holmes had sprung out and seized the intruder by the collar. The other dived down the hole, and I heard the sound of rending cloth as Jones clutched at his skirts. The light flashed upon the barrel of a revolver, but Holmes’ hunting crop came down on the man’s wrist, and the pistol clinked upon the stone floor.","“It’s no use, John Clay,” said Holmes blandly. “You have no chance at all.”","“So I see,” the other answered with the utmost coolness. “I fancy that my pal is all right, though I see you have got his coat-tails.”","“There are three men waiting for him at the door,” said Holmes.","“Oh, indeed! You seem to have done the thing very completely. I must compliment you.”","“And I you,” Holmes answered. “Your red-headed idea was very new and effective.”","“You’ll see your pal again presently,” said Jones. “He’s quicker at climbing down holes than I am. Just hold out while I fix the derbies.”","“I beg that you will not touch me with your filthy hands,” remarked our prisoner as the handcuffs clattered upon his wrists. “You may not be aware that I have royal blood in my veins. Have the goodness, also, when you address me always to say ‘sir’ and ‘please.’”","“All right,” said Jones with a stare and a snigger. “Well, would you please, sir, march upstairs, where we can get a cab to carry your Highness to the police-station?”","“That is better,” said John Clay serenely. He made a sweeping bow to the three of us and walked quietly off in the custody of the detective.","“Really, Mr. Holmes,” said Mr. Merryweather as we followed them from the cellar, “I do not know how the bank can thank you or repay you. There is no doubt that you have detected and defeated in the most complete manner one of the most determined attempts at bank robbery that have ever come within my experience.”","“I have had one or two little scores of my own to settle with Mr. John Clay,” said Holmes. “I have been at some small expense over this matter, which I shall expect the bank to refund, but beyond that I am amply repaid by having had an experience which is in many ways unique, and by hearing the very remarkable narrative of the Red-headed League.”","“You see, Watson,” he explained in the early hours of the morning as we sat over a glass of whisky and soda in Baker Street, “it was perfectly obvious from the first that the only possible object of this rather fantastic business of the advertisement of the League, and the copying of the Encyclopædia, must be to get this not over-bright pawnbroker out of the way for a number of hours every day. It was a curious way of managing it, but, really, it would be difficult to suggest a better. The method was no doubt suggested to Clay’s ingenious mind by the colour of his accomplice’s hair. The £4 a week was a lure which must draw him, and what was it to them, who were playing for thousands? They put in the advertisement, one rogue has the temporary office, the other rogue incites the man to apply for it, and together they manage to secure his absence every morning in the week. From the time that I heard of the assistant having come for half wages, it was obvious to me that he had some strong motive for securing the situation.”","“But how could you guess what the motive was?”","“Had there been women in the house, I should have suspected a mere vulgar intrigue. That, however, was out of the question. The man’s business was a small one, and there was nothing in his house which could account for such elaborate preparations, and such an expenditure as they were at. It must, then, be something out of the house. What could it be? I thought of the assistant’s fondness for photography, and his trick of vanishing into the cellar. The cellar! There was the end of this tangled clue. Then I made inquiries as to this mysterious assistant and found that I had to deal with one of the coolest and most daring criminals in London. He was doing something in the cellar—something which took many hours a day for months on end. What could it be, once more? I could think of nothing save that he was running a tunnel to some other building.","“So far I had got when we went to visit the scene of action. I surprised you by beating upon the pavement with my stick. I was ascertaining whether the cellar stretched out in front or behind. It was not in front. Then I rang the bell, and, as I hoped, the assistant answered it. We have had some skirmishes, but we had never set eyes upon each other before. I hardly looked at his face. His knees were what I wished to see. You must yourself have remarked how worn, wrinkled, and stained they were. They spoke of those hours of burrowing. The only remaining point was what they were burrowing for. I walked round the corner, saw the City and Suburban Bank abutted on our friend’s premises, and felt that I had solved my problem. When you drove home after the concert I called upon Scotland Yard and upon the chairman of the bank directors, with the result that you have seen.”","“And how could you tell that they would make their attempt to-night?” I asked.","“Well, when they closed their League offices that was a sign that they cared no longer about Mr. Jabez Wilson’s presence—in other words, that they had completed their tunnel. But it was essential that they should use it soon, as it might be discovered, or the bullion might be removed. Saturday would suit them better than any other day, as it would give them two days for their escape. For all these reasons I expected them to come to-night.”","“You reasoned it out beautifully,” I exclaimed in unfeigned admiration. “It is so long a chain, and yet every link rings true.”","“It saved me from ennui,” he answered, yawning. “Alas! I already feel it closing in upon me. My life is spent in one long effort to escape from the commonplaces of existence. These little problems help me to do so.”","“And you are a benefactor of the race,” said I.","He shrugged his shoulders. “Well, perhaps, after all, it is of some little use,” he remarked. “‘L’homme c’est rien—l’œuvre c’est tout,’ as Gustave Flaubert wrote to George Sand.”"],"glossary":{"bob":"kısa baş eğme, selam","settee":"kanepe, ikili koltuk","humdrum":"sıkıcı, tekdüze","relish":"keyif, zevk","embellish":"süslemek, abartarak anlatmak","chagrin":"hayal kırıklığı, bozulma","snuff":"enfiye (burna çekilen tütün)","bequest":"vasiyetle bırakılan miras","berths":"rahat işler, kadrolar","crib":"iş, mevki (argo); hırsız argosunda ev","billet":"iş, kadro","nominal":"göstermelik, sembolik","forfeit":"hakkını yitirmek","budge":"yerinden kıpırdamak","hoax":"düzmece oyun, aldatmaca","foolscap":"büyük boy yazı kâğıdı","sovereigns":"altın sterlinler","rueful":"pişman, kederli","solicitor":"avukat (İngiliz hukukunda)","conundrums":"bilmeceler, zor sorunlar","hansoms":"iki tekerlekli kiralık at arabaları","smasher":"kalpazan, sahte para basan","derbies":"kelepçe (argo)","bullion":"külçe altın","compunction":"vicdan azabı, tereddüt","lurid":"korkunç, şüpheli kızıl parıltılı","chink":"ince çatlak, aralık"},"phrases":[{"en":"prick up my ears","tr":"kulak kabartmak"},{"en":"at their wits’ end","tr":"ne yapacağını şaşırmış, çaresiz"},{"en":"stretch a point","tr":"kuralı biraz esnetmek"},{"en":"Well, I never!","tr":"Vay canına! Hayret!"},{"en":"off you go at scratch","tr":"baştan başlayıp anlatmaya koyul"},{"en":"turned on his heel","tr":"arkasını dönüp gitti"},{"en":"play a deep game","tr":"gizli ve sinsi bir oyun oynamak"},{"en":"come to a head","tr":"kritik noktaya gelmek"},{"en":"set eyes upon","tr":"gözü ilişmek, görmek"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the advertisement offer to red-headed men?","options":["A job guarding a bank","A good weekly salary for almost no real work","A free journey to America","A prize for the reddest hair"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"İlan, kızıl saçlı erkeklere haftada dört sterlin ödenen \"sözde\" bir iş vaat eder."},{"q":"What work does Jabez Wilson have to do for the League?","options":["Guard the office door","Teach a class","Copy out the Encyclopaedia Britannica","Deliver letters"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Wilson her sabah on ile iki arasında Encyclopaedia Britannica'yı elle kopyalamak zorundadır."},{"q":"What does Wilson find when he arrives at the office one morning?","options":["A card on the locked door saying the League is dissolved","The office full of policemen","Mr. Duncan Ross waiting with his wages","A new advertisement for another vacancy"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Kapıda \"Kızıl Saçlılar Birliği feshedilmiştir\" yazan bir karton vardır ve Birlik ortadan kaybolmuştur."},{"q":"Why does Holmes strike the pavement with his stick in Saxe-Coburg Square?","options":["To signal to the police","To test whether the stones are loose","To frighten the assistant","To find out whether the cellar extends in front of the shop"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Holmes, bodrumun dükkânın önüne doğru uzanıp uzanmadığını anlamak için kaldırımı vurur; uzanmadığını fark eder."},{"q":"What was the real purpose of the Red-headed League?","options":["To collect money from red-headed men","To find a missing heir","To keep Wilson away from his shop while thieves dug a tunnel to a bank","To hide a murder in the cellar"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Birlik uydurmaydı: Wilson her gün dükkândan uzak tutuluyor, bu sırada yardımcısı Clay dükkânın bodrumundan bankaya tünel kazıyordu."}],"words":9189,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-room-between-entries","type":"story","title":"The Room Between Entries","author":"Lantern Original","level":"C1","genre":"horror","summaryTr":"Yıkım öncesi son inceleme için eski bir konaklama binasına giren Iona, annesinin tuttuğu tahliye kayıtlarında açıklanamayan boşluklar bulur. Binanın boş olduğunu onaylamadan önce, kayıtlarda yer almayan insanların seslerini anlaması gerekir.","paragraphs":["The council had given Iona Vale a form with three boxes. The first asked whether the electricity had been disconnected. The second asked whether dangerous materials had been removed. The third asked whether all persons had left the premises. Beneath the boxes was a sentence in smaller print: The undersigned accepts responsibility for the accuracy of this declaration. Iona had signed hundreds of such forms. She had never previously considered how much weight the word all was expected to bear.","Morrow House stood behind the flood wall, a long brick building with windows too narrow for its broad, patient face. For thirty years it had provided temporary rooms for people who had nowhere else to go. After the flood, the council had closed it. Now a contractor wanted to demolish it before winter. Iona's mother had worked at its reception desk, and for that reason Iona should have refused the assignment. Instead, she had opened the folder and said she knew the building.","She arrived at four in the afternoon with Noor, a trainee surveyor whose yellow coat made the entrance look almost cheerful. The key turned reluctantly. Inside, the air smelled of wet wool and the powder used to clean old telephones. A man waited beside the reception desk, holding two paper cups. He introduced himself as Davi, the last porter. He had retired twelve years earlier, but apparently the council still had his number. He had brought coffee because the inspection would take longer than they thought.","Noor asked whether he had been there on the night of the flood. Davi looked at Iona before answering. 'I was on the other side of the river. Your mother sent me home early.' Iona took a cup she did not want. Her mother, Ruth Vale, had drowned while helping with the evacuation. That was the account in the newspaper and the account Iona had repeated whenever somebody asked. Her body had never been recovered. Neither fact was mentioned on the clearance form.","They began with the ground floor. Noor photographed the kitchen while Iona checked the service cupboards. Davi stayed close enough for his keys to be audible. In the dining room, fifty chairs stood on twenty tables. Their legs made a small forest against the ceiling. On one table someone had arranged a plate, a knife and a folded paper napkin. Iona touched the plate. It was warm. Davi put it into a cupboard without comment and closed the door with both hands.","At reception, Noor found a heavy register beneath a plastic sheet. The final page was dated twenty-one years earlier, the date of the flood. Most entries contained a room number, a name and a destination: sports hall, hospital, relative. Six entries had a straight line through the destination column. The last sentence read, All remaining residents transferred. Below it was Ruth Vale's signature. Iona recognised the unusual shape of the capital R, which resembled a woman turning to look over her shoulder.","'Don't copy that sentence,' Davi said. Noor's pencil stopped. 'Photographs are all right. Measurements are all right. But don't write that nobody's here.' He sounded embarrassed, as though giving instructions about a neighbour's difficult dog. Iona asked whether there were squatters. 'Sometimes there are people,' he replied. 'That isn't quite the same thing.' She told him that if he knew about unauthorised occupants, he had to say so. He looked at the three boxes on her form and said he was trying.","Upstairs, the first corridor contained twelve doors. The council plan showed twelve rooms. Noor numbered her photographs aloud, and Iona tested each handle. Room eight was locked. Room nine contained a bed frame and a wardrobe whose mirror faced the wall. Room ten was bare except for a damp rectangle where a picture had hung. When they reached the stairs, Noor said she had photographed thirteen doors. She showed Iona the images. Between nine and ten was a narrow green door with a brass number that had been polished beyond recognition.","They walked back. The green door was there. Iona measured the corridor, then measured the rooms on either side. The sums left no space for it. She tried the handle. From behind the door came the sound of someone dragging a suitcase across bare boards, followed by three polite knocks. Davi caught her wrist before she could knock in reply. 'Ask a question,' he whispered. 'Something the register couldn't answer.' Iona stared at him. He released her immediately, but the three knocks came again.","'What can you see?' she asked. A woman's voice answered, 'Room eleven, Miriam Saye, transferred to the sports hall.' Noor bent close to the brass plate. 'What can you see now?' Iona repeated. The woman said exactly the same words, with exactly the same breath between her name and her destination. There was no suitcase this time. Davi shook his head. 'Not her. Only what they wrote about her.' He led them downstairs before Iona could decide whether to be frightened or furious.","At the desk, he produced a biscuit tin containing letters, photographs and a notebook. He had tried to get the building examined before. Each time an inspection had ended early. Tools disappeared. Corridors changed. Once a contractor had been found outside, unable to remember the surname he had used for forty years. 'The house listens to the official account,' Davi said. 'When somebody tries to make it final, it corrects whatever doesn't fit.' Iona heard the careful tone of a person who had learned that speaking plainly made him less believable.","She called the council's duty officer. There was no signal, although her phone showed four bars. Noor offered hers and got the same result. Davi pointed to a red box beside the entrance: an old emergency telephone powered by a hand crank. It had connected to the flood station beyond the wall. A thick cable still ran from its back through the brickwork. 'That line doesn't go through reception,' he said. 'The others do.' Iona tried the front door. It opened onto the street. She could still leave.","That was the moment she would return to later, in the brief interval before sleep: the ordinary grey pavement, a delivery bicycle going past, the possibility of stepping out. Instead, she wanted an explanation that could survive daylight. She told Noor to record the discrepancies and wait near the entrance. Then she asked Davi to show her what he meant by the official account. He opened his notebook to a list of six names. Beside each name he had written things no council form would ask.","Leonie Price disliked pears because of their texture. Tomas Reed kept a broken radio because his daughter had given it to him. Safiya Bell counted staircases in sets of four. Iona recognised none of them. 'Those are the six without destinations,' Davi said. 'They were in the laundry corridor when the flood doors shut. Ruth thought the rescue team had taken them through the yard. The rescue team thought she'd moved them upstairs.' He rubbed the edge of the notebook. 'Nobody went back to look until the next day.'","Iona said her mother had gone back. It came out too sharply. Davi nodded. 'After she signed. After the officer told her the count was complete.' Outside, a bus sighed at the stop. The sound seemed unnecessarily distant. Davi had one letter from Ruth, written that evening and pushed under the porter's office door. It said: I have made them absent. I have to change it where I said it. Beneath that, in a hurried line: A name isn't enough. Tell someone who can answer you.","Noor called from the entrance. She was holding Iona's form against her clipboard. 'Which building number is this? Seventeen or nineteen?' Iona said seventeen. Noor looked down, made a short mark, then frowned. 'I didn't write that.' Across the third box, in Ruth Vale's unmistakable handwriting, were the words No persons remaining. The front door shut softly. Davi ran towards it. He pulled at the handle, but when it opened the dining room was on the other side, fifty upturned chairs and one warm plate.","Iona took the form. The paper was wet around the edges. She tore it across the third box, then into smaller pieces. The fragments fell onto the desk, where their fibres moved together like the legs of insects. A minute later the form lay whole beneath the register. Noor had gone very still. 'I only wrote the address,' she said. 'I know,' Iona answered. She made herself say it again. 'I know what you wrote.' Behind them, someone upstairs dragged a suitcase across bare boards.","The register now had a new line. Noor Awan, assistant inspector. In the destination column, a word appeared slowly, darkening from pale grey to black: transferred. Noor said that her hands felt cold. Iona reached for one, and for an instant could not remember whether the young woman had come with her or had been waiting inside. She said Noor's name aloud. The uncertainty passed. Davi opened his notebook. 'Tell me something about her. Something that happened today.' Iona described the yellow coat. 'Not what she is wearing. Something she did.'","That morning Noor had put a coin beneath a crooked café table. When the waiter offered to move them, she had said it would be crooked for the next customer too. Iona repeated the exchange, clinging to its unimportance. Davi wrote it down. Noor's fingers grew warmer. 'Now ask her about it,' he said. Iona asked why she had used a coin rather than folded paper. 'Paper goes soft when they wash the floor,' Noor replied. The word transferred faded, though it did not disappear.","For the first time, Davi looked hopeful. The house could repeat a record, he explained, but it could not carry on a conversation beyond what it held. The fragments behind the doors were statements wearing voices. Living people could answer, disagree, remember differently. The six residents had survived somewhere inside the building because he had continued writing to the council about them. Every unanswered letter had kept their disappearance unresolved. 'I thought I was achieving nothing,' he said. 'Perhaps nothing was what kept them alive.'","They needed someone outside to receive a correction. The red telephone was their only connection that the reception system did not control. Iona lifted its receiver while Davi turned the crank. There was a click, then a distant ringing. A woman answered, 'Flood station. State the number of persons awaiting transfer.' Her voice was Ruth's. Iona dropped the receiver so hard that it struck the wall. Davi checked the box and found the cable cleanly severed beneath it. The voice had come from an instrument connected to nothing.","The actual cable continued through a maintenance duct to the cellar, where its test terminal could be reached. Davi knew because he had repaired it during the building's last winter. They would need the crank unit and two lengths of wire. He fetched a screwdriver from his tin. Noor volunteered to read the six residents' names while they worked. At the third name she stopped. Her yellow sleeve had become the dull colour of the wall behind it. In the register, transferred was returning to black.","Iona asked about the coin again. Noor answered correctly, but her sleeve continued fading. They could not keep repeating the same memory. The house was learning their account as they spoke. Iona asked what Noor wanted for dinner. 'I want to go home,' she said angrily. 'I want you to stop testing whether I'm real.' The colour returned so abruptly that Iona flinched. She apologised. Noor was not a lock to be opened with the right question. She was afraid, and Iona had been treating her fear as a useful mechanism.","Getting the telephone apart took ten minutes. During that time they discussed ordinary things, badly. Davi confessed he had brought instant coffee in a flask because the café charged too much. Noor said she had known. Iona admitted she had taken this assignment to discover whether her mother had died courageously. 'Would you love her less if she hadn't?' Noor asked. Iona could not answer at once. The register rustled, but no new writing appeared. An honest hesitation, it seemed, was harder for the house to use than a confident lie.","They descended with the crank unit, the notebook and a torch. The cellar stairs were dry, yet each step made the sound of water being displaced. At the bottom, the walls carried a brown flood mark level with Iona's mouth. Davi pointed out the maintenance duct behind an iron grille. Its screws were deeply rusted. While he worked on them, Noor shone the torch along the passage. Six coats hung on hooks beside a closed fire door. Beneath them, on the clean concrete, six pairs of wet footprints waited without moving.","Then the footprints changed. One pair turned towards them. Behind the fire door, a man said, 'If you are taking names, mine is Tomas Reed.' Iona remembered the broken radio. Instead of asking about it, she asked what he had been doing before she arrived. 'Trying to make that woman stop humming,' he answered. Someone else said she would hum whatever she pleased. Noor gave a sudden, almost painful laugh. These voices interrupted one another. They were untidy and impatient. They belonged to people who did not know what answer was required.","The fire door had no handle on their side. Its inspection window was covered by a sheet of paper stating that the room had been cleared. Iona scraped away a corner with her thumbnail. Through the glass she saw a laundry trolley, a strip of fluorescent light and a woman sitting on an upturned bucket. The woman looked perhaps forty. In Davi's photograph, taken twenty-one years earlier, she had looked exactly the same. Behind her, the corridor extended farther than the building itself. Its far end was lost in a bright, depthless grey.","Davi spoke to the woman through the door. 'Leonie. It's Davi.' She stood so quickly the bucket rolled away. 'You said you'd bring blankets.' He closed his eyes. 'I know.' She asked how long they had been waiting. He told her. Silence followed, then a man began to swear. Iona had expected gratitude, recognition, relief. Instead, the people behind the door were furious with them. The sound steadied her. Whatever the house offered in exchange for obedience, it was unlikely to include the right to be inconvenient.","Noor asked whether Ruth Vale was with them. Leonie shook her head. Ruth had come to the window carrying the register. She had tried to cross out the final sentence, but the ink returned. A woman at reception had told her that one person could amend an address, but a completed transfer required acknowledgement at its destination. Ruth had gone to make a call. Later, water came under the door. 'We heard her in the passage,' Leonie said. 'Then we didn't.' She would not tell Iona anything kinder than what she knew.","While Davi worked, Tomas pushed a folded sheet beneath the laundry door. It was a list of people to contact, written on the back of a washing schedule. Several telephone numbers had too few digits. Iona recognised an address where a supermarket now stood. She could have pocketed the sheet and promised to make the calls, but Tomas was watching her through the cleared corner of glass. She told him that some addresses might have changed. He asked whether his daughter would still be at school. Iona said no.","He sat down where she could no longer see him. For a moment the only sound was Davi scraping rust from a screw. Then Tomas asked what happened to a parent who missed the whole of a child's growing up. Noor crouched beside the window. 'I don't know,' she said. 'But she may have spent those years thinking you chose to leave.' He moved close enough to the door that they could hear his breathing. 'I didn't,' he said. Noor wrote those two words beneath his daughter's old telephone number.","Leonie wanted to know whether they would be allowed to leave the hospital afterwards. The question startled Iona. Leonie explained that, before the flood, temporary accommodation had repeatedly turned into somewhere she needed permission to leave. She would not exchange this corridor for another locked door merely because the people on the other side used kinder voices. Iona could promise no hospital policy. She promised to record that Leonie had asked, and to stay until someone answered her directly. Behind her, the register's pages stirred as if irritated by the limits of that promise.","The two brothers asked for their bag to be passed out first. It caught beneath the door, and they began blaming one another. Iona almost told them to abandon it. Then she remembered that every object she herself had chosen to keep was waiting safely in an apartment outside. She and Noor worked the straps through one at a time. Inside were a chess set, three notebooks and a pair of shoes much too small for either brother. None of it explained their determination; none of it needed to. The bag finally slid free, and for the first time something had crossed the boundary intact.","Davi removed the grille. The cable terminal was intact, but the cupboard surrounding it had become a miniature reception desk. On its shelf lay a clean form. A woman stood in the passage behind them, wearing a grey cardigan with a council badge. Iona had not heard her approach. Her face was perfectly ordinary until one tried to remember it. 'We can correct the assistant's entry,' she said. 'An administrative error. Sign here, and the current inspection party may leave.' She placed a fountain pen beside the form.","The document named Iona, Noor and Davi. Beneath their names it declared the remainder of the premises unoccupied. Iona understood the bargain. They would get out by closing the question that had kept the six residents alive. She asked the woman her name. 'Duty registrar.' She asked whether the woman had a family. 'My responsibility is to maintain an accurate count.' Iona asked what happened when the count was wrong. The woman smiled. 'Then the circumstances must be brought into agreement with the count.'","Davi reached past the form to the cable. The registrar did not stop him. Instead, she spoke in his own voice: 'I was on the other side of the river.' Then Ruth's: 'Go home, Davi. I'll finish here.' His hand shook. Iona saw what the house was doing. It could not invent a future, but it could arrange the past until regret became unbearable. Davi had spent twenty-one years wishing he had stayed. The registrar offered him a different memory. In it, Ruth survived because he returned in time. He only had to sign.","'That wouldn't be what happened,' Davi said. The registrar replied that the distinction had caused him enough suffering. He set the crank unit on the floor and began stripping the wires with his pocketknife. Noor sat beside him to hold the torch. Iona stayed by the laundry door. She asked the six residents to describe themselves for the outside operator, and corrected her request when it sounded like another official interview. 'Tell us what you want somebody to know.' For a while nobody spoke. Then Safiya said she wanted her son to know she had waited for him.","A thin metallic note came from the telephone. Davi turned the crank again. This time a man answered, with traffic behind his voice. 'River maintenance control. Who is using the old emergency circuit?' Iona almost cried at the irritation in his question. She gave her name, her department and the building's address. The man said Morrow House was empty. She told him that it was not. He asked how many people were inside. The registrar moved closer, and the cellar seemed to narrow around that simple, reasonable question.","Iona started to say nine. Six residents, three inspectors. Then she saw Noor's clipboard lying at the foot of the stairs. Noor herself was no longer holding the torch. The light rested against Davi's knee, and where she had been sitting there was a pale, dry patch on the floor. The telephone operator repeated his question. From behind the laundry door, in a voice stripped of anger, Noor called Iona's name. Her entry had been completed while they concentrated on the others. She was now someone who had already been transferred.","Iona gave the operator all nine names, including her own, but refused to certify that there were only nine people. 'The number is not established,' she said. 'Keep the incident open. Read that back to me.' The registrar put one hand over the terminal. The line filled with a sound like pages being turned very quickly. Iona shouted, asking the operator to repeat the message. She heard only pieces: address, persons present, number not established. Then the noise stopped. The operator had acknowledged the correction. Somewhere above them, a door slammed open against the street.","The laundry door shuddered, but remained shut. Its paper notice had changed. Now it read: Transfer requires confirmation of receiving location. The operator was dispatching a fire crew. They would take several minutes. The registrar said the circuit could not be maintained that long. She was telling the truth. The crank unit was hot beneath Davi's hand, and the stripped wires had begun to smoke. Iona asked the operator to stay with them while Davi disconnected one wire. In the sudden silence, the house seemed to inhale.","They needed a destination that already existed, and someone there to confirm it. The street was a destination. The operator could not see it from his control room, but there was a camera on the flood wall. Iona remembered passing it. When Davi restored the connection, she told the operator to turn the camera towards the entrance. He said the old camera could not turn; it faced the service yard. Davi pointed to a low door at the end of the cellar passage. Beyond it was that same yard. They would have to open it from inside.","Iona ran towards the door. Halfway there, she heard her mother calling from an open office. Ruth sat behind a desk, younger than Iona was now, rubbing ink from her thumb. The sight brought back an ache so precise that Iona nearly stopped breathing. Her mother's bag stood on the floor. An orange lay beside it, scored around the skin with a thumbnail. Everything was right. Ruth looked up and said, 'I went back for them.' Iona wanted to kneel beside her. She wanted the story she had carried to be true in exactly the way she had imagined.","Instead, she asked, 'Did you know the door was closed when you signed?' Ruth looked down. 'All remaining residents transferred.' Iona asked again, and the sentence repeated. It was another fragment, assembled from a signature and a daughter's need. The registrar stood in the doorway beyond the desk. 'You can keep her as she was,' she said. Iona looked at the orange. Her mother always peeled oranges in one long strip, and often broke the strip halfway through. This one was perfect. It had no accidental history. She walked past it.","The yard door was held by two bolts. The lower one moved. The upper one had been painted into its socket, and Iona could not shift it. She called for help. For several seconds nobody came, and she understood how easily courage could become another flattering account told after an event. She was simply a frightened woman with insufficient strength. Then Davi arrived with the screwdriver. Together they chipped away the paint. Behind them, the telephone receiver lay on the floor, carrying the operator's increasingly urgent voice into the passage.","When the bolt released, cold evening air entered at ankle height. Davi pushed the door wide and ran into the yard. Iona could see the camera above the flood wall. Its small red light faced them. Davi waved both arms, then went back to the receiver, dragging the wire as far as it would reach. 'I'm in the service yard,' he said. 'I am Davi Mercer. I will receive whoever comes out. I am not confirming that the building is empty.' The distinction was his own, and Iona loved him for understanding it.","The paper over the laundry window split down the middle. Iona heard the handle on the other side turn. Leonie emerged first, blinking in the torchlight, followed by Tomas carrying a radio without its back panel. Safiya supported an older man whose left foot was wrapped in a towel. Two brothers came next, arguing over a bag they had both carried for what felt to them like an hour. Noor came last. She put her palm against Iona's cheek, not gently. 'Next time,' she said, 'we leave when the door is still a door.'","The registrar waited beside the stairs. Without moving, she seemed farther away each time Iona looked at her. 'A final total is required,' she said. Outside, Davi answered each person by name as the operator repeated it over the telephone. Iona told the registrar that a search team would establish what it could, and that uncertainty would remain in the report where evidence was missing. The woman looked almost bewildered. Her badge was now blank. Iona passed her and stepped into the yard without promising that nobody remained behind.","The fire crew arrived to find nine people under the camera, wrapped in blankets from an emergency locker. The six residents were taken to hospital. Their documents were old; their bodies were not. Reconciling those facts would occupy other departments for years. That night, Iona sat with Noor while Davi gave his statement. She did not ask Noor to forgive her. When Noor began to shake, she held the paper cup steady so she could drink. Beyond the wall, Morrow House had the dark, vacant appearance of any abandoned building.","Demolition was suspended. The report Iona eventually submitted included her mother's signature, Davi's unanswered letters and the names of officials who had accepted a completed count without checking the laundry corridor. It also included Iona's decision to remain after the first warning. Some colleagues advised her to remove that paragraph. She kept it. About Ruth, she wrote only what the witnesses could support: she signed an incorrect declaration, recognised the error and returned to attempt a correction. The search for her remains continued. Iona no longer needed uncertainty to look like disgrace.","Three months later, Noor returned to work in another department. Davi began visiting Leonie, who had discovered that she still disliked pears and now had twenty-one years of other dislikes to catch up on. The emergency circuit at Morrow House remained connected. No one was asked to certify an empty building. On the final page of the register, beneath the old sentence, Iona had written a new entry in her own ordinary handwriting: The previous declaration is withdrawn. Further information may be added. This account is not complete. She left enough space for another person's hand."],"glossary":{"premises":"bina ve ona ait alanlar","undersigned":"aşağıda imzası bulunan kişi","clearance":"binanın boşaltıldığını onaylama","squatters":"izinsiz yerleşen kişiler","discrepancies":"kayıtlarla gözlemler arasındaki tutarsızlıklar","acknowledgement":"bildirimin alındığının doğrulanması","registrar":"kayıtları tutan görevli","terminal":"kablonun bağlantı noktası","crank":"elle çevrilen kol","grille":"metal koruyucu ızgara","socket":"sürgünün girdiği yuva","reconciling":"çelişen bilgileri bağdaştırma","withdrawn":"resmen geri çekilmiş","unresolved":"henüz açıklığa kavuşmamış"},"phrases":[{"en":"accepts responsibility","tr":"sorumluluğu kabul eder"},{"en":"for that reason","tr":"bu nedenle"},{"en":"on the other side","tr":"öteki tarafta"},{"en":"in exchange for","tr":"karşılığında"},{"en":"brought into agreement","tr":"uyumlu hâle getirilmiş"},{"en":"read that back to me","tr":"bunu bana tekrar okuyun"},{"en":"at ankle height","tr":"ayak bileği hizasında"},{"en":"catch up on","tr":"kaçırdığı şeylerin eksiğini kapatmak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Iona initially accept the inspection assignment?","options":["She expects to recover valuable furniture.","She wants to understand her mother's final actions.","Noor insists on inspecting the building.","She plans to move the residents herself."],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Iona, annesinin ölümüne ilişkin anlatıyı ve son davranışlarını anlamak istediği için görevi alır."},{"q":"What distinguishes the living residents from the voices copied by the house?","options":["They can respond beyond the information in the records.","They never disagree with the inspectors.","They speak only when their names are read.","They remember every event outside the building."],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Yaşayan insanlar kayıtlı bilgilerin ötesinde cevap verebilir, karşı çıkabilir ve yeni konuşmalar geliştirebilir."},{"q":"What has Davi's correspondence unintentionally achieved?","options":["It has persuaded the council to repair the house.","It has destroyed the original evacuation register.","It has kept the residents' disappearance unresolved.","It has prevented time from passing outside."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Cevapsız kalan mektuplar kaybolma olayının kesin biçimde kapanmasını engelleyerek içerideki insanların varlığını korumuştur."},{"q":"Why does Iona refuse to certify that there are only nine people inside?","options":["She cannot remember Davi's surname.","The operator forbids the use of numbers.","She wants to delay the fire crew.","A final total could exclude people who have not been found."],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Kesin bir toplam, henüz bulunmamış kişileri yok sayan yeni bir kapatma işlemine dönüşebilir; bu yüzden sayının belirlenmediğini söyler."},{"q":"What does Iona's final report show about her changed attitude?","options":["She accepts supported facts and uncertainty, including her own mistakes.","She decides all official records should be destroyed.","She proves her mother made no mistakes.","She considers a comforting account more useful than evidence."],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Iona annesini kusursuzlaştırmak yerine tanıklıkların desteklediği bilgileri, bilinmeyenleri ve kendi hatasını birlikte kayda geçirir."}],"words":4556,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-rules-inside-uncertainty","type":"article","title":"The Rules Inside Uncertainty","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C1","genre":"horror","summaryTr":"Korku anlatıları, okurun ne bildiği ve neyi henüz doğrulayamadığı arasındaki gerilimle nasıl çalışır? Bu deneme, özgün kurmaca örnekler üzerinden belirsizliği, karakter kararlarını, dildeki ipuçlarını ve etkili sonları inceler.","paragraphs":["## An ordinary world must first become legible","A frightening event does not automatically create a frightening story. A door may open by itself, a stranger may know a private name, or a familiar room may acquire another window. Such events offer material for horror, but their force depends on what the reader has already learned to expect. If every object behaves unpredictably from the first sentence, a moving door may barely register. To establish a pattern is therefore one of the writer's first tasks. The ordinary world supplies the measure against which a disturbance becomes noticeable.","Consider an invented story about Mara, who cleans a small railway waiting room after the last train. Each evening she stacks eight chairs, empties two bins and checks a timetable behind glass. On Tuesday she finds nine chairs. This detail works because the routine has made eight meaningful. We do not yet need an explanation. We need Mara's response, and perhaps a second detail that resists her first interpretation. She checks the store cupboard, where the spare chair remains folded. The event has become a question with a limited number of ordinary answers.","The routine should have value beyond providing a later shock. Perhaps Mara counts the chairs because a damaged leg once injured a passenger. Counting is then an expression of care, not an arbitrary habit inserted to support the plot. When the number changes, the disturbance threatens both her understanding of the room and her ability to keep it safe. This connection gives a practical action emotional depth. Readers can follow what she does while also understanding why abandoning the room would be difficult for her.","When reading an opening, ask which details teach you how the place normally works. Then distinguish those details from decoration. Rain may create atmosphere, but a rule requiring Mara to lock the outside gate before the waiting room establishes a sequence that can later trap her. A useful reading note records an expectation rather than merely an image: the gate should already be locked; the timetable should not change overnight. This practice makes it easier to notice when a story begins to put pressure on its own ordinary arrangements.","## Uncertainty needs boundaries","Horror can withhold the nature of a threat while making its effects precise. The reader may not know who adds the ninth chair, yet still learn that it appears only after Mara closes the gate. That boundary allows anticipation. She can experiment by leaving the gate open, and the result can alter her choices. Complete explanation is unnecessary. Consistent consequences are more useful. If the threat can do absolutely anything whenever the plot requires it, suspense may weaken because neither resistance nor interpretation has a stable basis.","An unexplained rule should also cost something to discover. Suppose Mara leaves the gate open and the chair does not appear, but a passenger enters after the last train and asks for a town absent from the timetable. She has prevented one event while enabling another. The experiment advances the story because it changes the situation rather than merely confirming information. Readers can now compare two risks. The threat remains mysterious, but the practical problem becomes clearer: which boundary can she maintain, and what does maintaining it exclude?","Ambiguity differs from missing information. An ambiguous detail supports more than one interpretation that the text has prepared. A passenger's wet coat might suggest ordinary rain or a journey through the flooded tunnel described earlier. Missing information, by contrast, provides no basis for choosing among possibilities. A writer who withholds every relevant fact may produce confusion rather than dread. To keep the reader in doubt, the story needs enough evidence for competing explanations to remain active. Doubt is an organised relation between possibilities, not simply an absence of knowledge.","A useful reading method is to separate observation, interpretation and prediction. Mara observes water beneath a chair. She interprets it as evidence that somebody has been sitting there. She predicts that the person will return. The narration may present these steps close together, making the prediction feel like a fact. Slowing down allows the reader to ask where certainty ends. This distinction is especially valuable when a narrator is frightened, ashamed or eager to prove that an earlier decision was reasonable. Their attention may be accurate while their explanation remains mistaken.","## A character needs reasons to remain","Readers often ask why a horror protagonist does not simply leave. The question deserves a serious answer. A locked door can supply an immediate obstacle, but emotional and social obligations may make the situation richer. Mara might be responsible for a child waiting for collection. Leaving could protect her while exposing someone else. Alternatively, she may fear losing a job that supports her household. These motives should appear before the danger demands them. Otherwise, they risk sounding like excuses invented to keep the character inside the plot.","A convincing motive does not require the protagonist to make perfect decisions. People can misjudge a situation while acting coherently within what they know. If Mara has previously dealt with practical jokes, interpreting the extra chair as another prank makes sense. When evidence accumulates against that interpretation, however, the story must allow her thinking to change. Repeating the same dismissal without development can make her seem artificially passive. Fear becomes more involving when a character learns, attempts a response and discovers that learning has not removed the cost of acting.","An effective escalation changes the nature of the choice. At first Mara wants to identify whoever moved a chair. Later she may have to decide whether to open the gate for the unknown passenger. Finally, she may realise that obeying the station's closing instructions will leave somebody in the tunnel. Each stage should grow from earlier actions. A sequence of louder noises can intensify sensation, but a sequence of harder decisions deepens the drama. The reader begins to fear what the protagonist might be required to do, as well as what might happen to her.","Responsibility also prevents a secondary character from becoming a mere instrument of danger. The stranded child should have preferences, misunderstandings and useful observations. Perhaps the child notices that the new timetable lists departures but no arrivals. This contribution gives the relationship substance while providing a clue. It is usually more unsettling to see two distinct people struggle to agree than to watch one clever protagonist escort a silent burden. Their disagreement may reveal different priorities without reducing either person to a mistake that the plot needs punished.","## Language directs the reader's attention","Horror often depends on the distribution of attention within a paragraph. A writer can mention a disturbing object casually, allowing the reader to notice its significance before the character does. In our invented waiting room, a paragraph might describe Mara checking windows, collecting cups and placing a ninth chair on the stack. If eight has already been established, the extra chair becomes visible without an announcement. The sentence trusts the reader to make the connection. That small act of recognition can be more effective than immediately declaring that something terrifying has occurred.","Precision helps, but excessive description can reveal the writer's intention too early. If every ordinary chair receives a page of sinister imagery, the reader expects a chair to become important. A more varied texture leaves room for discovery. Some details can remain practically useful or simply characteristic of the setting. The aim is not to hide clues among meaningless objects. It is to let the world retain enough ordinary activity that the threat appears to interrupt a place that existed before the story began.","Sentence rhythm offers another way to control attention. A long sentence can carry the reader through a familiar sequence, while a short one can interrupt that movement. Yet short sentences are not automatically frightening, and repeated fragments soon establish their own predictable rhythm. The relevant question is what changes at the interruption. If Mara counts the chairs, hears a train and turns towards the window, a pause may focus attention on an absence: the tracks are empty. Rhythm supports the perception of a change; it cannot substitute for that change.","For an English learner, modality is particularly useful to examine. Phrases such as must have, seemed to and could not have indicate different relationships to evidence. 'Someone had moved the chair' states an event. 'Someone must have moved the chair' presents an inference, however confident. 'The chair seemed closer' leaves room for a perceptual error. Marking these small distinctions can reveal a narrator's changing confidence across a scene. It also helps the reader avoid turning every uncertain impression into an established supernatural fact before the story itself has done so.","## An ending can resolve action without explaining everything","A satisfying horror ending does not have to supply a complete account of the threat's origin. It should, however, address the central actions and choices that the story has developed. If Mara's problem becomes whether to open the gate for the child, the ending should show what she decides and what follows. A final paragraph announcing that the station was haunted cannot replace that resolution. Explanation answers why an event may have happened; resolution answers what the characters do with the situation they have inherited or helped create.","Suppose Mara discovers that the timetable repeats the names of people previously left behind. She opens the gate, brings the child through and refuses to complete the closing report that would describe the station as empty. The child reaches safety, but an additional chair remains inside. This could offer an earned uncertainty if the story has linked official closure to abandonment. The immediate rescue is complete, while the place retains a consequence that has not been neatly removed. The ending grows from the story's rules and concerns instead of attaching an unrelated final shock.","Compare that with an ending in which Mara wakes and discovers that the entire night was a dream. Such a device can work when the story has developed the consequences of dreaming, but used without preparation it may cancel the reader's investment. Likewise, a new monster appearing in the final sentence may create surprise while weakening the earlier pattern. The useful question is not whether an ending contains a twist. It is whether the last change makes earlier details more meaningful, or merely tells the reader that those details no longer matter.","After finishing a horror story, return to one ordinary action from its opening. Counting chairs, checking a name or locking a gate may now carry a different significance. Trace the decisions that produced this change, and identify which uncertainties remain deliberate. You do not need to solve every symbol or reduce the threat to a single message. A careful reading can acknowledge both a completed human action and an unexplained presence. The lasting unease often comes from that combination: someone has done what they can, and the world still exceeds what they know."],"glossary":{"legible":"işleyişi anlaşılabilir","register":"fark edilir hâle gelmek","withhold":"bilgiyi henüz açıklamamak","anticipation":"olacakları bekleme duygusu","ambiguity":"birden fazla yoruma açık olma","inference":"kanıtlardan çıkarılan sonuç","modality":"dilde kesinlik ve olasılık bildirme","escalation":"tehlikenin veya gerilimin artması","resolution":"olay örgüsündeki temel eylemin sonuca bağlanması","unease":"huzursuzluk","passive":"tepki vermeyen, edilgen","perceptual":"algıyla ilgili"},"phrases":[{"en":"establish a pattern","tr":"bir düzen veya örüntü oluşturmak"},{"en":"put pressure on","tr":"üzerinde baskı oluşturmak"},{"en":"keep the reader in doubt","tr":"okuru kararsızlık içinde tutmak"},{"en":"within what they know","tr":"bildiklerinin sınırları içinde"},{"en":"grow from earlier actions","tr":"önceki eylemlerden doğmak"},{"en":"supply a complete account","tr":"eksiksiz bir açıklama sunmak"},{"en":"leaves room for","tr":"olanak tanır, yer bırakır"},{"en":"substitute for","tr":"yerini tutmak"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Mara's ordinary chair-counting routine useful?","options":["It explains the threat's entire history.","It makes a later change recognisable and connects counting to care.","It guarantees that Mara cannot make mistakes.","It removes the need for other details."],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Rutin hem değişikliği fark edilir kılar hem de Mara'nın insanları koruma isteğiyle bağlantı kurar."},{"q":"According to the article, what distinguishes ambiguity from missing information?","options":["Ambiguity supports prepared competing interpretations.","Ambiguity always reveals a supernatural explanation.","Missing information requires more evidence.","The two terms describe exactly the same effect."],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Belirsizlik, metnin dayanak sunduğu birden fazla yorumu canlı tutar; salt bilgi eksikliği yorum için dayanak vermeyebilir."},{"q":"Which development best illustrates escalation through choice?","options":["A corridor grows darker in every paragraph.","The same noise repeats without consequence.","Mara must decide whether opening the gate protects or endangers someone.","The narrator calls each chair increasingly frightening."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Tehlikeyi artıran zor kararlar, yalnızca duyusal şiddeti artırmaktan daha derin bir dramatik gerilim yaratır."},{"q":"What does 'Someone must have moved the chair' express?","options":["A direct command","An impossible action","A neutral description without interpretation","An inference about what happened"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Must have ifadesi geçmişe ilişkin güçlü bir çıkarım bildirir; doğrudan gözlemlenmiş olayı tek başına doğrulamaz."},{"q":"What should a horror ending resolve even if the threat remains partly unexplained?","options":["Every symbol in the setting","The central human actions and choices","All possible histories of the threat","The reader's every uncertainty"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Tehdidin kökeni açık kalabilir; temel karakter kararlarının ve eylemlerinin neye vardığı ise gösterilmelidir."}],"words":1793,"length":"medium"},{"id":"the-star","type":"story","title":"The Star","author":"H. G. Wells","year":1897,"level":"C1","genre":"scifi","source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #27365","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/27365","license":"Public domain"},"summaryTr":"Gökbilimciler, Neptün'ün yörüngesinde açıklanamaz bir sapma fark eder; kısa süre sonra gökyüzünde her geceyle birlikte büyüyen parlak bir yıldız belirir. İnsanlık yaklaşan felaketin ne olduğunu ve nasıl bir bedel ödeyeceğini öğrenmek üzeredir.","paragraphs":["It was on the first day of the new year that the announcement was made, almost simultaneously from three observatories, that the motion of the planet Neptune, the outermost of all the planets that wheel about the sun, had become very erratic. Ogilvy had already called attention to a suspected retardation in its velocity in December. Such a piece of news was scarcely calculated to interest a world the greater portion of whose inhabitants were unaware of the existence of the planet Neptune, nor outside the astronomical profession did the subsequent discovery of a faint remote speck of light in the region of the perturbed planet cause any very great excitement. Scientific people, however, found the intelligence remarkable enough, even before it became known that the new body was rapidly growing larger and brighter, that its motion was quite different from the orderly progress of the planets, and that the deflection of Neptune and its satellite was becoming now of an unprecedented kind.","Few people without a training in science can realise the huge isolation of the solar system. The sun with its specks of planets, its dust of planetoids, and its impalpable comets, swims in a vacant immensity that almost defeats the imagination. Beyond the orbit of Neptune there is space, vacant so far as human observation has penetrated, without warmth or light or sound, blank emptiness, for twenty million times a million miles. That is the smallest estimate of the distance to be traversed before the very nearest of the stars is attained. And, saving a few comets more unsubstantial than the thinnest flame, no matter had ever to human knowledge crossed this gulf of space, until early in the twentieth century this strange wanderer appeared. A vast mass of matter it was, bulky, heavy, rushing without warning out of the black mystery of the sky into the radiance of the sun. By the second day it was clearly visible to any decent instrument, as a speck with a barely sensible diameter, in the constellation Leo near Regulus. In a little while an opera glass could attain it.","On the third day of the new year the newspaper readers of two hemispheres were made aware for the first time of the real importance of this unusual apparition in the heavens. \"A Planetary Collision,\" one London paper headed the news, and proclaimed Duchaine's opinion that this strange new planet would probably collide with Neptune. The leader writers enlarged upon the topic. So that in most of the capitals of the world, on January 3rd, there was an expectation, however vague of some imminent phenomenon in the sky; and as the night followed the sunset round the globe, thousands of men turned their eyes skyward to see—the old familiar stars just as they had always been.","Until it was dawn in London and Pollux setting and the stars overhead grown pale. The Winter's dawn it was, a sickly filtering accumulation of daylight, and the light of gas and candles shone yellow in the windows to show where people were astir. But the yawning policeman saw the thing, the busy crowds in the markets stopped agape, workmen going to their work betimes, milkmen, the drivers of news-carts, dissipation going home jaded and pale, homeless wanderers, sentinels on their beats, and in the country, labourers trudging afield, poachers slinking home, all over the dusky quickening country it could be seen—and out at sea by seamen watching for the day—a great white star, come suddenly into the westward sky!","Brighter it was than any star in our skies; brighter than the evening star at its brightest. It still glowed out white and large, no mere twinkling spot of light, but a small round clear shining disc, an hour after the day had come. And where science has not reached, men stared and feared, telling one another of the wars and pestilences that are foreshadowed by these fiery signs in the Heavens. Sturdy Boers, Frenchmen, Spaniards, Portuguese, stood in the warmth of the sunrise watching the setting of this strange new star.","And in a hundred observatories there had been suppressed excitement, rising almost to shouting pitch, as the two remote bodies had rushed together, and a hurrying to and fro, to gather photographic apparatus and spectroscope, and this appliance and that, to record this novel astonishing sight, the destruction of a world. For it was a world, a sister planet of our earth, far greater than our earth indeed, that had so suddenly flashed into flaming death. Neptune it was, had been struck, fairly and squarely, by the strange planet from outer space and the heat of the concussion had incontinently turned two solid globes into one vast mass of incandescence. Round the world that day, two hours before the dawn, went the pallid great white star, fading only as it sank westward and the sun mounted above it. Everywhere men marvelled at it, but of all those who saw it none could have marvelled more than those sailors, habitual watchers of the stars, who far away at sea had heard nothing of its advent and saw it now rise like a pigmy moon and climb zenithward and hang overhead and sink westward with the passing of the night.","And when next it rose over Europe everywhere were crowds of watchers on hilly slopes, on house-roofs, in open spaces, staring eastward for the rising of the great new star. It rose with a white glow in front of it, like the glare of a white fire, and those who had seen it come into existence the night before cried out at the sight of it. \"It is larger,\" they cried. \"It is brighter!\" And, indeed the moon a quarter full and sinking in the west was in its apparent size beyond comparison, but scarcely in all its breadth had it as much brightness now as the little circle of the strange new star.","\"It is brighter!\" cried the people clustering in the streets. But in the dim observatories the watchers held their breath and peered at one another. \"It is nearer,\" they said. \"Nearer!\"","And voice after voice repeated, \"It is nearer,\" and the clicking telegraph took that up, and it trembled along telephone wires, and in a thousand cities grimy compositors fingered the type. \"It is nearer.\" Men writing in offices, struck with a strange realisation, flung down their pens, men talking in a thousand places suddenly came upon a grotesque possibility in those words, \"It is nearer.\" It hurried along awakening streets, it was shouted down the frost-stilled ways of quiet villages, men who had read these things from the throbbing tape stood in yellow-lit doorways shouting the news to the passers-by. \"It is nearer.\" Pretty women, flushed and glittering, heard the news told jestingly between the dances, and feigned an intelligent interest they did not feel. \"Nearer! Indeed. How curious! How very, very clever people must be to find out things like that!\"","Lonely tramps faring through the wintry night murmured those words to comfort themselves—looking skyward. \"It has need to be nearer, for the night's as cold as charity. Don't seem much warmth from it if it is nearer, all the same.\"","\"What is a new star to me?\" cried the weeping woman kneeling beside her dead.","The schoolboy, rising early for his examination work, puzzled it out for himself—with the great white star, shining broad and bright through the frost-flowers of his window. \"Centrifugal, centripetal,\" he said, with his chin on his fist. \"Stop a planet in its flight, rob it of its centrifugal force, what then? Centripetal has it, and down it falls into the sun! And this—!\"","\"Do we come in the way? I wonder—\"","The light of that day went the way of its brethren, and with the later watches of the frosty darkness rose the strange star again. And it was now so bright that the waxing moon seemed but a pale yellow ghost of itself, hanging huge in the sunset. In a South African city a great man had married, and the streets were alight to welcome his return with his bride. \"Even the skies have illuminated,\" said the flatterer. Under Capricorn, two lovers, daring the wild beasts and evil spirits, for love of one another, crouched together in a cane brake where the fire-flies hovered. \"That is our star,\" they whispered, and felt strangely comforted by the sweet brilliance of its light.","The master mathematician sat in his private room and pushed the papers from him. His calculations were already finished. In a small white phial there still remained a little of the drug that had kept him awake and active for four long nights. Each day, serene, explicit, patient as ever, he had given his lecture to his students, and then had come back at once to this momentous calculation. His face was grave, a little drawn and hectic from his drugged activity. For some time he seemed lost in thought. Then he went to the window, and the blind went up with a click. Half way up the sky, over the clustering roofs, chimneys and steeples of the city, hung the star.","He looked at it as one might look into the eyes of a brave enemy. \"You may kill me,\" he said after a silence. \"But I can hold you—and all the universe for that matter—in the grip of this little brain. I would not change. Even now.\"","He looked at the little phial. \"There will be no need of sleep again,\" he said. The next day at noon, punctual to the minute, he entered his lecture theatre, put his hat on the end of the table as his habit was, and carefully selected a large piece of chalk. It was a joke among his students that he could not lecture without that piece of chalk to fumble in his fingers, and once he had been stricken to impotence by their hiding his supply. He came and looked under his grey eyebrows at the rising tiers of young fresh faces, and spoke with his accustomed studied commonness of phrasing. \"Circumstances have arisen—circumstances beyond my control,\" he said and paused, \"which will debar me from completing the course I had designed. It would seem, gentlemen, if I may put the thing clearly and briefly, that—Man has lived in vain.\"","The students glanced at one another. Had they heard aright? Mad? Raised eyebrows and grinning lips there were, but one or two faces remained intent upon his calm grey-fringed face. \"It will be interesting,\" he was saying, \"to devote this morning to an exposition, so far as I can make it clear to you, of the calculations that have led me to this conclusion. Let us assume—\"","He turned towards the blackboard, meditating a diagram in the way that was usual to him. \"What was that about 'lived in vain?'\" whispered one student to another. \"Listen,\" said the other, nodding towards the lecturer.","And presently they began to understand.","That night the star rose later, for its proper eastward motion had carried it some way across Leo towards Virgo, and its brightness was so great that the sky became a luminous blue as it rose, and every star was hidden in its turn, save only Jupiter near the zenith, Capella, Aldebaran, Sirius and the pointers of the Bear. It was very white and beautiful. In many parts of the world that night a pallid halo encircled it about. It was perceptibly larger; in the clear refractive sky of the tropics it seemed as if it were nearly a quarter the size of the moon. The frost was still on the ground in England, but the world was as brightly lit as if it were midsummer moonlight. One could see to read quite ordinary print by that cold clear light, and in the cities the lamps burnt yellow and wan.","And everywhere the world was awake that night, and throughout Christendom a sombre murmur hung in the keen air over the countryside like the belling of bees in the heather, and this murmurous tumult grew to a clangour in the cities. It was the tolling of the bells in a million belfry towers and steeples, summoning the people to sleep no more, to sin no more, but to gather in their churches and pray. And overhead, growing larger and brighter, as the earth rolled on its way and the night passed, rose the dazzling star.","And the streets and houses were alight in all the cities, the shipyards glared, and whatever roads led to high country were lit and crowded all night long. And in all the seas about the civilised lands, ships with throbbing engines, and ships with bellying sails, crowded with men and living creatures, were standing out to ocean and the north. For already the warning of the master mathematician had been telegraphed all over the world, and translated into a hundred tongues. The new planet and Neptune, locked in a fiery embrace, were whirling headlong, ever faster and faster towards the sun. Already every second this blazing mass flew a hundred miles, and every second its terrific velocity increased. As it flew now, indeed, it must pass a hundred million of miles wide of the earth and scarcely affect it. But near its destined path, as yet only slightly perturbed, spun the mighty planet Jupiter and his moons sweeping splendid round the sun. Every moment now the attraction between the fiery star and the greatest of the planets grew stronger. And the result of that attraction? Inevitably Jupiter would be deflected from its orbit into an elliptical path, and the burning star, swung by his attraction wide of its sunward rush, would \"describe a curved path\" and perhaps collide with, and certainly pass very close to, our earth. \"Earthquakes, volcanic outbreaks, cyclones, sea waves, floods, and a steady rise in temperature to I know not what limit\"—so prophesied the master mathematician.","And overhead, to carry out his words, lonely and cold and livid, blazed the star of the coming doom.","To many who stared at it that night until their eyes ached, it seemed that it was visibly approaching. And that night, too, the weather changed, and the frost that had gripped all Central Europe and France and England softened towards a thaw.","But you must not imagine because I have spoken of people praying through the night and people going aboard ships and people fleeing towards mountainous country that the whole world was already in a terror because of the star. As a matter of fact, use and wont still ruled the world, and save for the talk of idle moments and the splendour of the night, nine human beings out of ten were still busy at their common occupations. In all the cities the shops, save one here and there, opened and closed at their proper hours, the doctor and the undertaker plied their trades, the workers gathered in the factories, soldiers drilled, scholars studied, lovers sought one another, thieves lurked and fled, politicians planned their schemes. The presses of the newspapers roared through the nights, and many a priest of this church and that would not open his holy building to further what he considered a foolish panic. The newspapers insisted on the lesson of the year 1000—for then, too, people had anticipated the end. The star was no star—mere gas—a comet; and were it a star it could not possibly strike the earth. There was no precedent for such a thing. Common sense was sturdy everywhere, scornful, jesting, a little inclined to persecute the obdurate fearful. That night, at seven-fifteen by Greenwich time, the star would be at its nearest to Jupiter. Then the world would see the turn things would take. The master mathematician's grim warnings were treated by many as so much mere elaborate self-advertisement. Common sense at last, a little heated by argument, signified its unalterable convictions by going to bed. So, too, barbarism and savagery, already tired of the novelty, went about their nightly business, and save for a howling dog here and there, the beast world left the star unheeded.","And yet, when at last the watchers in the European States saw the star rise, an hour later it is true, but no larger than it had been the night before, there were still plenty awake to laugh at the master mathematician—to take the danger as if it had passed.","But hereafter the laughter ceased. The star grew—it grew with a terrible steadiness hour after hour, a little larger each hour, a little nearer the midnight zenith, and brighter and brighter, until it had turned night into a second day. Had it come straight to the earth instead of in a curved path, had it lost no velocity to Jupiter, it must have leapt the intervening gulf in a day, but as it was it took five days altogether to come by our planet. The next night it had become a third the size of the moon before it set to English eyes, and the thaw was assured. It rose over America near the size of the moon, but blinding white to look at, and hot; and a breath of hot wind blew now with its rising and gathering strength, and in Virginia, and Brazil, and down the St. Lawrence valley, it shone intermittently through a driving reek of thunder-clouds, flickering violet lightning, and hail unprecedented. In Manitoba was a thaw and devastating floods. And upon all the mountains of the earth the snow and ice began to melt that night, and all the rivers coming out of high country flowed thick and turbid, and soon—in their upper reaches—with swirling trees and the bodies of beasts and men. They rose steadily, steadily in the ghostly brilliance, and came trickling over their banks at last, behind the flying population of their valleys.","And along the coast of Argentina and up the South Atlantic the tides were higher than had ever been in the memory of man, and the storms drove the waters in many cases scores of miles inland, drowning whole cities. And so great grew the heat during the night that the rising of the sun was like the coming of a shadow. The earthquakes began and grew until all down America from the Arctic Circle to Cape Horn, hillsides were sliding, fissures were opening, and houses and walls crumbling to destruction. The whole side of Cotopaxi slipped out in one vast convulsion, and a tumult of lava poured out so high and broad and swift and liquid that in one day it reached the sea.","So the star, with the wan moon in its wake, marched across the Pacific, trailed the thunderstorms like the hem of a robe, and the growing tidal wave that toiled behind it, frothing and eager, poured over island and island and swept them clear of men. Until that wave came at last—in a blinding light and with the breath of a furnace, swift and terrible it came—a wall of water, fifty feet high, roaring hungrily, upon the long coasts of Asia, and swept inland across the plains of China. For a space the star, hotter now and larger and brighter than the sun in its strength, showed with pitiless brilliance the wide and populous country; towns and villages with their pagodas and trees, roads, wide cultivated fields, millions of sleepless people staring in helpless terror at the incandescent sky; and then, low and growing, came the murmur of the flood. And thus it was with millions of men that night—a flight nowhither, with limbs heavy with heat and breath fierce and scant, and the flood like a wall swift and white behind. And then death.","China was lit glowing white, but over Japan and Java and all the islands of Eastern Asia the great star was a ball of dull red fire because of the steam and smoke and ashes the volcanoes were spouting forth to salute its coming. Above was the lava, hot gases and ash, and below the seething floods, and the whole earth swayed and rumbled with the earthquake shocks. Soon the immemorial snows of Thibet and the Himalaya were melting and pouring down by ten million deepening converging channels upon the plains of Burmah and Hindostan. The tangled summits of the Indian jungles were aflame in a thousand places, and below the hurrying waters around the stems were dark objects that still struggled feebly and reflected the blood-red tongues of fire. And in a rudderless confusion a multitude of men and women fled down the broad river-ways to that one last hope of men—the open sea.","Larger grew the star, and larger, hotter, and brighter with a terrible swiftness now. The tropical ocean had lost its phosphorescence, and the whirling steam rose in ghostly wreaths from the black waves that plunged incessantly, speckled with storm-tossed ships.","And then came a wonder. It seemed to those who in Europe watched for the rising of the star that the world must have ceased its rotation. In a thousand open spaces of down and upland the people who had fled thither from the floods and the falling houses and sliding slopes of hill watched for that rising in vain. Hour followed hour through a terrible suspense, and the star rose not. Once again men set their eyes upon the old constellations they had counted lost to them forever. In England it was hot and clear overhead, though the ground quivered perpetually, but in the tropics, Sirius and Capella and Aldebaran showed through a veil of steam. And when at last the great star rose near ten hours late, the sun rose close upon it, and in the centre of its white heart was a disc of black.","Over Asia it was the star had begun to fall behind the movement of the sky, and then suddenly, as it hung over India, its light had been veiled. All the plain of India from the mouth of the Indus to the mouths of the Ganges was a shallow waste of shining water that night, out of which rose temples and palaces, mounds and hills, black with people. Every minaret was a clustering mass of people, who fell one by one into the turbid waters, as heat and terror overcame them. The whole land seemed a-wailing, and suddenly there swept a shadow across that furnace of despair, and a breath of cold wind, and a gathering of clouds, out of the cooling air. Men looking up, near blinded, at the star, saw that a black disc was creeping across the light. It was the moon, coming between the star and the earth. And even as men cried to God at this respite, out of the East with a strange inexplicable swiftness sprang the sun. And then star, sun and moon rushed together across the heavens.","So it was that presently, to the European watchers, star and sun rose close upon each other, drove headlong for a space and then slower, and at last came to rest, star and sun merged into one glare of flame at the zenith of the sky. The moon no longer eclipsed the star but was lost to sight in the brilliance of the sky. And though those who were still alive regarded it for the most part with that dull stupidity that hunger, fatigue, heat and despair engender, there were still men who could perceive the meaning of these signs. Star and earth had been at their nearest, had swung about one another, and the star had passed. Already it was receding, swifter and swifter, in the last stage of its headlong journey downward into the sun.","And then the clouds gathered, blotting out the vision of the sky, the thunder and lightning wove a garment round the world; all over the earth was such a downpour of rain as men had never before seen, and where the volcanoes flared red against the cloud canopy there descended torrents of mud. Everywhere the waters were pouring off the land, leaving mud-silted ruins, and the earth littered like a storm-worn beach with all that had floated, and the dead bodies of the men and brutes, its children. For days the water streamed off the land, sweeping away soil and trees and houses in the way, and piling huge dykes and scooping out Titanic gullies over the country side. Those were the days of darkness that followed the star and the heat. All through them, and for many weeks and months, the earthquakes continued.","But the star had passed, and men, hunger-driven and gathering courage only slowly, might creep back to their ruined cities, buried granaries, and sodden fields. Such few ships as had escaped the storms of that time came stunned and shattered and sounding their way cautiously through the new marks and shoals of once familiar ports. And as the storms subsided men perceived that everywhere the days were hotter than of yore, and the sun larger, and the moon, shrunk to a third of its former size, took now fourscore days between its new and new.","But of the new brotherhood that grew presently among men, of the saving of laws and books and machines, of the strange change that had come over Iceland and Greenland and the shores of Baffin's Bay, so that the sailors coming there presently found them green and gracious, and could scarce believe their eyes, this story does not tell. Nor of the movement of mankind now that the earth was hotter, northward and southward towards the poles of the earth. It concerns itself only with the coming and the passing of the Star.","The Martian astronomers—for there are astronomers on Mars, although they are very different beings from men—were naturally profoundly interested by these things. They saw them from their own standpoint of course. \"Considering the mass and temperature of the missile that was flung through our solar system into the sun,\" one wrote, \"it is astonishing what a little damage the earth, which it missed so narrowly, has sustained. All the familiar continental markings and the masses of the seas remain intact, and indeed the only difference seems to be a shrinkage of the white discolouration (supposed to be frozen water) round either pole.\" Which only shows how small the vastest of human catastrophes may seem, at a distance of a few million miles."],"glossary":{"erratic":"düzensiz, öngörülemez","retardation":"yavaşlama, gecikme","perturbed":"yörüngesi bozulmuş","impalpable":"elle tutulamayacak kadar ince","traversed":"kat edilmiş, aşılmış","apparition":"ansızın beliren şey, hayalet","leader":"başyazı","imminent":"eli kulağında, çok yakın","betimes":"erkenden, vaktinde","agape":"ağzı açık, şaşkın","jaded":"bitkin, yorgun düşmüş","pallid":"soluk, solgun","incontinently":"hemen, derhal","concussion":"şiddetli çarpışma","incandescence":"kor gibi parlama","advent":"gelişi, beliriş","compositors":"dizgiciler","feigned":"yalandan yaptı, rol yaptı","brethren":"kardeşler (eski kullanım)","phial":"küçük cam şişe","debar":"engellemek, alıkoymak","sombre":"kasvetli, hüzünlü","clangour":"çın çın gürültü","turbid":"bulanık, çamurlu","livid":"kurşuni, mosmor","obdurate":"inatçı, dik kafalı","wont":"âdet, alışkanlık","yore":"eski zamanlar","fourscore":"seksen","dykes":"bentler, setler"},"phrases":[{"en":"as cold as charity","tr":"buz gibi, hiç merhamet etmeyen soğuklukta"},{"en":"held their breath","tr":"nefeslerini tuttular"},{"en":"to and fro","tr":"ileri geri, bir aşağı bir yukarı"},{"en":"use and wont","tr":"alışkanlık ve gelenek, olagelen düzen"},{"en":"of yore","tr":"eski zamanlarda"},{"en":"as a matter of fact","tr":"aslında, gerçekte"},{"en":"in vain","tr":"boşuna, nafile"},{"en":"puzzled it out","tr":"kafa yorup çözdü"},{"en":"in its wake","tr":"peşinden, arkasından"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What first draws astronomers' attention to something unusual?","options":["A sudden bright flash in the sky","The strange motion of the planet Neptune","A message from Mars","The disappearance of the moon"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Üç gözlemevi, Neptün'ün hareketinin çok düzensizleştiğini duyurur; sonra yeni bir cisim fark edilir."},{"q":"What happens to Neptune?","options":["It disappears behind the sun","It becomes a moon of Jupiter","It is struck by the new planet and the two become one fiery mass","It splits into two smaller planets"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Yabancı gezegen Neptün'e çarpar ve iki gök cismi tek bir devasa kor kütlesine dönüşür."},{"q":"How does the master mathematician announce his calculations to his students?","options":["He tells them that man has lived in vain","He says the star is a harmless comet","He asks them to leave the city","He refuses to give his lecture"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Matematikçi, \"İnsanlık boşuna yaşadı\" diyerek hesaplarının vardığı sonucu öğrencilerine duyurur."},{"q":"According to the narrator, how do most people behave in the first days of the star?","options":["Everyone flees to the mountains","They stop working and pray all day","They destroy the observatories","Nine out of ten keep to their ordinary work and many scorn the panic"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Anlatıcıya göre insanların çoğu, alışkanlıkların etkisiyle işine devam eder; sağduyu paniği küçümser."},{"q":"At the end, how do the Martian astronomers see the event?","options":["As a terrible disaster that destroyed the earth","As a small event that did little damage to the earth","As an attack by Neptune","As a sign of life on earth"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Marslı gökbilimciler için dünya neredeyse hiç zarar görmemiştir; böylece insanlığın en büyük felaketi uzaktan küçük görünür."}],"words":4461,"length":"long"},{"id":"the-tell-tale-heart","type":"story","title":"The Tell-Tale Heart","author":"Edgar Allan Poe","year":1843,"level":"C1","genre":"horror","source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #2148","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2148","license":"Public domain"},"summaryTr":"Bir adam, yaşlı bir adamın akbaba gibi soluk mavi gözünden kurtulmak için sabırlı ve sinsi bir plan yapar; bir yandan da delirmediğine seni ikna etmeye çalışır. Ama gecenin sessizliğinde duyduğu o boğuk ses, en soğukkanlı planı bile tehlikeye sokabilir.","paragraphs":["True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.","It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.","Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work! I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him. And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently! And then, when I had made an opening sufficient for my head, I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, that no light shone out, and then I thrust in my head. Oh, you would have laughed to see how cunningly I thrust it in! I moved it slowly—very, very slowly, so that I might not disturb the old man’s sleep. It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this? And then, when my head was well in the room, I undid the lantern cautiously—oh, so cautiously—cautiously (for the hinges creaked)—I undid it just so much that a single thin ray fell upon the vulture eye. And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye. And every morning, when the day broke, I went boldly into the chamber, and spoke courageously to him, calling him by name in a hearty tone, and inquiring how he has passed the night. So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.","Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch’s minute hand moves more quickly than did mine. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph. To think that there I was, opening the door, little by little, and he not even to dream of my secret deeds or thoughts. I fairly chuckled at the idea; and perhaps he heard me; for he moved on the bed suddenly, as if startled. Now you may think that I drew back—but no. His room was as black as pitch with the thick darkness, (for the shutters were close fastened, through fear of robbers,) and so I knew that he could not see the opening of the door, and I kept pushing it on steadily, steadily.","I had my head in, and was about to open the lantern, when my thumb slipped upon the tin fastening, and the old man sprang up in bed, crying out—“Who’s there?”","I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down. He was still sitting up in the bed listening;—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.","Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew the sound well. Many a night, just at midnight, when all the world slept, it has welled up from my own bosom, deepening, with its dreadful echo, the terrors that distracted me. I say I knew it well. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart. I knew that he had been lying awake ever since the first slight noise, when he had turned in the bed. His fears had been ever since growing upon him. He had been trying to fancy them causeless, but could not. He had been saying to himself—“It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor,” or “It is merely a cricket which has made a single chirp.” Yes, he had been trying to comfort himself with these suppositions: but he had found all in vain. All in vain; because Death, in approaching him had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim. And it was the mournful influence of the unperceived shadow that caused him to feel—although he neither saw nor heard—to feel the presence of my head within the room.","When I had waited a long time, very patiently, without hearing him lie down, I resolved to open a little—a very, very little crevice in the lantern. So I opened it—you cannot imagine how stealthily, stealthily—until, at length a single dim ray, like the thread of the spider, shot from out the crevice and fell full upon the vulture eye.","It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones; but I could see nothing else of the old man’s face or person: for I had directed the ray as if by instinct, precisely upon the damned spot.","And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense?—now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man’s heart. It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.","But even yet I refrained and kept still. I scarcely breathed. I held the lantern motionless. I tried how steadily I could maintain the ray upon the eye. Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant. The old man’s terror must have been extreme! It grew louder, I say, louder every moment!—do you mark me well? I have told you that I am nervous: so I am. And now at the dead hour of the night, amid the dreadful silence of that old house, so strange a noise as this excited me to uncontrollable terror. Yet, for some minutes longer I refrained and stood still. But the beating grew louder, louder! I thought the heart must burst. And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man’s hour had come! With a loud yell, I threw open the lantern and leaped into the room. He shrieked once—once only. In an instant I dragged him to the floor, and pulled the heavy bed over him. I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done. But, for many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound. This, however, did not vex me; it would not be heard through the wall. At length it ceased. The old man was dead. I removed the bed and examined the corpse. Yes, he was stone, stone dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.","If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body. The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence. First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.","I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings. I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong. There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!","When I had made an end of these labors, it was four o’clock—still dark as midnight. As the bell sounded the hour, there came a knocking at the street door. I went down to open it with a light heart,—for what had I now to fear? There entered three men, who introduced themselves, with perfect suavity, as officers of the police. A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night; suspicion of foul play had been aroused; information had been lodged at the police office, and they (the officers) had been deputed to search the premises.","I smiled,—for what had I to fear? I bade the gentlemen welcome. The shriek, I said, was my own in a dream. The old man, I mentioned, was absent in the country. I took my visitors all over the house. I bade them search—search well. I led them, at length, to his chamber. I showed them his treasures, secure, undisturbed. In the enthusiasm of my confidence, I brought chairs into the room, and desired them here to rest from their fatigues, while I myself, in the wild audacity of my perfect triumph, placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse of the victim.","The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease. They sat, and while I answered cheerily, they chatted of familiar things. But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted. The ringing became more distinct:—it continued and became more distinct: I talked more freely to get rid of the feeling: but it continued and gained definiteness—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.","No doubt I now grew very pale;—but I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice. Yet the sound increased—and what could I do? It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not. I talked more quickly—more vehemently; but the noise steadily increased. I arose and argued about trifles, in a high key and with violent gesticulations; but the noise steadily increased. Why would they not be gone? I paced the floor to and fro with heavy strides, as if excited to fury by the observations of the men—but the noise steadily increased. Oh God! what could I do? I foamed—I raved—I swore! I swung the chair upon which I had been sitting, and grated it upon the boards, but the noise arose over all and continually increased. It grew louder—louder—louder! And still the men chatted pleasantly, and smiled. Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!—this I thought, and this I think. But anything was better than this agony! Anything was more tolerable than this derision! I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer! I felt that I must scream or die! and now—again!—hark! louder! louder! louder! louder!","“Villains!” I shrieked, “dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!”"],"glossary":{"hearken":"kulak vermek, dinlemek","hearkening":"kulak vererek, dinleyerek","vexed":"canını sıktı, sinirlendirdi","vex":"sinirlendirmek, rahatsız etmek","dissimulation":"ikiyüzlülük, niyetini gizleme","sagacity":"feraset, sezgi gücü","stealthily":"sinsice, gizlice","crevice":"dar yarık, aralık","waned":"azaldı, sona yaklaştı","scantlings":"döşeme kirişleri","suavity":"ince nezaket, yumuşak tavır","deputed":"görevlendirilmiş","reposed":"yatıyordu, dinleniyordu","audacity":"cüret, pervasızlık","wary":"temkinli, tetikte","tattoo":"ritmik vuruş, tıkırtı","dissemble":"gizlemek, rol yapmak","gesticulations":"el kol hareketleri","ere":"-den önce","bade":"buyurdu, davet etti","fancy":"sanmak, hayal etmek","dismembered":"parçalara ayırdı","pulsation":"nabız atışı","profound":"kavrayışlı, derin görüşlü","mortal":"ölümcül, ölesiye","derision":"alay, küçümseme","enveloped":"sarılmış, örtülmüş","latch":"mandal, sürgü","causeless":"sebepsiz, nedensiz"},"phrases":[{"en":"made up my mind","tr":"karar verdim"},{"en":"my blood ran cold","tr":"kanım dondu, ürperdim"},{"en":"little by little","tr":"azar azar, yavaş yavaş"},{"en":"as black as pitch","tr":"zifiri karanlık, kapkara"},{"en":"stone dead","tr":"ölüp gitmiş, kesinlikle ölü"},{"en":"foul play","tr":"cinayet şüphesi, kirli bir iş"},{"en":"get rid of","tr":"kurtulmak, başından atmak"},{"en":"ere long","tr":"çok geçmeden (eski kullanım)"},{"en":"in vain","tr":"boşuna, nafile"},{"en":"to and fro","tr":"ileri geri"}],"quiz":[{"q":"According to the narrator, why does he decide to kill the old man?","options":["He wants the old man's gold","He cannot stand the old man's pale blue eye","The old man once insulted him","The old man plans to send him away"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Anlatıcı, yaşlı adama karşı bir kini ya da altın hırsı olmadığını söyler; tek sebep akbaba gibi soluk mavi gözüdür."},{"q":"How many nights does the narrator look in on the sleeping old man before he finally acts?","options":["Three","Five","Seven","Ten"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Yedi gece boyunca gözü hep kapalı bulur; sekizinci gece ise göz açıktır ve anlatıcı harekete geçer."},{"q":"What makes the old man sit up in bed on the eighth night?","options":["A knock at the street door","The narrator's shout","A mouse crossing the floor","The narrator's thumb slipping on the lantern's fastening"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Anlatıcının başparmağı fenerin teneke kilidinden kayar; ses duyan yaşlı adam yatağında doğrulup \"Kim var orada?\" diye bağırır."},{"q":"Why do the police officers come to the house?","options":["A neighbour heard a shriek during the night","The old man had asked them to visit","They are looking for stolen gold","The narrator has reported a crime"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Bir komşu gece bir çığlık duymuş ve polise haber vermiştir; memurlar evi aramak için gelirler."},{"q":"Why does the narrator finally confess to the officers?","options":["He is afraid of the old man's family","He believes the officers can hear the heart beating and are mocking him","The officers find blood under the floor","He feels sorry for the old man"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Anlatıcı kalp atışını yalnızca kendisinin duyduğunu fark etmez; memurların da duyduğunu ama onunla alay ettiğini sanır ve dayanamayıp itiraf eder."}],"words":2148,"length":"medium"},{"id":"the-work-a-dramatic-scene-hides","type":"article","title":"The Work a Dramatic Scene Hides","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C1","genre":"drama","summaryTr":"Bir aile kavgasının veya küçük bir vedanın ağırlığı, sahnede görünmeyen emekten gelebilir. Yazı; gündelik dramada bakım, para, suskunluk ve bakış açısının ahlaki yargılarımızı nasıl şekillendirdiğini özgün kurgu örnekleriyle ele alıyor.","paragraphs":["## The visible argument and the invisible arrangement","A dramatic scene often begins when an arrangement that has operated quietly becomes impossible to maintain. A daughter arrives late with groceries; her brother remarks that the milk is warm; she puts the bags down more forcefully than necessary. On the surface, the disagreement concerns a small inconvenience. Beneath it may lie an unspoken division of work: she shops, he visits, their father thanks whichever child happens to be present. The scene acquires weight when the narrative makes that arrangement legible. Its force depends less on the volume of the argument than on the history carried by an apparently ordinary remark.","Consider an invented family preparing to sell an unused boat. The father, Tomas, regards it as the place where his children learned patience and courage. His daughter, Eva, remembers cleaning it while the others sailed. His son, Kit, remembers being too frightened to admit that he disliked deep water. None of these memories has to cancel the others. The boat becomes dramatically useful because it holds incompatible experiences within the same physical space. When Tomas calls it their happiest possession, he is describing something sincerely. He is also making a claim that his children may find difficult to recognise.","A weaker version of this scene would reveal that one character is simply lying. That would settle the disagreement by distributing truth and error. A more demanding version lets each account contain something real and something omitted. Tomas may have worked extra hours to afford the boat, while failing to notice who cleaned it. Eva may have enjoyed several trips, while resenting the expectation that she organise them. Kit may have hidden his fear so successfully that nobody could reasonably have understood it. Drama emerges from the consequences of these incomplete accounts when the family must finally make a shared decision.","As readers, we can distinguish between information the characters lack and information they prefer not to examine. Tomas cannot be blamed for knowing nothing about a fear Kit never expressed in any visible way. He can, however, be questioned if he repeatedly interrupted attempts to discuss it. Eva's anger may be justified without making every accusation accurate. This distinction keeps a scene morally active. Instead of deciding who is good and who is selfish, we consider what each person could have known, what they chose to notice, and what opportunities to respond were available to them.","## Practical labour gives emotion a measurable form","Care becomes easier to understand when a narrative gives it concrete tasks. 'Eva has always supported the family' asks the reader to accept a summary. Showing her compare repair estimates, arrange transport, and spend a lunch break calling the harbour office makes that support visible. These details need not become an inventory of suffering. Their purpose is to establish what the work requires and who has assumed responsibility for it. Once we know that Eva has made six calls, a casual request for a seventh can carry emotional force without an accompanying explanation of how exhausted she feels.","Money can serve a similar function, provided it is connected to actual choices. Suppose selling the boat would pay for repairs to Tomas's roof. Keeping it would require contributions from both children. Kit offers to cover the fee for another year, expecting his generosity to settle the matter. Eva asks who will organise maintenance and inspections. The question reveals that money and coordination are different burdens. Kit's offer is still valuable, but it does not absorb every cost. The scene becomes richer if he understands that difference slowly, rather than being instantly exposed as either a saviour or a fraud.","The distribution of inconvenience also matters. A plan can appear equal when viewed only through money and remain unequal in time. If Eva lives nearby, every delayed delivery may become her responsibility. Kit may sincerely believe that transferring half the fee means they have divided the work fairly. A scene in which he waits all afternoon for a repairer can alter his understanding more effectively than a speech about invisible labour. The inconvenience gives him access to an experience he previously understood only as a line in a message. His response then determines whether that access produces any change.","Yet practical detail should not make affection disappear into accounting. Tomas may have taught Eva a skill she still values, or listened to Kit during a difficult period. These memories need not be entered as credits that erase present responsibilities. They show why the relationship matters enough for its failures to hurt. Drama can hold gratitude and resentment together without forcing a final balance. The question is not whether Tomas has earned unlimited care. It is how these people can acknowledge what they have given one another while making a sustainable arrangement for what comes next.","## Silence has several possible meanings","Silence is not automatically depth. A character who never explains a decision may appear mysterious, but mystery alone does not make the decision emotionally convincing. In the boat story, Tomas might refuse to discuss the sale because he fears losing his last connection to his younger self. He might also be unable to understand the financial documents or ashamed of unpaid fees. Each possibility would give the same silence a different meaning. The surrounding action must help us discriminate among them, even if the narrative withholds a direct explanation for a while.","One way to make silence readable is to show where it breaks. Tomas may say almost nothing about the sale but correct a buyer who names a piece of equipment wrongly. His correction suggests that expertise remains important to his identity. He may ignore a question about price yet ask whether the buyer intends to change the boat's name. That shift directs attention towards continuity and loss. Neither response proves the whole explanation, but each supplies evidence. Readers are invited to infer from a pattern of choices rather than admire an empty space that could mean anything.","Silence can also distribute power. If Tomas refuses to answer until the sale deadline passes, his apparent withdrawal may determine the outcome as effectively as an order. Eva's response then requires more than patience. She may need to state that the current fees cannot continue and ask for a decision by a specific time. The scene should allow this boundary to be difficult without presenting it as a failure of love. Compassion does not require one character to absorb every consequence of another character's refusal. A boundary can create the conditions in which an honest conversation finally becomes possible.","At the same time, readers should examine the pressure to speak. A family meeting arranged around a vulnerable person's chair can become an interrogation, even when everyone claims to be helping. Kit might interrupt Eva's questions and suggest that Tomas inspect the boat with them before deciding. This could be a compassionate adjustment or another delay; the subsequent action will clarify which. Dramatic writing gains precision when it allows the same gesture to remain uncertain briefly and then tests it. Meaning emerges through what people do with the space they have requested, not through a label attached to the gesture.","## Point of view changes the moral balance","If the story remains close to Eva, the reader will probably experience the sale as overdue relief. We hear her calculations and remember the calls that interrupted her work. Tomas's resistance may initially look unreasonable because its private meaning is unavailable. This is not necessarily a flaw. A limited viewpoint can create a productive need to revise our judgment. When Eva discovers that Tomas has kept a notebook of every family trip, the object may complicate her account without disproving it. His attachment becomes more particular, while the cost of preserving the boat remains unchanged.","A change of viewpoint should offer more than a defence speech for the character who has looked least sympathetic. Suppose a short passage follows Kit as he travels home after the argument. He considers sending more money because it is the form of help he knows how to offer. Then he notices that he has not once asked Eva which task she wants removed. The passage adds something his confident behaviour concealed: uncertainty about how to be useful. It also establishes a choice. Insight has dramatic value only if the story eventually asks what he will do with it.","Even without entering another mind, a narrative can broaden its moral field through observation. Eva might see Tomas checking the boat's cover in the rain, despite claiming that he no longer cares what happens. She might notice Kit deleting a message before offering a practical suggestion. Such details do not give her complete access to either person. They interrupt her certainty. This is often enough. A story need not explain everyone equally in order to resist a narrow verdict; it can show that the central viewpoint is capable of learning from evidence that does not fit its first interpretation.","There is also a danger in making every perspective equally reasonable by removing the consequences of harmful behaviour. If Tomas has spent money promised for essential repairs, understanding his attachment does not restore the missing funds. If Eva has spoken cruelly, her exhaustion does not make the words harmless. Broader understanding should sharpen our account of responsibility, not dissolve it. We can recognise why someone acted and still ask for an apology, repayment, or changed access to shared money. A mature dramatic scene allows sympathy and accountability to occupy the same room.","## Resolution is a changed arrangement","The sale itself need not provide the resolution. The family could sell the boat and preserve all the resentments that made the decision painful. Conversely, they might keep it under a genuinely different arrangement. What matters is whether the central pattern changes. If Eva has always carried unnamed responsibilities, an ending in which she quietly organises one final complicated transfer may repeat the problem while calling it closure. A more convincing ending makes the new distribution visible: Kit handles the paperwork, Tomas speaks to the buyer, and Eva decides which task she can actually undertake.","A meaningful apology identifies both an action and its consequence. Tomas could say he is sorry that everyone became upset, which leaves responsibility vague. Or he could acknowledge that he allowed Eva to manage work he publicly described as a shared pleasure. The second apology is harder because it revises the story he tells about himself. Eva's answer does not have to be immediate forgiveness. She might accept the admission, correct one part of it, and continue discussing the sale. Drama often becomes more credible when emotional repair occurs alongside practical work rather than replacing it.","The final scene can preserve loss without treating loss as proof that the decision was wrong. Tomas may watch the boat leave and feel miserable, even after agreeing freely to sell it. Eva may cry over a remembered afternoon despite wanting the sale. Kit may keep the old compass without pretending it will replace the trips they never enjoyed together. These responses give the past its complexity. A sound decision does not guarantee a pleasant feeling at every moment. The narrative can honour the sadness while showing that the family is no longer using it to avoid necessary action.","When reading domestic drama, follow the arrangements beneath the speeches. Ask who performs the work, who can refuse, who knows the cost, and whose memory has become the official version. Notice when silence protects vulnerability and when it transfers a burden. Then look for an ending that alters the relationship's practice, however modestly. A roof repair booked by the person who once only offered advice may carry more weight than a final declaration of unity. The change is small enough to observe and large enough to matter because the story has taught us what it previously cost someone else."],"glossary":{"legible":"anlaşılır, okunabilir","coordination":"işlerin düzenlenmesi ve takibi","interrogation":"sorguya çekme","accountability":"davranışın sorumluluğunu üstlenme","verdict":"kesin yargı","closure":"duygusal olarak bir konuyu sonuca bağlama"},"phrases":[{"en":"on the surface","tr":"görünüşte, yüzeyde"},{"en":"division of work","tr":"iş bölümü"},{"en":"in order to","tr":"... amacıyla"},{"en":"at the same time","tr":"aynı zamanda"},{"en":"making a sustainable arrangement","tr":"sürdürülebilir bir düzen kurmak"},{"en":"a different meaning","tr":"farklı bir anlam"},{"en":"a limited viewpoint","tr":"sınırlı bir bakış açısı"},{"en":"alongside practical work","tr":"pratik işlerle birlikte"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can the boat support a complex dramatic conflict?","options":["It proves that Tomas has always been dishonest","It carries different, partly valid experiences for the family members","It removes the need for practical decisions","It has no connection to the family's present life"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Tekne her aile üyesi için farklı ve kısmen haklı anılar taşır; bu deneyimler aynı nesnede bir araya gelir."},{"q":"What does Kit's offer of money fail to address initially?","options":["The boat's colour","The existence of the harbour","The burden of organising maintenance and dealing with delays","The price of a new compass"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Mali katkı, bakım takibi ve bekleme gibi zaman ve koordinasyon yüklerini kendiliğinden ortadan kaldırmaz."},{"q":"How can Tomas's silence exercise power?","options":["By delaying a decision until the deadline determines the outcome","By revealing every financial fact clearly","By ensuring nobody has to pay any fees","By making Eva's choices unlimited"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Yanıt vermemek kararı erteleyerek sürenin dolmasına ve sonucu fiilen belirlemesine yol açabilir."},{"q":"What should greater understanding of a character's motives do?","options":["Erase all consequences of harmful behaviour","Require instant forgiveness","Make every action equally acceptable","Clarify responsibility while allowing sympathy"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Güdüleri anlamak sorumluluğu yok etmez; hem empati hem hesap verebilirlik korunabilir."},{"q":"Which ending best answers the article's central concern?","options":["Eva silently completes every remaining task","The family changes how practical responsibilities are shared","The narrator declares that no one was ever hurt","A buyer pays so much that all relationships become perfect"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Yazının temel meselesi görünmeyen yüklerin dağılımıdır; anlamlı sonuç bu dağılımı davranış düzeyinde değiştirir."}],"words":1952,"length":"medium"},{"id":"to-build-a-fire","type":"story","title":"To Build a Fire","author":"Jack London","year":1908,"level":"C1","genre":"adventure","source":{"name":"Project Gutenberg #2429","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2429","license":"Public domain"},"summaryTr":"Yukon'un dondurucu soğuğunda, yanında yalnızca köpeğiyle patikada yürüyen deneyimsiz bir adam, uyarıları umursamadan yola çıkar. Aşırı soğukla baş başa kalan adamın hayatta kalması, bir avuç çalı çırpıyla yakacağı ateşe bağlıdır.","paragraphs":["Day had broken cold and grey, exceedingly cold and grey, when the man turned aside from the main Yukon trail and climbed the high earth-bank, where a dim and little-travelled trail led eastward through the fat spruce timberland. It was a steep bank, and he paused for breath at the top, excusing the act to himself by looking at his watch. It was nine o’clock. There was no sun nor hint of sun, though there was not a cloud in the sky. It was a clear day, and yet there seemed an intangible pall over the face of things, a subtle gloom that made the day dark, and that was due to the absence of sun. This fact did not worry the man. He was used to the lack of sun. It had been days since he had seen the sun, and he knew that a few more days must pass before that cheerful orb, due south, would just peep above the sky-line and dip immediately from view.","The man flung a look back along the way he had come. The Yukon lay a mile wide and hidden under three feet of ice. On top of this ice were as many feet of snow. It was all pure white, rolling in gentle undulations where the ice-jams of the freeze-up had formed. North and south, as far as his eye could see, it was unbroken white, save for a dark hair-line that curved and twisted from around the spruce-covered island to the south, and that curved and twisted away into the north, where it disappeared behind another spruce-covered island. This dark hair-line was the trail—the main trail—that led south five hundred miles to the Chilcoot Pass, Dyea, and salt water; and that led north seventy miles to Dawson, and still on to the north a thousand miles to Nulato, and finally to St. Michael on Bering Sea, a thousand miles and half a thousand more.","But all this—the mysterious, far-reaching hairline trail, the absence of sun from the sky, the tremendous cold, and the strangeness and weirdness of it all—made no impression on the man. It was not because he was long used to it. He was a new-comer in the land, a chechaquo, and this was his first winter. The trouble with him was that he was without imagination. He was quick and alert in the things of life, but only in the things, and not in the significances. Fifty degrees below zero meant eighty odd degrees of frost. Such fact impressed him as being cold and uncomfortable, and that was all. It did not lead him to meditate upon his frailty as a creature of temperature, and upon man’s frailty in general, able only to live within certain narrow limits of heat and cold; and from there on it did not lead him to the conjectural field of immortality and man’s place in the universe. Fifty degrees below zero stood for a bite of frost that hurt and that must be guarded against by the use of mittens, ear-flaps, warm moccasins, and thick socks. Fifty degrees below zero was to him just precisely fifty degrees below zero. That there should be anything more to it than that was a thought that never entered his head.","As he turned to go on, he spat speculatively. There was a sharp, explosive crackle that startled him. He spat again. And again, in the air, before it could fall to the snow, the spittle crackled. He knew that at fifty below spittle crackled on the snow, but this spittle had crackled in the air. Undoubtedly it was colder than fifty below—how much colder he did not know. But the temperature did not matter. He was bound for the old claim on the left fork of Henderson Creek, where the boys were already. They had come over across the divide from the Indian Creek country, while he had come the roundabout way to take a look at the possibilities of getting out logs in the spring from the islands in the Yukon. He would be in to camp by six o’clock; a bit after dark, it was true, but the boys would be there, a fire would be going, and a hot supper would be ready. As for lunch, he pressed his hand against the protruding bundle under his jacket. It was also under his shirt, wrapped up in a handkerchief and lying against the naked skin. It was the only way to keep the biscuits from freezing. He smiled agreeably to himself as he thought of those biscuits, each cut open and sopped in bacon grease, and each enclosing a generous slice of fried bacon.","He plunged in among the big spruce trees. The trail was faint. A foot of snow had fallen since the last sled had passed over, and he was glad he was without a sled, travelling light. In fact, he carried nothing but the lunch wrapped in the handkerchief. He was surprised, however, at the cold. It certainly was cold, he concluded, as he rubbed his numbed nose and cheek-bones with his mittened hand. He was a warm-whiskered man, but the hair on his face did not protect the high cheek-bones and the eager nose that thrust itself aggressively into the frosty air.","At the man’s heels trotted a dog, a big native husky, the proper wolf-dog, grey-coated and without any visible or temperamental difference from its brother, the wild wolf. The animal was depressed by the tremendous cold. It knew that it was no time for travelling. Its instinct told it a truer tale than was told to the man by the man’s judgment. In reality, it was not merely colder than fifty below zero; it was colder than sixty below, than seventy below. It was seventy-five below zero. Since the freezing-point is thirty-two above zero, it meant that one hundred and seven degrees of frost obtained. The dog did not know anything about thermometers. Possibly in its brain there was no sharp consciousness of a condition of very cold such as was in the man’s brain. But the brute had its instinct. It experienced a vague but menacing apprehension that subdued it and made it slink along at the man’s heels, and that made it question eagerly every unwonted movement of the man as if expecting him to go into camp or to seek shelter somewhere and build a fire. The dog had learned fire, and it wanted fire, or else to burrow under the snow and cuddle its warmth away from the air.","The frozen moisture of its breathing had settled on its fur in a fine powder of frost, and especially were its jowls, muzzle, and eyelashes whitened by its crystalled breath. The man’s red beard and moustache were likewise frosted, but more solidly, the deposit taking the form of ice and increasing with every warm, moist breath he exhaled. Also, the man was chewing tobacco, and the muzzle of ice held his lips so rigidly that he was unable to clear his chin when he expelled the juice. The result was that a crystal beard of the colour and solidity of amber was increasing its length on his chin. If he fell down it would shatter itself, like glass, into brittle fragments. But he did not mind the appendage. It was the penalty all tobacco-chewers paid in that country, and he had been out before in two cold snaps. They had not been so cold as this, he knew, but by the spirit thermometer at Sixty Mile he knew they had been registered at fifty below and at fifty-five.","He held on through the level stretch of woods for several miles, crossed a wide flat of tussocks, and dropped down a bank to the frozen bed of a small stream. This was Henderson Creek, and he knew he was ten miles from the forks. He looked at his watch. It was ten o’clock. He was making four miles an hour, and he calculated that he would arrive at the forks at half-past twelve. He decided to celebrate that event by eating his lunch there.","The dog dropped in again at his heels, with a tail drooping discouragement, as the man swung along the creek-bed. The furrow of the old sled-trail was plainly visible, but a dozen inches of snow covered the marks of the last runners. In a month no man had come up or down that silent creek. The man held steadily on. He was not much given to thinking, and just then particularly he had nothing to think about save that he would eat lunch at the forks and that at six o’clock he would be in camp with the boys. There was nobody to talk to and, had there been, speech would have been impossible because of the ice-muzzle on his mouth. So he continued monotonously to chew tobacco and to increase the length of his amber beard.","Once in a while the thought reiterated itself that it was very cold and that he had never experienced such cold. As he walked along he rubbed his cheek-bones and nose with the back of his mittened hand. He did this automatically, now and again changing hands. But rub as he would, the instant he stopped his cheek-bones went numb, and the following instant the end of his nose went numb. He was sure to frost his cheeks; he knew that, and experienced a pang of regret that he had not devised a nose-strap of the sort Bud wore in cold snaps. Such a strap passed across the cheeks, as well, and saved them. But it didn’t matter much, after all. What were frosted cheeks? A bit painful, that was all; they were never serious.","Empty as the man’s mind was of thoughts, he was keenly observant, and he noticed the changes in the creek, the curves and bends and timber-jams, and always he sharply noted where he placed his feet. Once, coming around a bend, he shied abruptly, like a startled horse, curved away from the place where he had been walking, and retreated several paces back along the trail. The creek he knew was frozen clear to the bottom—no creek could contain water in that arctic winter—but he knew also that there were springs that bubbled out from the hillsides and ran along under the snow and on top the ice of the creek. He knew that the coldest snaps never froze these springs, and he knew likewise their danger. They were traps. They hid pools of water under the snow that might be three inches deep, or three feet. Sometimes a skin of ice half an inch thick covered them, and in turn was covered by the snow. Sometimes there were alternate layers of water and ice-skin, so that when one broke through he kept on breaking through for a while, sometimes wetting himself to the waist.","That was why he had shied in such panic. He had felt the give under his feet and heard the crackle of a snow-hidden ice-skin. And to get his feet wet in such a temperature meant trouble and danger. At the very least it meant delay, for he would be forced to stop and build a fire, and under its protection to bare his feet while he dried his socks and moccasins. He stood and studied the creek-bed and its banks, and decided that the flow of water came from the right. He reflected awhile, rubbing his nose and cheeks, then skirted to the left, stepping gingerly and testing the footing for each step. Once clear of the danger, he took a fresh chew of tobacco and swung along at his four-mile gait.","In the course of the next two hours he came upon several similar traps. Usually the snow above the hidden pools had a sunken, candied appearance that advertised the danger. Once again, however, he had a close call; and once, suspecting danger, he compelled the dog to go on in front. The dog did not want to go. It hung back until the man shoved it forward, and then it went quickly across the white, unbroken surface. Suddenly it broke through, floundered to one side, and got away to firmer footing. It had wet its forefeet and legs, and almost immediately the water that clung to it turned to ice. It made quick efforts to lick the ice off its legs, then dropped down in the snow and began to bite out the ice that had formed between the toes. This was a matter of instinct. To permit the ice to remain would mean sore feet. It did not know this. It merely obeyed the mysterious prompting that arose from the deep crypts of its being. But the man knew, having achieved a judgment on the subject, and he removed the mitten from his right hand and helped tear out the ice-particles. He did not expose his fingers more than a minute, and was astonished at the swift numbness that smote them. It certainly was cold. He pulled on the mitten hastily, and beat the hand savagely across his chest.","At twelve o’clock the day was at its brightest. Yet the sun was too far south on its winter journey to clear the horizon. The bulge of the earth intervened between it and Henderson Creek, where the man walked under a clear sky at noon and cast no shadow. At half-past twelve, to the minute, he arrived at the forks of the creek. He was pleased at the speed he had made. If he kept it up, he would certainly be with the boys by six. He unbuttoned his jacket and shirt and drew forth his lunch. The action consumed no more than a quarter of a minute, yet in that brief moment the numbness laid hold of the exposed fingers. He did not put the mitten on, but, instead, struck the fingers a dozen sharp smashes against his leg. Then he sat down on a snow-covered log to eat. The sting that followed upon the striking of his fingers against his leg ceased so quickly that he was startled, he had had no chance to take a bite of biscuit. He struck the fingers repeatedly and returned them to the mitten, baring the other hand for the purpose of eating. He tried to take a mouthful, but the ice-muzzle prevented. He had forgotten to build a fire and thaw out. He chuckled at his foolishness, and as he chuckled he noted the numbness creeping into the exposed fingers. Also, he noted that the stinging which had first come to his toes when he sat down was already passing away. He wondered whether the toes were warm or numbed. He moved them inside the moccasins and decided that they were numbed.","He pulled the mitten on hurriedly and stood up. He was a bit frightened. He stamped up and down until the stinging returned into the feet. It certainly was cold, was his thought. That man from Sulphur Creek had spoken the truth when telling how cold it sometimes got in the country. And he had laughed at him at the time! That showed one must not be too sure of things. There was no mistake about it, it was cold. He strode up and down, stamping his feet and threshing his arms, until reassured by the returning warmth. Then he got out matches and proceeded to make a fire. From the undergrowth, where high water of the previous spring had lodged a supply of seasoned twigs, he got his firewood. Working carefully from a small beginning, he soon had a roaring fire, over which he thawed the ice from his face and in the protection of which he ate his biscuits. For the moment the cold of space was outwitted. The dog took satisfaction in the fire, stretching out close enough for warmth and far enough away to escape being singed.","When the man had finished, he filled his pipe and took his comfortable time over a smoke. Then he pulled on his mittens, settled the ear-flaps of his cap firmly about his ears, and took the creek trail up the left fork. The dog was disappointed and yearned back toward the fire. This man did not know cold. Possibly all the generations of his ancestry had been ignorant of cold, of real cold, of cold one hundred and seven degrees below freezing-point. But the dog knew; all its ancestry knew, and it had inherited the knowledge. And it knew that it was not good to walk abroad in such fearful cold. It was the time to lie snug in a hole in the snow and wait for a curtain of cloud to be drawn across the face of outer space whence this cold came. On the other hand, there was keen intimacy between the dog and the man. The one was the toil-slave of the other, and the only caresses it had ever received were the caresses of the whip-lash and of harsh and menacing throat-sounds that threatened the whip-lash. So the dog made no effort to communicate its apprehension to the man. It was not concerned in the welfare of the man; it was for its own sake that it yearned back toward the fire. But the man whistled, and spoke to it with the sound of whip-lashes, and the dog swung in at the man’s heels and followed after.","The man took a chew of tobacco and proceeded to start a new amber beard. Also, his moist breath quickly powdered with white his moustache, eyebrows, and lashes. There did not seem to be so many springs on the left fork of the Henderson, and for half an hour the man saw no signs of any. And then it happened. At a place where there were no signs, where the soft, unbroken snow seemed to advertise solidity beneath, the man broke through. It was not deep. He wetted himself half-way to the knees before he floundered out to the firm crust.","He was angry, and cursed his luck aloud. He had hoped to get into camp with the boys at six o’clock, and this would delay him an hour, for he would have to build a fire and dry out his foot-gear. This was imperative at that low temperature—he knew that much; and he turned aside to the bank, which he climbed. On top, tangled in the underbrush about the trunks of several small spruce trees, was a high-water deposit of dry firewood—sticks and twigs principally, but also larger portions of seasoned branches and fine, dry, last-year’s grasses. He threw down several large pieces on top of the snow. This served for a foundation and prevented the young flame from drowning itself in the snow it otherwise would melt. The flame he got by touching a match to a small shred of birch-bark that he took from his pocket. This burned even more readily than paper. Placing it on the foundation, he fed the young flame with wisps of dry grass and with the tiniest dry twigs.","He worked slowly and carefully, keenly aware of his danger. Gradually, as the flame grew stronger, he increased the size of the twigs with which he fed it. He squatted in the snow, pulling the twigs out from their entanglement in the brush and feeding directly to the flame. He knew there must be no failure. When it is seventy-five below zero, a man must not fail in his first attempt to build a fire—that is, if his feet are wet. If his feet are dry, and he fails, he can run along the trail for half a mile and restore his circulation. But the circulation of wet and freezing feet cannot be restored by running when it is seventy-five below. No matter how fast he runs, the wet feet will freeze the harder.","All this the man knew. The old-timer on Sulphur Creek had told him about it the previous fall, and now he was appreciating the advice. Already all sensation had gone out of his feet. To build the fire he had been forced to remove his mittens, and the fingers had quickly gone numb. His pace of four miles an hour had kept his heart pumping blood to the surface of his body and to all the extremities. But the instant he stopped, the action of the pump eased down. The cold of space smote the unprotected tip of the planet, and he, being on that unprotected tip, received the full force of the blow. The blood of his body recoiled before it. The blood was alive, like the dog, and like the dog it wanted to hide away and cover itself up from the fearful cold. So long as he walked four miles an hour, he pumped that blood, willy-nilly, to the surface; but now it ebbed away and sank down into the recesses of his body. The extremities were the first to feel its absence. His wet feet froze the faster, and his exposed fingers numbed the faster, though they had not yet begun to freeze. Nose and cheeks were already freezing, while the skin of all his body chilled as it lost its blood.","But he was safe. Toes and nose and cheeks would be only touched by the frost, for the fire was beginning to burn with strength. He was feeding it with twigs the size of his finger. In another minute he would be able to feed it with branches the size of his wrist, and then he could remove his wet foot-gear, and, while it dried, he could keep his naked feet warm by the fire, rubbing them at first, of course, with snow. The fire was a success. He was safe. He remembered the advice of the old-timer on Sulphur Creek, and smiled. The old-timer had been very serious in laying down the law that no man must travel alone in the Klondike after fifty below. Well, here he was; he had had the accident; he was alone; and he had saved himself. Those old-timers were rather womanish, some of them, he thought. All a man had to do was to keep his head, and he was all right. Any man who was a man could travel alone. But it was surprising, the rapidity with which his cheeks and nose were freezing. And he had not thought his fingers could go lifeless in so short a time. Lifeless they were, for he could scarcely make them move together to grip a twig, and they seemed remote from his body and from him. When he touched a twig, he had to look and see whether or not he had hold of it. The wires were pretty well down between him and his finger-ends.","All of which counted for little. There was the fire, snapping and crackling and promising life with every dancing flame. He started to untie his moccasins. They were coated with ice; the thick German socks were like sheaths of iron half-way to the knees; and the moccasin strings were like rods of steel all twisted and knotted as by some conflagration. For a moment he tugged with his numbed fingers, then, realizing the folly of it, he drew his sheath-knife.","But before he could cut the strings, it happened. It was his own fault or, rather, his mistake. He should not have built the fire under the spruce tree. He should have built it in the open. But it had been easier to pull the twigs from the brush and drop them directly on the fire. Now the tree under which he had done this carried a weight of snow on its boughs. No wind had blown for weeks, and each bough was fully freighted. Each time he had pulled a twig he had communicated a slight agitation to the tree—an imperceptible agitation, so far as he was concerned, but an agitation sufficient to bring about the disaster. High up in the tree one bough capsized its load of snow. This fell on the boughs beneath, capsizing them. This process continued, spreading out and involving the whole tree. It grew like an avalanche, and it descended without warning upon the man and the fire, and the fire was blotted out! Where it had burned was a mantle of fresh and disordered snow.","The man was shocked. It was as though he had just heard his own sentence of death. For a moment he sat and stared at the spot where the fire had been. Then he grew very calm. Perhaps the old-timer on Sulphur Creek was right. If he had only had a trail-mate he would have been in no danger now. The trail-mate could have built the fire. Well, it was up to him to build the fire over again, and this second time there must be no failure. Even if he succeeded, he would most likely lose some toes. His feet must be badly frozen by now, and there would be some time before the second fire was ready.","Such were his thoughts, but he did not sit and think them. He was busy all the time they were passing through his mind, he made a new foundation for a fire, this time in the open; where no treacherous tree could blot it out. Next, he gathered dry grasses and tiny twigs from the high-water flotsam. He could not bring his fingers together to pull them out, but he was able to gather them by the handful. In this way he got many rotten twigs and bits of green moss that were undesirable, but it was the best he could do. He worked methodically, even collecting an armful of the larger branches to be used later when the fire gathered strength. And all the while the dog sat and watched him, a certain yearning wistfulness in its eyes, for it looked upon him as the fire-provider, and the fire was slow in coming.","When all was ready, the man reached in his pocket for a second piece of birch-bark. He knew the bark was there, and, though he could not feel it with his fingers, he could hear its crisp rustling as he fumbled for it. Try as he would, he could not clutch hold of it. And all the time, in his consciousness, was the knowledge that each instant his feet were freezing. This thought tended to put him in a panic, but he fought against it and kept calm. He pulled on his mittens with his teeth, and threshed his arms back and forth, beating his hands with all his might against his sides. He did this sitting down, and he stood up to do it; and all the while the dog sat in the snow, its wolf-brush of a tail curled around warmly over its forefeet, its sharp wolf-ears pricked forward intently as it watched the man. And the man as he beat and threshed with his arms and hands, felt a great surge of envy as he regarded the creature that was warm and secure in its natural covering.","After a time he was aware of the first far-away signals of sensation in his beaten fingers. The faint tingling grew stronger till it evolved into a stinging ache that was excruciating, but which the man hailed with satisfaction. He stripped the mitten from his right hand and fetched forth the birch-bark. The exposed fingers were quickly going numb again. Next he brought out his bunch of sulphur matches. But the tremendous cold had already driven the life out of his fingers. In his effort to separate one match from the others, the whole bunch fell in the snow. He tried to pick it out of the snow, but failed. The dead fingers could neither touch nor clutch. He was very careful. He drove the thought of his freezing feet; and nose, and cheeks, out of his mind, devoting his whole soul to the matches. He watched, using the sense of vision in place of that of touch, and when he saw his fingers on each side the bunch, he closed them—that is, he willed to close them, for the wires were drawn, and the fingers did not obey. He pulled the mitten on the right hand, and beat it fiercely against his knee. Then, with both mittened hands, he scooped the bunch of matches, along with much snow, into his lap. Yet he was no better off.","After some manipulation he managed to get the bunch between the heels of his mittened hands. In this fashion he carried it to his mouth. The ice crackled and snapped when by a violent effort he opened his mouth. He drew the lower jaw in, curled the upper lip out of the way, and scraped the bunch with his upper teeth in order to separate a match. He succeeded in getting one, which he dropped on his lap. He was no better off. He could not pick it up. Then he devised a way. He picked it up in his teeth and scratched it on his leg. Twenty times he scratched before he succeeded in lighting it. As it flamed he held it with his teeth to the birch-bark. But the burning brimstone went up his nostrils and into his lungs, causing him to cough spasmodically. The match fell into the snow and went out.","The old-timer on Sulphur Creek was right, he thought in the moment of controlled despair that ensued: after fifty below, a man should travel with a partner. He beat his hands, but failed in exciting any sensation. Suddenly he bared both hands, removing the mittens with his teeth. He caught the whole bunch between the heels of his hands. His arm-muscles not being frozen enabled him to press the hand-heels tightly against the matches. Then he scratched the bunch along his leg. It flared into flame, seventy sulphur matches at once! There was no wind to blow them out. He kept his head to one side to escape the strangling fumes, and held the blazing bunch to the birch-bark. As he so held it, he became aware of sensation in his hand. His flesh was burning. He could smell it. Deep down below the surface he could feel it. The sensation developed into pain that grew acute. And still he endured it, holding the flame of the matches clumsily to the bark that would not light readily because his own burning hands were in the way, absorbing most of the flame.","At last, when he could endure no more, he jerked his hands apart. The blazing matches fell sizzling into the snow, but the birch-bark was alight. He began laying dry grasses and the tiniest twigs on the flame. He could not pick and choose, for he had to lift the fuel between the heels of his hands. Small pieces of rotten wood and green moss clung to the twigs, and he bit them off as well as he could with his teeth. He cherished the flame carefully and awkwardly. It meant life, and it must not perish. The withdrawal of blood from the surface of his body now made him begin to shiver, and he grew more awkward. A large piece of green moss fell squarely on the little fire. He tried to poke it out with his fingers, but his shivering frame made him poke too far, and he disrupted the nucleus of the little fire, the burning grasses and tiny twigs separating and scattering. He tried to poke them together again, but in spite of the tenseness of the effort, his shivering got away with him, and the twigs were hopelessly scattered. Each twig gushed a puff of smoke and went out. The fire-provider had failed. As he looked apathetically about him, his eyes chanced on the dog, sitting across the ruins of the fire from him, in the snow, making restless, hunching movements, slightly lifting one forefoot and then the other, shifting its weight back and forth on them with wistful eagerness.","The sight of the dog put a wild idea into his head. He remembered the tale of the man, caught in a blizzard, who killed a steer and crawled inside the carcass, and so was saved. He would kill the dog and bury his hands in the warm body until the numbness went out of them. Then he could build another fire. He spoke to the dog, calling it to him; but in his voice was a strange note of fear that frightened the animal, who had never known the man to speak in such way before. Something was the matter, and its suspicious nature sensed danger,—it knew not what danger but somewhere, somehow, in its brain arose an apprehension of the man. It flattened its ears down at the sound of the man’s voice, and its restless, hunching movements and the liftings and shiftings of its forefeet became more pronounced but it would not come to the man. He got on his hands and knees and crawled toward the dog. This unusual posture again excited suspicion, and the animal sidled mincingly away.","The man sat up in the snow for a moment and struggled for calmness. Then he pulled on his mittens, by means of his teeth, and got upon his feet. He glanced down at first in order to assure himself that he was really standing up, for the absence of sensation in his feet left him unrelated to the earth. His erect position in itself started to drive the webs of suspicion from the dog’s mind; and when he spoke peremptorily, with the sound of whip-lashes in his voice, the dog rendered its customary allegiance and came to him. As it came within reaching distance, the man lost his control. His arms flashed out to the dog, and he experienced genuine surprise when he discovered that his hands could not clutch, that there was neither bend nor feeling in the fingers. He had forgotten for the moment that they were frozen and that they were freezing more and more. All this happened quickly, and before the animal could get away, he encircled its body with his arms. He sat down in the snow, and in this fashion held the dog, while it snarled and whined and struggled.","But it was all he could do, hold its body encircled in his arms and sit there. He realized that he could not kill the dog. There was no way to do it. With his helpless hands he could neither draw nor hold his sheath-knife nor throttle the animal. He released it, and it plunged wildly away, with tail between its legs, and still snarling. It halted forty feet away and surveyed him curiously, with ears sharply pricked forward. The man looked down at his hands in order to locate them, and found them hanging on the ends of his arms. It struck him as curious that one should have to use his eyes in order to find out where his hands were. He began threshing his arms back and forth, beating the mittened hands against his sides. He did this for five minutes, violently, and his heart pumped enough blood up to the surface to put a stop to his shivering. But no sensation was aroused in the hands. He had an impression that they hung like weights on the ends of his arms, but when he tried to run the impression down, he could not find it.","A certain fear of death, dull and oppressive, came to him. This fear quickly became poignant as he realized that it was no longer a mere matter of freezing his fingers and toes, or of losing his hands and feet, but that it was a matter of life and death with the chances against him. This threw him into a panic, and he turned and ran up the creek-bed along the old, dim trail. The dog joined in behind and kept up with him. He ran blindly, without intention, in fear such as he had never known in his life. Slowly, as he ploughed and floundered through the snow, he began to see things again—the banks of the creek, the old timber-jams, the leafless aspens, and the sky. The running made him feel better. He did not shiver. Maybe, if he ran on, his feet would thaw out; and, anyway, if he ran far enough, he would reach camp and the boys. Without doubt he would lose some fingers and toes and some of his face; but the boys would take care of him, and save the rest of him when he got there. And at the same time there was another thought in his mind that said he would never get to the camp and the boys; that it was too many miles away, that the freezing had too great a start on him, and that he would soon be stiff and dead. This thought he kept in the background and refused to consider. Sometimes it pushed itself forward and demanded to be heard, but he thrust it back and strove to think of other things.","It struck him as curious that he could run at all on feet so frozen that he could not feel them when they struck the earth and took the weight of his body. He seemed to himself to skim along above the surface and to have no connection with the earth. Somewhere he had once seen a winged Mercury, and he wondered if Mercury felt as he felt when skimming over the earth.","His theory of running until he reached camp and the boys had one flaw in it: he lacked the endurance. Several times he stumbled, and finally he tottered, crumpled up, and fell. When he tried to rise, he failed. He must sit and rest, he decided, and next time he would merely walk and keep on going. As he sat and regained his breath, he noted that he was feeling quite warm and comfortable. He was not shivering, and it even seemed that a warm glow had come to his chest and trunk. And yet, when he touched his nose or cheeks, there was no sensation. Running would not thaw them out. Nor would it thaw out his hands and feet. Then the thought came to him that the frozen portions of his body must be extending. He tried to keep this thought down, to forget it, to think of something else; he was aware of the panicky feeling that it caused, and he was afraid of the panic. But the thought asserted itself, and persisted, until it produced a vision of his body totally frozen. This was too much, and he made another wild run along the trail. Once he slowed down to a walk, but the thought of the freezing extending itself made him run again.","And all the time the dog ran with him, at his heels. When he fell down a second time, it curled its tail over its forefeet and sat in front of him facing him curiously eager and intent. The warmth and security of the animal angered him, and he cursed it till it flattened down its ears appeasingly. This time the shivering came more quickly upon the man. He was losing in his battle with the frost. It was creeping into his body from all sides. The thought of it drove him on, but he ran no more than a hundred feet, when he staggered and pitched headlong. It was his last panic. When he had recovered his breath and control, he sat up and entertained in his mind the conception of meeting death with dignity. However, the conception did not come to him in such terms. His idea of it was that he had been making a fool of himself, running around like a chicken with its head cut off—such was the simile that occurred to him. Well, he was bound to freeze anyway, and he might as well take it decently. With this new-found peace of mind came the first glimmerings of drowsiness. A good idea, he thought, to sleep off to death. It was like taking an anæsthetic. Freezing was not so bad as people thought. There were lots worse ways to die.","He pictured the boys finding his body next day. Suddenly he found himself with them, coming along the trail and looking for himself. And, still with them, he came around a turn in the trail and found himself lying in the snow. He did not belong with himself any more, for even then he was out of himself, standing with the boys and looking at himself in the snow. It certainly was cold, was his thought. When he got back to the States he could tell the folks what real cold was. He drifted on from this to a vision of the old-timer on Sulphur Creek. He could see him quite clearly, warm and comfortable, and smoking a pipe.","“You were right, old hoss; you were right,” the man mumbled to the old-timer of Sulphur Creek.","Then the man drowsed off into what seemed to him the most comfortable and satisfying sleep he had ever known. The dog sat facing him and waiting. The brief day drew to a close in a long, slow twilight. There were no signs of a fire to be made, and, besides, never in the dog’s experience had it known a man to sit like that in the snow and make no fire. As the twilight drew on, its eager yearning for the fire mastered it, and with a great lifting and shifting of forefeet, it whined softly, then flattened its ears down in anticipation of being chidden by the man. But the man remained silent. Later, the dog whined loudly. And still later it crept close to the man and caught the scent of death. This made the animal bristle and back away. A little longer it delayed, howling under the stars that leaped and danced and shone brightly in the cold sky. Then it turned and trotted up the trail in the direction of the camp it knew, where were the other food-providers and fire-providers."],"glossary":{"spruce":"ladin ağacı","chechaquo":"çaylak, yeni gelen (Yukon argosu)","claim":"altın arama sahası, maden hakkı","divide":"su ayrım çizgisi, sırt","forks":"derenin çatallandığı yer","mittens":"tek parmaklı kalın eldiven","moccasins":"mokasen (yumuşak deri ayakkabı)","husky":"kızak köpeği","frost":"don; sıfırın altındaki derece","amber":"kehribar","jowls":"çene ve yanaklar","muzzle":"ağız-burun bölgesi; ağzı saran buz","gingerly":"ihtiyatla, çok dikkatli","skirted":"kenarından dolaştı","floundered":"çırpına çırpına ilerledi","smote":"vurdu, çarptı","imperative":"şart, zorunlu","flotsam":"suyun sürüklediği döküntü","capsized":"devrildi, ters döndü","boughs":"kalın ağaç dalları","brimstone":"kükürt","excruciating":"dayanılmaz derecede acı veren","wistful":"özlem dolu, hasret dolu","sidled":"yan yan, çekinerek yürüdü","peremptorily":"buyurgan bir sesle","poignant":"yakıcı, içe işleyen","apprehension":"kaygı, tedirginlik","extremities":"uç kısımlar (el, ayak, burun)","pall":"kasvetli örtü"},"phrases":[{"en":"close call","tr":"ucuz kurtulma, kıl payı kurtuluş"},{"en":"now and again","tr":"ara sıra"},{"en":"willy-nilly","tr":"istesin istemesin, mecburen"},{"en":"keep his head","tr":"soğukkanlılığını korumak"},{"en":"it was up to him","tr":"iş ona kalmıştı, sorumluluk ondaydı"},{"en":"no better off","tr":"durumu hiç de iyi değil"},{"en":"making a fool of himself","tr":"kendini rezil etmek, saçma sapan davranmak"},{"en":"like a chicken with its head cut off","tr":"başı kesilmiş tavuk gibi, çılgınca telaşla"},{"en":"kept up with him","tr":"ona ayak uydurdu"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the man's first fire go out?","options":["A strong wind blows it out","Snow falls from the spruce tree onto it","The dog knocks over the sticks","His matches get wet"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Adam ateşi ladin ağacının altında yakar; çalı çırpı çektikçe ağaç sarsılır ve dallardaki kar ateşin üzerine yığılır."},{"q":"Why does the dog seem uneasy about travelling in the extreme cold?","options":["It is hungry and wants the man's biscuits","The man has hurt it before","Its instinct warns it that the cold is dangerous","It knows the trail is blocked"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Köpeğin içgüdüsü, bu soğukta yolculuk yapılmaması gerektiğini söyler; adamın ise sağduyusu yoktur."},{"q":"How does the man's attitude toward the old-timer's warning about travelling alone change?","options":["At first he thinks it is exaggerated, but later he admits the old man was right","He follows the advice carefully from the start","He ignores it and never thinks of it again","He asks the dog for advice"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Adam başta Sulphur Creek'teki yaşlının uyarısını abartılı bulur; başı belaya girince yaşlının haklı olduğunu kabul eder."},{"q":"What does the man try to do to the dog when his hands are frozen?","options":["Send it to the camp for help","Tie his hands to its fur","Share his biscuits with it","Kill it and warm his hands inside its body"],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Adam, köpeği öldürüp ellerini sıcak gövdesine gömmeyi düşünür; ama donmuş elleriyle bunu başaramaz."},{"q":"How does the story end?","options":["The man reaches the camp and the boys save him","The man builds a third fire and survives","The man drifts into a comfortable sleep and freezes to death","The man kills the dog and wears its skin"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Adam koşmaktan yorulur, ölümü onurla karşılamaya karar verir ve huzurlu bir uykuyla donarak ölür; köpek kampa doğru yola çıkar."}],"words":7184,"length":"long"},{"id":"speculation-under-constraint","type":"article","title":"Speculation Under Constraint","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C2","genre":"scifi","summaryTr":"Bilimkurmacada icat edilmiş bir teknoloji, sınırları ve toplumsal sonuçları sayesinde nasıl anlamlı bir hikâyeye dönüşür? Deneme; özgün kurmaca örneklerle açıklama, karakter seçimi, bakış açısı ve teknolojiye verilen ahlaki anlam arasındaki ilişkiyi ele alır.","paragraphs":["## A premise becomes a system of consequences","A speculative premise is an invitation to imagine a difference. Perhaps a city can store yesterday's weather, or a spacecraft can exchange mass with a distant warehouse. Either invention might produce striking images. Yet an image does not establish a narrative. For that, the difference must enter a system of consequences: someone relies on it, someone controls access to it, and someone encounters a limit that matters. The reader begins to understand the invented world through the obligations it creates, rather than through a catalogue of extraordinary equipment.","Consider a wholly fictional coastal town whose reservoir can store a limited number of hours of rain. The device is an invention for this discussion, not a proposal about real technology. During a drought, the council releases stored rain to protect food crops. Doing so empties the reserve that usually supplies the town's cooling gardens. A technician discovers that the remaining capacity cannot serve both uses. The premise has become dramatic because the same mechanism supports competing needs. Nobody has to be foolish or malicious for a conflict to arise.","The mechanism needs enough definition for readers to assess the choices. How quickly can rain be stored? Does it decay? Can capacity be expanded during the emergency? If the story postpones every answer until the climax, the eventual solution may feel arbitrary. However, exhaustive technical explanation is unnecessary. Readers need the constraints that will govern action. A single failed attempt to store a brief shower might communicate the intake limit more effectively than a page describing the fictional device's construction. The limit becomes memorable because somebody has already encountered it.","When reading, formulate the premise as a conditional statement and then trace its consequences. If stored rain occupies finite capacity, keeping one reserve may require surrendering another. If access is centrally controlled, arguments about weather become arguments about authority. This exercise moves beyond asking whether an invention is impressive. It asks which relationships the invention changes. Strong speculative writing often develops its world most clearly at the point where a technical possibility becomes an obligation that a character would prefer not to have.","## Constraints must survive the climax","A narrative constraint is a promise that the story will take certain limitations seriously. If the reservoir cannot create rain, a final scene in which it suddenly produces an unlimited supply requires substantial preparation. Otherwise, the apparent problem has been dissolved by changing the rules rather than addressed through action. This does not mean characters must fail. An inventive solution can combine established capacities in an unexpected way. Its satisfaction comes from recognising that the possibility existed within the system, even when the characters had not previously seen it.","Suppose the technician discovers that unused household tanks can receive smaller releases. This may distribute water more effectively, but it should not make the total shortage disappear. The plan might save particular gardens while requiring residents to carry water, surrender private storage or accept uneven coverage. A solution becomes richer when it transfers effort or risk instead of abolishing cost. Readers can then evaluate the arrangement as an arrangement among people. The invention creates possibilities, but those possibilities still require negotiation, labour and decisions about whose inconvenience is acceptable.","A useful distinction separates a limit from an unexplored feature. A limit restricts what can happen. An unexplored feature might offer another capability if someone thinks to use it. Stories sometimes present an obstacle as an absolute limit, then reveal that it was merely a feature nobody had mentioned. That reversal can feel unearned. To test an ending, return to the earlier scenes and ask whether they contain the ingredients of the solution, including its price. Preparation need not announce the answer; it should make the answer intelligible in retrospect.","Constraints can also conflict with one another. A method that preserves water may consume the electricity needed to operate the reservoir valves. Another method may protect the valves while making a neighbourhood inaccessible. Such interactions prevent the technology from functioning as a single magical obstacle with a single correct response. They also give different specialists legitimate perspectives. The engineer, gardener and carer may each notice a consequence the others overlook. Expertise becomes situated knowledge rather than a badge that entitles one character to settle every question.","## A technical decision contains a social arrangement","In our invented town, the instruction to preserve the most valuable crops appears practical until somebody asks how value is measured. Sale price, nutritional use, cultural significance and the survival of small growers could produce different rankings. The device does not answer that question. A computer might implement the chosen ranking consistently, but consistency would not make the underlying judgement neutral. Science fiction can make this distinction visible by showing how apparently technical settings carry earlier decisions about what deserves protection and who is authorised to decide.","An effective story need not convert this conflict into a debate between a humane individual and a heartless machine. The machine may do exactly what it was designed to do, while several humane individuals disagree about the design's priorities. A hospital administrator may defend cooling gardens because vulnerable patients use them. A grower may argue that losing a harvest will leave families without income. Both positions can be sincere. The difficulty lies in the distribution of harm, not in identifying the person who lacks compassion.","Point of view determines which consequences initially appear central. Through the technician's eyes, an unstable valve may dominate the scene. Through a resident's eyes, the same delay may mean carrying an exhausted parent upstairs to a cooler room. A story can retain one viewpoint while allowing other people to interrupt its assumptions. Dialogue, practical resistance and requests for explanation can widen the moral field without introducing an omniscient lecture. The viewpoint character's expertise remains useful, but the narrative need not endorse every priority that accompanies it.","For a close reading, track who is allowed to define the problem. Does the technician describe residents as obstacles to an efficient plan? Do residents have opportunities to explain what the plan overlooks? Who can refuse, and who must endure the result? These questions reveal the social structure carried by the speculative premise. They are especially useful when a story presents sacrifice as inevitable. Some scarcity may be built into the invented mechanism; the allocation of that scarcity may still be a choice made by identifiable people.","## Explanation should arrive where it changes interpretation","Readers need information at the point where it enables meaningful inference. If a valve can open only once after overheating, that fact matters before the protagonist chooses to use it. Explaining it afterwards would turn a decision into a surprise imposed retrospectively. Conversely, the complete history of valve manufacturing may not matter at all. Exposition earns its place when it changes what the reader can anticipate, understand or judge. The appropriate amount is therefore related to the scene's decisions, not to the writer's enthusiasm for the invented system.","One efficient method is to make explanation answer a reasonable question from another character. A resident asks why the reservoir cannot collect the cloud overhead. The technician explains that the intake needs a particular pressure difference, then demonstrates that the current reading is insufficient. The answer clarifies a rule, reveals a limitation in public understanding and exposes the technician to further questions. It becomes part of a relationship. A conversation in which everyone asks only the questions needed to deliver background information, however, will still feel artificial.","Technical vocabulary should create distinctions that ordinary vocabulary cannot make as economically. If two kinds of storage behave differently, separate terms may help readers follow a later choice. If five invented terms all mean approximately 'power', they may obscure the problem without enriching the world. When encountering an unfamiliar word, ask what work it performs. Can its meaning be inferred from action? Does the distinction return? A reader need not memorise every label to understand the conflict, provided the prose consistently connects specialised language with observable consequences.","At an advanced language level, causal and concessive structures deserve particular attention. Sentences beginning with provided that, even if or insofar as delimit an argument. A plan may work provided that residents surrender private tanks; it may remain unjust even if it increases total water access. These structures prevent a qualified claim from being mistaken for an absolute one. They also give speculative prose much of its intellectual texture. The world is not merely described; proposals about the world are tested, limited and revised within the syntax itself.","## An ending should preserve the cost of its solution","Imagine that the town survives by sharing household tanks, restricting crop irrigation and converting part of the reservoir's control hall into a public cooling space. The practical emergency is reduced. Yet the ending would feel evasive if every resident immediately agreed that the allocation had been fair. Some growers have lost crops. Some households contributed more space than others. An ending can acknowledge these differences without reopening every decision. Resolution concerns what has been done; evaluation concerns what that action means. The two processes need not finish at the same moment.","A final scene might show the technician attending a review meeting where residents question the emergency settings. This does not erase the achievement of preventing worse harm. It places that achievement within continuing accountability. Alternatively, a quieter scene could show a neighbour returning a borrowed tank with a repair the owner did not request but needed. The appropriate scale depends on the story. What matters is that the ending retains material evidence of the choices made, rather than using survival as proof that every earlier judgement was correct.","The speculative element should remain active in this aftermath. If the reservoir becomes merely a backdrop once the emotional conflict is resolved, the story may have underused its premise. Perhaps the town changes who can inspect the allocation settings, or decides to maintain a less efficient reserve that more people can access. Neither reform has to be presented as a universal recommendation. Within the fictional circumstances, it shows characters learning from the particular powers and failures of their system. The future becomes a consequence of the plot rather than an unrelated epilogue.","After reading, reconstruct the chain from premise to limit, from limit to choice, and from choice to consequence. Notice where the chain depends on physical rules and where it depends on a social decision that could have been otherwise. Then ask whose account of the outcome the ending privileges. This method allows appreciation of both invention and argument without reducing a story to a prediction or a policy proposal. The most revealing question is often not whether this world could exist, but what its characters make possible, necessary or acceptable once they believe that it does."],"glossary":{"premise":"kurmacanın dayandığı başlangıç varsayımı","conditional":"koşula bağlı","intake":"sistemin maddeyi içeri alma bölümü veya işlemi","retrospect":"geçmişe dönük değerlendirme","situated":"belirli bir konum ve deneyime bağlı","allocation":"kaynakların paylaştırılması","exposition":"kurmaca dünyaya ilişkin açıklayıcı bilgi","concessive":"karşıt durumu kabul edip savı sürdüren","delimit":"bir savın geçerlilik sınırını belirlemek","qualified":"koşul ve sınırlarla ifade edilmiş","evasive":"zor sonuçları ele almaktan kaçınan","accountability":"kararlar için açıklama ve hesap verme sorumluluğu"},"phrases":[{"en":"a system of consequences","tr":"birbirine bağlı sonuçlar sistemi"},{"en":"trace its consequences","tr":"sonuçlarının izini sürmek"},{"en":"intelligible in retrospect","tr":"geriye dönüp bakıldığında anlaşılabilir"},{"en":"the distribution of harm","tr":"zararın kimlere nasıl dağıldığı"},{"en":"at the point where","tr":"tam ... olduğu noktada"},{"en":"provided that","tr":"koşuluyla"},{"en":"insofar as","tr":"... olduğu ölçüde"},{"en":"within continuing accountability","tr":"süren bir hesap verme sorumluluğu içinde"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What turns the invented rain reservoir from an image into a dramatic premise?","options":["Its appearance is described in unusual colours.","It supports competing needs within limited capacity.","Its complete manufacturing history is provided.","It removes every ordinary social conflict."],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Aynı sınırlı mekanizmanın birbiriyle çatışan ihtiyaçları desteklemesi, icadı dramatik bir soruna dönüştürür."},{"q":"What makes an unexpected technical solution feel earned?","options":["It introduces unlimited power at the climax.","It proves every specialist previously foolish.","Its capacities and costs are prepared by earlier scenes.","It cancels the consequences of previous choices."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Çözümün araçları ve bedelleri önceden hazırlanmışsa okur yeni birleşimi kuralların keyfî değişimi olarak görmez."},{"q":"Why is consistent computer allocation not necessarily neutral?","options":["The ranking it implements contains prior value judgements.","Computers must always misunderstand instructions.","Technical settings cannot be inspected.","Consistency eliminates all practical benefits."],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Bilgisayarın tutarlı uyguladığı sıralama, önceden verilmiş değer ve öncelik kararlarını taşıyabilir."},{"q":"When does exposition most clearly earn its place?","options":["When it introduces the largest number of invented terms.","When it postpones essential constraints until after the choice.","When it replaces every practical exchange with a lecture.","When it changes what readers can anticipate, understand or judge."],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Açıklama, okurun sonuçları öngörmesini ve kararları değerlendirmesini değiştirdiği yerde işlev kazanır."},{"q":"Why should an ending retain the costs of a successful solution?","options":["Because no science-fiction character should succeed.","Because survival does not establish that every allocation was just.","Because the speculative mechanism must always fail.","Because every dispute requires a second climax."],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Hayatta kalmak ya da acili çözmek, alınan tüm kararların adil olduğunu kanıtlamaz; sonuçlar ve hesap verme sorumluluğu sürebilir."}],"words":1767,"length":"medium"},{"id":"tomorrows-mirror","type":"article","title":"Tomorrow's Mirror: Science Fiction and the Anxieties of Its Age","author":"Lantern Editorial","level":"C2","genre":"scifi","summaryTr":"Bilimkurgu geleceği değil, kendi çağının korkularını yansıtır. Yazı; Shelley ve Wells'ten Soğuk Savaş paranoyasına, distopyalardan yapay zekâya uzanarak türün 'ayna' işlevini tartışıyor.","paragraphs":["Science fiction has a reputation for predicting the future, and it is a reputation the genre has never quite deserved. Its celebrated hits, the satellite, the hand-held computer, the surveillance state, are remembered precisely because they are rare; the countless misses have been politely forgotten. What the genre does with remarkable consistency is something rather different. It takes the worries of its own present, enlarges them, projects them into an imaginary tomorrow and holds up a mirror to the result. The future, in other words, is a disguise worn by the present. Ursula K. Le Guin made a similar point when she insisted that science fiction is descriptive rather than predictive.","## A monster for the machine age","Many scholars trace the genre to Mary Shelley's Frankenstein (1818), written when electricity was still a startling mystery and the boundary between life and death seemed newly negotiable. Shelley's creature is less a prophecy than a question: what does a creator owe to what he has created? The industrial nineteenth century supplied the raw material for the genre's later flourishing, and in the 1890s H. G. Wells, who had studied biology under T. H. Huxley, turned scientific ideas into parables. The Time Machine (1895) projects class division so far into the future that the working poor and the idle rich have evolved into separate species, Victorian inequality made literal. The War of the Worlds (1898) opens with a pointed comparison: Wells reminds his readers of the fate of the Tasmanians at European hands, and invites them to imagine what it feels like to be on the receiving end of a technologically superior empire.","\"The Star\" (1897) shows Wells at his most coolly devastating. A massive body from deep space plunges towards the sun, and the Earth is shaken by heat, floods and upheaval, yet the narration remains almost clinical. The closing pages shift to a vantage point from which the catastrophe is only a minor astronomical incident, and the scale of human tragedy shrinks to nothing. It is a vertigo peculiar to the late nineteenth century, when, arguably, Darwin and the new astronomy had together dislodged humanity from the centre of creation.","## Atomic dread and Cold War paranoia","After 1945 the genre acquired a darker palette. Science fiction of the 1950s was saturated with invasion narratives, and later critics have often read them as projections of Cold War suspicion. Jack Finney's novel The Body Snatchers (serialised in 1954), and the 1956 film adapted from it, in which neighbours are quietly replaced by emotionless duplicates, have been interpreted, depending on the critic, as an allegory of communist infiltration or, conversely, of the enforced conformity of McCarthyism. The very elasticity of the allegory is revealing. In Japan, the film Godzilla (1954) turned the memory of nuclear catastrophe into a monster awakened by atomic testing, a case in point of how a nation's trauma can acquire a body and a roar.","Meanwhile, the dystopian tradition, represented by Yevgeny Zamyatin's We (written in the early 1920s), Aldous Huxley's Brave New World (1932) and George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949), asked what might happen if the political and technological tendencies of the twentieth century were pushed to their logical conclusion. Orwell's novel is sometimes mistaken for a forecast, but its enduring force lies in its diagnosis of how language and propaganda corrode the very idea of truth. The title is often said to be an inversion of 1948, the year in which he finished the manuscript.","## Estrangement: how the mirror works","Why does the genre need distant planets and alien species at all? Why not simply write about contemporary politics? The critic Darko Suvin offered an influential answer in Metamorphoses of Science Fiction (1979): the genre operates through cognitive estrangement, making the familiar strange while keeping the strangeness plausible and rationally explored. By transplanting a social problem to a distant planet, an author disarms the reflex defences that direct political argument provokes. Readers who would bristle at a lecture on prejudice may absorb the same insight when it arrives at one remove, wearing the skin of an alien species. Le Guin's The Left Hand of Darkness (1969), set on a world whose inhabitants have no fixed gender, invited readers to examine their assumptions about masculinity and femininity in a way that a realist novel of the time could scarcely have managed.","## From robots to algorithms","Each decade's technology supplies new imagery, but the underlying anxieties tend to recur. The word robot entered English through Karel Čapek's play R.U.R. (1920), which dramatised the fear that mass production might reduce human beings to mere labour; the Czech word robota refers to forced work. Cyberpunk, whose defining text is William Gibson's Neuromancer (1984), answered the dawn of the computer age and the growing power of corporations, and Gibson did much to popularise the word cyberspace. Today's fiction wrestles with machine intelligence, climate collapse and the quiet harvesting of personal data. The questions, however, remain stubbornly familiar: who controls the technology, who is excluded from its benefits, and what, in the end, remains distinctively human?","This is why old science fiction can stand the test of time even when its science has become obsolete. A story about a Martian invasion no longer persuades us about Mars, but it still tells us a great deal about empire. A tale of a runaway star no longer teaches astronomy, but it still captures a Victorian vertigo before an indifferent universe. The best science fiction fails as prophecy and succeeds as testimony. To read it well is to ask not \"Did it come true?\" but \"What was the author afraid of, and what are we afraid of now?\" The mirror, after all, shows whoever happens to be standing in front of it."],"glossary":{"dystopia":"distopya; baskıcı ve kötü bir toplum düzeni kurgusu","allegory":"alegori, soyut bir düşüncenin hikâye aracılığıyla mecazi anlatımı","cognitive estrangement":"bilişsel yabancılaştırma; tanıdık olanı mantıklı biçimde yabancı göstermek","parable":"ibretlik hikâye, mesel","cyberpunk":"siberpunk; teknoloji ve şirket gücü temalı bilimkurgu alt türü","vertigo":"baş dönmesi, boşluk karşısındaki sersemlik duygusu","clinical":"soğukkanlı, duygudan arınmış (klinik)","obsolete":"miadını doldurmuş, modası geçmiş"},"phrases":[{"en":"on the receiving end of","tr":"-in hedefi olan, -in mağduru durumunda"},{"en":"a case in point","tr":"buna iyi bir örnek"},{"en":"pushed to their logical conclusion","tr":"mantıksal sonucuna kadar götürülmüş"},{"en":"at one remove","tr":"dolaylı biçimde, bir adım uzaktan"},{"en":"stand the test of time","tr":"zamanın sınavına dayanmak"},{"en":"lies in","tr":"-de yatar, -e dayanır"},{"en":"scarcely have managed","tr":"güçlükle başarabilirdi"},{"en":"in other words","tr":"başka bir deyişle"},{"en":"bristle at","tr":"-e karşı tüyleri diken diken olmak, öfkelenmek"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the central claim of the article about science fiction?","options":["It mainly reflects the anxieties of its own age rather than accurately predicting the future","It has been proved right about most technological developments","It should be judged only by the accuracy of its science","It was invented in the twentieth century to describe the atomic age"],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Yazar, bilimkurgunun geleceği tahmin etmekten çok kendi çağının kaygılarını büyütüp yansıttığını savunur."},{"q":"Why does the article mention the Tasmanians in connection with The War of the Worlds?","options":["Wells wanted to explain how Martians travelled to Earth","Wells used their fate to help readers imagine being the victims of a superior power","Wells set part of the novel in Tasmania","Wells wanted to criticise Australian science"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Wells, Avrupalıların Tasmanyalılara yaptıklarını hatırlatarak okuru üstün bir gücün hedefi olmanın nasıl bir şey olduğunu düşünmeye çağırır."},{"q":"How does the concept of 'cognitive estrangement' explain the use of aliens and distant planets?","options":["It shows that readers prefer to avoid thinking about real problems","It proves that science fiction must be written by scientists","Setting a problem at a distance lowers readers' defences so they can absorb a difficult idea","It argues that alien characters are always symbols of the same political group"],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Sorun uzak bir gezegene taşındığında okurun savunma refleksi zayıflar; böylece zor bir fikir daha kolay kabul edilir."},{"q":"What does the final paragraph imply about reading older science fiction today?","options":["Such stories are useless once their science is outdated","Their main value is as a record of the fears of their time and a mirror for our own","They should be rewritten with accurate science","They are valuable only to historians of astronomy"],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Son paragraf, eski bilimkurgunun bilimi eskise de dönemin korkularına tanıklık ettiğini ve bizim korkularımızı da yansıttığını söyler."}],"words":923,"length":"short"},{"id":"what-the-mass-remembers","type":"story","title":"What the Mass Remembers","author":"Lantern Original","level":"C2","genre":"scifi","summaryTr":"Bir yerleşimden kurtarılan insanların ve o yerleşimin son arşivinin bulunduğu yük gemisi, güvenli bir buluşma için yeterince yavaşlayamaz. Seyrüsefer uzmanı Eda, yolcuların yaşamını korurken geçmişten neyin kurtarılacağına kimin karar vereceğiyle yüzleşir.","paragraphs":["The first thing Eda Sorel noticed was the absence of vibration. For eleven days the salvage vessel Parallax had shuddered around her like an elderly animal enduring a difficult journey. Now the deck beneath her boots was unnaturally still. In the navigation display, their projected rendezvous with the relief carrier became a close approach, then a miss. The computer did not use the word disaster. It preferred insufficient braking reserve, a description whose restraint Eda found personally offensive.","Captain Irin called from propulsion before the alarm sounded. A fragment shed by the outer shielding had punctured the reserve tank. Its contents were streaming behind them in a pale fan that looked, through the inspection camera, almost ornamental. The isolation valve had closed. It had also isolated most of the propellant they needed, on the wrong side of the rupture. They were carrying forty-seven people, one sleeping dog and the municipal archive of Vesper Station. None of those things would survive another circuit through the debris belt ahead.","The relief carrier could receive them if they matched its velocity at the rendezvous plane. It could not come farther in. Its great habitat drums had neither the acceleration nor the shielding for the belt, and the smaller rescue boats were already committed to other vessels. The carrier's commander stated these limitations without apology. Eda preferred that to sympathy. She asked for their final permissible arrival corridor and laid it across her display: a narrow volume of space, six hours away, that seemed an absurdly small place to put a future.","Irin came onto the bridge carrying a pressure gauge she had removed from its socket. She set it beside Eda, an obsolete gesture of verification. 'Tell me the options before you tell me the recommendation.' Eda ran the model again. They could burn what remained and arrive too fast. They could coast and miss the carrier entirely. Or they could feed nine tonnes of material through the conversion drive, whose chamber reduced suitable matter to a controllable reaction stream. The only compatible material aboard was the archive.","Vesper had stored its records in laminated ceramic plates. The medium was durable, compact and resistant to the intermittent power failures that had finally made the station uninhabitable. Its other useful property was unfortunate: once stripped of their protective frames, the plates were excellent conversion feedstock. Bas Neri, the archivist, had calculated the mass himself when they loaded the ship. Eda remembered his pleasure at finding an unexpected margin in the cargo allowance. He had brought an additional cabinet of oral histories because there had been room.","The archive weighed twelve tonnes. Nine would have to disappear into the drive. The figure already accounted for the ship becoming lighter as it consumed the plates; it was not a rough estimate that could be improved by remembering a clever principle. Eda checked the model against the manual tables. Irin checked it independently. Then they sat without speaking while the cooling pumps resumed their ordinary hum. Somewhere below them, a child was practising a tune on a plastic recorder, getting the same three notes wrong with unwavering confidence.","Bas arrived with a data slate tucked beneath his arm. He was sixty-eight and dressed as though a committee might unexpectedly inspect his cuffs. Eda explained the failure. He listened to the whole account, including the requirement for nine tonnes, then asked whether the cargo restraints counted. They did not. Their alloy would foul the conversion chamber. Neither would the ship's hull, the water supply or the furniture serve as usable feedstock. The emergency drive had been built for a particular class of industrial cargo. Their luck consisted of having that cargo and their misfortune of needing it.","'There are death certificates in those cabinets,' Bas said. 'And ownership records. And the testimony from the ventilation inquiry.' Eda knew. Her mother's final statement was in the inquiry collection, describing the maintenance contractor's decision to disable alarm thresholds. The company had dismissed that testimony as uncorroborated. Three weeks later, Vesper's central habitat had become impossible to ventilate. The evacuation had cost lives before the wider station failure began. Eda had taken this navigation contract partly to ensure that the records reached a place where somebody would have to read them.","Bas asked about copying the archive. The answer was less straightforward. The plates stored data as patterns through their depth. Reading them required a calibrated phase oscillator, and the archive's normal reader had been destroyed during loading. Parallax had one compatible oscillator in its guidance unit. They could disconnect it and improvise a reader at the feed throat. The computer could then decode each plate immediately before the drive destroyed it. But the guidance unit would lose the component it needed to coordinate the braking pulses precisely.","Their portable storage had ample capacity. Capacity was not the bottleneck. Nor was transmission: once decoded, files could be sent to the carrier through the directional link. The bottleneck was the single oscillator and the cooling capacity around it. Switching it repeatedly between reading and guidance would require recalibration, taking longer than their remaining flight. They had to decide which task it would perform during the burn. Eda explained this twice, because every person aboard would naturally think of copying, and nobody should be asked to accept a sacrifice concealed behind jargon.","Irin authorised a trial. While Eda computed a burn sequence that could be controlled manually, Bas and the propulsion engineer, Fen, carried a plate to the workshop. It contained drainage inspections from a district that no longer existed. Bas handled it with the same care he gave the oral histories. Fen removed a strip from its edge and ran it through the test reader. A three-dimensional map appeared on her slate, followed by a complaint about a leaking ceiling. They had recovered the contents. The physical strip crumbled when she touched it.","The result established possibility, not adequacy. At that rate they could read less than half the mass they needed to consume before reaching the rendezvous plane. Feeding the plates faster would destroy patterns before the oscillator resolved them. Feeding them slowly enough to preserve everything would leave them travelling too fast to dock. Eda asked Fen to test overlapping passes. The next strip produced a file with every fourth segment missing. Its reconstruction software invented a drainage pipe through a bedroom. Bas turned off automatic repair. 'A plausible record is not a surviving record,' he said.","They could save three tonnes intact and decode another portion during the burn. The rest would be lost. The amount recovered would depend on how many unread plates they interleaved with the slower scanning passes to maintain thrust. Eda built a schedule around the drive's minimum stable flow. If anything went wrong, the scanner would have to be bypassed immediately. Irin insisted on a physical lever that could do this without software permission. Bas watched Fen install it. He did not object, but his hands remained folded tightly enough to whiten the knuckles.","Manual guidance was the other uncertainty. Parallax's old optical sight had survived because nobody had considered it worth removing. Eda could use the carrier's beacon as a reference and adjust the pulses from calculated tables. Her instructors had taught the method as a historical exercise. The examination had allowed three attempts and a quiet room in which to review mistakes afterwards. She had practised it; she had never used it with people depending on the outcome. The distinction occupied her thoughts more than she wanted to admit.","They ran a simulation with the archive reader drawing its predicted power. For the first forty seconds Eda held the ship inside the corridor. Then a delay in the actuator response made her overcorrect. The simulated vessel crossed the carrier's plane beyond the receiving envelope. A red annotation appeared on the display. Fen, watching from the workshop, said they should use automatic guidance and accept the loss. She was not being unimaginative. She had seen the actual heat damage in the actuators, which the software model represented with a tidy probability distribution.","Eda asked for one more trial, this time with Irin calling the table entries and Fen reporting actuator lag in real time. They did not improve because Eda became more gifted. They improved because the task stopped being hers alone. Irin anticipated the next correction; Fen identified a sticking valve before Eda compensated in the wrong direction. They reached the receiving envelope with a small margin. On a third trial, using the least favourable measured lag, they did so again. Irin authorised the conversion. The authority did not make her look relieved.","They had four hours left. Bas wanted to begin selecting plates immediately, but Irin called a meeting in the passenger compartment. The forty-four evacuees who were not crew had a right to know what would happen to the cargo they had helped carry aboard. There would be no vote on whether to sacrifice passengers for documents. Irin made that boundary explicit. There would be a decision about how the recoverable portion was chosen, and about whether anyone with a personal stake should control that choice. Eda found herself looking at her own hands.","The compartment smelled of warm fabric, disinfectant and the orange sweets somebody had distributed to the children. Bas explained the archive's structure. Its public catalogue survived on his slate, so they knew what each cabinet contained. Some plates held thousands of short records; others held a few hours of dense testimony. Files were grouped into context bundles, preserving the links between a claim, its source and its subsequent corrections. Saving isolated fragments might produce more readable words while making those words less trustworthy. The archive was not a heap of interchangeable memories.","A former station manager proposed preserving the legal records first. A nurse asked whether patient histories counted as legal records. A young woman who had lost her father asked why maintenance reports should outrank the last recording of a person's voice. A man near the door wanted everything concerning the ventilation contractor saved, because otherwise the deaths would become nobody's fault. His argument was Eda's own. Hearing it spoken in that room, beside people with different losses, made it less self-evidently universal than it had sounded in her head.","Bas had prepared a priority list. It favoured official proceedings, engineering plans and records likely to establish rights after relocation. The list was defensible. It also reproduced the station's old hierarchy with unnerving fidelity: the people who had generated the most paperwork would remain the most visible. Informal carers, unregistered lodgers and people who distrusted institutions were represented mainly in private deposits and oral histories. Eda asked how much of that material the list preserved. Bas looked down at the slate. 'Very little,' he said. His embarrassment was more persuasive than a defence would have been.","The boy with the recorder was named Tavi. He asked whether his grandmother's recipes were in the archive. His father said this was not the time. Bas replied that it was exactly the time, although he could not promise that the recipes had been deposited. Tavi explained that his grandmother never measured the spice because she recognised its smell. If they saved the recipe without her explanation, he thought, it would not be a very good recipe. The nurse nodded. The adults had been discussing information; the child had described dependence between its parts.","Eda proposed preserving one small mandatory set: current medical information volunteered by the passengers, the catalogue itself and the archive's authentication keys. The remainder would be selected by a public draw among complete context bundles, weighted so that each bundle had the same chance per unit of storage. Large bundles would not be split merely because their survival was inconvenient. The method would not recognise importance. That was both its weakness and its purpose. Nobody could turn a position of authority aboard Parallax into the privilege of deciding whose past counted.","Bas objected that the draw might destroy irreplaceable evidence. Eda said every method would destroy irreplaceable evidence. A woman asked whether randomness was simply a way of avoiding responsibility. Eda considered that before replying. 'Only if we pretend the method chose itself. We would be choosing it, and recording why. I don't think we should call the result fair to the people whose records disappear.' The woman remained dissatisfied, but she stopped arguing that Eda was hiding behind a machine. It was the beginning of agreement, rather than its completion.","They adopted the proposal with conditions. Anyone could identify dependencies missing from the catalogue before the draw. The selection procedure and its starting value would be transmitted to the carrier before any plates were moved. A passenger, not Bas, would operate the selection program, and two others would check the physical loading order. Eda disclosed her mother's testimony and its location. The former manager disclosed records that might expose his own decisions. He had not wanted the inquiry preserved, he admitted. That admission changed the room more than any speech about solidarity could have done.","Fen interrupted to report a new constraint. Several cabinets had protective backing that could not enter the drive. Stripping it would take time they did not have if the plates were processed one at a time. They needed volunteers at three workstations. Nobody was to handle cracked ceramic without gloves. The meeting dissolved into practical groups, and the ethical dispute became a queue for tools. Eda watched Tavi's father join the team assigned to private deposits. He had no idea whether his mother's recipes were there. He began removing the frames anyway.","Before the draw, Bas took Eda aside. He had found her mother's bundle. It occupied less than a twentieth of the intact allowance. He could include it in the authentication material without anyone noticing; its records had been used to challenge the contractor's own data, so the classification would not be wholly indefensible. He did not offer the exception casually. He believed the inquiry might prevent the same abuses elsewhere. That made the offer harder to refuse. Simple corruption would have been easier to recognise than an exception supported by a cause she believed in.","Eda asked whether Bas would make the same offer to the woman seeking her father's voice. He said the consequences were different. She agreed. Then she said those consequences were precisely what they had decided not to let two people rank in private. Bas closed the catalogue. For several seconds she hated him for having put the possibility within reach. Afterwards she understood that she had been grateful to have somebody else to blame for the temptation. Her mother had not become less valuable when she refused. Refusal had merely become something she would have to live with.","The carrier confirmed receipt of the selection procedure. The nurse entered the starting value while the passenger compartment watched. Cabinet identifiers rolled down the screen. Some bundles were assigned to the intact reserve, others to the scan sequence, and the remainder to direct conversion. Eda found the ventilation inquiry halfway down the final group. Its companion maintenance logs had been selected for scanning. The distinction was devastatingly impersonal. Her mother's spoken account would be destroyed; some of the measurements that supported it might survive. She asked Irin to take her console for five minutes.","In the small washroom, Eda pressed her palms against the wall and tried to recall the exact cadence of her mother's voice. She could remember what it had said more easily than how it had sounded. This seemed a betrayal until she recognised it as one of the ordinary limits the archive had helped her postpone. There was no time to read the plates separately. The oscillator was already being transferred, and recalibrating it for a private preview would reduce the recoverable amount. She rinsed her face, dried the water from her wrists and returned to the bridge.","Irin did not ask whether she was ready. She asked Eda to verify the optical sight's alignment. The question gave her something she could answer. They checked the carrier beacon against two background sources and locked the reference. Fen reported that the conversion chamber was ready. Bas reported the loading order, with two passenger witnesses confirming each cabinet number. Eda read back the first pulse duration. Her mouth was dry, and she could hear the slight roughness in Irin's breathing. Competence did not abolish fear; it supplied fear with a sequence of tasks.","The first plates entered unread. Their destruction was needed to bring the chamber to its stable operating temperature before scanning could begin. On the cargo camera, they vanished between rollers without ceremony. Eda had expected some visual event proportionate to what was happening. Instead there was a pressure rise, a correction to the ship's orientation and a line of green indicators. She kept the beacon inside the sight's central bracket. Fen called the actuator delay. Irin counted the seconds until the next planned interruption. Below them, Bas said the first scanned bundle had been recovered.","It was a set of tenancy disputes concerning a corridor now open to vacuum. The carrier acknowledged the files and their authentication values. A passenger in the workshop cheered, then apologised, uncertain whether celebration was appropriate while other records were being destroyed. Bas told her not to apologise. The next bundle contained recordings of a school concert, a complaint about the concert's noise and the school's reply. Eda heard this inventory over the working channel and understood why context mattered. A vanished place had contained people who could dislike one another without either of them being wrong.","At the first long pulse, the port actuator lagged beyond the measured range. Fen called it immediately. Eda held the correction instead of adding another, waiting for the valve to move. The beacon slid towards the edge of the bracket. Her training urged an intervention that the previous simulation had shown would be excessive. She counted once, twice. The actuator responded. The beacon returned, though less neatly than she wanted. Irin marked the consumed margin. They had recovered it physically, but not mathematically: the next correction would have less room for error.","The scanner began reporting heat saturation. Its cooling loop shared a radiator with the drive controls, and the sustained pulse had warmed the return flow. Fen could reduce the scanning rate, bypass a sequence of plates or pause thrust while the loop cooled. Pausing would require a later pulse longer than the damaged actuator could safely sustain. They bypassed the next sequence. Bas read its identifiers aloud as it entered direct conversion. Eda asked why he was doing that. 'Because otherwise the log will only say temperature corrected,' he answered.","A sharp crack came over the cargo microphone. The rollers stopped. One plate had fractured diagonally and lodged across the feed throat, blocking both the scan path and the bypass. The chamber's reserve was falling. Fen shut the admission gate before the reaction became unstable. Eda watched their projected arrival move towards the edge of the carrier's receiving envelope. They had minutes to clear the obstruction, not hours. The archive was no longer merely something they were choosing to sacrifice. Its material resistance was now an immediate threat to everyone aboard.","The service hatch could be opened only after the throat had depressurised. Fen started the sequence, then discovered that the fractured plate was preventing the inner seal from seating. Bas proposed reaching through the access sleeve with a clamp. Fen rejected the clamp: if its jaws broke, its alloy would contaminate the chamber. Eda looked at the workshop feed. The protective frames they had removed lay stacked along one wall. Their ceramic spacers were compatible with the drive. Could they use a spacer as a wedge? Fen checked its composition label and said yes.","The nurse and Tavi's father held the access sleeve steady while Fen worked the wedge through it. Bas used the catalogue image to identify the plate's internal seam, a detail he had once dismissed as a manufacturing nuisance. Fen pressed there. The plate separated into two smaller pieces, both of which dropped into the bypass channel. She withdrew the sleeve before the seal closed. No one had performed an impossible feat. Four people had used information and materials that happened to remain available. Eda found that more moving than heroism would have been.","Restarting cost them another block of unread plates. Their revised trajectory still intersected the receiving envelope, but close to its outer limit. The carrier suggested releasing the intact reserve as inert mass to reduce the remaining braking requirement. Eda ran the calculation. It would help, though far less than feeding that material through the drive. The released cabinets would continue on their old trajectory into the belt. There would be no later rescue. Irin asked whether preserving the reserve still left a defensible arrival margin. Eda answered that it did, provided the last two pulses behaved within the new measured limits.","'And if they don't?' Irin asked. 'Then we use the bypass and increase feed. We destroy whatever remains in the scan queue.' The intact reserve was already secured away from the throat and could not be moved quickly enough to serve as an emergency correction. Irin considered the calculation, asked Fen for an independent assessment and kept the reserve aboard. It was a risk, but every available course now contained one. The relevant question was whether they understood its bounds, not whether they could describe their preferred option as safe.","While Fen reset the chamber, Bas discovered an error in the loading queue. Two crates had been exchanged after their frames were removed. One contained the ventilation inquiry. The other contained private deposits selected for direct conversion. Both were scheduled to disappear, so the exchange had not altered the draw's outcome. But the inquiry now stood beside the throat at the exact point where scanning was due to resume. Leaving it there would preserve Eda's mother's testimony while destroying a later bundle that had actually been selected. Nobody on the bridge would have known if Bas had remained silent.","He reported the error over the open channel. Eda closed her eyes for one breath, then thanked him. There was enough time to restore the order if the passengers shifted two crates together. She could see them doing it on the camera: ordinary backs bending, gloved fingers finding purchase, a label turned towards the verification lens. The decision she had made earlier was being made again in its physical form. It did not become easier through repetition. She concentrated on the sight while her mother's cabinet moved into the unread queue.","The penultimate pulse began. Eda's world narrowed to the beacon, the timing calls and Fen's reports. The drive shook harder than it had before the rupture. From the workshop came a sequence of file acknowledgements, each interrupted by the hiss of plates entering conversion. A maintenance technician's inspection notes survived. A family's holiday recordings did not. One bundle contained conflicting versions of a neighbourhood meeting, preserved together because nobody had agreed which minutes were accurate. Bas sounded almost amused when he announced it. Even under these conditions, the archive had refused to become a coherent monument.","Then the carrier's beacon flickered out. Eda froze the current setting. Irin called the backup frequency, and the carrier replied that its transmitter had switched power buses. The interruption would last several seconds. Automatic guidance would have extrapolated the target's position through the gap. Eda had tables and a secondary reference star, but the last actuator response had introduced uncertainty into their orientation. She could continue the pulse blind or cut it short. Continuing might save more records by avoiding an extra restart. She cut it short. The archive would absorb the cost of that choice.","The beacon returned after nine seconds. Fen reported sufficient chamber reserve for one restart, but the thermal margin for further scanning had gone. Eda verified the revised timing. Bas was already standing beside the bypass lever. He asked whether the interruption meant the rest of the selected scan queue would be lost. Irin said yes. Bas laid one hand on the final crate as if to steady himself. Then he pulled the lever. The scanner fell silent. Plates moved directly into the throat, and the ship began its last sustained deceleration.","Eda kept her eyes on the beacon. It drifted, settled and drifted again. Irin's calls were now spaced between Fen's warnings about the port actuator. Eda made the smallest corrections the valve would reliably accept. The ship's projected arrival entered the receiving envelope and continued towards its centre, but stopping at the centre was no longer possible without another correction they could not afford. She ended the pulse inside the boundary with the valve still functioning. On the display, insufficient braking reserve disappeared. In its place came rendezvous solution valid.","Nobody cheered on the bridge. Eda waited for the carrier to confirm the measured result rather than trust the prediction. The reply took four seconds. She would remember those seconds more clearly than the entire final burn: a loose screw trembling beside the pressure gauge, Irin's thumb pressed against a split in the console covering, a faint smell of hot dust. Then the carrier confirmed that a receiving boat was on its way. Eda unclipped her harness and discovered that her legs were too weak to support the confidence she had been performing.","Bas came up carrying the catalogue slate. They had preserved three tonnes intact and successfully decoded a little under two more. Some recovered files were damaged; those had been marked as incomplete rather than repaired into plausibility. More than half the archive was gone. Eda asked about the inquiry's maintenance logs. Two associated bundles had survived, including a sequence of alarm adjustments signed by the contractor's supervisor. The evidence might matter. It did not contain her mother's voice. Bas said he was sorry without trying to convert one fact into compensation for the other.","The receiving boat attached through the forward lock. Its crew brought fresh air cylinders and a list of instructions that seemed extravagantly ordinary. Passengers were to keep bags small, wear their transfer straps and leave the passage clear. Tavi asked whether the dog needed a strap. His father said yes, probably, and began searching for one. Eda watched the boy offer the dog his recorder as a distraction. The animal declined. For the first time since the tank rupture, something happened aboard Parallax that had no bearing on whether they would live.","Bas refused to leave until the intact cabinets had been secured for transfer. The relief crew initially classified them as nonessential cargo. Irin supplied their mass and attachment requirements, then informed the crew that the passengers had already paid more than enough for the distinction between essential and expendable. The boat could take the cabinets on a second trip. There was no need to abandon them now. Eda remained aboard with Bas while the others crossed. Through the lock's small window, she saw strangers become a receiving crowd, faces turning towards each new arrival.","Waiting for the second boat, Bas asked whether she regretted the draw. Eda said she regretted several of its results. He waited for her to distinguish that from regretting the decision itself. She could not give him the distinction cleanly. A method could remain defensible while its consequences hurt more than one had anticipated. Bas sat beside her on an empty restraint frame. Neither suggested that having survived entitled them to stop examining what they had done. When the docking signal sounded again, they rose together to guide the cabinets through the lock.","On the carrier, the archive was given a temporary berth in a room that had previously stored exercise equipment. Bas put a handwritten notice on the door: Incomplete collection. Catalogue includes destroyed holdings. Someone suggested removing the lost items from the public index to avoid disappointing researchers. He refused. A missing record was not the same as a record that had never existed. Eda helped him add the conversion log, the selection procedure and the names of those who had checked the loading. Their decisions became part of what later readers would have to examine.","The ventilation contractor's representatives challenged the surviving maintenance logs. They questioned the emergency reader, the chain of custody and Eda's involvement. These were questions she had expected, though expectation did not make them pleasant. The transmitted authentication values and the independently received conversion log established which files had survived without alteration. They did not establish every claim her mother had made. At the preliminary hearing, Eda resisted an invitation to describe the archive as complete evidence. She said precisely what it could support, and precisely what had been destroyed.","The hearing produced an order preserving the contractor's own remaining records. It was a beginning, not a verdict. Eda left the room feeling neither vindicated nor defeated. Outside, the former station manager waited to give his account of the decisions he had once hoped would disappear. He asked whether she wanted to hear it first. She said no; he should give it to the investigators in his own words. She had learned how tempting it was to shape testimony before it entered the record, especially when one believed the intended shape was true.","Several weeks later, Bas sent her a file recovered from the private deposits. It was a recording of a communal kitchen argument about whether a window should remain open. Eda's mother appeared briefly in the background, complaining that somebody had used the good scissors to cut wire. Her voice was distracted, mildly exasperated, entirely alive. The file did not restore her final statement. It did not make the draw providential. Had it belonged to another woman, its survival would have been no less valuable. Eda listened once, then sent a copy to her brother without adding an explanation.","When Parallax was cleared for repairs, Eda returned to retrieve the pressure gauge Irin had left on the bridge. The cargo hold was nearly empty. A fine ceramic dust remained along the edges of the feed throat, though the chamber itself had been cleaned. She resisted the impulse to collect some in a bag. It was not her mother, nor was it the station reduced to a symbol she could own. It was residue from a decision whose consequences would continue beyond her ability to measure them. She closed the service panel and signed the inspection record.","The archive opened to visitors on a quiet morning. Tavi came with his father to search for the recipes. They found none, but an elderly passenger offered to show him how she judged the spice by its smell. Bas asked permission to record their session. Tavi considered the request with unexpected seriousness, then agreed on condition that mistakes were kept in. Eda, waiting by the door, heard the recorder begin. There was a clatter of utensils, a question asked twice and laughter that obscured the first answer. The collection, irreparably diminished, acquired something new."],"glossary":{"rendezvous":"uzay araçlarının planlı buluşması","propellant":"itki üretmek için kullanılan madde","feedstock":"dönüştürme motoruna verilen ham madde","oscillator":"hassas salınım üreten aygıt","bottleneck":"sistemin kapasitesini sınırlayan dar nokta","actuator":"komutu fiziksel harekete dönüştüren düzenek","corroborated":"bağımsız kanıtlarla desteklenmiş","authentication":"verinin gerçekliğini ve bütünlüğünü doğrulama","throat":"motorun malzeme giriş boğazı","extrapolated":"eldeki veriden hareketle ilerisi hesaplanmış","vindicated":"haklılığı doğrulanmış","providential":"sanki kader tarafından özellikle sağlanmış","custody":"kanıtın korunması ve el değiştirme sorumluluğu","residue":"işlemden kalan artık"},"phrases":[{"en":"on the wrong side","tr":"yanlış tarafta"},{"en":"in real time","tr":"gerçek zamanlı olarak"},{"en":"with a personal stake","tr":"sonuçtan kişisel olarak etkilenen"},{"en":"a position of authority","tr":"yetki sahibi bir konum"},{"en":"chain of custody","tr":"kanıtın korunma ve el değiştirme kaydı"},{"en":"take her console","tr":"onun kumanda görevini devralmak"},{"en":"had no bearing on","tr":"üzerinde hiçbir etkisi yoktu"},{"en":"on condition that","tr":"şartıyla"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can the crew not simply copy the entire archive before destroying the plates?","options":["Their portable storage is too small.","The records have no surviving catalogue.","Reading speed, cooling and the shared guidance oscillator impose limits.","The carrier refuses to receive civilian files."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Sorun depolama kapasitesi değildir; okuma hızı, soğutma sınırı ve tek osilatörün rehberlik sistemiyle paylaşılması bütün arşivin kopyalanmasını engeller."},{"q":"What is a principal objection to Bas's initial priority list?","options":["It favours people already well represented in official paperwork.","It gives every context bundle an equal chance.","It excludes all engineering records.","It reserves most space for children's recipes."],"answer":0,"explainTr":"Resmî kayıtları öne alan liste, zaten kurumlarda daha görünür olan kişileri yeniden ayrıcalıklı kılar; özel anlatılarla temsil edilenler geri planda kalır."},{"q":"Why does Eda reject the private exception for her mother's testimony?","options":["She considers the testimony unreliable.","She believes the inquiry has already ended.","She knows another copy is aboard the carrier.","It would privately override the selection method agreed by the passengers."],"answer":3,"explainTr":"Eda tanıklığın önemini kabul eder ama iki kişinin özel kararıyla ortak yöntemin değiştirilmesini reddeder."},{"q":"Why does Eda stop the pulse when the carrier's beacon disappears?","options":["The archive reader has completed every selected file.","She prioritises a controlled arrival over saving the remaining scan queue.","She wants the carrier to move closer.","The optical sight no longer needs a reference."],"answer":1,"explainTr":"Eda belirsiz yönelimle itkiyi sürdürmek yerine güvenli yaklaşımı önceler; yeniden başlatmanın bedeli kalan tarama sırasının kaybıdır."},{"q":"What does Bas's refusal to remove destroyed holdings from the catalogue express?","options":["He expects every lost file to be reconstructed.","He wants to conceal the emergency selection.","Documented absence differs from pretending a record never existed.","He considers the surviving archive complete."],"answer":2,"explainTr":"Kayıp kayıtların katalogda kalması, neyin var olup yok edildiğini görünür tutar; kaybı hiç var olmamışlıkla eşitlemez."}],"words":5194,"length":"long"}];
